window.WIKI_SEARCH = [{"id":"introduction","title":"Introduction","text":"Introduction A practical guide to modding Ballistic.exe with UE4SS and Lua. This wiki covers setting up your mod folder, finding game objects, hooking functions and troubleshooting scripts. Start with a small change, check it in-game, and build from there. You don’t need Unreal Editor for the Lua examples here. UE4SS loads your scripts alongside the game and lets them interact with objects and functions that Unreal exposes at runtime. Before you start The examples come from the Windows demo. Game updates can change class names, functions and file layouts. Keep a backup of your saves and check your own build’s log when something behaves differently. Getting started If you’re new to Unreal modding, follow these pages in order. Guide What you’ll do Installing UE4SS Set up the loader and check that it starts. Your first mod Create a Lua script and find its output in the log. Working with hooks Run code when a game function is called. Debugging Track down missing objects, Lua errors and crashes. What can you do with Lua? Lua mods can inspect loaded objects, call reflected functions, respond to game events and create UI using assets the game already contains. Some changes need only a few lines of code. Adjust workshop behaviour and add controls to existing menus. Read weapon or target information for tools and overlays. Spawn and arrange existing assets at runtime. Add keybinds, diagnostics and small quality-of-life changes. These are starting points, not a guarantee that every game system is accessible. See Custom content for the limits of a Lua-only mod. A small example A mod begins with Scripts/main.lua . This is enough to check that the loader is running your file: print(\"[MyFirstMod] Hello from Ballistic!\\n\") The message appears in ue4ss/UE4SS.log after you launch the game. The first mod guide shows the complete folder structure. Using this wiki Use the sidebar to browse, or press Ctrl + K to search the text of every guide. Each article has links to its sections and a copy button beside code examples. For the complete loader API, use the UE4SS documentation . The game-specific paths here are observations from the demo, rather than a supported game SDK. For the underlying engine concepts, use Reflection , Unreal types , Containers and Transforms . For a specific failure or term, use the FAQ , glossary or hook reference . Choose a learning path You can follow the wiki in order, but you do not need to understand every game system before writing a small mod. Pick the path that matches the change you want to make, and stop at a result you can actually verify. I have not written a Lua mod Install the loader and confirm a fresh log. Run a single startup message before calling any game function. Work through Lua basics until tables, callbacks and nil checks make sense. Split the mod into modules only once the single-file version works. Build the read-only weapon inspector and compare its snapshots. Your first milestone is not an elaborate menu. It is a script that you can install, identify in a log, trigger once and disable cleanly. I want to change a weapon or attachment Read Weapon assemblies before editing a mesh or part. Then follow Attachments & rails and Weapon stats . The workflow is: identify the actual live gun, record a baseline, use one normal attachment action, inspect the changed branch and verify the result after leaving the menu. Save/reload is a separate milestone. I want to add a menu control Start with Finding game objects and Workshop UI . Learn which widget owns the canvas and how it is rebuilt. Create a visible but inactive control first, confirm its placement, then connect exactly one action. Test closing the menu while that action is pending. I want targets, scoring or a range feature Follow target discovery , hit attribution and range travel . Keep a cosmetic scene, a scoring mode and a cooked map as separate goals. A new card and a row name do not produce a new map package. Read an example’s context before copying it A block labelled “inside the callback above” is a fragment, not a complete main.lua. A block marked “pure Lua” does not need a live Unreal object. A block marked “mutating” changes state and should not be used as a discovery probe. When a result is shown as \u003craw numeric value> , it describes an output format rather than a captured measurement. Exact numeric expectations appear only where a specific recorded fixture supports them. Evidence levels explain the difference. When you get stuck Reduce the problem to the last result you could prove. Did the file load? Was the class loaded? Did the callback run? Was it the active instance? Did the normal game action complete? Those are different questions. Use Debugging to work through them instead of adding more code around an unknown failure. All guides Getting started Introduction Installing UE4SS Your first mod Lua basics for modding Organising a mod Changelog Lua scripting Working with hooks Finding game objects Timers & cleanup Reading data tables Debugging Performance & profiling Settings & local files Unreal fundamentals Reflection, classes & instances Names, text & soft references Arrays, sets & maps Vectors, rotations & spaces Worlds, players & controllers Reading object dumps Walkthroughs Build a weapon inspector Test a mod step by step Build a target inspector Plan a runtime range Weapons Weapon assemblies Attachments & rails Weapon stats Shots & ballistics Cartridges & projectiles Magazines & reloading Aiming, cameras & recoil Ranges & targets Finding & tracking targets Hits & scoring Range menus & travel Menus & saves Workshop UI Ownership & unlocks Reading & backing up saves Input & UI Input, focus & cursors UMG panels & layout HUDs, markers & nameplates Runtime content Asset paths & loading Spawning & actor ownership Meshes, materials & textures Lighting & exposure diagnosis Collision & physics Weapon audio & spatial sound Muzzle flashes, tracers & impacts Networking Multiplayer architecture Steam sessions & invites Messages & reliable events Movement interpolation Clocks, latency & round timing Chat, names & text validation Reference Game paths & classes Custom content Packaging a Lua mod Checking game updates Coverage & testing notes Tools & resources Game hook reference Modding glossary Troubleshooting FAQ","sections":[{"id":"getting-started","title":"Getting started","text":"If you’re new to Unreal modding, follow these pages in order. Guide What you’ll do Installing UE4SS Set up the loader and check that it starts. Your first mod Create a Lua script and find its output in the log. Working with hooks Run code when a game function is called. Debugging Track down missing objects, Lua errors and crashes."},{"id":"what-lua-can-do","title":"What can you do with Lua?","text":"Lua mods can inspect loaded objects, call reflected functions, respond to game events and create UI using assets the game already contains. Some changes need only a few lines of code. Adjust workshop behaviour and add controls to existing menus. Read weapon or target information for tools and overlays. Spawn and arrange existing assets at runtime. Add keybinds, diagnostics and small quality-of-life changes. These are starting points, not a guarantee that every game system is accessible. See Custom content for the limits of a Lua-only mod."},{"id":"a-small-example","title":"A small example","text":"A mod begins with Scripts/main.lua . This is enough to check that the loader is running your file: print(\"[MyFirstMod] Hello from Ballistic!\\n\") The message appears in ue4ss/UE4SS.log after you launch the game. The first mod guide shows the complete folder structure."},{"id":"using-this-wiki","title":"Using this wiki","text":"Use the sidebar to browse, or press Ctrl + K to search the text of every guide. Each article has links to its sections and a copy button beside code examples. For the complete loader API, use the UE4SS documentation . The game-specific paths here are observations from the demo, rather than a supported game SDK. For the underlying engine concepts, use Reflection , Unreal types , Containers and Transforms . For a specific failure or term, use the FAQ , glossary or hook reference ."},{"id":"choose-a-learning-path","title":"Choose a learning path","text":"You can follow the wiki in order, but you do not need to understand every game system before writing a small mod. Pick the path that matches the change you want to make, and stop at a result you can actually verify."},{"id":"beginner-path","title":"I have not written a Lua mod","text":"Install the loader and confirm a fresh log. Run a single startup message before calling any game function. Work through Lua basics until tables, callbacks and nil checks make sense. Split the mod into modules only once the single-file version works. Build the read-only weapon inspector and compare its snapshots. Your first milestone is not an elaborate menu. It is a script that you can install, identify in a log, trigger once and disable cleanly."},{"id":"weapon-path","title":"I want to change a weapon or attachment","text":"Read Weapon assemblies before editing a mesh or part. Then follow Attachments & rails and Weapon stats . The workflow is: identify the actual live gun, record a baseline, use one normal attachment action, inspect the changed branch and verify the result after leaving the menu. Save/reload is a separate milestone."},{"id":"ui-path","title":"I want to add a menu control","text":"Start with Finding game objects and Workshop UI . Learn which widget owns the canvas and how it is rebuilt. Create a visible but inactive control first, confirm its placement, then connect exactly one action. Test closing the menu while that action is pending."},{"id":"range-path","title":"I want targets, scoring or a range feature","text":"Follow target discovery , hit attribution and range travel . Keep a cosmetic scene, a scoring mode and a cooked map as separate goals. A new card and a row name do not produce a new map package."},{"id":"read-examples-correctly","title":"Read an example’s context before copying it","text":"A block labelled “inside the callback above” is a fragment, not a complete main.lua. A block marked “pure Lua” does not need a live Unreal object. A block marked “mutating” changes state and should not be used as a discovery probe. When a result is shown as \u003craw numeric value> , it describes an output format rather than a captured measurement. Exact numeric expectations appear only where a specific recorded fixture supports them. Evidence levels explain the difference."},{"id":"when-you-get-stuck","title":"When you get stuck","text":"Reduce the problem to the last result you could prove. Did the file load? Was the class loaded? Did the callback run? Was it the active instance? Did the normal game action complete? Those are different questions. Use Debugging to work through them instead of adding more code around an unknown failure."},{"id":"guide-index","title":"All guides","text":""},{"id":"guide-group-0","title":"Getting started","text":"Introduction Installing UE4SS Your first mod Lua basics for modding Organising a mod Changelog"},{"id":"guide-group-1","title":"Lua scripting","text":"Working with hooks Finding game objects Timers & cleanup Reading data tables Debugging Performance & profiling Settings & local files"},{"id":"guide-group-2","title":"Unreal fundamentals","text":"Reflection, classes & instances Names, text & soft references Arrays, sets & maps Vectors, rotations & spaces Worlds, players & controllers Reading object dumps"},{"id":"guide-group-3","title":"Walkthroughs","text":"Build a weapon inspector Test a mod step by step Build a target inspector Plan a runtime range"},{"id":"guide-group-4","title":"Weapons","text":"Weapon assemblies Attachments & rails Weapon stats Shots & ballistics Cartridges & projectiles Magazines & reloading Aiming, cameras & recoil"},{"id":"guide-group-5","title":"Ranges & targets","text":"Finding & tracking targets Hits & scoring Range menus & travel"},{"id":"guide-group-6","title":"Menus & saves","text":"Workshop UI Ownership & unlocks Reading & backing up saves"},{"id":"guide-group-7","title":"Input & UI","text":"Input, focus & cursors UMG panels & layout HUDs, markers & nameplates"},{"id":"guide-group-8","title":"Runtime content","text":"Asset paths & loading Spawning & actor ownership Meshes, materials & textures Lighting & exposure diagnosis Collision & physics Weapon audio & spatial sound Muzzle flashes, tracers & impacts"},{"id":"guide-group-9","title":"Networking","text":"Multiplayer architecture Steam sessions & invites Messages & reliable events Movement interpolation Clocks, latency & round timing Chat, names & text validation"},{"id":"guide-group-10","title":"Reference","text":"Game paths & classes Custom content Packaging a Lua mod Checking game updates Coverage & testing notes Tools & resources Game hook reference Modding glossary Troubleshooting FAQ"}]},{"id":"installation","title":"Installing UE4SS","text":"Installing UE4SS Set up the loader, then check the log before adding a mod. What you need The Windows version of Ballistic.exe. A UE4SS release compatible with your game build. A text editor, such as Visual Studio Code. The local demo tests used UE4SS 3.0.1 Beta #0 , commit 5e627997 . This records a tested setup; it is not a recommendation to overwrite a working installation with a different release. Downloads: UE4SS releases · Experimental builds . Find the game folder In Steam, open the game’s settings and choose Manage → Browse local files . From there, open: Ballistic/Binaries/Win64/ You should see Ballistic-Win64-Shipping.exe . The smaller Ballistic.exe in the game’s top-level folder is a launcher. Install the loader beside the shipping executable. Install the loader Close the game. Extract the chosen UE4SS package beside the shipping executable, preserving the package’s folder layout. Merge folders carefully if you already have other mods installed. Launch the game once, then close it and inspect the log. The setup used for these guides has the following layout. Other loader packages can use a different working directory; follow the installation instructions for your release . Win64/ ├── Ballistic-Win64-Shipping.exe ├── dwmapi.dll └── ue4ss/ ├── UE4SS.dll ├── UE4SS-settings.ini ├── UE4SS.log └── Mods/ Check the log Open ue4ss/UE4SS.log . Look for the loader version and startup messages. The file should have a fresh timestamp from your last launch. If it is missing or unchanged, check the extraction location and that the files match your loader release. If the game crashes before mods load, resolve the loader setup before adding scripts. Next step Continue to Your first mod to create a script and verify it with a unique log message. Step-by-step installation check 1. Preserve a working setup If mods already work, record the current loader version from the log and copy its configuration before replacing anything. Keep the existing Mods directory separate from downloaded loader files. A new loader is not automatically the answer to a broken script. Use the release archive’s contents as the authority for its layout. Some packages keep UE4SS.dll in a ue4ss subfolder; others use a different root/working-directory arrangement. Do not move individual DLLs into a layout guessed from an unrelated tutorial. 2. Check the executable you are beside Walk from the Steam install folder through Ballistic → Binaries → Win64. Confirm the filename is Ballistic-Win64-Shipping.exe. If the directory only contains the small top-level launcher, go deeper before extracting. This distinction matters because a proxy loader must be discovered by the process it is intended to load into. A correctly named file beside the wrong executable can leave you with no loader startup and no useful Lua error. 3. Extract without adding an extra directory After extraction, inspect the result rather than trusting the extraction dialog. A common mistake is an extra archive-name folder between Win64 and the loader files, or between Mods and the mod itself. Compare your final paths with the package’s instructions. Do not rename a dependency to “fix” a path problem. Preserve filenames and move back to a known layout if the first launch fails. 4. Do a loader-only launch Start the game before adding your test script. Wait for a normal menu, exit, and inspect a log with a fresh timestamp. Record the loader’s version and whether startup reached its normal event loop. This creates a baseline for distinguishing an installation problem from a later mod problem. Observed result Interpretation Next action No fresh loader log The loader may not have started, or you are reading the wrong working directory. Recheck the shipping directory and package layout. Fresh log, no test-mod prefix The loader started; your mod discovery or script load still needs checking. Follow the first-mod folder check. Lua syntax error naming your file The script was found but could not compile. Fix the indicated source before engine debugging. Crash before your script loads The first failure is not proof your callback ran. Compare against the loader-only baseline. 5. Add one test mod Use the minimal first-mod layout , launch again and look for its unique prefix. Keep the loader configuration unchanged during this step. If you change loader version, file layout and script behaviour together, a successful or failed launch is harder to explain. Upgrading and removing Close the game before updating. Identify files owned by the loader and files owned by individual mods, then follow the chosen release’s update instructions. Preserve your mod configuration and remove obsolete loader files only when you know they belong to that old installation. To disable one example mod, remove its enable marker with the game closed. Do not delete shared loader files to disable a single script. Uninstalling a behaviour mod may leave changes already saved by the game, so refit or restore a disposable assembly before testing removal. For version-specific details, consult the loader installation guide . This wiki’s local setup records are not a compatibility list for every release.","sections":[{"id":"what-you-need","title":"What you need","text":"The Windows version of Ballistic.exe. A UE4SS release compatible with your game build. A text editor, such as Visual Studio Code. The local demo tests used UE4SS 3.0.1 Beta #0 , commit 5e627997 . This records a tested setup; it is not a recommendation to overwrite a working installation with a different release. Downloads: UE4SS releases · Experimental builds ."},{"id":"find-the-game-folder","title":"Find the game folder","text":"In Steam, open the game’s settings and choose Manage → Browse local files . From there, open: Ballistic/Binaries/Win64/ You should see Ballistic-Win64-Shipping.exe . The smaller Ballistic.exe in the game’s top-level folder is a launcher. Install the loader beside the shipping executable."},{"id":"install-the-loader","title":"Install the loader","text":"Close the game. Extract the chosen UE4SS package beside the shipping executable, preserving the package’s folder layout. Merge folders carefully if you already have other mods installed. Launch the game once, then close it and inspect the log. The setup used for these guides has the following layout. Other loader packages can use a different working directory; follow the installation instructions for your release . Win64/ ├── Ballistic-Win64-Shipping.exe ├── dwmapi.dll └── ue4ss/ ├── UE4SS.dll ├── UE4SS-settings.ini ├── UE4SS.log └── Mods/"},{"id":"check-the-log","title":"Check the log","text":"Open ue4ss/UE4SS.log . Look for the loader version and startup messages. The file should have a fresh timestamp from your last launch. If it is missing or unchanged, check the extraction location and that the files match your loader release. If the game crashes before mods load, resolve the loader setup before adding scripts."},{"id":"next-step","title":"Next step","text":"Continue to Your first mod to create a script and verify it with a unique log message."},{"id":"installation-walkthrough","title":"Step-by-step installation check","text":""},{"id":"step-1-preserve-a-working-setup","title":"1. Preserve a working setup","text":"If mods already work, record the current loader version from the log and copy its configuration before replacing anything. Keep the existing Mods directory separate from downloaded loader files. A new loader is not automatically the answer to a broken script. Use the release archive’s contents as the authority for its layout. Some packages keep UE4SS.dll in a ue4ss subfolder; others use a different root/working-directory arrangement. Do not move individual DLLs into a layout guessed from an unrelated tutorial."},{"id":"step-2-check-the-executable","title":"2. Check the executable you are beside","text":"Walk from the Steam install folder through Ballistic → Binaries → Win64. Confirm the filename is Ballistic-Win64-Shipping.exe. If the directory only contains the small top-level launcher, go deeper before extracting. This distinction matters because a proxy loader must be discovered by the process it is intended to load into. A correctly named file beside the wrong executable can leave you with no loader startup and no useful Lua error."},{"id":"step-3-extract-without-nesting","title":"3. Extract without adding an extra directory","text":"After extraction, inspect the result rather than trusting the extraction dialog. A common mistake is an extra archive-name folder between Win64 and the loader files, or between Mods and the mod itself. Compare your final paths with the package’s instructions. Do not rename a dependency to “fix” a path problem. Preserve filenames and move back to a known layout if the first launch fails."},{"id":"step-4-do-a-loader-only-launch","title":"4. Do a loader-only launch","text":"Start the game before adding your test script. Wait for a normal menu, exit, and inspect a log with a fresh timestamp. Record the loader’s version and whether startup reached its normal event loop. This creates a baseline for distinguishing an installation problem from a later mod problem. Observed result Interpretation Next action No fresh loader log The loader may not have started, or you are reading the wrong working directory. Recheck the shipping directory and package layout. Fresh log, no test-mod prefix The loader started; your mod discovery or script load still needs checking. Follow the first-mod folder check. Lua syntax error naming your file The script was found but could not compile. Fix the indicated source before engine debugging. Crash before your script loads The first failure is not proof your callback ran. Compare against the loader-only baseline."},{"id":"step-5-add-one-test-mod","title":"5. Add one test mod","text":"Use the minimal first-mod layout , launch again and look for its unique prefix. Keep the loader configuration unchanged during this step. If you change loader version, file layout and script behaviour together, a successful or failed launch is harder to explain."},{"id":"upgrading-and-removing","title":"Upgrading and removing","text":"Close the game before updating. Identify files owned by the loader and files owned by individual mods, then follow the chosen release’s update instructions. Preserve your mod configuration and remove obsolete loader files only when you know they belong to that old installation. To disable one example mod, remove its enable marker with the game closed. Do not delete shared loader files to disable a single script. Uninstalling a behaviour mod may leave changes already saved by the game, so refit or restore a disposable assembly before testing removal. For version-specific details, consult the loader installation guide . This wiki’s local setup records are not a compatibility list for every release."}]},{"id":"first-mod","title":"Your first mod","text":"Your first mod Create a mod folder, write one Lua file, and check that it runs. Create the folder Inside your loader’s Mods folder, create MyFirstMod . Add a Scripts folder and an empty file named enabled.txt . ue4ss/Mods/ └── MyFirstMod/ ├── enabled.txt └── Scripts/ └── main.lua Enable file extensions in Windows Explorer so the script isn’t accidentally saved as main.lua.txt . Write the script Open main.lua in a text editor and add: local function log(message) print(\"[MyFirstMod] \" .. message .. \"\\n\") end log(\"Loaded successfully.\") Use a distinct prefix in every log message. It makes your output easy to find when several mods are enabled. Run the mod Save the file. Fully close and restart the game. Open ue4ss/UE4SS.log . Search for [MyFirstMod] Loaded successfully. If the message appears, your script is loading. If it doesn’t, check the folder names and the log’s Lua errors. Add a keybind This example logs a message when you press F9. Choose a key that does not conflict with another mod. RegisterKeyBind(Key.F9, function() print(\"[MyFirstMod] F9 pressed.\\n\") end Keep engine work on the game thread A log message is a small first test. If your keybind changes Unreal objects, schedule that work with ExecuteInGameThread . See Finding game objects . Disable or share the mod With the game closed, remove the mod’s enabled.txt to disable it. For a small Lua-only release, package your mod folder, scripts and enable marker with clear installation instructions. Don’t bundle the game, saves, logs or your entire UE4SS installation. Walkthrough: prove each stage separately 1. Check the real filenames In Explorer, enable file-name extensions. Create main.lua as a text file and check the final extension after saving. A file named main.lua.txt can look correct when extensions are hidden, but it is not the expected entry script. The enable marker belongs next to Scripts, not inside it. The Scripts directory belongs inside your mod folder. Verify those three relationships before changing Lua code. 2. Understand when the file runs Top-level Lua statements execute when the loader starts the mod. A function definition creates a function; it does not execute its body until called. RegisterKeyBind stores a callback that will run later. Put the startup message outside that callback so you can distinguish “file loaded” from “key pressed”. 3. Add a counter to the key callback local presses = 0 RegisterKeyBind(Key.F9, function() presses = presses + 1 print(string.format(\"[MyFirstMod] Press count: %d\\n\", presses)) end) Restart and press once, then again. Expect counts one and two. If each press prints two messages, check for duplicate registrations or a second enabled copy of the mod before blaming input timing. The counter is in-memory; restarting resets it. 4. Introduce a game-thread boundary RegisterKeyBind(Key.F9, function() ExecuteInGameThread(function() print(\"[MyFirstMod] Game-thread callback reached.\\n\") end) end) This still only logs. It teaches where future engine calls belong without introducing object lookup at the same time. Execution is scheduled, so don’t assume the result exists synchronously on the line after ExecuteInGameThread returns. 5. Test disabling Close the game, remove the enable marker and launch again. Your startup prefix and key response should be absent. If they remain, find the running copy by its startup version/path and check for another installed folder. Do not delete arbitrary files while the process is active. What to build next Read Lua basics if the syntax is unfamiliar. Use Organising a mod to add a configuration module. Then follow Build a weapon inspector for a complete read-only feature instead of a context-dependent fragment. Your completion check for this page is small and concrete: one startup line, one increment per chosen input, no callback when disabled, and a log from the current process. Nothing here proves that a weapon or widget is ready yet.","sections":[{"id":"create-the-folder","title":"Create the folder","text":"Inside your loader’s Mods folder, create MyFirstMod . Add a Scripts folder and an empty file named enabled.txt . ue4ss/Mods/ └── MyFirstMod/ ├── enabled.txt └── Scripts/ └── main.lua Enable file extensions in Windows Explorer so the script isn’t accidentally saved as main.lua.txt ."},{"id":"write-the-script","title":"Write the script","text":"Open main.lua in a text editor and add: local function log(message) print(\"[MyFirstMod] \" .. message .. \"\\n\") end log(\"Loaded successfully.\") Use a distinct prefix in every log message. It makes your output easy to find when several mods are enabled."},{"id":"run-the-mod","title":"Run the mod","text":"Save the file. Fully close and restart the game. Open ue4ss/UE4SS.log . Search for [MyFirstMod] Loaded successfully. If the message appears, your script is loading. If it doesn’t, check the folder names and the log’s Lua errors."},{"id":"add-a-keybind","title":"Add a keybind","text":"This example logs a message when you press F9. Choose a key that does not conflict with another mod. RegisterKeyBind(Key.F9, function() print(\"[MyFirstMod] F9 pressed.\\n\") end Keep engine work on the game thread A log message is a small first test. If your keybind changes Unreal objects, schedule that work with ExecuteInGameThread . See Finding game objects ."},{"id":"disable-or-share","title":"Disable or share the mod","text":"With the game closed, remove the mod’s enabled.txt to disable it. For a small Lua-only release, package your mod folder, scripts and enable marker with clear installation instructions. Don’t bundle the game, saves, logs or your entire UE4SS installation."},{"id":"first-mod-lab","title":"Walkthrough: prove each stage separately","text":""},{"id":"lab-check-file-extensions","title":"1. Check the real filenames","text":"In Explorer, enable file-name extensions. Create main.lua as a text file and check the final extension after saving. A file named main.lua.txt can look correct when extensions are hidden, but it is not the expected entry script. The enable marker belongs next to Scripts, not inside it. The Scripts directory belongs inside your mod folder. Verify those three relationships before changing Lua code."},{"id":"lab-understand-startup","title":"2. Understand when the file runs","text":"Top-level Lua statements execute when the loader starts the mod. A function definition creates a function; it does not execute its body until called. RegisterKeyBind stores a callback that will run later. Put the startup message outside that callback so you can distinguish “file loaded” from “key pressed”."},{"id":"lab-add-an-operation-counter","title":"3. Add a counter to the key callback","text":"local presses = 0 RegisterKeyBind(Key.F9, function() presses = presses + 1 print(string.format(\"[MyFirstMod] Press count: %d\\n\", presses)) end) Restart and press once, then again. Expect counts one and two. If each press prints two messages, check for duplicate registrations or a second enabled copy of the mod before blaming input timing. The counter is in-memory; restarting resets it."},{"id":"lab-move-to-the-game-thread","title":"4. Introduce a game-thread boundary","text":"RegisterKeyBind(Key.F9, function() ExecuteInGameThread(function() print(\"[MyFirstMod] Game-thread callback reached.\\n\") end) end) This still only logs. It teaches where future engine calls belong without introducing object lookup at the same time. Execution is scheduled, so don’t assume the result exists synchronously on the line after ExecuteInGameThread returns."},{"id":"lab-test-disable","title":"5. Test disabling","text":"Close the game, remove the enable marker and launch again. Your startup prefix and key response should be absent. If they remain, find the running copy by its startup version/path and check for another installed folder. Do not delete arbitrary files while the process is active."},{"id":"first-mod-next-steps","title":"What to build next","text":"Read Lua basics if the syntax is unfamiliar. Use Organising a mod to add a configuration module. Then follow Build a weapon inspector for a complete read-only feature instead of a context-dependent fragment. Your completion check for this page is small and concrete: one startup line, one increment per chosen input, no callback when disabled, and a log from the current process. Nothing here proves that a weapon or widget is ready yet."}]},{"id":"lua-basics","title":"Lua basics for modding","text":"Lua basics for modding Understand the language before adding Unreal objects to the mix. Before you start Finish Your first mod so you have a script whose log message you can find. Use a disposable test mod for the snippets here. Copy one example at a time, restart, and compare its output with the explanation before combining examples. The examples use Lua, as loaded by UE4SS. Luau is a related language with different features; don’t paste Roblox services, Luau type annotations or Roblox instance APIs into a UE4SS script and expect them to exist. 1. Values and local variables local enabled = true local sampleLimit = 12 local label = \"My mod\" local currentWeapon = nil print(label .. \": \" .. tostring(sampleLimit) .. \"\\n\") local limits a variable to its surrounding scope. true and false are booleans; 12 is a number; quoted text is a string; nil means no value. Two dots join strings. Use tostring when a value might be a number, boolean or nil. Lua treats only false and nil as false in a condition. The number 0 and an empty string are both truthy. If a setting must be a boolean, validate its type instead of assuming that zero disables it. 2. Conditions and early returns local function describe(value) if value == nil then return \"No value\" end if type(value) ~= \"number\" then return \"Not a number\" end if value \u003c 0 then return \"Negative number\" end return \"Zero or positive number\" end print(describe(nil) .. \"\\n\") print(describe(-2) .. \"\\n\") Expected output is No value , then Negative number . == compares values; = assigns a value; ~= means not equal. Each if needs then and end . Early returns make object checks easier to read because the main operation does not sit inside several nested conditions. 3. Functions and callbacks A function is a value you can call or hand to another function. A callback is a function handed to something that runs it later. Do not call the callback while registering it. local function onPressed() print(\"[MyMod] Key callback ran.\\n\") end RegisterKeyBind(Key.F9, onPressed) -- Do not write RegisterKeyBind(Key.F9, onPressed()). The final comment shows a common mistake: the parentheses execute onPressed immediately, then pass its result to RegisterKeyBind. Without the parentheses, the function itself is registered. Choose a key not already used by another mod. 4. Tables and loops local names = {\"barrel\", \"rail\", \"grip\"} for i, name in ipairs(names) do print(i .. \": \" .. name .. \"\\n\") end local settings = {Enabled = true, MaxParts = 12} print(tostring(settings.MaxParts) .. \"\\n\") Ordinary Lua list indices conventionally start at one. ipairs walks a dense sequence until the first nil. A keyed table is useful for settings or named state. settings.MaxParts is shorthand for settings[\"MaxParts\"] . These rules describe Lua tables. A UE4SS wrapper for an Unreal array, set or map is a different object with its own supported operations. Don’t use a table example as proof that an engine container supports the same iterator. See Arrays and containers . 5. Dot versus colon local counter = {value = 0} function counter:add(amount) self.value = self.value + amount end counter:add(3) -- Equivalent ordinary Lua call: counter.add(counter, 3) print(counter.value .. \"\\n\") A colon call supplies the receiver as the first argument. Use the form expected by the API you’re calling. object:IsValid() and module.create() are not interchangeable conventions. In engine bindings, an incorrectly called function can fail before your intended operation happens. 6. Errors and type checks A syntax error prevents the chunk from loading. A runtime error occurs while executing a loaded chunk. If a variable is nil, ask why it never received a value rather than adding a default that hides the missing object. pcall returns a success boolean followed by the result or error. It catches Lua errors, not every native access violation. assert is useful for rejecting an invalid configuration immediately; it is less helpful as a replacement for waiting for an expected menu to load. Practice before engine work Change the sample limit and predict the output. Pass a string to describe and confirm the type branch. Make a list of three labels and print each once. Put the counter in its own module, following Organising a mod . Only then introduce a real weapon or widget reference. If a plain Lua example fails, solve that first. Adding Unreal state makes a language mistake harder to isolate.","sections":[{"id":"before-you-start","title":"Before you start","text":"Finish Your first mod so you have a script whose log message you can find. Use a disposable test mod for the snippets here. Copy one example at a time, restart, and compare its output with the explanation before combining examples. The examples use Lua, as loaded by UE4SS. Luau is a related language with different features; don’t paste Roblox services, Luau type annotations or Roblox instance APIs into a UE4SS script and expect them to exist."},{"id":"values-and-locals","title":"1. Values and local variables","text":"local enabled = true local sampleLimit = 12 local label = \"My mod\" local currentWeapon = nil print(label .. \": \" .. tostring(sampleLimit) .. \"\\n\") local limits a variable to its surrounding scope. true and false are booleans; 12 is a number; quoted text is a string; nil means no value. Two dots join strings. Use tostring when a value might be a number, boolean or nil. Lua treats only false and nil as false in a condition. The number 0 and an empty string are both truthy. If a setting must be a boolean, validate its type instead of assuming that zero disables it."},{"id":"conditions-and-early-returns","title":"2. Conditions and early returns","text":"local function describe(value) if value == nil then return \"No value\" end if type(value) ~= \"number\" then return \"Not a number\" end if value \u003c 0 then return \"Negative number\" end return \"Zero or positive number\" end print(describe(nil) .. \"\\n\") print(describe(-2) .. \"\\n\") Expected output is No value , then Negative number . == compares values; = assigns a value; ~= means not equal. Each if needs then and end . Early returns make object checks easier to read because the main operation does not sit inside several nested conditions."},{"id":"functions-and-callbacks","title":"3. Functions and callbacks","text":"A function is a value you can call or hand to another function. A callback is a function handed to something that runs it later. Do not call the callback while registering it. local function onPressed() print(\"[MyMod] Key callback ran.\\n\") end RegisterKeyBind(Key.F9, onPressed) -- Do not write RegisterKeyBind(Key.F9, onPressed()). The final comment shows a common mistake: the parentheses execute onPressed immediately, then pass its result to RegisterKeyBind. Without the parentheses, the function itself is registered. Choose a key not already used by another mod."},{"id":"tables-and-loops","title":"4. Tables and loops","text":"local names = {\"barrel\", \"rail\", \"grip\"} for i, name in ipairs(names) do print(i .. \": \" .. name .. \"\\n\") end local settings = {Enabled = true, MaxParts = 12} print(tostring(settings.MaxParts) .. \"\\n\") Ordinary Lua list indices conventionally start at one. ipairs walks a dense sequence until the first nil. A keyed table is useful for settings or named state. settings.MaxParts is shorthand for settings[\"MaxParts\"] . These rules describe Lua tables. A UE4SS wrapper for an Unreal array, set or map is a different object with its own supported operations. Don’t use a table example as proof that an engine container supports the same iterator. See Arrays and containers ."},{"id":"dot-versus-colon","title":"5. Dot versus colon","text":"local counter = {value = 0} function counter:add(amount) self.value = self.value + amount end counter:add(3) -- Equivalent ordinary Lua call: counter.add(counter, 3) print(counter.value .. \"\\n\") A colon call supplies the receiver as the first argument. Use the form expected by the API you’re calling. object:IsValid() and module.create() are not interchangeable conventions. In engine bindings, an incorrectly called function can fail before your intended operation happens."},{"id":"errors-and-types","title":"6. Errors and type checks","text":"A syntax error prevents the chunk from loading. A runtime error occurs while executing a loaded chunk. If a variable is nil, ask why it never received a value rather than adding a default that hides the missing object. pcall returns a success boolean followed by the result or error. It catches Lua errors, not every native access violation. assert is useful for rejecting an invalid configuration immediately; it is less helpful as a replacement for waiting for an expected menu to load."},{"id":"practice-before-engine-work","title":"Practice before engine work","text":"Change the sample limit and predict the output. Pass a string to describe and confirm the type branch. Make a list of three labels and print each once. Put the counter in its own module, following Organising a mod . Only then introduce a real weapon or widget reference. If a plain Lua example fails, solve that first. Adding Unreal state makes a language mistake harder to isolate."}]},{"id":"project-structure","title":"Organising a mod","text":"Organising a mod Keep startup, settings, engine access and feature state in separate places. 1. Decide what each file owns For a small feature, four files are often enough. This is a practical starting point rather than a rule that every function needs its own module. File Responsibility main.lua Validate startup settings and register callbacks once. config.lua Return defaults a user can edit with the game closed. log.lua Provide a consistent message prefix. inspect.lua Read engine state and return a result. The weapon inspector walkthrough uses exactly this layout. For a larger mod, group related modules under folders such as ui/ and gameplay/ rather than growing main.lua into every subsystem. 2. Return a module -- Scripts/counter.lua local Counter = {} function Counter.create() local state = {value = 0} function state:add(amount) self.value = self.value + amount end return state end return Counter local Counter = require(\"counter\") local first = Counter.create() local second = Counter.create() first:add(2) print(first.value .. \", \" .. second.value .. \"\\n\") The output should be 2, 0 . Returning a constructor lets two features have separate state without copying module code. Keep the module’s public operations small; callers shouldn’t need to reach into every internal field. 3. Validate settings before registering work local Config = require(\"config\") assert(type(Config.Enabled) == \"boolean\", \"Enabled must be a boolean\") assert(type(Config.MaxParts) == \"number\", \"MaxParts must be a number\") assert(Config.MaxParts % 1 == 0, \"MaxParts must be an integer\") assert(Config.MaxParts >= 1 and Config.MaxParts \u003c= 64, \"MaxParts must be from 1 to 64\") Explain each setting in the installation guide and pick bounded defaults. A typo should produce a useful log message, not an enormous actor loop or an unexplained half-initialised feature. Do not store live actor or widget objects in a user configuration file. A persisted preference can survive travel; a world-owned object usually cannot. 4. Keep dependencies one-way Have main.lua import the feature module and supply configuration. Avoid a feature importing main.lua to ask for a value while main.lua is still loading that feature. Pass a callback or state object instead. require normally caches a loaded module within its Lua environment. Editing a module on disk does not prove that a running instance has reloaded it. Use the full restart workflow that was validated for your loader, and log a version string to identify the code actually running. 5. Separate three kinds of state Configuration: user choices, limits and feature switches. Session state: a custom round, network epoch or queued action. World state: controllers, actors, widgets and cached components. A single “reset everything” function can erase preferences unnecessarily. Reset world caches on travel, cancel actions when their context changes, and reset round scores only when the round flow calls for it. 6. Keep names portable Use consistent filename casing and relative module names. Avoid a personal absolute install path inside a shared script. Keep mod-owned data under a deliberate data directory, not the current working directory by accident. If you change a module’s name, update its requires and the release archive’s explicit file list together. Test an upgrade from the old release: a removed module left on disk can mask a packaging mistake. Check the refactor Confirm the same startup message appears once. Trigger the feature and compare behaviour with the previous version. Use an invalid setting and check the diagnostic. Disable the feature and confirm no action callback is registered. Restart after removing an obsolete module to confirm nothing secretly depends on it.","sections":[{"id":"start-with-responsibilities","title":"1. Decide what each file owns","text":"For a small feature, four files are often enough. This is a practical starting point rather than a rule that every function needs its own module. File Responsibility main.lua Validate startup settings and register callbacks once. config.lua Return defaults a user can edit with the game closed. log.lua Provide a consistent message prefix. inspect.lua Read engine state and return a result. The weapon inspector walkthrough uses exactly this layout. For a larger mod, group related modules under folders such as ui/ and gameplay/ rather than growing main.lua into every subsystem."},{"id":"return-a-module","title":"2. Return a module","text":"-- Scripts/counter.lua local Counter = {} function Counter.create() local state = {value = 0} function state:add(amount) self.value = self.value + amount end return state end return Counter local Counter = require(\"counter\") local first = Counter.create() local second = Counter.create() first:add(2) print(first.value .. \", \" .. second.value .. \"\\n\") The output should be 2, 0 . Returning a constructor lets two features have separate state without copying module code. Keep the module’s public operations small; callers shouldn’t need to reach into every internal field."},{"id":"validate-settings","title":"3. Validate settings before registering work","text":"local Config = require(\"config\") assert(type(Config.Enabled) == \"boolean\", \"Enabled must be a boolean\") assert(type(Config.MaxParts) == \"number\", \"MaxParts must be a number\") assert(Config.MaxParts % 1 == 0, \"MaxParts must be an integer\") assert(Config.MaxParts >= 1 and Config.MaxParts \u003c= 64, \"MaxParts must be from 1 to 64\") Explain each setting in the installation guide and pick bounded defaults. A typo should produce a useful log message, not an enormous actor loop or an unexplained half-initialised feature. Do not store live actor or widget objects in a user configuration file. A persisted preference can survive travel; a world-owned object usually cannot."},{"id":"avoid-circular-requires","title":"4. Keep dependencies one-way","text":"Have main.lua import the feature module and supply configuration. Avoid a feature importing main.lua to ask for a value while main.lua is still loading that feature. Pass a callback or state object instead. require normally caches a loaded module within its Lua environment. Editing a module on disk does not prove that a running instance has reloaded it. Use the full restart workflow that was validated for your loader, and log a version string to identify the code actually running."},{"id":"world-state-versus-settings","title":"5. Separate three kinds of state","text":"Configuration: user choices, limits and feature switches. Session state: a custom round, network epoch or queued action. World state: controllers, actors, widgets and cached components. A single “reset everything” function can erase preferences unnecessarily. Reset world caches on travel, cancel actions when their context changes, and reset round scores only when the round flow calls for it."},{"id":"file-layout-and-names","title":"6. Keep names portable","text":"Use consistent filename casing and relative module names. Avoid a personal absolute install path inside a shared script. Keep mod-owned data under a deliberate data directory, not the current working directory by accident. If you change a module’s name, update its requires and the release archive’s explicit file list together. Test an upgrade from the old release: a removed module left on disk can mask a packaging mistake."},{"id":"check-the-refactor","title":"Check the refactor","text":"Confirm the same startup message appears once. Trigger the feature and compare behaviour with the previous version. Use an invalid setting and check the diagnostic. Disable the feature and confirm no action callback is registered. Restart after removing an obsolete module to confirm nothing secretly depends on it."}]},{"id":"changelog","title":"Changelog","text":"Changelog New guides, corrections and changes to the wiki. Newest updates come first. These entries track the documentation and website, not changes to Ballistic.exe or individual mods. Next update Added Thirty more chapters covering Unreal fundamentals, typed containers, transforms, input/UI, ammunition and handling, runtime assets, physics, lighting, sound/effects, Steam/protocol/interpolation/timing/chat, and settings files. A read-only target snapshot walkthrough and source , plus a staged runtime-range planning lab . A curated 20-entry function catalog generated from evidence-labelled metadata, a 45-term glossary , and an 18-question troubleshooting FAQ . Five detailed guides: Lua basics for modding , Organising a mod , Performance & profiling , Build a weapon inspector and Test a mod step by step . A downloadable read-only WorkshopInspector teaching example, with every Lua file shown and explained in its walkthrough. Numbered exercises, expected results, parameter explanations, diagnostic tables, lifecycle checks and acceptance tests throughout the existing 25 guides. A repeatable testing workflow that distinguishes plain Lua checks, native fixtures, actual game use and real two-PC sessions. Changed Expanded the topic hierarchy and complete index for 60 guides plus the changelog. Search now ranks title/heading matches, shows match counts and links matching sections directly; up to 20 page results can be browsed. The sidebar and mobile menu reveal the current page when opened, including chapters deep in the reference groups. The hook reference rebuilds from validated local metadata while keeping dump-only findings distinct from recorded integration checks. Moved Changelog into Getting started in the sidebar and guide index. Expanded the setup, scripting and game-system articles from short overviews into practical step-by-step references. Explained native versus Blueprint hook phases and linked the version-specific API reference. Added detailed investigations for attachment removal, stat baselines, projectile attribution, target bodies, UI rebuilds, range arrival, save snapshots and ownership filtering. The build now syncs the inspector’s displayed code with its source files and rebuilds its archive from an explicit file list. Fixed The mobile navigation close control stays visible when scrolling to chapters near the bottom of a long menu. Rebuilt the no-JavaScript navigation with consistent separators and links to every page. The contents list now tracks the reading position on long pages, including the last section at the bottom of an article. 7 November 2026 Added The first nine guides: introduction, UE4SS installation, a first Lua mod, hooks, objects, debugging, game paths, custom content and tools. Sixteen more guides covering timers and cleanup, data tables, weapon assemblies, attachments and rails, weapon stats, shot events, targets, scoring, workshop UI, ranges, saves, ownership, multiplayer architecture, packaging, update checks and coverage. A grouped index of all guides and a coverage page explaining the limits of the current documentation. Full-text search, keyboard shortcuts, navigation filtering, section links and code-copy buttons. Dark mode by default, a saved theme choice, mobile navigation and print styles. A contributor guide, pull request template and a manual GitHub Pages workflow. This changelog, with a place to record future additions and fixes. Changed Organised the site into separate HTML guides with shared CSS and JavaScript. Moved page titles, order and sidebar groups into one shared navigation file. Grouped the expanded sidebar by topic and opened the current section automatically. Added checks for page listings, local links and section anchors before building the site. Rewrote the README as short setup and editing instructions, with badges for guide count and estimated coverage. Fixed Long reference tables now scroll inside the article on phones instead of squeezing the text into narrow columns. The README coverage badge now opens the coverage page. Search can be closed with Escape, and the header search label stays on one line. Corrected Windows path handling in the local preview and build tools.","sections":[{"id":"next-update","title":"Next update","text":""},{"id":"next-added","title":"Added","text":"Thirty more chapters covering Unreal fundamentals, typed containers, transforms, input/UI, ammunition and handling, runtime assets, physics, lighting, sound/effects, Steam/protocol/interpolation/timing/chat, and settings files. A read-only target snapshot walkthrough and source , plus a staged runtime-range planning lab . A curated 20-entry function catalog generated from evidence-labelled metadata, a 45-term glossary , and an 18-question troubleshooting FAQ . Five detailed guides: Lua basics for modding , Organising a mod , Performance & profiling , Build a weapon inspector and Test a mod step by step . A downloadable read-only WorkshopInspector teaching example, with every Lua file shown and explained in its walkthrough. Numbered exercises, expected results, parameter explanations, diagnostic tables, lifecycle checks and acceptance tests throughout the existing 25 guides. A repeatable testing workflow that distinguishes plain Lua checks, native fixtures, actual game use and real two-PC sessions."},{"id":"next-changed","title":"Changed","text":"Expanded the topic hierarchy and complete index for 60 guides plus the changelog. Search now ranks title/heading matches, shows match counts and links matching sections directly; up to 20 page results can be browsed. The sidebar and mobile menu reveal the current page when opened, including chapters deep in the reference groups. The hook reference rebuilds from validated local metadata while keeping dump-only findings distinct from recorded integration checks. Moved Changelog into Getting started in the sidebar and guide index. Expanded the setup, scripting and game-system articles from short overviews into practical step-by-step references. Explained native versus Blueprint hook phases and linked the version-specific API reference. Added detailed investigations for attachment removal, stat baselines, projectile attribution, target bodies, UI rebuilds, range arrival, save snapshots and ownership filtering. The build now syncs the inspector’s displayed code with its source files and rebuilds its archive from an explicit file list."},{"id":"next-fixed","title":"Fixed","text":"The mobile navigation close control stays visible when scrolling to chapters near the bottom of a long menu. Rebuilt the no-JavaScript navigation with consistent separators and links to every page. The contents list now tracks the reading position on long pages, including the last section at the bottom of an article."},{"id":"2026-11-07","title":"7 November 2026","text":""},{"id":"2026-11-07-added","title":"Added","text":"The first nine guides: introduction, UE4SS installation, a first Lua mod, hooks, objects, debugging, game paths, custom content and tools. Sixteen more guides covering timers and cleanup, data tables, weapon assemblies, attachments and rails, weapon stats, shot events, targets, scoring, workshop UI, ranges, saves, ownership, multiplayer architecture, packaging, update checks and coverage. A grouped index of all guides and a coverage page explaining the limits of the current documentation. Full-text search, keyboard shortcuts, navigation filtering, section links and code-copy buttons. Dark mode by default, a saved theme choice, mobile navigation and print styles. A contributor guide, pull request template and a manual GitHub Pages workflow. This changelog, with a place to record future additions and fixes."},{"id":"2026-11-07-changed","title":"Changed","text":"Organised the site into separate HTML guides with shared CSS and JavaScript. Moved page titles, order and sidebar groups into one shared navigation file. Grouped the expanded sidebar by topic and opened the current section automatically. Added checks for page listings, local links and section anchors before building the site. Rewrote the README as short setup and editing instructions, with badges for guide count and estimated coverage."},{"id":"2026-11-07-fixed","title":"Fixed","text":"Long reference tables now scroll inside the article on phones instead of squeezing the text into narrow columns. The README coverage badge now opens the coverage page. Search can be closed with Escape, and the header search label stays on one line. Corrected Windows path handling in the local preview and build tools."}]},{"id":"hooks","title":"Working with hooks","text":"Working with hooks A hook runs your code when the game calls an Unreal function. Find a function Use the UE4SS object viewer or an object dump to locate the exact reflected function path. Copy class and function names exactly as shown in the current dump. A Blueprint function often has a path like this: /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/BunkerHUDItems/Attach_POI.Attach_POI_C:GetCompatibleMountsRecursive This path was observed in the demo’s attachment UI. It may not be loaded until workshop assets are present. Wait for the function Don’t assume a Blueprint is available when main.lua first runs. Check for it on the game thread, and register your hook once. local path = \"/Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/BunkerHUDItems/Attach_POI.Attach_POI_C:GetCompatibleMountsRecursive\" local installed = false LoopAsync(2000, function() if installed then return end ExecuteInGameThread(function() if installed then return end local fn = StaticFindObject(path) if not fn or not fn:IsValid() then return end RegisterHook(path, function(context) local object = context:get() if not object or not object:IsValid() then return end print(\"[MyFirstMod] Workshop lookup called.\\n\") end) installed = true print(\"[MyFirstMod] Hook installed.\\n\") end) end Read parameters carefully Hook arguments can be UE4SS parameter wrappers. Use the access pattern documented for the parameter’s type, and inspect the signature before reading or changing it. Callback phase and return-parameter behaviour can differ between native and Blueprint functions. The sample above only reads the context and logs a call. It does not alter mount compatibility or attachments. Avoid common mistakes Don’t install the same hook on every timer tick. Don’t call the hooked function from its own callback unless you have a deliberate recursion guard. Don’t assume a path from an older game build is still correct. Don’t use a Lua error handler as protection against native memory crashes. Check the UE4SS API documentation for your loader version before writing to parameters or relying on pre/post callbacks. Walkthrough: investigate one hook 1. Find the owner and full signature Search the object dump for the function name, then read the owning class and parameter properties around it. Keep native paths beginning with /Script separate from Blueprint paths beginning with /Game. A similarly named function on a derived class can be a different integration point. Record parameter order and types. A bool, object, struct, out array and return value require different handling. Don’t infer the whole signature from one callback that happened not to crash. 2. Wait for the function to exist A cooked Blueprint can be present in the archive but absent from the current object registry. Trigger the normal menu or range that loads it, then retry StaticFindObject at a low frequency. Register once after the lookup is valid. Do not run LoadAsset repeatedly from a background callback just to force progress. RegisterHook targets reflected UFunctions. The current API reference does not support delegate functions through that registration path; a widget delegate name is not automatically a hookable action. 3. Choose the correct callback phase The current RegisterHook reference distinguishes native and Blueprint paths: the second argument is a pre callback for /Script functions and an after callback for other paths; an optional third argument supplies a native post callback. Match the docs to your installed loader rather than naming every second-argument callback a “pre hook”. Start with a callback that logs and returns nothing. A hook return value can change a function’s result; accidentally returning a helper’s value can therefore change gameplay. 4. Read context before parameters local function onCalled(context) local actor = context:get() if not actor or not actor:IsValid() then return end print(\"[MyMod] Called on \" .. actor:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end Confirm this runs on the expected instance. Only then add one parameter at a time. Copy numeric values or component coordinates into ordinary Lua tables if they need to outlive the callback; don’t assume a parameter wrapper remains a safe reference after the call finishes. 5. Keep the hook identifiers local preId, postId = RegisterHook(path, onCalled) local function removeMyHook() if preId == nil then return end UnregisterHook(path, preId, postId) preId, postId = nil, nil end Keep both returned IDs with the path. This is your hook’s identity; don’t try to unregister another feature’s callback by guessing its IDs. For a reload/reinitialisation path, remove the old registration before installing the replacement where supported. Recursion and reentrancy A hooked function can call related functions that eventually reach the same hook again. Calling it yourself from its callback can create direct recursion. Use a deliberate guard around the mutating operation, release it on Lua failure, and avoid treating every nested call as a separate user action. For UI hooks, a function may run during both construction and refresh. Adding a button each time without checking its existing parent creates duplicates. The fix belongs in your construction lifecycle, not in an arbitrary delay. When a UI return parameter is missing In the recorded attachment popup test, the Blueprint post callback did not supply the return parameter expected by the first tab implementation. Reading the widget’s stored AttachmentsList property supplied the needed list instead. That is a specific observed workaround, not a universal rule that return parameters never work. Dump the signature, log what the callback actually supplies and verify the resulting visible list. A registered hook with zero useful callbacks is not a completed feature.","sections":[{"id":"find-a-function","title":"Find a function","text":"Use the UE4SS object viewer or an object dump to locate the exact reflected function path. Copy class and function names exactly as shown in the current dump. A Blueprint function often has a path like this: /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/BunkerHUDItems/Attach_POI.Attach_POI_C:GetCompatibleMountsRecursive This path was observed in the demo’s attachment UI. It may not be loaded until workshop assets are present."},{"id":"wait-for-the-function","title":"Wait for the function","text":"Don’t assume a Blueprint is available when main.lua first runs. Check for it on the game thread, and register your hook once. local path = \"/Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/BunkerHUDItems/Attach_POI.Attach_POI_C:GetCompatibleMountsRecursive\" local installed = false LoopAsync(2000, function() if installed then return end ExecuteInGameThread(function() if installed then return end local fn = StaticFindObject(path) if not fn or not fn:IsValid() then return end RegisterHook(path, function(context) local object = context:get() if not object or not object:IsValid() then return end print(\"[MyFirstMod] Workshop lookup called.\\n\") end) installed = true print(\"[MyFirstMod] Hook installed.\\n\") end) end"},{"id":"read-parameters","title":"Read parameters carefully","text":"Hook arguments can be UE4SS parameter wrappers. Use the access pattern documented for the parameter’s type, and inspect the signature before reading or changing it. Callback phase and return-parameter behaviour can differ between native and Blueprint functions. The sample above only reads the context and logs a call. It does not alter mount compatibility or attachments."},{"id":"avoid-common-mistakes","title":"Avoid common mistakes","text":"Don’t install the same hook on every timer tick. Don’t call the hooked function from its own callback unless you have a deliberate recursion guard. Don’t assume a path from an older game build is still correct. Don’t use a Lua error handler as protection against native memory crashes. Check the UE4SS API documentation for your loader version before writing to parameters or relying on pre/post callbacks."},{"id":"hook-investigation-lab","title":"Walkthrough: investigate one hook","text":""},{"id":"hook-step-find-the-owner","title":"1. Find the owner and full signature","text":"Search the object dump for the function name, then read the owning class and parameter properties around it. Keep native paths beginning with /Script separate from Blueprint paths beginning with /Game. A similarly named function on a derived class can be a different integration point. Record parameter order and types. A bool, object, struct, out array and return value require different handling. Don’t infer the whole signature from one callback that happened not to crash."},{"id":"hook-step-wait-for-memory","title":"2. Wait for the function to exist","text":"A cooked Blueprint can be present in the archive but absent from the current object registry. Trigger the normal menu or range that loads it, then retry StaticFindObject at a low frequency. Register once after the lookup is valid. Do not run LoadAsset repeatedly from a background callback just to force progress. RegisterHook targets reflected UFunctions. The current API reference does not support delegate functions through that registration path; a widget delegate name is not automatically a hookable action."},{"id":"hook-step-choose-the-phase","title":"3. Choose the correct callback phase","text":"The current RegisterHook reference distinguishes native and Blueprint paths: the second argument is a pre callback for /Script functions and an after callback for other paths; an optional third argument supplies a native post callback. Match the docs to your installed loader rather than naming every second-argument callback a “pre hook”. Start with a callback that logs and returns nothing. A hook return value can change a function’s result; accidentally returning a helper’s value can therefore change gameplay."},{"id":"hook-step-unwrap-once","title":"4. Read context before parameters","text":"local function onCalled(context) local actor = context:get() if not actor or not actor:IsValid() then return end print(\"[MyMod] Called on \" .. actor:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end Confirm this runs on the expected instance. Only then add one parameter at a time. Copy numeric values or component coordinates into ordinary Lua tables if they need to outlive the callback; don’t assume a parameter wrapper remains a safe reference after the call finishes."},{"id":"hook-step-keep-identifiers","title":"5. Keep the hook identifiers","text":"local preId, postId = RegisterHook(path, onCalled) local function removeMyHook() if preId == nil then return end UnregisterHook(path, preId, postId) preId, postId = nil, nil end Keep both returned IDs with the path. This is your hook’s identity; don’t try to unregister another feature’s callback by guessing its IDs. For a reload/reinitialisation path, remove the old registration before installing the replacement where supported."},{"id":"hook-recursion-and-reentrancy","title":"Recursion and reentrancy","text":"A hooked function can call related functions that eventually reach the same hook again. Calling it yourself from its callback can create direct recursion. Use a deliberate guard around the mutating operation, release it on Lua failure, and avoid treating every nested call as a separate user action. For UI hooks, a function may run during both construction and refresh. Adding a button each time without checking its existing parent creates duplicates. The fix belongs in your construction lifecycle, not in an arbitrary delay."},{"id":"hook-ui-return-parameters","title":"When a UI return parameter is missing","text":"In the recorded attachment popup test, the Blueprint post callback did not supply the return parameter expected by the first tab implementation. Reading the widget’s stored AttachmentsList property supplied the needed list instead. That is a specific observed workaround, not a universal rule that return parameters never work. Dump the signature, log what the callback actually supplies and verify the resulting visible list. A registered hook with zero useful callbacks is not a completed feature."}]},{"id":"objects","title":"Finding game objects","text":"Finding game objects Find an object, check that it is valid, then use it while it still belongs to the current world. Loaded objects StaticFindObject looks up an object that is already loaded. It does not automatically load every asset from the game archive. ExecuteInGameThread(function() local object = StaticFindObject( \"/Game/Data/Weapons/Attachment_Data.Attachment_Data\" ) if not object or not object:IsValid() then print(\"[MyFirstMod] Attachment table is not loaded yet.\\n\") return end print(\"[MyFirstMod] \" .. object:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end Instances and defaults A class describes an object type. A class default object contains its default values. A live actor or widget is an instance. Editing a default is not the same as changing the weapon or menu the player is currently using. Read the full object name and confirm which object you found before writing to it. World changes Travelling from the bunker to a range replaces many actors and widgets. A reference that worked before travel can become invalid afterwards. Reacquire world-specific objects after travel. Check validity immediately before use. Clear cached widget references when the menu is destroyed. Keep track of actors your mod spawns so you can clean them up. Arrays and containers Unreal containers exposed through UE4SS are not always ordinary Lua tables. In this local loader, using ipairs on some Unreal arrays continued beyond the reported length. A bounded loop worked for those arrays: local points = editor.POIs local count = #points assert(count \u003c= 512, \"Unexpected attachment-point count\") for i = 1, count do local point = points[i] if point and point:IsValid() then print(point:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end end This example requires a valid editor instance. Set and map containers have different APIs; don’t apply the array pattern to every container. Reflected methods Use methods present in the dump for your build. For actor transforms, the local bindings exposed K2_GetActorLocation and K2_GetActorRotation . Calling an unavailable method produced a TrivialObject error rather than a normal Lua function call. Walkthrough: find the right instance 1. Identify what kind of name you have An asset package path, a generated class path, an instance full name and a short class name are different identifiers. For example, a Blueprint-generated class can end in _C ; an actor instance can add a runtime suffix. Don’t strip or append suffixes blindly to turn one into another. Lookup Use it for Do not assume StaticFindObject An exact object already loaded under that name. That it reads unloaded archive content. LoadAsset Request a known cooked asset on the game thread. That a table label is a valid asset path. FindAllOf Inspect loaded instances by the expected short class name. That the first result is the active menu or player. 2. Establish current-world context Start with a valid viewport, get its world and use that world to resolve GameplayStatics context. Log the world full name and compare it after travel. A globally loaded class can be reused across worlds; its live actors and widgets cannot be treated the same way. For a weapon query, decide whether you mean the range pawn’s equipped gun or the workshop’s displayed gun. Both can be valid objects while only one is relevant to the action. 3. Filter candidates deliberately If a global query returns several editors, inspect validity, visibility, parent/owner and the referenced current weapon. Log candidate names before selecting one. “First valid” is a useful discovery shortcut but a weak production rule if old or hidden widgets remain loaded. The downloadable inspector reports visible editor candidates explicitly. It doesn’t claim that visibility uniquely proves input focus. 4. Copy values you need later local raw = actor:K2_GetActorLocation() local position = {X = raw.X, Y = raw.Y, Z = raw.Z} -- position is plain Lua data for a comparison or message. -- Reacquire actor before a later engine operation. A copied position is not a live binding. It stays unchanged if the actor moves. That makes it useful as a baseline, but you must capture another sample to detect movement. 5. Guard the container and its length Confirm whether an output is a plain Lua table, a parameter wrapper or an Unreal container. Use a bounded iteration count, handle a missing result, and avoid continuing to use an array whose owner is being rebuilt by the operation you just performed. A bounded log is useful for discovery. For a production operation, silently truncating a larger array can be a correctness bug; decide whether to reject the operation, continue in batches or report the limit. Expected result and failure checks You should be able to name the world, the live instance and why you selected it. After travel, the old cached reference should be discarded and the new instance discovered. If the field is unavailable, check the actual owning class and its reflection signature instead of treating the unavailable value as zero. If a method call reports TrivialObject, inspect the dump for a reflected alternative. A C++ method name visible in an engine tutorial is not proof that your Lua binding exposes it.","sections":[{"id":"loaded-objects","title":"Loaded objects","text":"StaticFindObject looks up an object that is already loaded. It does not automatically load every asset from the game archive. ExecuteInGameThread(function() local object = StaticFindObject( \"/Game/Data/Weapons/Attachment_Data.Attachment_Data\" ) if not object or not object:IsValid() then print(\"[MyFirstMod] Attachment table is not loaded yet.\\n\") return end print(\"[MyFirstMod] \" .. object:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end"},{"id":"instances-and-defaults","title":"Instances and defaults","text":"A class describes an object type. A class default object contains its default values. A live actor or widget is an instance. Editing a default is not the same as changing the weapon or menu the player is currently using. Read the full object name and confirm which object you found before writing to it."},{"id":"world-changes","title":"World changes","text":"Travelling from the bunker to a range replaces many actors and widgets. A reference that worked before travel can become invalid afterwards. Reacquire world-specific objects after travel. Check validity immediately before use. Clear cached widget references when the menu is destroyed. Keep track of actors your mod spawns so you can clean them up."},{"id":"arrays-and-containers","title":"Arrays and containers","text":"Unreal containers exposed through UE4SS are not always ordinary Lua tables. In this local loader, using ipairs on some Unreal arrays continued beyond the reported length. A bounded loop worked for those arrays: local points = editor.POIs local count = #points assert(count \u003c= 512, \"Unexpected attachment-point count\") for i = 1, count do local point = points[i] if point and point:IsValid() then print(point:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end end This example requires a valid editor instance. Set and map containers have different APIs; don’t apply the array pattern to every container."},{"id":"reflected-methods","title":"Reflected methods","text":"Use methods present in the dump for your build. For actor transforms, the local bindings exposed K2_GetActorLocation and K2_GetActorRotation . Calling an unavailable method produced a TrivialObject error rather than a normal Lua function call."},{"id":"object-discovery-walkthrough","title":"Walkthrough: find the right instance","text":""},{"id":"object-step-name","title":"1. Identify what kind of name you have","text":"An asset package path, a generated class path, an instance full name and a short class name are different identifiers. For example, a Blueprint-generated class can end in _C ; an actor instance can add a runtime suffix. Don’t strip or append suffixes blindly to turn one into another. Lookup Use it for Do not assume StaticFindObject An exact object already loaded under that name. That it reads unloaded archive content. LoadAsset Request a known cooked asset on the game thread. That a table label is a valid asset path. FindAllOf Inspect loaded instances by the expected short class name. That the first result is the active menu or player."},{"id":"object-step-context","title":"2. Establish current-world context","text":"Start with a valid viewport, get its world and use that world to resolve GameplayStatics context. Log the world full name and compare it after travel. A globally loaded class can be reused across worlds; its live actors and widgets cannot be treated the same way. For a weapon query, decide whether you mean the range pawn’s equipped gun or the workshop’s displayed gun. Both can be valid objects while only one is relevant to the action."},{"id":"object-step-filter-candidates","title":"3. Filter candidates deliberately","text":"If a global query returns several editors, inspect validity, visibility, parent/owner and the referenced current weapon. Log candidate names before selecting one. “First valid” is a useful discovery shortcut but a weak production rule if old or hidden widgets remain loaded. The downloadable inspector reports visible editor candidates explicitly. It doesn’t claim that visibility uniquely proves input focus."},{"id":"object-step-copy-values","title":"4. Copy values you need later","text":"local raw = actor:K2_GetActorLocation() local position = {X = raw.X, Y = raw.Y, Z = raw.Z} -- position is plain Lua data for a comparison or message. -- Reacquire actor before a later engine operation. A copied position is not a live binding. It stays unchanged if the actor moves. That makes it useful as a baseline, but you must capture another sample to detect movement."},{"id":"object-step-guard-containers","title":"5. Guard the container and its length","text":"Confirm whether an output is a plain Lua table, a parameter wrapper or an Unreal container. Use a bounded iteration count, handle a missing result, and avoid continuing to use an array whose owner is being rebuilt by the operation you just performed. A bounded log is useful for discovery. For a production operation, silently truncating a larger array can be a correctness bug; decide whether to reject the operation, continue in batches or report the limit."},{"id":"object-success-criteria","title":"Expected result and failure checks","text":"You should be able to name the world, the live instance and why you selected it. After travel, the old cached reference should be discarded and the new instance discovered. If the field is unavailable, check the actual owning class and its reflection signature instead of treating the unavailable value as zero. If a method call reports TrivialObject, inspect the dump for a reflected alternative. A C++ method name visible in an engine tutorial is not proof that your Lua binding exposes it."}]},{"id":"runtime","title":"Timers & cleanup","text":"Timers & cleanup Make a small mod behave properly after menu changes, travel and repeated clicks. Pick a timing source Use a slow discovery loop to wait for assets, a game-thread callback for engine work, and a game clock for elapsed-time calculations. They solve different problems. Task Approach Wait for a widget class A bounded or low-frequency discovery loop. Read or change Unreal objects Run the operation on the game thread. Animate an overlay Use elapsed time; don’t tie speed to callback count. Expire a notification Compare timestamps rather than assuming an exact timer interval. Avoid overlapping work A background loop can queue a new callback before the previous one has run. Keep one pending callback and clear the flag even after a Lua error: local queued = false LoopAsync(1000, function() if queued then return end queued = true ExecuteInGameThread(function() local ok, err = pcall(function() -- Read or update the current world's objects here. end) queued = false if not ok then print(\"[MyMod] \" .. tostring(err) .. \"\\n\") end end) end Cancel stale actions For a queued attachment operation, capture the weapon identity and an operation number when the user clicks. Before every delayed step, check that the same editor is open, the weapon is unchanged and the operation number still matches. Stop when any check fails. Setting a Lua boolean doesn’t cancel a callback already in the queue. The callback needs to check that boolean before doing engine work. Separate world state from settings Keep preferences such as overlay size separate from actor references. On world change, clear actor/widget caches, pending shot IDs and world-specific timers. Rebuild them from the new world. An object name alone can be reused after travel; a world identity plus object identity is a better cache key. Clean up what you create Track only the actors and widgets your mod creates. Remove its widgets from their parents and destroy its actors while they are still valid in that world. Don’t run a global actor sweep and delete everything with a similar class name. If you temporarily change an existing actor’s visibility, collision or physics, remember its original values and restore those values when your feature closes. Walkthrough: make a cancellable job Use this pattern for work spread over multiple callbacks, such as clearing an attachment list one point at a time. The example below is pure Lua state; it does not remove attachments. local generation = 0 local current = nil local function begin(contextId) generation = generation + 1 current = {id = generation, context = contextId, cancelled = false} return current end local function stillCurrent(job, contextId) return current == job and not job.cancelled and job.id == generation and job.context == contextId end local function cancel() generation = generation + 1 if current then current.cancelled = true end current = nil end 1. Capture the context when starting Use the current world and weapon/menu identity to construct contextId. If an action is already pending, reject a second click or cancel the old action explicitly. Do not queue several identical destructive jobs because the button still looks enabled. 2. Validate inside the delayed callback A check made before scheduling only proves the context was valid at scheduling time. Check stillCurrent inside the callback, then reacquire the editor and weapon. If the user left the menu or switched guns, stop before touching an attachment. ExecuteWithDelay schedules asynchronous work in the local API used here. The engine operation should still be dispatched onto the game thread. The nominal delay is not a promise that the callback runs at an exact instant. 3. Perform one bounded operation Process one point or a small fixed batch. If that changes the menu’s point list, return and reacquire on the next step. Mark a target done only when the intended action is accepted; otherwise keep a bounded retry policy and a visible failure result. 4. Finish every path Success, cancellation and Lua failure should all release the pending state and update the UI. A “Clearing…” button that never re-enables after an error is a lifecycle bug even if the underlying removal succeeded. If the feature owns a hook, widget or actor, give that object a specific teardown path. Merely dropping the Lua variable does not guarantee that an engine delegate or panel stops referencing it. Clock and unit checklist Write milliseconds or seconds in the variable name when it helps. ExecuteWithDelay’s integer delay and GetRealTimeSeconds do not use the same unit. Use a single clock for an elapsed-time calculation; don’t subtract an os.time wall-clock value from a world-relative game clock. When a world clock restarts, reset any next-update deadline and interpolation history using it. A negative time delta can otherwise keep an update gated for an unexpectedly long time. Acceptance test Start the job and confirm only one pending operation exists. Click again and confirm your documented duplicate-click behaviour. Change context before the next step and confirm no further action occurs. Force a Lua failure in a disposable test callback and confirm the busy state clears. Travel and confirm the old job cannot act on the new world. What was checked Queued-work guards and per-world cleanup are used in the local UI, target and attachment fixtures. This guide is a lifecycle pattern, not a universal timer-cancellation API. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build.","sections":[{"id":"pick-a-timing-source","title":"Pick a timing source","text":"Use a slow discovery loop to wait for assets, a game-thread callback for engine work, and a game clock for elapsed-time calculations. They solve different problems. Task Approach Wait for a widget class A bounded or low-frequency discovery loop. Read or change Unreal objects Run the operation on the game thread. Animate an overlay Use elapsed time; don’t tie speed to callback count. Expire a notification Compare timestamps rather than assuming an exact timer interval."},{"id":"avoid-overlapping-work","title":"Avoid overlapping work","text":"A background loop can queue a new callback before the previous one has run. Keep one pending callback and clear the flag even after a Lua error: local queued = false LoopAsync(1000, function() if queued then return end queued = true ExecuteInGameThread(function() local ok, err = pcall(function() -- Read or update the current world's objects here. end) queued = false if not ok then print(\"[MyMod] \" .. tostring(err) .. \"\\n\") end end) end"},{"id":"cancel-stale-actions","title":"Cancel stale actions","text":"For a queued attachment operation, capture the weapon identity and an operation number when the user clicks. Before every delayed step, check that the same editor is open, the weapon is unchanged and the operation number still matches. Stop when any check fails. Setting a Lua boolean doesn’t cancel a callback already in the queue. The callback needs to check that boolean before doing engine work."},{"id":"world-owned-state","title":"Separate world state from settings","text":"Keep preferences such as overlay size separate from actor references. On world change, clear actor/widget caches, pending shot IDs and world-specific timers. Rebuild them from the new world. An object name alone can be reused after travel; a world identity plus object identity is a better cache key."},{"id":"clean-up-what-you-create","title":"Clean up what you create","text":"Track only the actors and widgets your mod creates. Remove its widgets from their parents and destroy its actors while they are still valid in that world. Don’t run a global actor sweep and delete everything with a similar class name. If you temporarily change an existing actor’s visibility, collision or physics, remember its original values and restore those values when your feature closes."},{"id":"runtime-job-walkthrough","title":"Walkthrough: make a cancellable job","text":"Use this pattern for work spread over multiple callbacks, such as clearing an attachment list one point at a time. The example below is pure Lua state; it does not remove attachments. local generation = 0 local current = nil local function begin(contextId) generation = generation + 1 current = {id = generation, context = contextId, cancelled = false} return current end local function stillCurrent(job, contextId) return current == job and not job.cancelled and job.id == generation and job.context == contextId end local function cancel() generation = generation + 1 if current then current.cancelled = true end current = nil end"},{"id":"runtime-step-start","title":"1. Capture the context when starting","text":"Use the current world and weapon/menu identity to construct contextId. If an action is already pending, reject a second click or cancel the old action explicitly. Do not queue several identical destructive jobs because the button still looks enabled."},{"id":"runtime-step-schedule","title":"2. Validate inside the delayed callback","text":"A check made before scheduling only proves the context was valid at scheduling time. Check stillCurrent inside the callback, then reacquire the editor and weapon. If the user left the menu or switched guns, stop before touching an attachment. ExecuteWithDelay schedules asynchronous work in the local API used here. The engine operation should still be dispatched onto the game thread. The nominal delay is not a promise that the callback runs at an exact instant."},{"id":"runtime-step-one-operation","title":"3. Perform one bounded operation","text":"Process one point or a small fixed batch. If that changes the menu’s point list, return and reacquire on the next step. Mark a target done only when the intended action is accepted; otherwise keep a bounded retry policy and a visible failure result."},{"id":"runtime-step-finish","title":"4. Finish every path","text":"Success, cancellation and Lua failure should all release the pending state and update the UI. A “Clearing…” button that never re-enables after an error is a lifecycle bug even if the underlying removal succeeded. If the feature owns a hook, widget or actor, give that object a specific teardown path. Merely dropping the Lua variable does not guarantee that an engine delegate or panel stops referencing it."},{"id":"runtime-time-units","title":"Clock and unit checklist","text":"Write milliseconds or seconds in the variable name when it helps. ExecuteWithDelay’s integer delay and GetRealTimeSeconds do not use the same unit. Use a single clock for an elapsed-time calculation; don’t subtract an os.time wall-clock value from a world-relative game clock. When a world clock restarts, reset any next-update deadline and interpolation history using it. A negative time delta can otherwise keep an update gated for an unexpectedly long time."},{"id":"runtime-acceptance","title":"Acceptance test","text":"Start the job and confirm only one pending operation exists. Click again and confirm your documented duplicate-click behaviour. Change context before the next step and confirm no further action occurs. Force a Lua failure in a disposable test callback and confirm the busy state clears. Travel and confirm the old job cannot act on the new world."},{"id":"tested-scope","title":"What was checked","text":"Queued-work guards and per-world cleanup are used in the local UI, target and attachment fixtures. This guide is a lifecycle pattern, not a universal timer-cancellation API. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."}]},{"id":"data-tables","title":"Reading data tables","text":"Reading data tables Find the table, inspect a row, and keep discovery separate from mutation. Find a table The demo has tables for attachment mount definitions and range menu data. You can inspect them once the asset is loaded: ExecuteInGameThread(function() local data = LoadAsset(\"/Game/Data/Ranges/RangeData.RangeData\") if not data or not data:IsValid() then return end local names = data:GetRowNames() for i = 1, #names do print(\"[MyMod] Range row: \" .. tostring(names[i]) .. \"\\n\") end end Read a row In the tested loader, FindRow accepted a Lua string as the row name. Passing an FName in that particular call returned no row. This is different from APIs that explicitly expect an FName . local row = data:FindRow(\"PistolRange\") if row then local map = row.MapName_3_02B8F3864CDF2B67468FDD80A1766519 print(\"[MyMod] Map: \" .. map:ToString() .. \"\\n\") end The long suffix belongs to a Blueprint struct field. Copy the field name from your build’s dump instead of shortening it to MapName and assuming that will work. Useful tables Asset What it describes /Game/Data/Ranges/RangeData.RangeData Range rows, display information and destination names. /Game/Data/Weapons/Attachment_Data.Attachment_Data Attachment mount relationships. The attachment table contained 86 mount rows in the inspected build. That is a build observation, not a constant your mod should require forever. Watch the field types A row can contain names, text, class references and soft class references. A soft reference is not the same thing as a loaded class. Converting, loading or iterating them can trigger additional engine work. Keep an initial probe small: list names, read one known row, then inspect one field. Don’t start by copying the entire table into Lua. Before changing data Changing a row affects every consumer that reads the live table. Rewriting recursive mount relationships can also introduce cycles. A previous broad attachment-row copying experiment froze the game; a targeted lookup hook was used instead. Make any edits reversible, preserve original values and test teardown. Adding a menu row doesn’t create its map or asset. See Range menus & travel . Walkthrough: document one table field 1. List names without changing rows Run the row-name example in a read-only test and save a bounded log. Confirm the table is valid and the result count is plausible. Treat the count as a discovery result; a hardcoded 86-row assertion is appropriate only for a fixture tied to that exact build, not for every future installation. 2. Pick one known row Use a row the normal game menu already displays. In the local demo, PistolRange was useful because the menu and actual pool destination were known. Record the exact string and whether FindRow accepts the representation you passed. 3. Read the schema next to the field A Blueprint-generated struct’s display label can differ from its reflected property identifier. Look for the full generated field name and property type in the dump. In a range row, a MapName field is a name; a Displayname field is text. Both can be converted for logging, but that does not make their original types interchangeable. 4. Compare the row with the UI Verify that the row’s label appears where expected and that selecting it reaches the recorded package. If the card is absent, inspect unlock filtering and menu construction. If the card is visible but travel fails, inspect destination availability instead. Those failures occur at different layers. 5. Record a useful finding Table asset: Game build: Row name and argument type: Field identifier and reflected type: Read value: Normal UI action used to verify it: What was not tested: This is more useful than publishing a large unannotated dump. It tells another modder which representation worked and which visible behaviour confirmed the interpretation. Copy plain values, not the entire row For a menu list, extract only the ordinary fields your feature needs, such as a display string and a verified destination ID. Keep soft-reference loading out of a broad initial dump. An accessor that is safe on one name field does not prove that every nested soft class array can be copied safely. If you mutate a row, keep the original values and a precise restoration path. Never assume that disabling your mod automatically rolls back a live table change or data already persisted by the game. What was checked Row-name listing and string FindRow calls were exercised locally. Broad soft-reference copying is a known failed experiment, not a recommended technique. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build.","sections":[{"id":"find-a-table","title":"Find a table","text":"The demo has tables for attachment mount definitions and range menu data. You can inspect them once the asset is loaded: ExecuteInGameThread(function() local data = LoadAsset(\"/Game/Data/Ranges/RangeData.RangeData\") if not data or not data:IsValid() then return end local names = data:GetRowNames() for i = 1, #names do print(\"[MyMod] Range row: \" .. tostring(names[i]) .. \"\\n\") end end"},{"id":"read-a-row","title":"Read a row","text":"In the tested loader, FindRow accepted a Lua string as the row name. Passing an FName in that particular call returned no row. This is different from APIs that explicitly expect an FName . local row = data:FindRow(\"PistolRange\") if row then local map = row.MapName_3_02B8F3864CDF2B67468FDD80A1766519 print(\"[MyMod] Map: \" .. map:ToString() .. \"\\n\") end The long suffix belongs to a Blueprint struct field. Copy the field name from your build’s dump instead of shortening it to MapName and assuming that will work."},{"id":"useful-tables","title":"Useful tables","text":"Asset What it describes /Game/Data/Ranges/RangeData.RangeData Range rows, display information and destination names. /Game/Data/Weapons/Attachment_Data.Attachment_Data Attachment mount relationships. The attachment table contained 86 mount rows in the inspected build. That is a build observation, not a constant your mod should require forever."},{"id":"soft-references","title":"Watch the field types","text":"A row can contain names, text, class references and soft class references. A soft reference is not the same thing as a loaded class. Converting, loading or iterating them can trigger additional engine work. Keep an initial probe small: list names, read one known row, then inspect one field. Don’t start by copying the entire table into Lua."},{"id":"changing-data","title":"Before changing data","text":"Changing a row affects every consumer that reads the live table. Rewriting recursive mount relationships can also introduce cycles. A previous broad attachment-row copying experiment froze the game; a targeted lookup hook was used instead. Make any edits reversible, preserve original values and test teardown. Adding a menu row doesn’t create its map or asset. See Range menus & travel ."},{"id":"table-investigation-walkthrough","title":"Walkthrough: document one table field","text":""},{"id":"table-step-list","title":"1. List names without changing rows","text":"Run the row-name example in a read-only test and save a bounded log. Confirm the table is valid and the result count is plausible. Treat the count as a discovery result; a hardcoded 86-row assertion is appropriate only for a fixture tied to that exact build, not for every future installation."},{"id":"table-step-one-row","title":"2. Pick one known row","text":"Use a row the normal game menu already displays. In the local demo, PistolRange was useful because the menu and actual pool destination were known. Record the exact string and whether FindRow accepts the representation you passed."},{"id":"table-step-read-the-schema","title":"3. Read the schema next to the field","text":"A Blueprint-generated struct’s display label can differ from its reflected property identifier. Look for the full generated field name and property type in the dump. In a range row, a MapName field is a name; a Displayname field is text. Both can be converted for logging, but that does not make their original types interchangeable."},{"id":"table-step-compare-ui","title":"4. Compare the row with the UI","text":"Verify that the row’s label appears where expected and that selecting it reaches the recorded package. If the card is absent, inspect unlock filtering and menu construction. If the card is visible but travel fails, inspect destination availability instead. Those failures occur at different layers."},{"id":"table-step-record-evidence","title":"5. Record a useful finding","text":"Table asset: Game build: Row name and argument type: Field identifier and reflected type: Read value: Normal UI action used to verify it: What was not tested: This is more useful than publishing a large unannotated dump. It tells another modder which representation worked and which visible behaviour confirmed the interpretation."},{"id":"table-safe-copying","title":"Copy plain values, not the entire row","text":"For a menu list, extract only the ordinary fields your feature needs, such as a display string and a verified destination ID. Keep soft-reference loading out of a broad initial dump. An accessor that is safe on one name field does not prove that every nested soft class array can be copied safely. If you mutate a row, keep the original values and a precise restoration path. Never assume that disabling your mod automatically rolls back a live table change or data already persisted by the game."},{"id":"tested-scope","title":"What was checked","text":"Row-name listing and string FindRow calls were exercised locally. Broad soft-reference copying is a known failed experiment, not a recommended technique. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."}]},{"id":"debugging","title":"Debugging","text":"Debugging Start with the log and a repeatable set of steps. Read the log In the layout used here, the loader log is Ballistic/Binaries/Win64/ue4ss/UE4SS.log . Check its timestamp and search for your mod’s prefix. Log when your script loads, when a hook installs and when the relevant callback runs. Those are three different checks. local function log(message) print(\"[MyFirstMod] \" .. tostring(message) .. \"\\n\") end log(\"Script loaded.\") Common errors Symptom What to check No log message Mod folder, enabled.txt, script filename and extraction location. Object is invalid Whether the asset is loaded and whether the world or menu changed. Hook never runs Exact function path, loaded class and whether the game uses that event. TrivialObject is not callable Whether the method is reflected in this game build. Freeze while iterating Container type, loop bounds and recursive hook calls. Native access violation The full crash stack, last callback and recently accessed engine objects. Handle Lua errors A protected call can log a Lua failure and stop that operation: local ok, err = pcall(function() -- Run one operation you are investigating here. end) if not ok then print(\"[MyFirstMod] \" .. tostring(err) .. \"\\n\") end pcall does not make an invalid native pointer safe. If the process crashes inside the engine or loader, simplify the callback and inspect the native stack. Reproduce a problem Write down the game build, loader version and mod version. Use a minimal setup with the mods needed for the test. Record the exact weapon, part or menu involved. Compare the result before and after the operation. Restart fully, then repeat the same steps. For a stat problem, record the value before fitting a part, after fitting it, and after removing it. “It went negative” doesn’t show whether the value dropped below its original baseline. After a game update Check function paths and signatures again. Confirm asset availability, menu construction and save behaviour separately. A successful startup log only proves that startup completed. Walkthrough: locate the first broken layer 1. Is the code you edited actually loaded? Add a unique startup version message, fully restart, and check the log timestamp. If an old version still appears, identify the installed copy before editing more files. A source folder and a packaged archive can both exist without either being the one the loader runs. 2. Does the relevant callback run? Log the first call, the context’s full name and a counter. If there are zero calls, inspect the path, class loading and the real UI flow. If there are too many calls, inspect nested recursion and construction/refresh events. Rate-limit repeated diagnostics after the first few samples. 3. Is the data what you think it is? Log the Lua type, reflected field type and an ordinary copied value. A non-nil wrapper is not automatically a loaded UObject. A property returning a placeholder is not automatically a callable function. Distinguish unavailable from a real zero, false or empty result. 4. Does the smallest native action work? Use a disposable fixture if the action can change an assembly or save. Call one known function with one known compatible argument. Confirm the actual engine state afterwards. A successful pcall means no Lua error was reported; it does not establish the visible result or absence of a later native crash. 5. Does the UI reflect the result? Check the model, label or menu after the action and after closing/reopening it. If the engine state changed but the display did not, investigate the refresh path. Avoid rewriting the state a second time simply because the first redraw was delayed. 6. Does the result survive the intended lifecycle? Travel, return, restart and load a disposable save only when persistence is part of the feature. If the behaviour works until travel, focus on stale references and world resets. If it fails only after restart, focus on the persisted representation and reconstruction path. Reading a Lua traceback Start with the first error message and the file/line where your code failed. Follow the call stack outward to learn which callback entered it. A later cleanup error may be secondary; keep the original failure visible rather than replacing it with another generic message. Use xpcall with debug.traceback when a small diagnostic operation needs a complete Lua stack. Do not wrap an entire feature in a silent pcall and continue as though it succeeded. local ok, result = xpcall(function() return inspectCurrentContext() end, debug.traceback) if not ok then print(\"[MyMod] Inspection failed: \" .. tostring(result) .. \"\\n\") return end Reading a native crash report Keep the exception address, module names and the last relevant fresh log lines. A UE4SS frame in a stack identifies where execution passed, not automatically which mod caused the invalid state. Compare a loader-only run, a minimal feature run and the exact reproduction. If the fault only appears after several menu rebuilds, test object lifetime and delegate ownership. If it appears during container membership checks, check reference representation and the binding’s expected types. Do not “fix” a native fault by adding a Lua error handler that cannot intercept it. A good final report State the smallest reproduction, observed values, versions, test method and remaining uncertainty. Attach a relevant excerpt rather than a complete private save or an enormous log. The testing walkthrough provides a template and an acceptance matrix.","sections":[{"id":"read-the-log","title":"Read the log","text":"In the layout used here, the loader log is Ballistic/Binaries/Win64/ue4ss/UE4SS.log . Check its timestamp and search for your mod’s prefix. Log when your script loads, when a hook installs and when the relevant callback runs. Those are three different checks. local function log(message) print(\"[MyFirstMod] \" .. tostring(message) .. \"\\n\") end log(\"Script loaded.\")"},{"id":"common-errors","title":"Common errors","text":"Symptom What to check No log message Mod folder, enabled.txt, script filename and extraction location. Object is invalid Whether the asset is loaded and whether the world or menu changed. Hook never runs Exact function path, loaded class and whether the game uses that event. TrivialObject is not callable Whether the method is reflected in this game build. Freeze while iterating Container type, loop bounds and recursive hook calls. Native access violation The full crash stack, last callback and recently accessed engine objects."},{"id":"handle-lua-errors","title":"Handle Lua errors","text":"A protected call can log a Lua failure and stop that operation: local ok, err = pcall(function() -- Run one operation you are investigating here. end) if not ok then print(\"[MyFirstMod] \" .. tostring(err) .. \"\\n\") end pcall does not make an invalid native pointer safe. If the process crashes inside the engine or loader, simplify the callback and inspect the native stack."},{"id":"reproduce-a-problem","title":"Reproduce a problem","text":"Write down the game build, loader version and mod version. Use a minimal setup with the mods needed for the test. Record the exact weapon, part or menu involved. Compare the result before and after the operation. Restart fully, then repeat the same steps. For a stat problem, record the value before fitting a part, after fitting it, and after removing it. “It went negative” doesn’t show whether the value dropped below its original baseline."},{"id":"after-an-update","title":"After a game update","text":"Check function paths and signatures again. Confirm asset availability, menu construction and save behaviour separately. A successful startup log only proves that startup completed."},{"id":"debugging-decision-tree","title":"Walkthrough: locate the first broken layer","text":""},{"id":"debug-step-startup","title":"1. Is the code you edited actually loaded?","text":"Add a unique startup version message, fully restart, and check the log timestamp. If an old version still appears, identify the installed copy before editing more files. A source folder and a packaged archive can both exist without either being the one the loader runs."},{"id":"debug-step-callback","title":"2. Does the relevant callback run?","text":"Log the first call, the context’s full name and a counter. If there are zero calls, inspect the path, class loading and the real UI flow. If there are too many calls, inspect nested recursion and construction/refresh events. Rate-limit repeated diagnostics after the first few samples."},{"id":"debug-step-data","title":"3. Is the data what you think it is?","text":"Log the Lua type, reflected field type and an ordinary copied value. A non-nil wrapper is not automatically a loaded UObject. A property returning a placeholder is not automatically a callable function. Distinguish unavailable from a real zero, false or empty result."},{"id":"debug-step-action","title":"4. Does the smallest native action work?","text":"Use a disposable fixture if the action can change an assembly or save. Call one known function with one known compatible argument. Confirm the actual engine state afterwards. A successful pcall means no Lua error was reported; it does not establish the visible result or absence of a later native crash."},{"id":"debug-step-refresh","title":"5. Does the UI reflect the result?","text":"Check the model, label or menu after the action and after closing/reopening it. If the engine state changed but the display did not, investigate the refresh path. Avoid rewriting the state a second time simply because the first redraw was delayed."},{"id":"debug-step-persistence","title":"6. Does the result survive the intended lifecycle?","text":"Travel, return, restart and load a disposable save only when persistence is part of the feature. If the behaviour works until travel, focus on stale references and world resets. If it fails only after restart, focus on the persisted representation and reconstruction path."},{"id":"read-a-lua-traceback","title":"Reading a Lua traceback","text":"Start with the first error message and the file/line where your code failed. Follow the call stack outward to learn which callback entered it. A later cleanup error may be secondary; keep the original failure visible rather than replacing it with another generic message. Use xpcall with debug.traceback when a small diagnostic operation needs a complete Lua stack. Do not wrap an entire feature in a silent pcall and continue as though it succeeded. local ok, result = xpcall(function() return inspectCurrentContext() end, debug.traceback) if not ok then print(\"[MyMod] Inspection failed: \" .. tostring(result) .. \"\\n\") return end"},{"id":"native-crash-triage","title":"Reading a native crash report","text":"Keep the exception address, module names and the last relevant fresh log lines. A UE4SS frame in a stack identifies where execution passed, not automatically which mod caused the invalid state. Compare a loader-only run, a minimal feature run and the exact reproduction. If the fault only appears after several menu rebuilds, test object lifetime and delegate ownership. If it appears during container membership checks, check reference representation and the binding’s expected types. Do not “fix” a native fault by adding a Lua error handler that cannot intercept it."},{"id":"debugging-report","title":"A good final report","text":"State the smallest reproduction, observed values, versions, test method and remaining uncertainty. Attach a relevant excerpt rather than a complete private save or an enormous log. The testing walkthrough provides a template and an acceptance matrix."}]},{"id":"performance","title":"Performance & profiling","text":"Performance & profiling Measure the expensive boundary between Lua and the engine before changing update rates. 1. Make a baseline Record the game scene, mod configuration and action sequence. Compare the same range and weapon with the feature disabled and enabled. A different target layout or gun assembly can change workload enough to invalidate the comparison. Separate idle cost from action cost. A UI might be cheap while closed but hitch when rebuilding every widget after a click. An actor registry might be cheap to update but expensive to rediscover. 2. Count work before timing it local counters = {discoveries = 0, samples = 0, setters = 0} local function report() print(string.format( \"[MyMod] discoveries=%d samples=%d setters=%d\\n\", counters.discoveries, counters.samples, counters.setters )) end For an unchanged menu, how many labels are set again each second? For a static target, how often do you reload its mesh? A counter often exposes redundant work before a profiler is needed. Report periodically, not on every frame. Logging can become part of the hitch you are trying to diagnose. 3. Give tasks different cadences Work Reasonable starting approach Find loaded classes Slow retry until ready; stop once installed. Rediscover a target registry Occasional refresh plus world-change invalidation. Update known moving actors Bounded frequent samples. Render interpolated visuals A local visual cadence independent of network sends. Capture weapon meshes On identity changes, with a slower periodic verification. These are design starting points, not universal tick rates. Measure your scene and adjust the budget. Reducing cosmetic updates must not silently reduce gameplay-event reliability. 4. Avoid catch-up bursts A stalled frame can leave several nominal deadlines behind. For a cosmetic update, replaying all missed steps in one frame makes the stall worse. Advance from elapsed time and cap work instead. local nextUpdate = nil local lastNow = nil local function shouldUpdate(now, rate) local period = 1 / rate if not nextUpdate or not lastNow or now \u003c lastNow then nextUpdate = now end lastNow = now if now \u003c nextUpdate then return false end nextUpdate = math.max(nextUpdate + period, now + period * 0.1) return true end Call this with one coherent clock. The backwards-clock branch matters after a world reset when a world-relative clock starts over. A setting change should explicitly reset the schedule too, rather than waiting on a deadline calculated for the old rate. 5. Reuse unchanged engine objects Cache asset references after successful loading. For a weapon proxy, compare the mesh identity before replacing an actor. For a menu, compare the intended text, enabled state and visibility with the last successful values before issuing setters. Only mark a cached value as applied after the setter succeeds. If a native operation fails, caching the desired value too early can prevent the retry that would restore the UI. 6. Budget large registries Process a fixed number of ordinary targets per update and carry a cursor forward. Prioritise moving or recently hit targets separately, with its own cap. Without the second cap, “priority” can grow into another unbounded full-world pass. Keep event queues bounded and define backpressure. Silently dropping a cosmetic sample is different from dropping an acknowledged hit or save-transfer chunk. Verify the improvement Repeat the baseline sequence, compare counters, and check that the feature still works after travel, a missing object and a setter failure. Faster code that displays stale state is not a successful optimisation.","sections":[{"id":"make-a-baseline","title":"1. Make a baseline","text":"Record the game scene, mod configuration and action sequence. Compare the same range and weapon with the feature disabled and enabled. A different target layout or gun assembly can change workload enough to invalidate the comparison. Separate idle cost from action cost. A UI might be cheap while closed but hitch when rebuilding every widget after a click. An actor registry might be cheap to update but expensive to rediscover."},{"id":"count-work","title":"2. Count work before timing it","text":"local counters = {discoveries = 0, samples = 0, setters = 0} local function report() print(string.format( \"[MyMod] discoveries=%d samples=%d setters=%d\\n\", counters.discoveries, counters.samples, counters.setters )) end For an unchanged menu, how many labels are set again each second? For a static target, how often do you reload its mesh? A counter often exposes redundant work before a profiler is needed. Report periodically, not on every frame. Logging can become part of the hitch you are trying to diagnose."},{"id":"separate-cadences","title":"3. Give tasks different cadences","text":"Work Reasonable starting approach Find loaded classes Slow retry until ready; stop once installed. Rediscover a target registry Occasional refresh plus world-change invalidation. Update known moving actors Bounded frequent samples. Render interpolated visuals A local visual cadence independent of network sends. Capture weapon meshes On identity changes, with a slower periodic verification. These are design starting points, not universal tick rates. Measure your scene and adjust the budget. Reducing cosmetic updates must not silently reduce gameplay-event reliability."},{"id":"schedule-without-bursts","title":"4. Avoid catch-up bursts","text":"A stalled frame can leave several nominal deadlines behind. For a cosmetic update, replaying all missed steps in one frame makes the stall worse. Advance from elapsed time and cap work instead. local nextUpdate = nil local lastNow = nil local function shouldUpdate(now, rate) local period = 1 / rate if not nextUpdate or not lastNow or now \u003c lastNow then nextUpdate = now end lastNow = now if now \u003c nextUpdate then return false end nextUpdate = math.max(nextUpdate + period, now + period * 0.1) return true end Call this with one coherent clock. The backwards-clock branch matters after a world reset when a world-relative clock starts over. A setting change should explicitly reset the schedule too, rather than waiting on a deadline calculated for the old rate."},{"id":"reuse-engine-objects","title":"5. Reuse unchanged engine objects","text":"Cache asset references after successful loading. For a weapon proxy, compare the mesh identity before replacing an actor. For a menu, compare the intended text, enabled state and visibility with the last successful values before issuing setters. Only mark a cached value as applied after the setter succeeds. If a native operation fails, caching the desired value too early can prevent the retry that would restore the UI."},{"id":"budget-large-registries","title":"6. Budget large registries","text":"Process a fixed number of ordinary targets per update and carry a cursor forward. Prioritise moving or recently hit targets separately, with its own cap. Without the second cap, “priority” can grow into another unbounded full-world pass. Keep event queues bounded and define backpressure. Silently dropping a cosmetic sample is different from dropping an acknowledged hit or save-transfer chunk."},{"id":"verify-the-improvement","title":"Verify the improvement","text":"Repeat the baseline sequence, compare counters, and check that the feature still works after travel, a missing object and a setter failure. Faster code that displays stale state is not a successful optimisation."}]},{"id":"settings-files","title":"Settings & local files","text":"Settings & local files Validate preferences, bound local reads and avoid confusing mod data with game saves. 1. Separate the data you own Keep user configuration, mod preferences, temporary diagnostics and the game’s actual save slot as distinct categories. A UI preference file should not become a back door for loading arbitrary Lua or replacing progression. Name the data directory and ownership in your mod’s instructions. The local examples derive a mod-owned path from their script location rather than guessing the process working directory. Working directories can differ between launcher, shipping executable and test tools. 2. Parse into typed defaults local function parseBoolean(value) if value == \"1\" then return true end if value == \"0\" then return false end return nil end local enabled = parseBoolean(rawValue) if enabled == nil then enabled = true end Check nil explicitly so a valid false value is not replaced by the default. Validate numeric settings for type, integer/range requirements and finite values. Unknown keys can be ignored with a diagnostic rather than becoming new executable behaviour. 3. Read a bounded amount local function readSmallFile(path, limit) local file = io.open(path, \"rb\") if not file then return nil, \"missing\" end local bytes = file:read(limit + 1) file:close() if not bytes then return \"\" end if #bytes > limit then return nil, \"too large\" end return bytes end A missing preference file can mean first-run defaults. A file exceeding its size limit is a different result. Close handles before returning and keep read errors visible enough to diagnose a repeated failure. 4. Pick a deliberate write policy Do not rewrite preferences on every render tick. Save when the user changes a setting, preferably after serialising validated plain values. Use a temporary file and an appropriate replacement/backup strategy for the platform. Removing the old file and then renaming is not a universal atomic replacement guarantee. Treat a write/rename failure as a failure. Do not update your “saved” status before the operation succeeds. Keep the previous usable file when recovery matters; a log message cannot recover bytes already deleted. 5. Keep filenames constrained A fixed filename or narrow allowlist is easier to reason about than accepting an arbitrary path from UI or a network peer. Reject path separators and traversal where the feature only expects a preference basename. Never accept remote Lua source and evaluate it as a configuration update. For a genuine save-transfer feature, use the separate consent, slot restriction, backup and reload workflow in Reading & backing up saves . A chat or settings handler should not share that file authority. Test the edge cases No file: defaults load and a useful first-run state appears. Invalid boolean or number: the documented default/rejection occurs. Unknown key: no new action is executed. Oversized file: the read is rejected without allocating an unbounded buffer. Write failure: the UI reports it and does not pretend the preference persisted. Reset defaults: intended settings reset while unrelated user data stays intact.","sections":[{"id":"separate-data","title":"1. Separate the data you own","text":"Keep user configuration, mod preferences, temporary diagnostics and the game’s actual save slot as distinct categories. A UI preference file should not become a back door for loading arbitrary Lua or replacing progression. Name the data directory and ownership in your mod’s instructions. The local examples derive a mod-owned path from their script location rather than guessing the process working directory. Working directories can differ between launcher, shipping executable and test tools."},{"id":"typed-defaults","title":"2. Parse into typed defaults","text":"local function parseBoolean(value) if value == \"1\" then return true end if value == \"0\" then return false end return nil end local enabled = parseBoolean(rawValue) if enabled == nil then enabled = true end Check nil explicitly so a valid false value is not replaced by the default. Validate numeric settings for type, integer/range requirements and finite values. Unknown keys can be ignored with a diagnostic rather than becoming new executable behaviour."},{"id":"bounded-read","title":"3. Read a bounded amount","text":"local function readSmallFile(path, limit) local file = io.open(path, \"rb\") if not file then return nil, \"missing\" end local bytes = file:read(limit + 1) file:close() if not bytes then return \"\" end if #bytes > limit then return nil, \"too large\" end return bytes end A missing preference file can mean first-run defaults. A file exceeding its size limit is a different result. Close handles before returning and keep read errors visible enough to diagnose a repeated failure."},{"id":"write-policy","title":"4. Pick a deliberate write policy","text":"Do not rewrite preferences on every render tick. Save when the user changes a setting, preferably after serialising validated plain values. Use a temporary file and an appropriate replacement/backup strategy for the platform. Removing the old file and then renaming is not a universal atomic replacement guarantee. Treat a write/rename failure as a failure. Do not update your “saved” status before the operation succeeds. Keep the previous usable file when recovery matters; a log message cannot recover bytes already deleted."},{"id":"paths","title":"5. Keep filenames constrained","text":"A fixed filename or narrow allowlist is easier to reason about than accepting an arbitrary path from UI or a network peer. Reject path separators and traversal where the feature only expects a preference basename. Never accept remote Lua source and evaluate it as a configuration update. For a genuine save-transfer feature, use the separate consent, slot restriction, backup and reload workflow in Reading & backing up saves . A chat or settings handler should not share that file authority."},{"id":"tests","title":"Test the edge cases","text":"No file: defaults load and a useful first-run state appears. Invalid boolean or number: the documented default/rejection occurs. Unknown key: no new action is executed. Oversized file: the read is rejected without allocating an unbounded buffer. Write failure: the UI reports it and does not pretend the preference persisted. Reset defaults: intended settings reset while unrelated user data stays intact."}]},{"id":"reflection","title":"Reflection, classes & instances","text":"Reflection, classes & instances Understand what Lua can see, which object you found and why a C++ name may not be callable. The four objects to keep separate Start with four distinct ideas: an asset package, a generated class, a class default object and a live instance. The workbench gun is an instance. Its class describes its type. A default object supplies class defaults. None of those is interchangeable with a string containing a package path. Question What you need What kind of gun is this? The live object’s class. What is the player editing? The editor’s current live weapon. Which defaults does this type use? The class default object, inspected deliberately. Can the game load this content? The actual cooked asset and its dependencies. A matching class name is a starting point, not proof that you selected the right live object. The range pawn and workbench can reference different guns at the same time. Record the current world and the selection reason beside your object name. 1. Check the reflected surface UE4SS exposes reflected Unreal properties and functions through its bindings. A function mentioned in C++ documentation is not automatically callable from Lua. Inspect the exact owning class and loaded function before writing the call. The local TrivialObject failures are a practical example of why this check matters. Use the object-dump workflow to identify a member, then a small read-only callback to observe it. Avoid guessing offsets or using an address from an old process. The UObject binding reference describes the current API; compare it with your installed loader. 2. Inspect identity before values -- Context: object is a valid live UObject on the game thread. local class = object:GetClass() print(\"[MyMod] Object: \" .. object:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") if class and class:IsValid() then print(\"[MyMod] Class: \" .. class:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end Log the full object name, not just the display label. Two widgets may show the same text while belonging to different menus. A runtime suffix can identify an instance during a session, but it is not a permanent save key or a cross-PC identity. 3. Check inheritance deliberately A derived Blueprint can inherit a property, override a function or add another event path. Find the declaring class and the actual runtime class. Registering a base hook and a derived hook may observe one action twice; failing to handle a derived target can miss it entirely. local expected = StaticFindObject(\"/Script/Ballistic.C_WeaponBase\") if expected and expected:IsValid() and object:IsA(expected) then print(\"[MyMod] This is a weapon-base instance.\\n\") end Passing IsA establishes type compatibility, not readiness for a particular action. A weapon can have the right base class but no selected ammunition, a missing child assembly or an uninitialised menu context. 4. Separate read, call and write Reading a numeric property, calling a getter, assigning a property and invoking a gameplay action are different operations. A property setter may bypass the normal action’s refresh, bookkeeping and save logic. A function that sounds like a refresh may also change derived gameplay state. Build a research note with the member’s owner, reflected type, expected context, observed result and whether it changed anything. When moving from inspection to mutation, add explicit action ownership, cancellation and recovery rather than putting a setter into an always-running discovery loop. Verify your interpretation Find the object in the actual menu or range. Read class and identity before the member. Trigger the normal game action once and compare before/after values. Repeat after a menu rebuild or world travel. Keep dump-only findings separate from operations exercised on a live object. For the full selection problem, continue to Finding game objects . For lifetime, use Spawning & actor ownership .","sections":[{"id":"mental-model","title":"The four objects to keep separate","text":"Start with four distinct ideas: an asset package, a generated class, a class default object and a live instance. The workbench gun is an instance. Its class describes its type. A default object supplies class defaults. None of those is interchangeable with a string containing a package path. Question What you need What kind of gun is this? The live object’s class. What is the player editing? The editor’s current live weapon. Which defaults does this type use? The class default object, inspected deliberately. Can the game load this content? The actual cooked asset and its dependencies. A matching class name is a starting point, not proof that you selected the right live object. The range pawn and workbench can reference different guns at the same time. Record the current world and the selection reason beside your object name."},{"id":"reflected-surface","title":"1. Check the reflected surface","text":"UE4SS exposes reflected Unreal properties and functions through its bindings. A function mentioned in C++ documentation is not automatically callable from Lua. Inspect the exact owning class and loaded function before writing the call. The local TrivialObject failures are a practical example of why this check matters. Use the object-dump workflow to identify a member, then a small read-only callback to observe it. Avoid guessing offsets or using an address from an old process. The UObject binding reference describes the current API; compare it with your installed loader."},{"id":"inspect-identity","title":"2. Inspect identity before values","text":"-- Context: object is a valid live UObject on the game thread. local class = object:GetClass() print(\"[MyMod] Object: \" .. object:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") if class and class:IsValid() then print(\"[MyMod] Class: \" .. class:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end Log the full object name, not just the display label. Two widgets may show the same text while belonging to different menus. A runtime suffix can identify an instance during a session, but it is not a permanent save key or a cross-PC identity."},{"id":"inheritance","title":"3. Check inheritance deliberately","text":"A derived Blueprint can inherit a property, override a function or add another event path. Find the declaring class and the actual runtime class. Registering a base hook and a derived hook may observe one action twice; failing to handle a derived target can miss it entirely. local expected = StaticFindObject(\"/Script/Ballistic.C_WeaponBase\") if expected and expected:IsValid() and object:IsA(expected) then print(\"[MyMod] This is a weapon-base instance.\\n\") end Passing IsA establishes type compatibility, not readiness for a particular action. A weapon can have the right base class but no selected ammunition, a missing child assembly or an uninitialised menu context."},{"id":"read-vs-write","title":"4. Separate read, call and write","text":"Reading a numeric property, calling a getter, assigning a property and invoking a gameplay action are different operations. A property setter may bypass the normal action’s refresh, bookkeeping and save logic. A function that sounds like a refresh may also change derived gameplay state. Build a research note with the member’s owner, reflected type, expected context, observed result and whether it changed anything. When moving from inspection to mutation, add explicit action ownership, cancellation and recovery rather than putting a setter into an always-running discovery loop."},{"id":"verification","title":"Verify your interpretation","text":"Find the object in the actual menu or range. Read class and identity before the member. Trigger the normal game action once and compare before/after values. Repeat after a menu rebuild or world travel. Keep dump-only findings separate from operations exercised on a live object. For the full selection problem, continue to Finding game objects . For lifetime, use Spawning & actor ownership ."}]},{"id":"unreal-types","title":"Names, text & soft references","text":"Names, text & soft references Choose the right representation for names, display text, classes and references. Names and display text are different A Lua string is ordinary script text. Unreal also has FString, FName and FText. Epic describes FName as an identifier type and FText as the user-facing/localisation text type. Those purposes do not mean every binding accepts either one automatically. See Epic’s string-handling reference . Value Typical use in these guides Lua string Logging, configuration and the tested FindRow argument. FName Row/identifier values where the engine signature expects a name. FText Setting a native widget’s displayed text. Loaded UClass Choosing the class to instantiate or fit. Soft class reference A deferred content reference stored in progression/templates. 1. Follow the called API’s signature The local range-table test used FindRow(\"PistolRange\"). Passing an FName to that particular binding did not return the row. Other APIs in the same game do expect FName. The correct conclusion is to match each API, not to replace every name with a string everywhere. -- Each line illustrates a different destination type. local row = data:FindRow(\"PistolRange\") local id = FName(\"PistolRange\") label:SetText(FText(\"My range\")) Keep reflected member spellings exact, including generated suffixes. FName’s comparison behaviour does not justify changing filenames, Blueprint property spellings or authored asset paths arbitrarily. 2. Convert only for the purpose you need Convert a name/text value to a Lua-readable string for logging or a copied UI snapshot. That does not preserve every localisation or identity property of the original object. Don’t use a translated display label as the persistent ID for a target or part. If an object lookup expects a class path, keep that path in a distinct field from its display name. A stable internal ID and a user-facing label can change independently; a language switch should not change gameplay identity. 3. Treat soft references as a separate type A stored soft reference is not necessarily an already loaded UObject. Inspect its type and the supported conversion/load path. An ownership set storing soft class references cannot safely be treated as a set of arbitrary loaded UClass values. The local ownership experiment crashed after using the wrong representation in a native set check. A later isolated conversion probe established one safe call, not a complete ownership solution. Use Ownership & unlocks for the full investigation and its remaining limits. 4. Copy small struct values explicitly Positions, rotations and colours are structs with named fields in these bindings. Copy the fields you need into ordinary Lua data for later comparison. Don’t assume that retaining a borrowed wrapper makes it independent of the native owner or callback lifetime. local raw = actor:K2_GetActorLocation() local saved = {X = raw.X, Y = raw.Y, Z = raw.Z} -- saved is a snapshot, not a live engine reference. Troubleshooting type mismatches Write down the stored type and the function’s expected type. Inspect the original path before normalising it. Distinguish unavailable from a real nil/false/zero value. Test a single known positive and negative case before a whole catalog. Do not use pcall as a claim of native memory safety. Use Arrays, sets & maps for container-specific handling and Reading data tables for generated row fields.","sections":[{"id":"string-families","title":"Names and display text are different","text":"A Lua string is ordinary script text. Unreal also has FString, FName and FText. Epic describes FName as an identifier type and FText as the user-facing/localisation text type. Those purposes do not mean every binding accepts either one automatically. See Epic’s string-handling reference . Value Typical use in these guides Lua string Logging, configuration and the tested FindRow argument. FName Row/identifier values where the engine signature expects a name. FText Setting a native widget’s displayed text. Loaded UClass Choosing the class to instantiate or fit. Soft class reference A deferred content reference stored in progression/templates."},{"id":"follow-signature","title":"1. Follow the called API’s signature","text":"The local range-table test used FindRow(\"PistolRange\"). Passing an FName to that particular binding did not return the row. Other APIs in the same game do expect FName. The correct conclusion is to match each API, not to replace every name with a string everywhere. -- Each line illustrates a different destination type. local row = data:FindRow(\"PistolRange\") local id = FName(\"PistolRange\") label:SetText(FText(\"My range\")) Keep reflected member spellings exact, including generated suffixes. FName’s comparison behaviour does not justify changing filenames, Blueprint property spellings or authored asset paths arbitrarily."},{"id":"copy-values","title":"2. Convert only for the purpose you need","text":"Convert a name/text value to a Lua-readable string for logging or a copied UI snapshot. That does not preserve every localisation or identity property of the original object. Don’t use a translated display label as the persistent ID for a target or part. If an object lookup expects a class path, keep that path in a distinct field from its display name. A stable internal ID and a user-facing label can change independently; a language switch should not change gameplay identity."},{"id":"soft-references","title":"3. Treat soft references as a separate type","text":"A stored soft reference is not necessarily an already loaded UObject. Inspect its type and the supported conversion/load path. An ownership set storing soft class references cannot safely be treated as a set of arbitrary loaded UClass values. The local ownership experiment crashed after using the wrong representation in a native set check. A later isolated conversion probe established one safe call, not a complete ownership solution. Use Ownership & unlocks for the full investigation and its remaining limits."},{"id":"struct-values","title":"4. Copy small struct values explicitly","text":"Positions, rotations and colours are structs with named fields in these bindings. Copy the fields you need into ordinary Lua data for later comparison. Don’t assume that retaining a borrowed wrapper makes it independent of the native owner or callback lifetime. local raw = actor:K2_GetActorLocation() local saved = {X = raw.X, Y = raw.Y, Z = raw.Z} -- saved is a snapshot, not a live engine reference."},{"id":"checklist","title":"Troubleshooting type mismatches","text":"Write down the stored type and the function’s expected type. Inspect the original path before normalising it. Distinguish unavailable from a real nil/false/zero value. Test a single known positive and negative case before a whole catalog. Do not use pcall as a claim of native memory safety. Use Arrays, sets & maps for container-specific handling and Reading data tables for generated row fields."}]},{"id":"containers","title":"Arrays, sets & maps","text":"Arrays, sets & maps Read Unreal arrays, sets and maps without confusing them with ordinary Lua tables. 1. Identify the container first An ordinary Lua table and a reflected Unreal container can look similar in a short snippet while supporting different operations. Read the field type from the dump before choosing an iterator. The local PurchasedParts field is a set, UnlockedRanges is an array, and SavedWeaponTemplates is a map. Container Question it answers Array / TArray What is at this index, in this ordered collection? Set / TSet Does this typed value belong to the collection? Map / TMap Which value is associated with this typed key? Plain Lua table What state did my script construct itself? 2. Don’t mix index bases The tested UE4SS array wrappers in these guides used numeric indices starting at one. UMG panel methods such as GetChildAt used zero-based indices. C++ examples also commonly use zero-based array indexing. Copy the convention belonging to the actual API rather than assuming a language-wide rule. -- A tested reflected-array pattern: for i = 1, #points do local point = points[i] -- Validate point before using it. end -- A UMG panel method pattern: for i = 0, panel:GetChildrenCount() - 1 do local widget = panel:GetChildAt(i) -- Validate widget before using it. end 3. Bound discovery and report truncation The original attachment/range investigation found that ipairs on a particular Unreal wrapper could continue past the reported length. Numeric iteration using the reported count avoided that failure. This is a recorded binding-specific result, not a claim that ipairs is broken on Lua tables. A diagnostic can print the first twelve items and say the rest were omitted. A clear-all feature cannot silently process twelve and report that everything was removed. Decide whether an excessive count causes a stop, a visible partial result or a bounded multi-step job. 4. Reacquire after mutation Removing a child part can rebuild the menu’s array of POI widgets. Iterating the old array after the native action may use stale references. Perform one action, let the refresh happen, then acquire the new collection before continuing. Copying every element into a Lua list does not necessarily solve lifetime: those elements may still point at native widgets that were just destroyed. Copy plain identifiers for your plan and resolve live objects at the moment of the next action. 5. Match set and map key types A set of soft class references requires the representation expected by its binding. A string that prints like the class path and a loaded UClass are not automatically equivalent keys. Start with a known contained value and a known absent value; inspect result and stability independently. Map enumeration can also be loader-specific. Don’t publish a generic pairs call over a reflected map just because it works on your own Lua dictionary. Use the supported API for your loader and keep a small native fixture for sensitive membership operations. A useful container debug record field / owner: reflected container type: key or element type: reported count: iteration method and index base: sample values: operation that may rebuild the owner: positive / negative membership results: If the process freezes rather than throwing a Lua error, inspect unbounded iteration and recursion before adding another error handler. For the actual failed ownership filters, see the ownership guide .","sections":[{"id":"identify-container","title":"1. Identify the container first","text":"An ordinary Lua table and a reflected Unreal container can look similar in a short snippet while supporting different operations. Read the field type from the dump before choosing an iterator. The local PurchasedParts field is a set, UnlockedRanges is an array, and SavedWeaponTemplates is a map. Container Question it answers Array / TArray What is at this index, in this ordered collection? Set / TSet Does this typed value belong to the collection? Map / TMap Which value is associated with this typed key? Plain Lua table What state did my script construct itself?"},{"id":"index-bases","title":"2. Don’t mix index bases","text":"The tested UE4SS array wrappers in these guides used numeric indices starting at one. UMG panel methods such as GetChildAt used zero-based indices. C++ examples also commonly use zero-based array indexing. Copy the convention belonging to the actual API rather than assuming a language-wide rule. -- A tested reflected-array pattern: for i = 1, #points do local point = points[i] -- Validate point before using it. end -- A UMG panel method pattern: for i = 0, panel:GetChildrenCount() - 1 do local widget = panel:GetChildAt(i) -- Validate widget before using it. end"},{"id":"bound-iteration","title":"3. Bound discovery and report truncation","text":"The original attachment/range investigation found that ipairs on a particular Unreal wrapper could continue past the reported length. Numeric iteration using the reported count avoided that failure. This is a recorded binding-specific result, not a claim that ipairs is broken on Lua tables. A diagnostic can print the first twelve items and say the rest were omitted. A clear-all feature cannot silently process twelve and report that everything was removed. Decide whether an excessive count causes a stop, a visible partial result or a bounded multi-step job."},{"id":"mutation","title":"4. Reacquire after mutation","text":"Removing a child part can rebuild the menu’s array of POI widgets. Iterating the old array after the native action may use stale references. Perform one action, let the refresh happen, then acquire the new collection before continuing. Copying every element into a Lua list does not necessarily solve lifetime: those elements may still point at native widgets that were just destroyed. Copy plain identifiers for your plan and resolve live objects at the moment of the next action."},{"id":"membership","title":"5. Match set and map key types","text":"A set of soft class references requires the representation expected by its binding. A string that prints like the class path and a loaded UClass are not automatically equivalent keys. Start with a known contained value and a known absent value; inspect result and stability independently. Map enumeration can also be loader-specific. Don’t publish a generic pairs call over a reflected map just because it works on your own Lua dictionary. Use the supported API for your loader and keep a small native fixture for sensitive membership operations."},{"id":"debug-record","title":"A useful container debug record","text":"field / owner: reflected container type: key or element type: reported count: iteration method and index base: sample values: operation that may rebuild the owner: positive / negative membership results: If the process freezes rather than throwing a Lua error, inspect unbounded iteration and recursion before adding another error handler. For the actual failed ownership filters, see the ownership guide ."}]},{"id":"transforms","title":"Vectors, rotations & spaces","text":"Vectors, rotations & spaces Place objects in the right coordinate space and diagnose wrong scale or orientation. Start with a coordinate-space label Epic documents Unreal’s usual world convention as left-handed, Z-up, with X forward and Y right. World, local and screen space are different contexts; importing a model from another tool can require a basis conversion. See coordinate systems and spaces for that convention. Label every stored transform in your own code: worldPosition, weaponLocalPosition or screenPosition. The label prevents a large class of bugs that otherwise look like a mysterious constant offset. 1. Separate position, rotation and scale Value Local binding shape seen in examples Location vector X, Y, Z Rotator Pitch, Yaw, Roll Transform rotation Quaternion fields X, Y, Z, W Transform scale Scale3D with X, Y, Z A quaternion’s W is not a rotator roll. Don’t copy three quaternion components into Pitch/Yaw/Roll or assume imported Euler angles use the same order and units. 2. Calibrate one object Start with one known cooked mesh and neutral scale. Place it a small safe distance from a known reference and log requested versus actual location. Check its bounds and facing before assembling twenty objects. A mesh’s modelling origin may be far from the part you think of as its centre. For a weapon preview, distinguish model-space scale from the root actor and parent component transforms. Repeated arbitrary scale changes can hide a wrong parent transform rather than solve it. 3. Capture relative transforms for attachments A proxy gun should preserve the child mesh’s relationship to the gun root, not copy the original player’s world position. Capture the root transform, transform child world positions into root-local space, then rebuild them against the destination root. The local proxy code used KismetMathLibrary inverse-transform helpers rather than subtracting world positions alone. Position subtraction ignores root rotation and scale. Verify moving, turning, aiming and switching weapons, not just a single static pose. 4. Interpolate yaw across the wrap local function yawBetween(a, b, alpha) local difference = (b - a + 180) % 360 - 180 return a + difference * alpha end -- The halfway orientation follows the short arc near 180 degrees. local middle = yawBetween(179, -179, 0.5) A plain average of 179 and -179 is zero, which points the wrong way for the intended short transition. General 3D rotation interpolation needs the appropriate rotation representation; the yaw example is intentionally limited to a single axis. 5. Validate positions before applying them Reject non-numeric, NaN, infinite or unreasonably large coordinates in network or imported data. Keep a deliberate bound for the feature’s world scale. A malformed pose should not teleport a proxy to a huge coordinate or enter a physics call. local function finite(n) return type(n) == \"number\" and n == n and math.abs(n) \u003c 10000000 end local function positionOK(p) return type(p) == \"table\" and finite(p.X) and finite(p.Y) and finite(p.Z) end Diagnose by symptom A model following the player but facing backwards suggests a basis/orientation issue. A model drifting when the gun turns suggests a space-conversion error. Correct position with wrong size suggests scale/origin assumptions. A successful location setter with nothing visible also requires checking occlusion and the current world. Keep those tests separate.","sections":[{"id":"spaces","title":"Start with a coordinate-space label","text":"Epic documents Unreal’s usual world convention as left-handed, Z-up, with X forward and Y right. World, local and screen space are different contexts; importing a model from another tool can require a basis conversion. See coordinate systems and spaces for that convention. Label every stored transform in your own code: worldPosition, weaponLocalPosition or screenPosition. The label prevents a large class of bugs that otherwise look like a mysterious constant offset."},{"id":"position-rotation-scale","title":"1. Separate position, rotation and scale","text":"Value Local binding shape seen in examples Location vector X, Y, Z Rotator Pitch, Yaw, Roll Transform rotation Quaternion fields X, Y, Z, W Transform scale Scale3D with X, Y, Z A quaternion’s W is not a rotator roll. Don’t copy three quaternion components into Pitch/Yaw/Roll or assume imported Euler angles use the same order and units."},{"id":"known-object","title":"2. Calibrate one object","text":"Start with one known cooked mesh and neutral scale. Place it a small safe distance from a known reference and log requested versus actual location. Check its bounds and facing before assembling twenty objects. A mesh’s modelling origin may be far from the part you think of as its centre. For a weapon preview, distinguish model-space scale from the root actor and parent component transforms. Repeated arbitrary scale changes can hide a wrong parent transform rather than solve it."},{"id":"relative-capture","title":"3. Capture relative transforms for attachments","text":"A proxy gun should preserve the child mesh’s relationship to the gun root, not copy the original player’s world position. Capture the root transform, transform child world positions into root-local space, then rebuild them against the destination root. The local proxy code used KismetMathLibrary inverse-transform helpers rather than subtracting world positions alone. Position subtraction ignores root rotation and scale. Verify moving, turning, aiming and switching weapons, not just a single static pose."},{"id":"angle-wrap","title":"4. Interpolate yaw across the wrap","text":"local function yawBetween(a, b, alpha) local difference = (b - a + 180) % 360 - 180 return a + difference * alpha end -- The halfway orientation follows the short arc near 180 degrees. local middle = yawBetween(179, -179, 0.5) A plain average of 179 and -179 is zero, which points the wrong way for the intended short transition. General 3D rotation interpolation needs the appropriate rotation representation; the yaw example is intentionally limited to a single axis."},{"id":"vector-validation","title":"5. Validate positions before applying them","text":"Reject non-numeric, NaN, infinite or unreasonably large coordinates in network or imported data. Keep a deliberate bound for the feature’s world scale. A malformed pose should not teleport a proxy to a huge coordinate or enter a physics call. local function finite(n) return type(n) == \"number\" and n == n and math.abs(n) \u003c 10000000 end local function positionOK(p) return type(p) == \"table\" and finite(p.X) and finite(p.Y) and finite(p.Z) end"},{"id":"diagnosis","title":"Diagnose by symptom","text":"A model following the player but facing backwards suggests a basis/orientation issue. A model drifting when the gun turns suggests a space-conversion error. Correct position with wrong size suggests scale/origin assumptions. A successful location setter with nothing visible also requires checking occlusion and the current world. Keep those tests separate."}]},{"id":"player-world","title":"Worlds, players & controllers","text":"Worlds, players & controllers Resolve the right world, controller, pawn, game instance and network role. The context chain For a local feature, begin with the game viewport’s world and resolve the local controller from that world. The controller owns input context; its pawn is the possessed actor. Game instance, game mode and game state have different lifetimes and authority roles, so don’t treat them as synonyms for “the game”. Object Why a mod may need it Viewport / UWorld Identify the current runtime world. PlayerController Local input, pawn and camera context. Pawn Equipped weapon and player movement context. GameInstance Cross-map selections and observed progression data. GameMode Authoritative mode/travel decisions where available. GameState / PlayerState Shared roster and player information. 1. Resolve and check each link local H = require(\"UEHelpers\") ExecuteInGameThread(function() local viewport = H.GetGameViewportClient() if not viewport or not viewport:IsValid() then return end local world = viewport:GetWorld() if not world or not world:IsValid() then return end local gameplay = StaticFindObject(\"/Script/Engine.Default__GameplayStatics\") if not gameplay or not gameplay:IsValid() then return end local pc = gameplay:GetPlayerController(world, 0) if not pc or not pc:IsValid() then return end local pawn = pc:K2_GetPawn() print(\"[MyMod] World: \" .. world:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") print(\"[MyMod] Pawn ready: \" .. tostring(pawn and pawn:IsValid()) .. \"\\n\") end) 2. Treat not-ready as a state A controller or pawn can be absent during loading, menu transition or travel. Return a clear not-ready result and retry through your normal lifecycle. Don’t manufacture a replacement pawn merely to satisfy a diagnostic query. An object becoming valid does not prove every component is initialised. Resolve the specific weapon, camera or editor only when the feature needs it, then validate that link too. 3. Reset world-owned caches on travel Capture a world identity with caches and jobs. When it changes, stop old actions and discard references to the previous world’s actors/widgets. Even if a new actor has a similar name, it is a new live instance. Game-instance data may survive the map change, but its selected weapon template does not automatically mean a usable gun has spawned for the new pawn. The local missing-gun investigation needed separate selected-loadout and live-actor checks. 4. Separate local convenience from authority The local controller is appropriate for opening a local overlay. It is not sufficient authority to move every connected player or award a shared round score. Verify host/guest role and the required authoritative context before acting. On clients, do not assume the host’s game mode is locally available. Use shared state and a validated request path rather than invoking a host-only operation on a nil object. 5. Inspect roster and camera separately The local implementation reads GameState.PlayerArray and obtains names/pawns from player states. It resolves the player camera through GameplayStatics for view-dependent effects and labels. Neither list order nor camera identity should be used as a permanent player ID. See HUDs and nameplates for rendering and Steam sessions for connection identity. Test this chain again after repeated range travel, not only at the first bunker load.","sections":[{"id":"context-chain","title":"The context chain","text":"For a local feature, begin with the game viewport’s world and resolve the local controller from that world. The controller owns input context; its pawn is the possessed actor. Game instance, game mode and game state have different lifetimes and authority roles, so don’t treat them as synonyms for “the game”. Object Why a mod may need it Viewport / UWorld Identify the current runtime world. PlayerController Local input, pawn and camera context. Pawn Equipped weapon and player movement context. GameInstance Cross-map selections and observed progression data. GameMode Authoritative mode/travel decisions where available. GameState / PlayerState Shared roster and player information."},{"id":"resolver","title":"1. Resolve and check each link","text":"local H = require(\"UEHelpers\") ExecuteInGameThread(function() local viewport = H.GetGameViewportClient() if not viewport or not viewport:IsValid() then return end local world = viewport:GetWorld() if not world or not world:IsValid() then return end local gameplay = StaticFindObject(\"/Script/Engine.Default__GameplayStatics\") if not gameplay or not gameplay:IsValid() then return end local pc = gameplay:GetPlayerController(world, 0) if not pc or not pc:IsValid() then return end local pawn = pc:K2_GetPawn() print(\"[MyMod] World: \" .. world:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") print(\"[MyMod] Pawn ready: \" .. tostring(pawn and pawn:IsValid()) .. \"\\n\") end)"},{"id":"not-ready","title":"2. Treat not-ready as a state","text":"A controller or pawn can be absent during loading, menu transition or travel. Return a clear not-ready result and retry through your normal lifecycle. Don’t manufacture a replacement pawn merely to satisfy a diagnostic query. An object becoming valid does not prove every component is initialised. Resolve the specific weapon, camera or editor only when the feature needs it, then validate that link too."},{"id":"travel-lifetime","title":"3. Reset world-owned caches on travel","text":"Capture a world identity with caches and jobs. When it changes, stop old actions and discard references to the previous world’s actors/widgets. Even if a new actor has a similar name, it is a new live instance. Game-instance data may survive the map change, but its selected weapon template does not automatically mean a usable gun has spawned for the new pawn. The local missing-gun investigation needed separate selected-loadout and live-actor checks."},{"id":"authority","title":"4. Separate local convenience from authority","text":"The local controller is appropriate for opening a local overlay. It is not sufficient authority to move every connected player or award a shared round score. Verify host/guest role and the required authoritative context before acting. On clients, do not assume the host’s game mode is locally available. Use shared state and a validated request path rather than invoking a host-only operation on a nil object."},{"id":"roster-camera","title":"5. Inspect roster and camera separately","text":"The local implementation reads GameState.PlayerArray and obtains names/pawns from player states. It resolves the player camera through GameplayStatics for view-dependent effects and labels. Neither list order nor camera identity should be used as a permanent player ID. See HUDs and nameplates for rendering and Steam sessions for connection identity. Test this chain again after repeated range travel, not only at the first bunker load."}]},{"id":"object-dumps","title":"Reading object dumps","text":"Reading object dumps Turn a large object dump into a small, testable integration note. 1. Capture the right context A loaded-object dump describes the process at a moment, not every cooked asset in the installation. Open the menu or range that owns the feature before searching for its widgets and Blueprint functions. Keep the game/loader version and capture context with your dump. Use the dump/viewer mechanism supported by your installed UE4SS release. Do not assume a keybind or developer command from another loader build exists locally. The normal loader API documentation is a starting reference, not proof your configuration enabled every utility. 2. Search for a narrow term rg -n \"Attach_POI_C:SetupAttachment\" UE4SS_ObjectDump.txt rg -n \"C_CartridgeBase:ChargeMass\" UE4SS_ObjectDump.txt rg -n \"Base_BoxMagazine_C:OnSnappedToSlot\" UE4SS_ObjectDump.txt Search the exact class/member once known. A broad word like “modifier” can return engine camera or animation systems unrelated to weapon stats. Read the owning path before deciding a match belongs to the gameplay feature. 3. Read entry type and owner A class entry names a type; a property entry gives an owner and reflected value type; a function entry identifies a callable integration candidate. Nearby property entries can describe function inputs, outputs, return values and Blueprint-generated local variables. Do not treat every property listed beneath a function as a public argument. Names such as CallFunc or temporary array indices often belong to Blueprint implementation locals. Compare the actual parameter sequence with a small callback observation. 4. Ignore process addresses for published recipes Addresses and offsets can help a native debugging session, but copied numbers from an old dump are not portable modding instructions. Prefer exact reflected names and typed APIs. Never use a pointer address as a network entity ID. A useful published excerpt removes irrelevant process-specific addresses and explains the member’s role. This also keeps a reference page readable instead of turning it into a second enormous raw dump. 5. Promote a finding only after a check Owner class: Exact member path: Reflected kind and types: Loaded in which menu/world: How the callback was observed: Which values were copied: Any mutation performed: Game/loader build and result: Remaining untested cases: A name found in a dump can be documented as a discovery point immediately. A claim that it safely removes a player’s fitted rail requires a different level of evidence. Keep that distinction visible in the hook reference . Archive and dump disagreement If an archive browser finds a package but the dump does not, the package may not be loaded. If a menu row names a map that cannot be entered, the reference alone did not prove a working destination. Use Asset paths & loading to test presence, loadability and behaviour in separate stages.","sections":[{"id":"capture-context","title":"1. Capture the right context","text":"A loaded-object dump describes the process at a moment, not every cooked asset in the installation. Open the menu or range that owns the feature before searching for its widgets and Blueprint functions. Keep the game/loader version and capture context with your dump. Use the dump/viewer mechanism supported by your installed UE4SS release. Do not assume a keybind or developer command from another loader build exists locally. The normal loader API documentation is a starting reference, not proof your configuration enabled every utility."},{"id":"search-first","title":"2. Search for a narrow term","text":"rg -n \"Attach_POI_C:SetupAttachment\" UE4SS_ObjectDump.txt rg -n \"C_CartridgeBase:ChargeMass\" UE4SS_ObjectDump.txt rg -n \"Base_BoxMagazine_C:OnSnappedToSlot\" UE4SS_ObjectDump.txt Search the exact class/member once known. A broad word like “modifier” can return engine camera or animation systems unrelated to weapon stats. Read the owning path before deciding a match belongs to the gameplay feature."},{"id":"read-entry","title":"3. Read entry type and owner","text":"A class entry names a type; a property entry gives an owner and reflected value type; a function entry identifies a callable integration candidate. Nearby property entries can describe function inputs, outputs, return values and Blueprint-generated local variables. Do not treat every property listed beneath a function as a public argument. Names such as CallFunc or temporary array indices often belong to Blueprint implementation locals. Compare the actual parameter sequence with a small callback observation."},{"id":"addresses","title":"4. Ignore process addresses for published recipes","text":"Addresses and offsets can help a native debugging session, but copied numbers from an old dump are not portable modding instructions. Prefer exact reflected names and typed APIs. Never use a pointer address as a network entity ID. A useful published excerpt removes irrelevant process-specific addresses and explains the member’s role. This also keeps a reference page readable instead of turning it into a second enormous raw dump."},{"id":"test-note","title":"5. Promote a finding only after a check","text":"Owner class: Exact member path: Reflected kind and types: Loaded in which menu/world: How the callback was observed: Which values were copied: Any mutation performed: Game/loader build and result: Remaining untested cases: A name found in a dump can be documented as a discovery point immediately. A claim that it safely removes a player’s fitted rail requires a different level of evidence. Keep that distinction visible in the hook reference ."},{"id":"archive-vs-dump","title":"Archive and dump disagreement","text":"If an archive browser finds a package but the dump does not, the package may not be loaded. If a menu row names a map that cannot be entered, the reference alone did not prove a working destination. Use Asset paths & loading to test presence, loadability and behaviour in separate stages."}]},{"id":"workshop-inspector","title":"Build a weapon inspector","text":"Build a weapon inspector A complete, read-only example that connects settings, keybinds, game-thread work and weapon inspection. What you will build Press one key to print a snapshot to UE4SS.log: the current world, the equipped weapon or visible workbench-editor candidates, selected raw stats and a bounded list of child actors. The example contains no attachment setter, score award, save/apply operation or travel action. Download the WorkshopInspector example . You can also open the individual source files below. This is teaching code adapted from the recorded local demo patterns, not a guarantee for every game or UE4SS build. Before you start A working UE4SS installation and a known log location. The first mod startup check completed. An unused key; the example defaults to F9. A workbench attachment editor or a gun equipped in a range. Close the game while creating or editing files. Keep this folder separate from other mods; do not merge its scripts into another mod’s Scripts folder. 1. Create the layout ue4ss/Mods/WorkshopInspector/ ├── enabled.txt ├── README.md └── Scripts/ ├── main.lua ├── config.lua ├── log.lua └── inspect.lua The archive uses a mod-folder root. Extract it into ue4ss/Mods , not into Scripts and not into a second WorkshopInspector directory. Check that the final path ends in WorkshopInspector/Scripts/main.lua . 2. Add configuration return { Enabled = true, KeyName = \"F9\", MaxParts = 12, MaxEditors = 8 } Open config.lua . Enabled is a boolean. KeyName names an entry in the loader’s Key table. MaxParts limits printed child names; it does not remove parts or limit the actual assembly. MaxEditors bounds the number of loaded editor candidates inspected. These limits are deliberately small so a key press does not produce an unbounded log dump. Change them with the game closed and restart to load the new values. 3. Add a logger local Log = {} function Log.info(message) print(\"[WorkshopInspector] \" .. tostring(message) .. \"\\n\") end return Log Open log.lua . One function gives every message the same prefix. tostring makes diagnostics tolerant of nil and non-string values. The newline is the Lua escape \\n , not an actual line break inside the quoted source string. 4. Read engine state local H = require(\"UEHelpers\") local Inspect = {} local function valid(object) if object == nil then return false end local ok, result = pcall(function() return object:IsValid() end) return ok and result == true end local function object(value) if valid(value) then return value end local ok, result = pcall(function() return value:get() end) if ok and valid(result) then return result end end local function name(value) if not valid(value) then return \"\u003cunavailable>\" end return value:GetFullName() or \"\u003cunnamed>\" end function Inspect.capture(config) local lines = {} local function add(text) lines[#lines + 1] = text end local viewport = H.GetGameViewportClient() if not valid(viewport) then return {\"Viewport is not ready.\"} end local world = viewport:GetWorld() if not valid(world) then return {\"World is not ready.\"} end add(\"World: \" .. name(world)) local gameplay = StaticFindObject(\"/Script/Engine.Default__GameplayStatics\") if not valid(gameplay) then return {\"GameplayStatics is unavailable.\"} end local pc = gameplay:GetPlayerController(world, 0) if not valid(pc) then add(\"Local controller is not ready.\") return lines end local function describe(gun, label) if not valid(gun) then return end add(label .. \": \" .. name(gun)) for _, field in ipairs({\"TotalErgo\", \"TotalInstalledErgo\", \"TotalRecoilControlModifier\"}) do local ok, value = pcall(function() return gun[field] end) if ok and type(value) == \"number\" then add(string.format(\" %s = %.6f\", field, value)) else add(\" \" .. field .. \" = \u003cunavailable>\") end end local children = {} gun:GetAllChildActors(children, true) add(\" Child actors reported: \" .. tostring(#children)) for i = 1, math.min(#children, config.MaxParts) do local child = object(children[i]) if child then add(\" Part \" .. i .. \": \" .. name(child)) end end if #children > config.MaxParts then add(\" Remaining part names omitted by MaxParts.\") end end local described = 0 local pawn = pc:K2_GetPawn() if valid(pawn) then local ok, gun = pcall(function() return pawn.EquippedWeapon end) if ok and valid(gun) then describe(gun, \"Equipped weapon\"); described = described + 1 end end local editors = FindAllOf(\"WeaponEditMOde_C\") or {} for i = 1, math.min(#editors, config.MaxEditors) do local editor = object(editors[i]) if editor then local ok, visible = pcall(function() return editor:IsVisible() end) if ok and visible then add(\"Visible editor candidate: \" .. name(editor)) describe(editor.CurrentWeapon, \"Workbench weapon\") described = described + 1 end end end if described == 0 then add(\"No equipped gun or visible workshop editor found.\") end add(\"Snapshot finished. No parts, stats or saves were changed by this example.\") return lines end return Inspect Open inspect.lua . Read this module in four stages: Resolve the world. The viewport supplies the active world. A missing world produces a diagnostic result, not an attempt to use an invalid object. Resolve local context. GameplayStatics returns local controller zero. A pawn without EquippedWeapon is normal in some menus. Describe a gun. The module reads three numeric fields independently, then lists child actors up to MaxParts. An unavailable field is reported without writing a replacement value. Inspect editor candidates. Loaded visible WeaponEditMOde widgets can point at a workbench gun. A visible candidate is not proof of unique focus; several can be reported. The wrapper helper accepts an ordinary valid UObject or tries to unwrap a parameter wrapper. The validity helper catches Lua failures while checking a reference. Neither helper protects against every native memory error, so the code still reacquires context for each snapshot instead of caching it across travel. 5. Wire startup and the keybind local Config = require(\"config\") local Log = require(\"log\") local Inspect = require(\"inspect\") assert(type(Config.Enabled) == \"boolean\", \"Enabled must be true or false\") assert(type(Config.KeyName) == \"string\" and Key[Config.KeyName], \"KeyName is not a known key\") for _, field in ipairs({\"MaxParts\", \"MaxEditors\"}) do local value = Config[field] assert(type(value) == \"number\" and value % 1 == 0 and value >= 1 and value \u003c= 64, field .. \" must be an integer from 1 to 64\") end if not Config.Enabled then Log.info(\"Disabled in config.lua.\") return end local queued = false RegisterKeyBind(Key[Config.KeyName], function() if queued then return end queued = true ExecuteInGameThread(function() local ok, result = xpcall(function() return Inspect.capture(Config) end, debug.traceback) queued = false if not ok then Log.info(\"Snapshot failed: \" .. tostring(result)); return end for _, line in ipairs(result) do Log.info(line) end end) end) Log.info(\"Example loaded. Press \" .. Config.KeyName .. \" while the game is focused.\") Open main.lua . Startup validates settings before registering work. The key callback sets a pending flag and queues engine access on the game thread. The flag is released whether the Lua operation succeeds or fails. The logger prints the collected result only after the snapshot function returns. This prevents overlapping queued snapshots. It is not a general-purpose task cancellation system. There is no automatic retry loop because the next key press reacquires the current world and objects. 6. Run and interpret the output Start the game and find [WorkshopInspector] Example loaded. in the log. Open the workbench attachment editor or equip a gun in a range. Focus the game window and press the configured key once. Search for the next [WorkshopInspector] World: message. Compare object names and raw field values, not just the number of printed lines. [WorkshopInspector] World: \u003ccurrent world full name> [WorkshopInspector] Equipped weapon: \u003clive weapon full name> [WorkshopInspector] TotalErgo = \u003craw numeric value> [WorkshopInspector] TotalInstalledErgo = \u003craw numeric value> [WorkshopInspector] Child actors reported: \u003ccount> The workbench route reports a visible editor candidate and a Workbench weapon instead of requiring an Equipped weapon line. A negative raw stat is not automatically a bug; see Weapon stats . 7. Troubleshoot Result Next check No startup line Check enabled.txt, filename extensions, archive root and the fresh loader log. Key does nothing Focus the game; check the configured key and conflicts; confirm main.lua loaded. Viewport/controller not ready Wait until the game has entered a playable menu or range, then press again. No weapon/editor found Open the actual attachment editor or equip a gun; inspect build-specific class names. Stat unavailable Confirm that field exists on the actual weapon class in your build. Snapshot failed Read the traceback; isolate the operation before changing the rest of the module. 8. Extend one thing at a time A useful next step is to collect two snapshots and compare a stat before and after a normal attachment action. Keep only ordinary Lua values, not live actor pointers, in that comparison. Report a changed world or weapon as a different context rather than subtracting unrelated values. For a UI overlay, keep capture separate from rendering and bound its update rate. For a release, follow Packaging a Lua mod . Do not turn this read-only example into an automatic mutation script without adding a clear action, cancellation and recovery flow. Verification limits The game-facing names and integration patterns come from local demo source and earlier native checks. Any mock checks of these files only validate the Lua/control flow they exercise; they do not prove that an actual workbench widget or a future build returns the same data. Use Test a mod step by step to record a real run.","sections":[{"id":"what-you-will-build","title":"What you will build","text":"Press one key to print a snapshot to UE4SS.log: the current world, the equipped weapon or visible workbench-editor candidates, selected raw stats and a bounded list of child actors. The example contains no attachment setter, score award, save/apply operation or travel action. Download the WorkshopInspector example . You can also open the individual source files below. This is teaching code adapted from the recorded local demo patterns, not a guarantee for every game or UE4SS build."},{"id":"prerequisites","title":"Before you start","text":"A working UE4SS installation and a known log location. The first mod startup check completed. An unused key; the example defaults to F9. A workbench attachment editor or a gun equipped in a range. Close the game while creating or editing files. Keep this folder separate from other mods; do not merge its scripts into another mod’s Scripts folder."},{"id":"create-the-layout","title":"1. Create the layout","text":"ue4ss/Mods/WorkshopInspector/ ├── enabled.txt ├── README.md └── Scripts/ ├── main.lua ├── config.lua ├── log.lua └── inspect.lua The archive uses a mod-folder root. Extract it into ue4ss/Mods , not into Scripts and not into a second WorkshopInspector directory. Check that the final path ends in WorkshopInspector/Scripts/main.lua ."},{"id":"configure-the-example","title":"2. Add configuration","text":"return { Enabled = true, KeyName = \"F9\", MaxParts = 12, MaxEditors = 8 } Open config.lua . Enabled is a boolean. KeyName names an entry in the loader’s Key table. MaxParts limits printed child names; it does not remove parts or limit the actual assembly. MaxEditors bounds the number of loaded editor candidates inspected. These limits are deliberately small so a key press does not produce an unbounded log dump. Change them with the game closed and restart to load the new values."},{"id":"add-logging","title":"3. Add a logger","text":"local Log = {} function Log.info(message) print(\"[WorkshopInspector] \" .. tostring(message) .. \"\\n\") end return Log Open log.lua . One function gives every message the same prefix. tostring makes diagnostics tolerant of nil and non-string values. The newline is the Lua escape \\n , not an actual line break inside the quoted source string."},{"id":"read-engine-state","title":"4. Read engine state","text":"local H = require(\"UEHelpers\") local Inspect = {} local function valid(object) if object == nil then return false end local ok, result = pcall(function() return object:IsValid() end) return ok and result == true end local function object(value) if valid(value) then return value end local ok, result = pcall(function() return value:get() end) if ok and valid(result) then return result end end local function name(value) if not valid(value) then return \"\u003cunavailable>\" end return value:GetFullName() or \"\u003cunnamed>\" end function Inspect.capture(config) local lines = {} local function add(text) lines[#lines + 1] = text end local viewport = H.GetGameViewportClient() if not valid(viewport) then return {\"Viewport is not ready.\"} end local world = viewport:GetWorld() if not valid(world) then return {\"World is not ready.\"} end add(\"World: \" .. name(world)) local gameplay = StaticFindObject(\"/Script/Engine.Default__GameplayStatics\") if not valid(gameplay) then return {\"GameplayStatics is unavailable.\"} end local pc = gameplay:GetPlayerController(world, 0) if not valid(pc) then add(\"Local controller is not ready.\") return lines end local function describe(gun, label) if not valid(gun) then return end add(label .. \": \" .. name(gun)) for _, field in ipairs({\"TotalErgo\", \"TotalInstalledErgo\", \"TotalRecoilControlModifier\"}) do local ok, value = pcall(function() return gun[field] end) if ok and type(value) == \"number\" then add(string.format(\" %s = %.6f\", field, value)) else add(\" \" .. field .. \" = \u003cunavailable>\") end end local children = {} gun:GetAllChildActors(children, true) add(\" Child actors reported: \" .. tostring(#children)) for i = 1, math.min(#children, config.MaxParts) do local child = object(children[i]) if child then add(\" Part \" .. i .. \": \" .. name(child)) end end if #children > config.MaxParts then add(\" Remaining part names omitted by MaxParts.\") end end local described = 0 local pawn = pc:K2_GetPawn() if valid(pawn) then local ok, gun = pcall(function() return pawn.EquippedWeapon end) if ok and valid(gun) then describe(gun, \"Equipped weapon\"); described = described + 1 end end local editors = FindAllOf(\"WeaponEditMOde_C\") or {} for i = 1, math.min(#editors, config.MaxEditors) do local editor = object(editors[i]) if editor then local ok, visible = pcall(function() return editor:IsVisible() end) if ok and visible then add(\"Visible editor candidate: \" .. name(editor)) describe(editor.CurrentWeapon, \"Workbench weapon\") described = described + 1 end end end if described == 0 then add(\"No equipped gun or visible workshop editor found.\") end add(\"Snapshot finished. No parts, stats or saves were changed by this example.\") return lines end return Inspect Open inspect.lua . Read this module in four stages: Resolve the world. The viewport supplies the active world. A missing world produces a diagnostic result, not an attempt to use an invalid object. Resolve local context. GameplayStatics returns local controller zero. A pawn without EquippedWeapon is normal in some menus. Describe a gun. The module reads three numeric fields independently, then lists child actors up to MaxParts. An unavailable field is reported without writing a replacement value. Inspect editor candidates. Loaded visible WeaponEditMOde widgets can point at a workbench gun. A visible candidate is not proof of unique focus; several can be reported. The wrapper helper accepts an ordinary valid UObject or tries to unwrap a parameter wrapper. The validity helper catches Lua failures while checking a reference. Neither helper protects against every native memory error, so the code still reacquires context for each snapshot instead of caching it across travel."},{"id":"wire-startup","title":"5. Wire startup and the keybind","text":"local Config = require(\"config\") local Log = require(\"log\") local Inspect = require(\"inspect\") assert(type(Config.Enabled) == \"boolean\", \"Enabled must be true or false\") assert(type(Config.KeyName) == \"string\" and Key[Config.KeyName], \"KeyName is not a known key\") for _, field in ipairs({\"MaxParts\", \"MaxEditors\"}) do local value = Config[field] assert(type(value) == \"number\" and value % 1 == 0 and value >= 1 and value \u003c= 64, field .. \" must be an integer from 1 to 64\") end if not Config.Enabled then Log.info(\"Disabled in config.lua.\") return end local queued = false RegisterKeyBind(Key[Config.KeyName], function() if queued then return end queued = true ExecuteInGameThread(function() local ok, result = xpcall(function() return Inspect.capture(Config) end, debug.traceback) queued = false if not ok then Log.info(\"Snapshot failed: \" .. tostring(result)); return end for _, line in ipairs(result) do Log.info(line) end end) end) Log.info(\"Example loaded. Press \" .. Config.KeyName .. \" while the game is focused.\") Open main.lua . Startup validates settings before registering work. The key callback sets a pending flag and queues engine access on the game thread. The flag is released whether the Lua operation succeeds or fails. The logger prints the collected result only after the snapshot function returns. This prevents overlapping queued snapshots. It is not a general-purpose task cancellation system. There is no automatic retry loop because the next key press reacquires the current world and objects."},{"id":"run-and-interpret","title":"6. Run and interpret the output","text":"Start the game and find [WorkshopInspector] Example loaded. in the log. Open the workbench attachment editor or equip a gun in a range. Focus the game window and press the configured key once. Search for the next [WorkshopInspector] World: message. Compare object names and raw field values, not just the number of printed lines. [WorkshopInspector] World: \u003ccurrent world full name> [WorkshopInspector] Equipped weapon: \u003clive weapon full name> [WorkshopInspector] TotalErgo = \u003craw numeric value> [WorkshopInspector] TotalInstalledErgo = \u003craw numeric value> [WorkshopInspector] Child actors reported: \u003ccount> The workbench route reports a visible editor candidate and a Workbench weapon instead of requiring an Equipped weapon line. A negative raw stat is not automatically a bug; see Weapon stats ."},{"id":"troubleshoot-the-example","title":"7. Troubleshoot","text":"Result Next check No startup line Check enabled.txt, filename extensions, archive root and the fresh loader log. Key does nothing Focus the game; check the configured key and conflicts; confirm main.lua loaded. Viewport/controller not ready Wait until the game has entered a playable menu or range, then press again. No weapon/editor found Open the actual attachment editor or equip a gun; inspect build-specific class names. Stat unavailable Confirm that field exists on the actual weapon class in your build. Snapshot failed Read the traceback; isolate the operation before changing the rest of the module."},{"id":"extend-it-carefully","title":"8. Extend one thing at a time","text":"A useful next step is to collect two snapshots and compare a stat before and after a normal attachment action. Keep only ordinary Lua values, not live actor pointers, in that comparison. Report a changed world or weapon as a different context rather than subtracting unrelated values. For a UI overlay, keep capture separate from rendering and bound its update rate. For a release, follow Packaging a Lua mod . Do not turn this read-only example into an automatic mutation script without adding a clear action, cancellation and recovery flow."},{"id":"verification-limit","title":"Verification limits","text":"The game-facing names and integration patterns come from local demo source and earlier native checks. Any mock checks of these files only validate the Lua/control flow they exercise; they do not prove that an actual workbench widget or a future build returns the same data. Use Test a mod step by step to record a real run."}]},{"id":"testing","title":"Test a mod step by step","text":"Test a mod step by step Prove the smallest claim first, then test the actual player workflow. 1. Write a concrete claim “Works” is too broad to test. Use a trigger and an observable result: “Removing the foregrip restores the installed-ergo baseline,” or “Closing the attachment editor hides the added button.” Write the expected result before changing the code. For a stat issue, a negative final value is not enough; compare it with the same assembly’s starting value. 2. Record the environment Game / build: UE4SS version / commit: Mod version / archive checksum: Other enabled mods: Map and menu: Weapon / rail / attachment class: Reproduction steps: Expected result: Observed result: Relevant log excerpt: Use exact class or display names where possible. Save a fresh log after reproducing, not a file from last week. Remove personal paths and network IDs before posting it publicly. 3. Check pure Lua logic Configuration validation, pagination boundaries, score deduplication and packet parsing can often be tested without Unreal objects. Give them ordinary values and assert the returned result. local seen = {} local function accept(id) if seen[id] then return false end seen[id] = true return true end assert(accept(\"round1:shot1\") == true) assert(accept(\"round1:shot1\") == false) assert(accept(\"round1:shot2\") == true) A mock can establish that your callback releases a busy flag after a Lua error. It cannot establish that an engine object is safe to dereference. State the distinction in the result. 4. Use native fixtures for engine behaviour A native fixture uses actual engine objects with controlled ownership. For a child-actor removal test, create a disposable component and point the test widget at it rather than touching the player’s saved gun. Keep a list of every actor, widget, hook and temporary file the fixture owns. Clean those up on success and on Lua failure. Do not stop an unrelated game process or alter another mod’s configuration as an unrecorded side effect. 5. Test the actual workflow Open the normal menu the player uses. Trigger the action through its real input path. Repeat it after the UI rebuilds. Change the weapon or close the menu while work is pending. Travel and come back. For a persisted change, restart and load a disposable save. A widget created by code can pass construction checks while still being off-screen or blocked by another panel. The real input path is a separate check. 6. Add a two-PC matrix Scenario Check on both screens Both fire at once Two distinct shot IDs and correct owner attribution. Same target hit No duplicate reward; consistent final target state. Switch guns Correct model and handling state; no orphaned meshes. Restart / repeated travel Both arrive with usable loadouts and reset registries. Disconnect near round end Clear cancellation or recovery; no old result applied. Save snapshot Consent, backup and successful reload on a disposable guest profile. Use separate Steam accounts and real peers for this stage. Simulated packet delay is useful for repeatability, but it is not a replacement for an actual session. 7. Write the result narrowly Record what ran, what passed, what failed and what the test did not cover. “Ten synthetic removals returned to baseline” is useful. “All attachment bugs fixed” does not follow from it. Once the relevant checks pass, save the tested artifact and its checksum. Re-run checks when code or an unresolved concern justifies it; repeating the same successful simulation does not broaden its scope.","sections":[{"id":"write-the-claim","title":"1. Write a concrete claim","text":"“Works” is too broad to test. Use a trigger and an observable result: “Removing the foregrip restores the installed-ergo baseline,” or “Closing the attachment editor hides the added button.” Write the expected result before changing the code. For a stat issue, a negative final value is not enough; compare it with the same assembly’s starting value."},{"id":"record-the-environment","title":"2. Record the environment","text":"Game / build: UE4SS version / commit: Mod version / archive checksum: Other enabled mods: Map and menu: Weapon / rail / attachment class: Reproduction steps: Expected result: Observed result: Relevant log excerpt: Use exact class or display names where possible. Save a fresh log after reproducing, not a file from last week. Remove personal paths and network IDs before posting it publicly."},{"id":"pure-lua-checks","title":"3. Check pure Lua logic","text":"Configuration validation, pagination boundaries, score deduplication and packet parsing can often be tested without Unreal objects. Give them ordinary values and assert the returned result. local seen = {} local function accept(id) if seen[id] then return false end seen[id] = true return true end assert(accept(\"round1:shot1\") == true) assert(accept(\"round1:shot1\") == false) assert(accept(\"round1:shot2\") == true) A mock can establish that your callback releases a busy flag after a Lua error. It cannot establish that an engine object is safe to dereference. State the distinction in the result."},{"id":"native-fixtures","title":"4. Use native fixtures for engine behaviour","text":"A native fixture uses actual engine objects with controlled ownership. For a child-actor removal test, create a disposable component and point the test widget at it rather than touching the player’s saved gun. Keep a list of every actor, widget, hook and temporary file the fixture owns. Clean those up on success and on Lua failure. Do not stop an unrelated game process or alter another mod’s configuration as an unrecorded side effect."},{"id":"actual-workflow","title":"5. Test the actual workflow","text":"Open the normal menu the player uses. Trigger the action through its real input path. Repeat it after the UI rebuilds. Change the weapon or close the menu while work is pending. Travel and come back. For a persisted change, restart and load a disposable save. A widget created by code can pass construction checks while still being off-screen or blocked by another panel. The real input path is a separate check."},{"id":"multiplayer-matrix","title":"6. Add a two-PC matrix","text":"Scenario Check on both screens Both fire at once Two distinct shot IDs and correct owner attribution. Same target hit No duplicate reward; consistent final target state. Switch guns Correct model and handling state; no orphaned meshes. Restart / repeated travel Both arrive with usable loadouts and reset registries. Disconnect near round end Clear cancellation or recovery; no old result applied. Save snapshot Consent, backup and successful reload on a disposable guest profile. Use separate Steam accounts and real peers for this stage. Simulated packet delay is useful for repeatability, but it is not a replacement for an actual session."},{"id":"write-the-result","title":"7. Write the result narrowly","text":"Record what ran, what passed, what failed and what the test did not cover. “Ten synthetic removals returned to baseline” is useful. “All attachment bugs fixed” does not follow from it. Once the relevant checks pass, save the tested artifact and its checksum. Re-run checks when code or an unresolved concern justifies it; repeating the same successful simulation does not broaden its scope."}]},{"id":"target-inspector","title":"Build a target inspector","text":"Build a target inspector Build a read-only target snapshot and use it to distinguish actor state from visible motion. What you will build A small single-file mod that logs the current world, target count and a bounded sample of target actor names, hit flags, values and actor positions. It does not call Hit, reset targets, grant score or change saves. Download the Lua source . This is teaching source adapted from the local discovery pattern. The documentation build checks the source/display relationship; that is not a fresh native-game compatibility test. Use a known range and record the build when checking it yourself. 1. Create a separate mod folder ue4ss/Mods/TargetSnapshot/ ├── enabled.txt └── Scripts/ └── main.lua Copy the downloaded Lua file into Scripts as main.lua and create the empty enable marker beside Scripts. The default key is F10; change it if another mod uses that key. Close and restart the game after editing. 2. Read the complete source local H = require(\"UEHelpers\") local MaxTargets = 12 local queued = false local function valid(object) if object == nil then return false end local ok, result = pcall(function() return object:IsValid() end) return ok and result == true end local function unwrap(value) if valid(value) then return value end local ok, object = pcall(function() return value:get() end) if ok and valid(object) then return object end end local function snapshot() local viewport = H.GetGameViewportClient() if not valid(viewport) then return {\"Viewport is not ready.\"} end local world = viewport:GetWorld() if not valid(world) then return {\"World is not ready.\"} end local gameplay = StaticFindObject(\"/Script/Engine.Default__GameplayStatics\") local class = LoadAsset(\"/Game/BlueprintLogic/Targets/TargetBase.TargetBase_C\") if not valid(gameplay) or not valid(class) then return {\"Target discovery API/class unavailable.\"} end local targets = {} gameplay:GetAllActorsOfClass(world, class, targets) local lines = {\"World: \" .. world:GetFullName(), \"Targets reported: \" .. #targets} for i = 1, math.min(#targets, MaxTargets) do local actor = unwrap(targets[i]) if actor then local position = actor:K2_GetActorLocation() lines[#lines + 1] = string.format( \"%s | hit=%s | value=%s | actor=(%.1f, %.1f, %.1f)\", actor:GetFullName(), tostring(actor.HasBeenHit), tostring(actor.HitValue), position.X, position.Y, position.Z) end end if #targets > MaxTargets then lines[#lines + 1] = \"Remaining target lines omitted by MaxTargets.\" end if #targets == 0 then lines[#lines + 1] = \"No targets found in this world; try a known range.\" end return lines end RegisterKeyBind(Key.F10, function() if queued then return end queued = true ExecuteInGameThread(function() local ok, lines = xpcall(snapshot, debug.traceback) queued = false if not ok then print(\"[TargetSnapshot] Failed: \" .. tostring(lines) .. \"\\n\"); return end for _, line in ipairs(lines) do print(\"[TargetSnapshot] \" .. line .. \"\\n\") end end) end) print(\"[TargetSnapshot] Teaching example loaded; F10 captures read-only target state.\\n\") Read it in this order: validate native references, resolve the current viewport/world, load the target base class on the game thread, discover actors, copy a limited set of ordinary values, then print the completed result. The pending flag prevents overlapping queued snapshots and is released on Lua failure. MaxTargets bounds printed detail, not the actual game target count. A truncation message is part of the result so a user does not mistake twelve printed entries for twelve targets in the range. 3. Capture a baseline Launch and find the TargetSnapshot startup prefix in the fresh log. Enter a known working range through the normal game flow. Focus the game and press the chosen key once. Record the world name, total reported count and one target you can identify. Keep the raw fields before firing at it. The output format is a diagnostic record, not a canonical complete target schema. Different subclasses can have additional fields and component behaviour. 4. Compare after a normal hit Use the normal game action to hit the target, then capture again. Compare HasBeenHit, HitValue and the actor transform. Do not manually reset anything between samples if the question is how the normal action changes state. If the mesh visibly falls but the actor position barely changes, the next investigation is the body component’s transform. The source intentionally labels its coordinates actor= rather than pretending they describe every visible physics body. 5. Extend one field or component at a time Inspect the actual target’s components and identify its visible mesh before adding component-position logging. Copy the numeric vector immediately. Keep the selected component’s name/class in the result so another target type is not silently treated as identical. For a periodic overlay, add the cadence and lifecycle policy from Performance & profiling instead of turning a full actor rediscovery into an every-frame render loop. Expected failures and next actions Result Meaning / next check No targets Confirm the actual current range/world, not just a selected card. Unavailable class/API Check loader and asset presence; do not call into a nil object. Snapshot failed Read the traceback and isolate that member/operation. Many more targets than visible Inspect subclasses and scope; don’t assume every discovered actor is the one in view. Unchanged actor position after falling Inspect the visible physics component. Different identities after restart Expected live-instance replacement; rebuild your registry. Completion check The useful result is a repeatable baseline/hit comparison with known target identities and a clear scope. It is not proof that all target reset rules, rewards or remote replication are now implemented. Continue with Finding & tracking targets and Hits & scoring .","sections":[{"id":"goal","title":"What you will build","text":"A small single-file mod that logs the current world, target count and a bounded sample of target actor names, hit flags, values and actor positions. It does not call Hit, reset targets, grant score or change saves. Download the Lua source . This is teaching source adapted from the local discovery pattern. The documentation build checks the source/display relationship; that is not a fresh native-game compatibility test. Use a known range and record the build when checking it yourself."},{"id":"setup","title":"1. Create a separate mod folder","text":"ue4ss/Mods/TargetSnapshot/ ├── enabled.txt └── Scripts/ └── main.lua Copy the downloaded Lua file into Scripts as main.lua and create the empty enable marker beside Scripts. The default key is F10; change it if another mod uses that key. Close and restart the game after editing."},{"id":"source","title":"2. Read the complete source","text":"local H = require(\"UEHelpers\") local MaxTargets = 12 local queued = false local function valid(object) if object == nil then return false end local ok, result = pcall(function() return object:IsValid() end) return ok and result == true end local function unwrap(value) if valid(value) then return value end local ok, object = pcall(function() return value:get() end) if ok and valid(object) then return object end end local function snapshot() local viewport = H.GetGameViewportClient() if not valid(viewport) then return {\"Viewport is not ready.\"} end local world = viewport:GetWorld() if not valid(world) then return {\"World is not ready.\"} end local gameplay = StaticFindObject(\"/Script/Engine.Default__GameplayStatics\") local class = LoadAsset(\"/Game/BlueprintLogic/Targets/TargetBase.TargetBase_C\") if not valid(gameplay) or not valid(class) then return {\"Target discovery API/class unavailable.\"} end local targets = {} gameplay:GetAllActorsOfClass(world, class, targets) local lines = {\"World: \" .. world:GetFullName(), \"Targets reported: \" .. #targets} for i = 1, math.min(#targets, MaxTargets) do local actor = unwrap(targets[i]) if actor then local position = actor:K2_GetActorLocation() lines[#lines + 1] = string.format( \"%s | hit=%s | value=%s | actor=(%.1f, %.1f, %.1f)\", actor:GetFullName(), tostring(actor.HasBeenHit), tostring(actor.HitValue), position.X, position.Y, position.Z) end end if #targets > MaxTargets then lines[#lines + 1] = \"Remaining target lines omitted by MaxTargets.\" end if #targets == 0 then lines[#lines + 1] = \"No targets found in this world; try a known range.\" end return lines end RegisterKeyBind(Key.F10, function() if queued then return end queued = true ExecuteInGameThread(function() local ok, lines = xpcall(snapshot, debug.traceback) queued = false if not ok then print(\"[TargetSnapshot] Failed: \" .. tostring(lines) .. \"\\n\"); return end for _, line in ipairs(lines) do print(\"[TargetSnapshot] \" .. line .. \"\\n\") end end) end) print(\"[TargetSnapshot] Teaching example loaded; F10 captures read-only target state.\\n\") Read it in this order: validate native references, resolve the current viewport/world, load the target base class on the game thread, discover actors, copy a limited set of ordinary values, then print the completed result. The pending flag prevents overlapping queued snapshots and is released on Lua failure. MaxTargets bounds printed detail, not the actual game target count. A truncation message is part of the result so a user does not mistake twelve printed entries for twelve targets in the range."},{"id":"run","title":"3. Capture a baseline","text":"Launch and find the TargetSnapshot startup prefix in the fresh log. Enter a known working range through the normal game flow. Focus the game and press the chosen key once. Record the world name, total reported count and one target you can identify. Keep the raw fields before firing at it. The output format is a diagnostic record, not a canonical complete target schema. Different subclasses can have additional fields and component behaviour."},{"id":"compare","title":"4. Compare after a normal hit","text":"Use the normal game action to hit the target, then capture again. Compare HasBeenHit, HitValue and the actor transform. Do not manually reset anything between samples if the question is how the normal action changes state. If the mesh visibly falls but the actor position barely changes, the next investigation is the body component’s transform. The source intentionally labels its coordinates actor= rather than pretending they describe every visible physics body."},{"id":"extend","title":"5. Extend one field or component at a time","text":"Inspect the actual target’s components and identify its visible mesh before adding component-position logging. Copy the numeric vector immediately. Keep the selected component’s name/class in the result so another target type is not silently treated as identical. For a periodic overlay, add the cadence and lifecycle policy from Performance & profiling instead of turning a full actor rediscovery into an every-frame render loop."},{"id":"cases","title":"Expected failures and next actions","text":"Result Meaning / next check No targets Confirm the actual current range/world, not just a selected card. Unavailable class/API Check loader and asset presence; do not call into a nil object. Snapshot failed Read the traceback and isolate that member/operation. Many more targets than visible Inspect subclasses and scope; don’t assume every discovered actor is the one in view. Unchanged actor position after falling Inspect the visible physics component. Different identities after restart Expected live-instance replacement; rebuild your registry."},{"id":"finish","title":"Completion check","text":"The useful result is a repeatable baseline/hit comparison with known target identities and a clear scope. It is not proof that all target reset rules, rewards or remote replication are now implemented. Continue with Finding & tracking targets and Hits & scoring ."}]},{"id":"range-lab","title":"Plan a runtime range","text":"Plan a runtime range Plan and verify a runtime scene in stages before calling it a new map. 1. Choose the actual deliverable A new range card, a runtime scene inside an existing world and a new cooked map are different deliverables. Decide which one the prototype is meant to produce. If it must be Lua-only, document that it reuses a host world and cooked assets instead of promising an independent cooked level. The recorded apartment/river experiment made a custom card and runtime geometry, but did not establish a finished lighting/layout result. Use that as a lesson in staging, not a claim this chapter ships a completed apartment range. 2. Write a scene brief with measurable constraints Host map: Player firing position and direction: Near / medium / far target distances: Safe firing lane and sight lines: Scenery and bridge / river placement: Required cooked meshes and materials: Collision and out-of-bounds policy: Target classes and scoring policy: Return / emergency recovery action: Cleanup and travel behaviour: A reference image can guide composition, but turn it into positions, dimensions and a player view to test. A screenshot of buildings somewhere in a pool is not the same composition as firing across a river beside a bridge. 3. Calibrate one object and the player view Use a known mesh at a verified transform close enough to see. Log the player position, forward direction and requested/actual object position. Confirm scale, mobility and material before placing distant structures. Choose a local scene origin so the plan is expressed relative to the firing position. Convert those local placements consistently using the chosen basis; do not mix world Y with the player’s current right direction. 4. Build a plan before spawning local layout = { {id=\"near-target\", forward=2500, right=-200, up=100}, {id=\"far-target\", forward=7000, right=600, up=150}, {id=\"bridge-reference\", forward=3500, right=1800, up=0} } -- These are example local-plan units, not a finished map or measured asset sizes. Review the plan from the intended shooting view. Keep a clear lane and enough room for the player’s weapon/camera. Do not assume a spawned object beyond an indoor host map’s wall is visible just because its coordinates are correct. 5. Add one subsystem per stage Stage Acceptance result Geometry The reference object and layout appear in the intended view. Presentation Materials, sky/ambient response and exposure are readable. Collision The spawn and firing lane are not blocked accidentally. Targets Real target actors are identifiable and respond through the intended path. Scoring The custom rule counts valid attributed events once. Lifecycle Exit, restart, travel and cancellation retire only owned objects. Keep a recovery path available while experimenting with placement. Teleporting the player into a detached scene should occur only after construction succeeds, not before a long failing spawn sequence. 6. Diagnose darkness with a fixed reference Do not repeatedly change view distance when you already have visible dark geometry. Compare an unchanged object/material and the camera’s actual render context. Confirm which light/skylight/postprocess components were affected and record a visible before/after result. An emissive fallback can prove placement but should be labelled diagnostic if the intended scene requires normal lighting. A bright gun does not by itself prove the remote apartment faces receive the same ambient environment. 7. Finish the teardown before increasing scale Track all created actors and target registries. Test feature close, failed partial construction, repeated entry and travel. A large runtime scene that leaks hundreds of objects each visit is not a finished range even if the first entry looks good. For shared travel, wait for both peer worlds/loadouts/targets to be ready. For an actual new cooked map, follow a separately verified asset pipeline; this runtime plan does not reconstruct the developer’s Unreal project.","sections":[{"id":"scope","title":"1. Choose the actual deliverable","text":"A new range card, a runtime scene inside an existing world and a new cooked map are different deliverables. Decide which one the prototype is meant to produce. If it must be Lua-only, document that it reuses a host world and cooked assets instead of promising an independent cooked level. The recorded apartment/river experiment made a custom card and runtime geometry, but did not establish a finished lighting/layout result. Use that as a lesson in staging, not a claim this chapter ships a completed apartment range."},{"id":"requirements","title":"2. Write a scene brief with measurable constraints","text":"Host map: Player firing position and direction: Near / medium / far target distances: Safe firing lane and sight lines: Scenery and bridge / river placement: Required cooked meshes and materials: Collision and out-of-bounds policy: Target classes and scoring policy: Return / emergency recovery action: Cleanup and travel behaviour: A reference image can guide composition, but turn it into positions, dimensions and a player view to test. A screenshot of buildings somewhere in a pool is not the same composition as firing across a river beside a bridge."},{"id":"calibration","title":"3. Calibrate one object and the player view","text":"Use a known mesh at a verified transform close enough to see. Log the player position, forward direction and requested/actual object position. Confirm scale, mobility and material before placing distant structures. Choose a local scene origin so the plan is expressed relative to the firing position. Convert those local placements consistently using the chosen basis; do not mix world Y with the player’s current right direction."},{"id":"layout","title":"4. Build a plan before spawning","text":"local layout = { {id=\"near-target\", forward=2500, right=-200, up=100}, {id=\"far-target\", forward=7000, right=600, up=150}, {id=\"bridge-reference\", forward=3500, right=1800, up=0} } -- These are example local-plan units, not a finished map or measured asset sizes. Review the plan from the intended shooting view. Keep a clear lane and enough room for the player’s weapon/camera. Do not assume a spawned object beyond an indoor host map’s wall is visible just because its coordinates are correct."},{"id":"stages","title":"5. Add one subsystem per stage","text":"Stage Acceptance result Geometry The reference object and layout appear in the intended view. Presentation Materials, sky/ambient response and exposure are readable. Collision The spawn and firing lane are not blocked accidentally. Targets Real target actors are identifiable and respond through the intended path. Scoring The custom rule counts valid attributed events once. Lifecycle Exit, restart, travel and cancellation retire only owned objects. Keep a recovery path available while experimenting with placement. Teleporting the player into a detached scene should occur only after construction succeeds, not before a long failing spawn sequence."},{"id":"lighting","title":"6. Diagnose darkness with a fixed reference","text":"Do not repeatedly change view distance when you already have visible dark geometry. Compare an unchanged object/material and the camera’s actual render context. Confirm which light/skylight/postprocess components were affected and record a visible before/after result. An emissive fallback can prove placement but should be labelled diagnostic if the intended scene requires normal lighting. A bright gun does not by itself prove the remote apartment faces receive the same ambient environment."},{"id":"teardown","title":"7. Finish the teardown before increasing scale","text":"Track all created actors and target registries. Test feature close, failed partial construction, repeated entry and travel. A large runtime scene that leaks hundreds of objects each visit is not a finished range even if the first entry looks good. For shared travel, wait for both peer worlds/loadouts/targets to be ready. For an actual new cooked map, follow a separately verified asset pipeline; this runtime plan does not reconstruct the developer’s Unreal project."}]},{"id":"weapons","title":"Weapon assemblies","text":"Weapon assemblies A visible gun is an assembly of actors and components, not necessarily one mesh. Find the equipped weapon In a range, the local pawn exposes EquippedWeapon . The workshop uses its own displayed weapon reference, so don’t expect the range pawn to identify the gun on the workbench. local H = require(\"UEHelpers\") RegisterKeyBind(Key.F9, function() ExecuteInGameThread(function() local pc = H.GetPlayerController() if not pc or not pc:IsValid() then return end local pawn = pc:K2_GetPawn() if not pawn or not pawn:IsValid() then return end local gun = pawn.EquippedWeapon if not gun or not gun:IsValid() then return end print(\"[MyMod] Equipped: \" .. gun:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end) end Parts and components Observed weapon classes include C_WeaponBase , C_WeaponPart and the Blueprint A_WeaponBase_C . Parts expose a ParentWeapon reference. ChildActorComponents can own attached part actors, which can have their own descendants. Enumerate the assembly before changing it. A core barrel, a fitted rail and a nested foregrip can live at different depths. Reading just the root actor’s mesh misses much of the weapon. local children = {} gun:GetAllChildActors(children, true) for i = 1, math.min(#children, 128) do local child = children[i] if child and child:IsValid() then print(\"[MyMod] Part: \" .. child:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end end Templates are different from live actors CurrentWeaponTemplate records a selected assembly. A spawned weapon is the live actor built from it. Changing the visible actor alone does not establish that the saved template changed, and changing a template does not automatically refresh every existing actor. The observed template has separate weapon-class, main-part, attachment and grip data. The generated field names have suffixes; inspect the current struct rather than copying offsets. Visible doesn’t mean usable A mesh proxy can show another player’s gun without implementing its firing mechanism. A usable gun also needs the correct actor class, parts, ammunition, ownership and input path. Test local use separately from remote appearance. Likewise, hiding a source gun and drawing a replacement mesh does not make a new inventory weapon. Test an assembly Record the selected template and visible part tree. Change one part and confirm only the expected branch changes. Leave the workbench and test handling and firing. Travel to a range and back. Restart and check the saved assembly separately. Walkthrough: inspect one complete assembly 1. Prepare a known gun Use a gun and normal part combination you can identify in the game. Record the selected base weapon, core parts, fitted attachments and currently gripped part. Keep a screenshot of the workbench view so a later actor list has a visual reference. Do not begin with an already complicated cross-weapon assembly. A shorter part tree makes it easier to identify which object was added by one operation. 2. Find the live gun for this context In a range, read the local pawn’s EquippedWeapon. In the attachment editor, inspect the editor’s CurrentWeapon. Log the world and full object name beside each snapshot. A class default object has defaults; it is not the gun the player is holding. If the object is missing, report “not ready” and stop the sample. Creating a replacement weapon just to make inspection return something changes the question you were trying to answer. 3. Capture the tree, not just a count List the root and descendant actors with a bounded output limit. For a deeper investigation, inspect each child’s owner/component relationship and any ParentWeapon reference. A flat list is enough to prove an actor exists, but it does not explain which rail owns a nested grip. Keep copied names and selected numeric values as the baseline. Reacquire live actors for the next sample. Holding old references across a rebuild can turn a useful comparison tool into the source of the failure. 4. Change one ordinary part Use the game’s normal UI to replace or remove one attachment. Capture a second snapshot after the action and compare the changed branch. Record which actors disappeared, which were recreated and whether the main gun identity stayed the same. A recreated actor can have the same class but a different instance suffix. Compare class identity when asking “is this the same type of part?” and instance identity when asking “is this the same live object?”. 5. Separate visible, usable and saved state State How to check it Visible assembly The displayed geometry and captured child tree match. Usable assembly Handling, aiming, magazine interaction and firing work in the range. Selected template The game’s selected loadout reconstructs the intended parts. Persisted assembly A restart and disposable-save reload reconstruct the intended parts. Passing the first row does not establish the other three. This distinction is especially important for model proxies and Lua-only replacement previews. Understanding relative transforms A remote weapon display needs each visible mesh in a common local coordinate system. Copying world positions directly produces offsets that depend on where the source player stood. The local implementation captured mesh positions relative to a weapon-root transform, then rebuilt that relationship for the visual proxy. A complete mesh capture also considers rotation, scale, deduplication and meshes on child actors. Verify the reconstructed assembly while moving, aiming and changing weapons; a correct static screenshot is only the first check. Troubleshooting the result If the root gun appears but attachments do not, inspect child-actor traversal and component selection. If the model appears late, inspect asset-loading and capture cadence. If it is offset or facing backwards, compare transform spaces before adding arbitrary constants. If the local usable gun disappears after travel, inspect loadout reconstruction separately from the friend’s cosmetic proxy. What was checked EquippedWeapon lookup and assembled mesh capture were used in local range fixtures. Arbitrary replacement weapons, imported models and every possible assembly are not verified. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build.","sections":[{"id":"find-the-equipped-weapon","title":"Find the equipped weapon","text":"In a range, the local pawn exposes EquippedWeapon . The workshop uses its own displayed weapon reference, so don’t expect the range pawn to identify the gun on the workbench. local H = require(\"UEHelpers\") RegisterKeyBind(Key.F9, function() ExecuteInGameThread(function() local pc = H.GetPlayerController() if not pc or not pc:IsValid() then return end local pawn = pc:K2_GetPawn() if not pawn or not pawn:IsValid() then return end local gun = pawn.EquippedWeapon if not gun or not gun:IsValid() then return end print(\"[MyMod] Equipped: \" .. gun:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end) end"},{"id":"parts-and-components","title":"Parts and components","text":"Observed weapon classes include C_WeaponBase , C_WeaponPart and the Blueprint A_WeaponBase_C . Parts expose a ParentWeapon reference. ChildActorComponents can own attached part actors, which can have their own descendants. Enumerate the assembly before changing it. A core barrel, a fitted rail and a nested foregrip can live at different depths. Reading just the root actor’s mesh misses much of the weapon. local children = {} gun:GetAllChildActors(children, true) for i = 1, math.min(#children, 128) do local child = children[i] if child and child:IsValid() then print(\"[MyMod] Part: \" .. child:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end end"},{"id":"templates-and-live-actors","title":"Templates are different from live actors","text":"CurrentWeaponTemplate records a selected assembly. A spawned weapon is the live actor built from it. Changing the visible actor alone does not establish that the saved template changed, and changing a template does not automatically refresh every existing actor. The observed template has separate weapon-class, main-part, attachment and grip data. The generated field names have suffixes; inspect the current struct rather than copying offsets."},{"id":"visible-does-not-mean-usable","title":"Visible doesn’t mean usable","text":"A mesh proxy can show another player’s gun without implementing its firing mechanism. A usable gun also needs the correct actor class, parts, ammunition, ownership and input path. Test local use separately from remote appearance. Likewise, hiding a source gun and drawing a replacement mesh does not make a new inventory weapon."},{"id":"test-an-assembly","title":"Test an assembly","text":"Record the selected template and visible part tree. Change one part and confirm only the expected branch changes. Leave the workbench and test handling and firing. Travel to a range and back. Restart and check the saved assembly separately."},{"id":"assembly-inspection-walkthrough","title":"Walkthrough: inspect one complete assembly","text":""},{"id":"assembly-step-prepare","title":"1. Prepare a known gun","text":"Use a gun and normal part combination you can identify in the game. Record the selected base weapon, core parts, fitted attachments and currently gripped part. Keep a screenshot of the workbench view so a later actor list has a visual reference. Do not begin with an already complicated cross-weapon assembly. A shorter part tree makes it easier to identify which object was added by one operation."},{"id":"assembly-step-find-context","title":"2. Find the live gun for this context","text":"In a range, read the local pawn’s EquippedWeapon. In the attachment editor, inspect the editor’s CurrentWeapon. Log the world and full object name beside each snapshot. A class default object has defaults; it is not the gun the player is holding. If the object is missing, report “not ready” and stop the sample. Creating a replacement weapon just to make inspection return something changes the question you were trying to answer."},{"id":"assembly-step-tree","title":"3. Capture the tree, not just a count","text":"List the root and descendant actors with a bounded output limit. For a deeper investigation, inspect each child’s owner/component relationship and any ParentWeapon reference. A flat list is enough to prove an actor exists, but it does not explain which rail owns a nested grip. Keep copied names and selected numeric values as the baseline. Reacquire live actors for the next sample. Holding old references across a rebuild can turn a useful comparison tool into the source of the failure."},{"id":"assembly-step-change-one","title":"4. Change one ordinary part","text":"Use the game’s normal UI to replace or remove one attachment. Capture a second snapshot after the action and compare the changed branch. Record which actors disappeared, which were recreated and whether the main gun identity stayed the same. A recreated actor can have the same class but a different instance suffix. Compare class identity when asking “is this the same type of part?” and instance identity when asking “is this the same live object?”."},{"id":"assembly-step-state","title":"5. Separate visible, usable and saved state","text":"State How to check it Visible assembly The displayed geometry and captured child tree match. Usable assembly Handling, aiming, magazine interaction and firing work in the range. Selected template The game’s selected loadout reconstructs the intended parts. Persisted assembly A restart and disposable-save reload reconstruct the intended parts. Passing the first row does not establish the other three. This distinction is especially important for model proxies and Lua-only replacement previews."},{"id":"assembly-read-transforms","title":"Understanding relative transforms","text":"A remote weapon display needs each visible mesh in a common local coordinate system. Copying world positions directly produces offsets that depend on where the source player stood. The local implementation captured mesh positions relative to a weapon-root transform, then rebuilt that relationship for the visual proxy. A complete mesh capture also considers rotation, scale, deduplication and meshes on child actors. Verify the reconstructed assembly while moving, aiming and changing weapons; a correct static screenshot is only the first check."},{"id":"assembly-failure-checks","title":"Troubleshooting the result","text":"If the root gun appears but attachments do not, inspect child-actor traversal and component selection. If the model appears late, inspect asset-loading and capture cadence. If it is offset or facing backwards, compare transform spaces before adding arbitrary constants. If the local usable gun disappears after travel, inspect loadout reconstruction separately from the friend’s cosmetic proxy."},{"id":"tested-scope","title":"What was checked","text":"EquippedWeapon lookup and assembled mesh capture were used in local range fixtures. Arbitrary replacement weapons, imported models and every possible assembly are not verified. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."}]},{"id":"attachments","title":"Attachments & rails","text":"Attachments & rails Separate the attachment list, the mounting point and the part fitted to it. The attachment flow The workbench creates Attach_POI_C widgets for attachment points. A point references its AttachPoint component, the current weapon and a popup list. Compatibility lookup decides which attachment classes that list can offer. Entry point Observed role GetCompatibleMountsRecursive Resolve compatible mount definitions. OnGetUserMenuContent Build the attachment popup. SetupAttachment Apply a class or remove a fitted part. AttachPoint.ChildActor The actor currently fitted at that point. Nested rails A rail can add mounting points of its own. A grip fitted to the rail is a descendant of that rail, not necessarily a direct child of the base weapon. Removing a parent can invalidate the child actor and its UI at the same time. When iterating points during a clear operation, reacquire the current point list after each change. Don’t keep using an old list after the workbench rebuilds it. Fit and remove through the game For a valid live attachment-point widget, the native action takes a class reference to fit a part. Passing nil removed it in local tests: -- Context: poi is a valid live Attach_POI widget. -- This changes the assembly; it is not an inspection call. poi:SetupAttachment(nil) Let the game handle default-part behaviour and its refresh chain. Directly destroying a child actor bypasses that UI action and may leave the template or derived stats out of date. Compatibility isn’t ownership A part can be mount-compatible but not bought. A shop entry can be visible but locked. A saved assembly may contain a part that a purchased-set-only check misses. Keep these questions separate; see Ownership & unlocks . Test repeated edits Record stats before fitting, after fitting and after removal. Repeat with the same rail, then try removing the rail while a child part is still mounted. Check that a cancelled operation doesn’t remove a part from a newly selected gun. For a stat report, keep the exact weapon, rail and child class names. A vague “negative stat” report is not enough to decide whether an extra subtraction occurred. Walkthrough: trace a fit/remove action 1. Identify the point Open the attachment editor and pick a point whose current part you can name. Record the POI widget, AttachPoint component and ChildActor before doing anything. If it is a rail’s own point, also record the rail actor that owns that branch. Several points can offer the same part classes. The class of an attachment does not uniquely identify the point the player selected. 2. Record what the list means Observe the menu before filtering or pagination. Count real attachment entries separately from the empty-slot icon and any tab row your mod adds. Decide whether your feature changes compatibility, ownership visibility or just presentation. Those changes should not be mixed into one undocumented “filter”. For tabbed lists, partition the original item list, then add your navigation controls outside that item count. Otherwise reopening or rebuilding the list can accidentally count your own tab bar as another attachment. 3. Fit one known-compatible part Use the normal click action first and confirm the visible child, the refreshed point list and a stat snapshot. If that action fails without your mod, investigate the base setup before expanding compatibility. A mutating Lua action should use the intended game path and verified class type. Passing an object instance where a class reference is expected is not the same operation. 4. Remove and reacquire After removal, do not use a cached child actor to ask what is now mounted. Reacquire ChildActor from the current AttachPoint. If the editor rebuilt its POIs, reacquire the point list too. For a clear-all action, finish one removal, let the normal refresh chain run and inspect the new list before the next step. Protect the job against duplicate clicks, changed weapon, closed editor and world travel. 5. Test parent removal separately Removing an empty rail and removing a rail with several children are different cases. Observe what happens to each descendant, its stats and its menu points. If the UI rebuild invalidates a child point, the job must skip that old reference rather than force another removal on it. Pagination boundaries Use ordinary Lua item arrays for your page calculation. For a positive page size, an item’s page is floor((index − 1) / pageSize) + 1 . The number of non-empty pages is ceil(itemCount / pageSize) . Decide whether an empty list shows no tabs or a single empty page, then test that policy explicitly. Items at page size 20 Non-empty pages 0 0; an empty-state design is still needed. 1–20 1 21–40 2 41–60 3 Do not add an extra empty tab just because the last page is exactly full unless that is an intentional UI requirement. Keep tab visibility independent from whether the game considers a part owned. Completion check Verify fitting, removing, replacing, parent-rail removal, empty clearing and cancelled clearing. For expanded combinations, test handling and persistence independently. Report a stable list and working tabs as those specific results; they do not prove every offered combination is physically or mechanically valid. What was checked Single native removals, synthetic three-point clear batches and rebuilding/cancellation guards passed local fixtures. These do not prove every real fitted assembly saves and reloads correctly. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build.","sections":[{"id":"the-attachment-flow","title":"The attachment flow","text":"The workbench creates Attach_POI_C widgets for attachment points. A point references its AttachPoint component, the current weapon and a popup list. Compatibility lookup decides which attachment classes that list can offer. Entry point Observed role GetCompatibleMountsRecursive Resolve compatible mount definitions. OnGetUserMenuContent Build the attachment popup. SetupAttachment Apply a class or remove a fitted part. AttachPoint.ChildActor The actor currently fitted at that point."},{"id":"nested-rails","title":"Nested rails","text":"A rail can add mounting points of its own. A grip fitted to the rail is a descendant of that rail, not necessarily a direct child of the base weapon. Removing a parent can invalidate the child actor and its UI at the same time. When iterating points during a clear operation, reacquire the current point list after each change. Don’t keep using an old list after the workbench rebuilds it."},{"id":"fit-and-remove","title":"Fit and remove through the game","text":"For a valid live attachment-point widget, the native action takes a class reference to fit a part. Passing nil removed it in local tests: -- Context: poi is a valid live Attach_POI widget. -- This changes the assembly; it is not an inspection call. poi:SetupAttachment(nil) Let the game handle default-part behaviour and its refresh chain. Directly destroying a child actor bypasses that UI action and may leave the template or derived stats out of date."},{"id":"compatibility-is-not-ownership","title":"Compatibility isn’t ownership","text":"A part can be mount-compatible but not bought. A shop entry can be visible but locked. A saved assembly may contain a part that a purchased-set-only check misses. Keep these questions separate; see Ownership & unlocks ."},{"id":"test-repeated-edits","title":"Test repeated edits","text":"Record stats before fitting, after fitting and after removal. Repeat with the same rail, then try removing the rail while a child part is still mounted. Check that a cancelled operation doesn’t remove a part from a newly selected gun. For a stat report, keep the exact weapon, rail and child class names. A vague “negative stat” report is not enough to decide whether an extra subtraction occurred."},{"id":"attachment-action-walkthrough","title":"Walkthrough: trace a fit/remove action","text":""},{"id":"attach-step-identify-point","title":"1. Identify the point","text":"Open the attachment editor and pick a point whose current part you can name. Record the POI widget, AttachPoint component and ChildActor before doing anything. If it is a rail’s own point, also record the rail actor that owns that branch. Several points can offer the same part classes. The class of an attachment does not uniquely identify the point the player selected."},{"id":"attach-step-record-menu","title":"2. Record what the list means","text":"Observe the menu before filtering or pagination. Count real attachment entries separately from the empty-slot icon and any tab row your mod adds. Decide whether your feature changes compatibility, ownership visibility or just presentation. Those changes should not be mixed into one undocumented “filter”. For tabbed lists, partition the original item list, then add your navigation controls outside that item count. Otherwise reopening or rebuilding the list can accidentally count your own tab bar as another attachment."},{"id":"attach-step-fit-normal","title":"3. Fit one known-compatible part","text":"Use the normal click action first and confirm the visible child, the refreshed point list and a stat snapshot. If that action fails without your mod, investigate the base setup before expanding compatibility. A mutating Lua action should use the intended game path and verified class type. Passing an object instance where a class reference is expected is not the same operation."},{"id":"attach-step-remove-normal","title":"4. Remove and reacquire","text":"After removal, do not use a cached child actor to ask what is now mounted. Reacquire ChildActor from the current AttachPoint. If the editor rebuilt its POIs, reacquire the point list too. For a clear-all action, finish one removal, let the normal refresh chain run and inspect the new list before the next step. Protect the job against duplicate clicks, changed weapon, closed editor and world travel."},{"id":"attach-step-rail-parent","title":"5. Test parent removal separately","text":"Removing an empty rail and removing a rail with several children are different cases. Observe what happens to each descendant, its stats and its menu points. If the UI rebuild invalidates a child point, the job must skip that old reference rather than force another removal on it."},{"id":"attachment-pagination-details","title":"Pagination boundaries","text":"Use ordinary Lua item arrays for your page calculation. For a positive page size, an item’s page is floor((index − 1) / pageSize) + 1 . The number of non-empty pages is ceil(itemCount / pageSize) . Decide whether an empty list shows no tabs or a single empty page, then test that policy explicitly. Items at page size 20 Non-empty pages 0 0; an empty-state design is still needed. 1–20 1 21–40 2 41–60 3 Do not add an extra empty tab just because the last page is exactly full unless that is an intentional UI requirement. Keep tab visibility independent from whether the game considers a part owned."},{"id":"attachment-completion-check","title":"Completion check","text":"Verify fitting, removing, replacing, parent-rail removal, empty clearing and cancelled clearing. For expanded combinations, test handling and persistence independently. Report a stable list and working tabs as those specific results; they do not prove every offered combination is physically or mechanically valid."},{"id":"tested-scope","title":"What was checked","text":"Single native removals, synthetic three-point clear batches and rebuilding/cancellation guards passed local fixtures. These do not prove every real fitted assembly saves and reloads correctly. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."}]},{"id":"weapon-stats","title":"Weapon stats","text":"Weapon stats Measure a baseline before trying to fix a stat that looks wrong. Part values and weapon totals Field Where it was observed ErgoModifier Per-part ergonomics modifier on C_WeaponPart. TotalErgo Weapon’s current ergonomics total. TotalInstalledErgo Separate installed-part ergonomics total. TotalRecoilControlModifier Weapon recoil-control aggregate. ParentWeapon Part’s associated weapon reference. Current and installed ergonomics are different fields. Don’t substitute one for the other in an overlay. A part may also affect recoil, weight, cooling or grip behaviour rather than just ergonomics. Read a baseline -- Context: gun is a valid live weapon, on the game thread. print(string.format( \"[MyMod] current=%.6f installed=%.6f recoil=%.6f\\n\", gun.TotalErgo, gun.TotalInstalledErgo, gun.TotalRecoilControlModifier )) Recalculation The Blueprint weapon base has UpdateCharacteristicsFromParts . Its nine boolean inputs in the inspected build are: Weight, Ergo, CenterMassLoc, Recoil, GripCenter, BarrelLength, EquippedScopeAndLaser, Cooling, TotalLength This is a mutating recalculation function. Confirm the signature in your own dump before calling it. Prefer observing the normal attachment action first, rather than forcing a recalculation on every frame and masking a broken refresh chain. A removal test A local Sten fixture used a barrel strap and nested ergonomic broom grip. The grip’s modifier was approximately +0.15 . Ten fit/remove cycles with the expanded compatibility hook active gave: Step Installed ergo Rail-only baseline −0.600000 Grip fitted −0.450000 Grip removed −0.600000 That fixture started negative and returned to the same negative baseline. It did not show a cumulative penalty. It was a synthetic assembly, so those numbers are not the correct expected totals for every player’s Sten. Report a stat bug Include weapon/rail/attachment names, all three snapshots, game and loader versions, and whether the result survives leaving the workbench or restarting. Compare ordinary and expanded compatibility where possible. Don’t clamp every negative number to zero. That can hide legitimate penalties and conceal the actual bug. Walkthrough: diagnose a reported stat penalty 1. Fix the context Record the game build, loader version, gun class, rail class, child attachment class and grip/stance state. Current ergonomics can differ from an installed total; comparing different fields or different grips can create a false apparent regression. Use a disposable known assembly. Keep the normal baseline before expanding compatibility so the comparison is meaningful. 2. Take three snapshots Capture the same fields in the same context at three points: rail present with no tested child; tested child fitted; tested child removed. Record the actual numbers to enough precision to see small changes. local function compare(before, fitted, removed, epsilon) local bonus = fitted - before local residue = removed - before return { fittedDelta = bonus, removalResidue = residue, returnedToBaseline = math.abs(residue) \u003c= epsilon } end local result = compare(-0.6, -0.45, -0.6, 0.00001) assert(result.returnedToBaseline) The fitted delta describes the child’s observed contribution in that setup. The removal residue is the quantity to investigate for an accumulated penalty. Its sign alone is not sufficient; compare its size with the baseline and an appropriate tolerance. 3. Repeat without masking the refresh path Run several fit/remove cycles through the normal action. Do not manually recalculate after every removal if the question is whether the normal refresh is broken. That could make the symptom disappear without fixing the action chain. Capture a separate “manual recalculation” experiment only if needed, and label it clearly. If manual recalculation restores the baseline but ordinary removal does not, the refresh path becomes a focused investigation target. 4. Compare three lifetimes Check the value immediately after the action, after leaving/re-entering the editor, and after a restart using a disposable saved assembly. A stale displayed label, a stale live aggregate and a persisted template error can produce similar-looking screenshots while requiring different fixes. The recalculation flags in detail Flag Area selected for investigation Weight / CenterMassLoc Mass-related aggregates and location. Ergo / GripCenter Ergonomics and grip-related location. Recoil Recoil modifier aggregates. BarrelLength / TotalLength Weapon dimensions. EquippedScopeAndLaser Equipped sight/laser references. Cooling Cooling-related aggregate. This table groups the observed input names; it is not a recovered implementation of every formula. Check the signature and the code path that normally calls the function before choosing flags. A function that recomputes several systems is not a harmless “refresh text” operation. What a conclusion can say If all repeated removals match the starting value within tolerance, say that the tested setup returned to baseline. If another player still has a different assembly, keep that report open until its exact setup is reproduced. If a residue appears, identify which field and lifecycle first diverged before writing a clamp or an extra subtraction. What was checked The ten-cycle fixture ran on 6 November 2026 and returned to baseline without manual refresh between cycles. The reported real-player rail-removal penalty remains unconfirmed. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build.","sections":[{"id":"part-and-weapon-values","title":"Part values and weapon totals","text":"Field Where it was observed ErgoModifier Per-part ergonomics modifier on C_WeaponPart. TotalErgo Weapon’s current ergonomics total. TotalInstalledErgo Separate installed-part ergonomics total. TotalRecoilControlModifier Weapon recoil-control aggregate. ParentWeapon Part’s associated weapon reference. Current and installed ergonomics are different fields. Don’t substitute one for the other in an overlay. A part may also affect recoil, weight, cooling or grip behaviour rather than just ergonomics."},{"id":"read-a-baseline","title":"Read a baseline","text":"-- Context: gun is a valid live weapon, on the game thread. print(string.format( \"[MyMod] current=%.6f installed=%.6f recoil=%.6f\\n\", gun.TotalErgo, gun.TotalInstalledErgo, gun.TotalRecoilControlModifier ))"},{"id":"recalculation","title":"Recalculation","text":"The Blueprint weapon base has UpdateCharacteristicsFromParts . Its nine boolean inputs in the inspected build are: Weight, Ergo, CenterMassLoc, Recoil, GripCenter, BarrelLength, EquippedScopeAndLaser, Cooling, TotalLength This is a mutating recalculation function. Confirm the signature in your own dump before calling it. Prefer observing the normal attachment action first, rather than forcing a recalculation on every frame and masking a broken refresh chain."},{"id":"a-removal-test","title":"A removal test","text":"A local Sten fixture used a barrel strap and nested ergonomic broom grip. The grip’s modifier was approximately +0.15 . Ten fit/remove cycles with the expanded compatibility hook active gave: Step Installed ergo Rail-only baseline −0.600000 Grip fitted −0.450000 Grip removed −0.600000 That fixture started negative and returned to the same negative baseline. It did not show a cumulative penalty. It was a synthetic assembly, so those numbers are not the correct expected totals for every player’s Sten."},{"id":"report-a-stat-bug","title":"Report a stat bug","text":"Include weapon/rail/attachment names, all three snapshots, game and loader versions, and whether the result survives leaving the workbench or restarting. Compare ordinary and expanded compatibility where possible. Don’t clamp every negative number to zero. That can hide legitimate penalties and conceal the actual bug."},{"id":"stat-comparison-walkthrough","title":"Walkthrough: diagnose a reported stat penalty","text":""},{"id":"stat-step-fixed-context","title":"1. Fix the context","text":"Record the game build, loader version, gun class, rail class, child attachment class and grip/stance state. Current ergonomics can differ from an installed total; comparing different fields or different grips can create a false apparent regression. Use a disposable known assembly. Keep the normal baseline before expanding compatibility so the comparison is meaningful."},{"id":"stat-step-three-samples","title":"2. Take three snapshots","text":"Capture the same fields in the same context at three points: rail present with no tested child; tested child fitted; tested child removed. Record the actual numbers to enough precision to see small changes. local function compare(before, fitted, removed, epsilon) local bonus = fitted - before local residue = removed - before return { fittedDelta = bonus, removalResidue = residue, returnedToBaseline = math.abs(residue) \u003c= epsilon } end local result = compare(-0.6, -0.45, -0.6, 0.00001) assert(result.returnedToBaseline) The fitted delta describes the child’s observed contribution in that setup. The removal residue is the quantity to investigate for an accumulated penalty. Its sign alone is not sufficient; compare its size with the baseline and an appropriate tolerance."},{"id":"stat-step-repetition","title":"3. Repeat without masking the refresh path","text":"Run several fit/remove cycles through the normal action. Do not manually recalculate after every removal if the question is whether the normal refresh is broken. That could make the symptom disappear without fixing the action chain. Capture a separate “manual recalculation” experiment only if needed, and label it clearly. If manual recalculation restores the baseline but ordinary removal does not, the refresh path becomes a focused investigation target."},{"id":"stat-step-three-lifetimes","title":"4. Compare three lifetimes","text":"Check the value immediately after the action, after leaving/re-entering the editor, and after a restart using a disposable saved assembly. A stale displayed label, a stale live aggregate and a persisted template error can produce similar-looking screenshots while requiring different fixes."},{"id":"stat-input-flags","title":"The recalculation flags in detail","text":"Flag Area selected for investigation Weight / CenterMassLoc Mass-related aggregates and location. Ergo / GripCenter Ergonomics and grip-related location. Recoil Recoil modifier aggregates. BarrelLength / TotalLength Weapon dimensions. EquippedScopeAndLaser Equipped sight/laser references. Cooling Cooling-related aggregate. This table groups the observed input names; it is not a recovered implementation of every formula. Check the signature and the code path that normally calls the function before choosing flags. A function that recomputes several systems is not a harmless “refresh text” operation."},{"id":"stat-report-conclusion","title":"What a conclusion can say","text":"If all repeated removals match the starting value within tolerance, say that the tested setup returned to baseline. If another player still has a different assembly, keep that report open until its exact setup is reproduced. If a residue appears, identify which field and lifecycle first diverged before writing a clamp or an extra subtraction."},{"id":"tested-scope","title":"What was checked","text":"The ten-cycle fixture ran on 6 November 2026 and returned to baseline without manual refresh between cycles. The reported real-player rail-removal penalty remains unconfirmed. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."}]},{"id":"ballistics","title":"Shots & ballistics","text":"Shots & ballistics A fired shot, a projectile impact and a valid target hit are separate events. The shot pipeline Event What it tells you A_WeaponBase_C:ProcessBulletFired Weapon firing event with cartridge, barrel and created bullet information. C_BulletBase:RegisterHit A bullet reported impact information. TargetBase_C:Hit Target hit path with projectile attribution in the inspected signature. TargetBase_C:OnHit Target reports validity, energy, velocity and bonus-related flags. A projectile can strike non-target geometry. A target callback can be invalid. A shot counter and a scoring system should not increment from the same undifferentiated “hit” event. Observe firing Once the Blueprint function is loaded, a read-only hook can establish when it runs: local path = \"/Game/BlueprintLogic/Weapons/A_WeaponBase.A_WeaponBase_C:ProcessBulletFired\" -- Register once, after StaticFindObject(path) is valid. RegisterHook(path, function(context) local gun = context:get() if gun and gun:IsValid() then print(\"[MyMod] Fired: \" .. gun:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end end) Observed shot arguments After the context, the inspected firing signature has Cartridge , bullet and gas recoil forces, recoil location/direction, Barrel , powder amount, exit pressure, powder type and a Bullet array. Read the exact signature and unwrap parameters according to their type before using them. Do not assume one event always produces exactly one bullet actor. Keep a shot ID and map each resulting projectile to it. Impacts, energy and units The target’s OnHit signature contains HitEnergy and HitVelocity , but a field name alone does not establish the units or all conversions used by the game. Record the raw values and compare controlled shots before labelling them in an overlay. Don’t replace the game’s trajectory with a camera ray and call it the actual bullet path. A straight line can be useful as a cosmetic tracer, but it does not reproduce drop, penetration or ricochets. What is not documented here This guide maps events, not the complete internal ballistics model. Cartridge construction, powder curves, drag coefficients, penetration materials and every ammunition unlock rule still need dedicated research. For score handling, continue to Hits & scoring . Walkthrough: connect a shot to an impact 1. Start with one slow, controlled shot Choose a gun and cartridge, keep the target distance fixed and fire once. Log a firing-event count and the weapon identity. Confirm a single trigger produces the expected firing callbacks before adding an impact counter. Do not use a rapid-fire sequence to discover attribution. Several projectiles in flight make “the latest shot” an unreliable shortcut immediately. 2. Read the firing signature Observed parameter Meaning to investigate Cartridge The cartridge object associated with the firing event. BulletRecoilForce / GasRecoilForce Two recoil-force inputs; not a substitute for muzzle velocity. RecoilLocation / RecoilDirection Location and direction inputs for recoil processing. Barrel The firing barrel object. PowderAmount / ExitPressure / PowderType Ammunition-related inputs with build-specific interpretation. Bullet Array of resulting projectile references. Keep raw values and reflected types in a research note. Do not infer units, valid ranges or a complete ballistic formula from these labels. 3. Assign a shot identity Increment a local shot number at the firing event. Associate every resulting bullet reference with that number and a timestamp. One shot can produce several projectile actors; a bullet’s runtime address is useful only inside the current process/world lifetime. For a network message, send an explicit shot ID and session/round context. Never send a local address and expect the other process to have an object at that address. 4. Observe impacts separately RegisterHit reports bullet impact information. Use the bullet-to-shot mapping to identify which shot produced it. A wall impact can matter for a tracer or replay without being a scoring target hit. Penetration or multiple contacts also make “one impact equals one shot” a weak assumption. 5. Observe the target decision Read the target’s valid-hit decision and attribution before awarding a custom score. A target can report a contact that is invalid for scoring, has already been spent, or belongs to a different player/round. Preserve the game’s own progression path. Cosmetic replication should not blindly replay a reward-granting callback. A measurement log that stays interpretable session / world: weapon and barrel class: cartridge identity: shot ID and creation time: projectile identities: impact order and target identities: raw hit energy / velocity: valid-hit and breaking-hit flags: whether any values were converted for display: Use a fixed distance and change one ammunition or barrel variable at a time. If two values differ, first check that they came from the same event and coordinate/time basis. Cosmetic effects and real trajectories A short-lived muzzle flash, sound or straight tracer can communicate that a friend fired. It does not establish the actual projectile’s full path. Keep effect lifetime bounded and clean up the actors/components your effect owns. To document drop, penetration or ricochet accurately, gather projectile/impact samples and the game’s relevant configuration. This wiki currently maps useful entry points and the investigation method; it does not claim those internal systems have all been reconstructed. What was checked Firing and impact hooks resolved in local range checks; native target hit callbacks were also exercised. No full trajectory or ammunition-physics replacement has been validated. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build.","sections":[{"id":"the-shot-pipeline","title":"The shot pipeline","text":"Event What it tells you A_WeaponBase_C:ProcessBulletFired Weapon firing event with cartridge, barrel and created bullet information. C_BulletBase:RegisterHit A bullet reported impact information. TargetBase_C:Hit Target hit path with projectile attribution in the inspected signature. TargetBase_C:OnHit Target reports validity, energy, velocity and bonus-related flags. A projectile can strike non-target geometry. A target callback can be invalid. A shot counter and a scoring system should not increment from the same undifferentiated “hit” event."},{"id":"observe-firing","title":"Observe firing","text":"Once the Blueprint function is loaded, a read-only hook can establish when it runs: local path = \"/Game/BlueprintLogic/Weapons/A_WeaponBase.A_WeaponBase_C:ProcessBulletFired\" -- Register once, after StaticFindObject(path) is valid. RegisterHook(path, function(context) local gun = context:get() if gun and gun:IsValid() then print(\"[MyMod] Fired: \" .. gun:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end end)"},{"id":"observed-shot-arguments","title":"Observed shot arguments","text":"After the context, the inspected firing signature has Cartridge , bullet and gas recoil forces, recoil location/direction, Barrel , powder amount, exit pressure, powder type and a Bullet array. Read the exact signature and unwrap parameters according to their type before using them. Do not assume one event always produces exactly one bullet actor. Keep a shot ID and map each resulting projectile to it."},{"id":"impacts-and-units","title":"Impacts, energy and units","text":"The target’s OnHit signature contains HitEnergy and HitVelocity , but a field name alone does not establish the units or all conversions used by the game. Record the raw values and compare controlled shots before labelling them in an overlay. Don’t replace the game’s trajectory with a camera ray and call it the actual bullet path. A straight line can be useful as a cosmetic tracer, but it does not reproduce drop, penetration or ricochets."},{"id":"what-is-not-documented","title":"What is not documented here","text":"This guide maps events, not the complete internal ballistics model. Cartridge construction, powder curves, drag coefficients, penetration materials and every ammunition unlock rule still need dedicated research. For score handling, continue to Hits & scoring ."},{"id":"shot-investigation-walkthrough","title":"Walkthrough: connect a shot to an impact","text":""},{"id":"shot-step-one-shot","title":"1. Start with one slow, controlled shot","text":"Choose a gun and cartridge, keep the target distance fixed and fire once. Log a firing-event count and the weapon identity. Confirm a single trigger produces the expected firing callbacks before adding an impact counter. Do not use a rapid-fire sequence to discover attribution. Several projectiles in flight make “the latest shot” an unreliable shortcut immediately."},{"id":"shot-step-signature","title":"2. Read the firing signature","text":"Observed parameter Meaning to investigate Cartridge The cartridge object associated with the firing event. BulletRecoilForce / GasRecoilForce Two recoil-force inputs; not a substitute for muzzle velocity. RecoilLocation / RecoilDirection Location and direction inputs for recoil processing. Barrel The firing barrel object. PowderAmount / ExitPressure / PowderType Ammunition-related inputs with build-specific interpretation. Bullet Array of resulting projectile references. Keep raw values and reflected types in a research note. Do not infer units, valid ranges or a complete ballistic formula from these labels."},{"id":"shot-step-identities","title":"3. Assign a shot identity","text":"Increment a local shot number at the firing event. Associate every resulting bullet reference with that number and a timestamp. One shot can produce several projectile actors; a bullet’s runtime address is useful only inside the current process/world lifetime. For a network message, send an explicit shot ID and session/round context. Never send a local address and expect the other process to have an object at that address."},{"id":"shot-step-impact","title":"4. Observe impacts separately","text":"RegisterHit reports bullet impact information. Use the bullet-to-shot mapping to identify which shot produced it. A wall impact can matter for a tracer or replay without being a scoring target hit. Penetration or multiple contacts also make “one impact equals one shot” a weak assumption."},{"id":"shot-step-target","title":"5. Observe the target decision","text":"Read the target’s valid-hit decision and attribution before awarding a custom score. A target can report a contact that is invalid for scoring, has already been spent, or belongs to a different player/round. Preserve the game’s own progression path. Cosmetic replication should not blindly replay a reward-granting callback."},{"id":"ballistic-measurements","title":"A measurement log that stays interpretable","text":"session / world: weapon and barrel class: cartridge identity: shot ID and creation time: projectile identities: impact order and target identities: raw hit energy / velocity: valid-hit and breaking-hit flags: whether any values were converted for display: Use a fixed distance and change one ammunition or barrel variable at a time. If two values differ, first check that they came from the same event and coordinate/time basis."},{"id":"ballistics-cosmetic-limits","title":"Cosmetic effects and real trajectories","text":"A short-lived muzzle flash, sound or straight tracer can communicate that a friend fired. It does not establish the actual projectile’s full path. Keep effect lifetime bounded and clean up the actors/components your effect owns. To document drop, penetration or ricochet accurately, gather projectile/impact samples and the game’s relevant configuration. This wiki currently maps useful entry points and the investigation method; it does not claim those internal systems have all been reconstructed."},{"id":"tested-scope","title":"What was checked","text":"Firing and impact hooks resolved in local range checks; native target hit callbacks were also exercised. No full trajectory or ammunition-physics replacement has been validated. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."}]},{"id":"ammunition","title":"Cartridges & projectiles","text":"Cartridges & projectiles Inspect cartridge and projectile data without pretending the full ballistic model has been recovered. 1. Separate cartridge, chamber, barrel and projectile The cartridge used by a firing event, the barrel it is chambered in and the created bullet actors are separate objects. A magazine’s inventory is another relationship. Read those identities before comparing a numeric ammunition field. A shot event may create several projectiles. Keep the cartridge identity with the shot ID and map each created bullet back to that event. This preserves attribution when several rounds are in flight. 2. Inspect the cartridge schema Observed C_CartridgeBase field Research meaning / limit ChamberVolume / ChargeMass Raw chamber/charge-related values; verify the game’s units and use. isBlackPowder / isCombustible Ammunition state/type flags. PropellantEnergy / PressureCurve Energy/curve inputs; not a complete recovered pressure calculation. Caliber / SubProjectilesCaliber / BulletWeight Projectile geometry/mass-related values. IsChambered / CChamberedBarrel Chamber state and related barrel reference. CurrentKelvinTemperature A temperature-labelled field observed in the native schema. These names were found in the local dump. Presence is not proof that changing one updates every derived field, nor that every cartridge class uses it identically. Keep read-only schema research separate from a released ammunition editor. 3. Inspect the bullet schema C_BulletBase exposes fields including MuzzleVelocity, BaseVelocity, DragCoefficient, SimulationStepTime, Tracer and several shot-context flags. It also has a reflected CurrentEnergyInJoules function in the inspected dump. Its inspected signature includes a SurfaceType enum input, so do not assume a zero-argument raw-energy call. That function is an investigation point; it was not freshly exercised by this documentation pass. Do not infer all units from a convenient variable name. The familiar kinetic-energy equation can be a cross-check only after the mass and velocity representations are known. It is not a replacement for the game’s pressure, drag, penetration or heat behaviour. 4. Make a controlled comparison Choose one known weapon, barrel and cartridge. Record identities and selected raw values before the shot. Capture firing-event arguments and resulting projectile IDs. Capture actual impacts and target validity separately. Change one input or cartridge type, then repeat at the same setup. Record what was measured versus merely found in a dump. A different target distance, barrel or grip can invalidate a quick comparison. Keep the original configuration in the research note and avoid modifying the player’s permanent inventory for an early experiment. 5. Before building an ammunition editor Determine where the editable template lives, which fields are inputs, which are computed outputs, how the game rebuilds a live cartridge and how purchases/selection persist. Directly assigning a projectile field after it is fired is a different feature from creating a valid selectable cartridge. An editor also needs limits, rejected-input behaviour and a reversible save workflow. Do not call an undocumented field assignment a complete custom-ammunition pipeline. What still needs dedicated research Complete cartridge construction, pressure/propellant equations, drag/penetration tuning, every ammunition unlock rule and all persistence cases are not fully documented. This guide gives a typed starting surface and a repeatable investigation, not a claim of complete simulation coverage. Continue with shot/impact attribution and dump reading .","sections":[{"id":"objects","title":"1. Separate cartridge, chamber, barrel and projectile","text":"The cartridge used by a firing event, the barrel it is chambered in and the created bullet actors are separate objects. A magazine’s inventory is another relationship. Read those identities before comparing a numeric ammunition field. A shot event may create several projectiles. Keep the cartridge identity with the shot ID and map each created bullet back to that event. This preserves attribution when several rounds are in flight."},{"id":"cartridge-fields","title":"2. Inspect the cartridge schema","text":"Observed C_CartridgeBase field Research meaning / limit ChamberVolume / ChargeMass Raw chamber/charge-related values; verify the game’s units and use. isBlackPowder / isCombustible Ammunition state/type flags. PropellantEnergy / PressureCurve Energy/curve inputs; not a complete recovered pressure calculation. Caliber / SubProjectilesCaliber / BulletWeight Projectile geometry/mass-related values. IsChambered / CChamberedBarrel Chamber state and related barrel reference. CurrentKelvinTemperature A temperature-labelled field observed in the native schema. These names were found in the local dump. Presence is not proof that changing one updates every derived field, nor that every cartridge class uses it identically. Keep read-only schema research separate from a released ammunition editor."},{"id":"projectile-fields","title":"3. Inspect the bullet schema","text":"C_BulletBase exposes fields including MuzzleVelocity, BaseVelocity, DragCoefficient, SimulationStepTime, Tracer and several shot-context flags. It also has a reflected CurrentEnergyInJoules function in the inspected dump. Its inspected signature includes a SurfaceType enum input, so do not assume a zero-argument raw-energy call. That function is an investigation point; it was not freshly exercised by this documentation pass. Do not infer all units from a convenient variable name. The familiar kinetic-energy equation can be a cross-check only after the mass and velocity representations are known. It is not a replacement for the game’s pressure, drag, penetration or heat behaviour."},{"id":"controlled-test","title":"4. Make a controlled comparison","text":"Choose one known weapon, barrel and cartridge. Record identities and selected raw values before the shot. Capture firing-event arguments and resulting projectile IDs. Capture actual impacts and target validity separately. Change one input or cartridge type, then repeat at the same setup. Record what was measured versus merely found in a dump. A different target distance, barrel or grip can invalidate a quick comparison. Keep the original configuration in the research note and avoid modifying the player’s permanent inventory for an early experiment."},{"id":"editor-design","title":"5. Before building an ammunition editor","text":"Determine where the editable template lives, which fields are inputs, which are computed outputs, how the game rebuilds a live cartridge and how purchases/selection persist. Directly assigning a projectile field after it is fired is a different feature from creating a valid selectable cartridge. An editor also needs limits, rejected-input behaviour and a reversible save workflow. Do not call an undocumented field assignment a complete custom-ammunition pipeline."},{"id":"gaps","title":"What still needs dedicated research","text":"Complete cartridge construction, pressure/propellant equations, drag/penetration tuning, every ammunition unlock rule and all persistence cases are not fully documented. This guide gives a typed starting surface and a repeatable investigation, not a claim of complete simulation coverage. Continue with shot/impact attribution and dump reading ."}]},{"id":"reloads","title":"Magazines & reloading","text":"Magazines & reloading Trace magazine release and insertion without counting every related event as a separate reload. 1. Observe the normal handling path Use one weapon and magazine first. Record removal, insertion, cancellation and any empty-magazine condition through the normal controls. Do not assume every weapon uses a detachable box magazine or the same fixed animation duration. A useful first mod logs handling states without moving the magazine or awarding anything. This shows which callbacks belong to one physical action before audio or network events are added. 2. Inspect the relevant entry points Observed function Use in investigation A_WeaponBase_C:ReleaseMagazine Weapon-level magazine release path used by local handling capture. Base_BoxMagazine_C:OnSnappedToSlot Insertion/snapping notification on a magazine instance. Base_BoxMagazine_C:ReleaseMagazine Magazine-class release function; inspect relation to weapon-level handling. Base_StripperClip_C:OnSnappedToSlot A distinct handling class/path, not automatically a box magazine. Hooking all similarly named release functions can produce duplicate “reload started” messages. Choose the owner/event that represents your rule, then verify callback counts with the actual weapon. 3. Capture the owner before relationships change The local insertion path checks the part’s ParentWeapon before associating a handling event with a gun. During detachment, owner relationships can change. If your design needs that relationship after release, capture a stable session/weapon identifier at the appropriate stage rather than retaining an invalidated part pointer indefinitely. -- Context: part was unwrapped from a verified callback. if part and part:IsValid() then local weapon = part.ParentWeapon if weapon and weapon:IsValid() then print(\"[MyMod] Handling on \" .. weapon:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end end 4. Distinguish physical and inventory state The inspected box-magazine class has fields such as IsFeedSlotFull and LockedInReciever; preserve that latter spelling from the schema. These observed fields do not by themselves define every cartridge-stack or feeding rule. A magazine mesh appearing in the gun does not establish a correctly linked usable magazine, and a reload sound playing does not prove insertion completed. Check the game’s real firing/feeding result separately. 5. Replicate cosmetics without simulating twice A friend’s visible handling state can drive a short cosmetic motion or sound. That does not require replaying every native inventory mutation on a visual proxy. Include a weapon identity and event ID so a late insertion event cannot animate a newly switched gun. Cancel or replace a pending cosmetic reload when the gun changes, the world travels or a newer authoritative handling state arrives. An arbitrary two-second timer is a presentation policy, not evidence of the game’s actual reload mechanism. Test matrix Ordinary release and insertion each produce the intended single event. Release followed by cancellation does not imply a completed reload. Gun switching during handling clears old cosmetic state. Different magazine/clip types do not share unverified assumptions. Remote retries do not duplicate sounds or inventory changes. After travel, the local gun remains usable independently of the remote model.","sections":[{"id":"normal-flow","title":"1. Observe the normal handling path","text":"Use one weapon and magazine first. Record removal, insertion, cancellation and any empty-magazine condition through the normal controls. Do not assume every weapon uses a detachable box magazine or the same fixed animation duration. A useful first mod logs handling states without moving the magazine or awarding anything. This shows which callbacks belong to one physical action before audio or network events are added."},{"id":"entry-points","title":"2. Inspect the relevant entry points","text":"Observed function Use in investigation A_WeaponBase_C:ReleaseMagazine Weapon-level magazine release path used by local handling capture. Base_BoxMagazine_C:OnSnappedToSlot Insertion/snapping notification on a magazine instance. Base_BoxMagazine_C:ReleaseMagazine Magazine-class release function; inspect relation to weapon-level handling. Base_StripperClip_C:OnSnappedToSlot A distinct handling class/path, not automatically a box magazine. Hooking all similarly named release functions can produce duplicate “reload started” messages. Choose the owner/event that represents your rule, then verify callback counts with the actual weapon."},{"id":"state","title":"3. Capture the owner before relationships change","text":"The local insertion path checks the part’s ParentWeapon before associating a handling event with a gun. During detachment, owner relationships can change. If your design needs that relationship after release, capture a stable session/weapon identifier at the appropriate stage rather than retaining an invalidated part pointer indefinitely. -- Context: part was unwrapped from a verified callback. if part and part:IsValid() then local weapon = part.ParentWeapon if weapon and weapon:IsValid() then print(\"[MyMod] Handling on \" .. weapon:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end end"},{"id":"magazine-fields","title":"4. Distinguish physical and inventory state","text":"The inspected box-magazine class has fields such as IsFeedSlotFull and LockedInReciever; preserve that latter spelling from the schema. These observed fields do not by themselves define every cartridge-stack or feeding rule. A magazine mesh appearing in the gun does not establish a correctly linked usable magazine, and a reload sound playing does not prove insertion completed. Check the game’s real firing/feeding result separately."},{"id":"replication","title":"5. Replicate cosmetics without simulating twice","text":"A friend’s visible handling state can drive a short cosmetic motion or sound. That does not require replaying every native inventory mutation on a visual proxy. Include a weapon identity and event ID so a late insertion event cannot animate a newly switched gun. Cancel or replace a pending cosmetic reload when the gun changes, the world travels or a newer authoritative handling state arrives. An arbitrary two-second timer is a presentation policy, not evidence of the game’s actual reload mechanism."},{"id":"tests","title":"Test matrix","text":"Ordinary release and insertion each produce the intended single event. Release followed by cancellation does not imply a completed reload. Gun switching during handling clears old cosmetic state. Different magazine/clip types do not share unverified assumptions. Remote retries do not duplicate sounds or inventory changes. After travel, the local gun remains usable independently of the remote model."}]},{"id":"aim-recoil","title":"Aiming, cameras & recoil","text":"Aiming, cameras & recoil Separate player aim, camera view, weapon pose, grip state and recoil modifiers. 1. Identify the layer you mean Layer Question Input / pawn aim Did the player request or enter an aiming state? Camera What view rotation and field of view is being rendered? Weapon pose Where is the live weapon or cosmetic proxy positioned? Grip / shoulder Which handling context contributes to behaviour? Recoil modifiers Which aggregate values the weapon exposes in this build? Changing a camera value is not automatically equivalent to the normal aiming action. Likewise, rotating a friend’s proxy is presentation, not another fully simulated weapon. 2. Read the aiming entry points The inspected weapon base includes SetAiming with a NewAiming boolean, plus CosmeticSetAiming and CosmeticUnsetAiming. The shooter class also has its own SetAiming path. Similar names at different layers are a reason to observe the normal call sequence before invoking one manually. The local outgoing pose capture read pawn.Aiming and the controller’s control rotation. These are concrete implementation choices, not proof that one field describes every camera/stance mode. 3. Compare camera and controller rotation Resolve the current player camera manager from the current world when the feature depends on the rendered view. Compare it with control rotation if the effect follows aim. Camera offsets, weapon sway and transitions can make a single rotation source inappropriate for every visual. For a muzzle effect, use the muzzle’s actual source location when available. A line projected from the camera is a useful fallback only if documented as cosmetic; it is not the true ballistic trajectory. 4. Inspect modifiers without replacing the whole model The native weapon base exposes getter names for translation/torque absorption, secondary-grip strength, recoil restabilisation and control. Several are only dump-observed entry points in this reference. Do not write arbitrary values to them or assume a getter name reveals the complete recoil equation. Record the selected grip/shoulder state, equipped parts and raw aggregate values. Use the baseline/remove/restore method from Weapon stats so a normal contextual difference is not mistaken for accumulated corruption. 5. Give remote handling a bounded presentation Use explicit states such as aiming, firing and reloading on the visual proxy. Interpolate an aim pitch within a chosen supported range, retire old states on weapon/world changes, and keep this separate from the local gun’s real mechanics. Walking or handling animations require suitable assets and a valid pose-driving path. Moving a primitive proxy smoothly does not establish a complete skeletal animation system. Verify the transitions Hip-to-aim and aim-to-hip follow the normal input. Camera, gun and proxy do not drift into different spaces. Grip/shoulder changes are recorded in stat comparisons. Switching guns cancels old handling state. Rapid fire/reload events remain attributed to the right gun. Travel reconstructs both local usable and remote visible weapons.","sections":[{"id":"layers","title":"1. Identify the layer you mean","text":"Layer Question Input / pawn aim Did the player request or enter an aiming state? Camera What view rotation and field of view is being rendered? Weapon pose Where is the live weapon or cosmetic proxy positioned? Grip / shoulder Which handling context contributes to behaviour? Recoil modifiers Which aggregate values the weapon exposes in this build? Changing a camera value is not automatically equivalent to the normal aiming action. Likewise, rotating a friend’s proxy is presentation, not another fully simulated weapon."},{"id":"aim-schema","title":"2. Read the aiming entry points","text":"The inspected weapon base includes SetAiming with a NewAiming boolean, plus CosmeticSetAiming and CosmeticUnsetAiming. The shooter class also has its own SetAiming path. Similar names at different layers are a reason to observe the normal call sequence before invoking one manually. The local outgoing pose capture read pawn.Aiming and the controller’s control rotation. These are concrete implementation choices, not proof that one field describes every camera/stance mode."},{"id":"camera","title":"3. Compare camera and controller rotation","text":"Resolve the current player camera manager from the current world when the feature depends on the rendered view. Compare it with control rotation if the effect follows aim. Camera offsets, weapon sway and transitions can make a single rotation source inappropriate for every visual. For a muzzle effect, use the muzzle’s actual source location when available. A line projected from the camera is a useful fallback only if documented as cosmetic; it is not the true ballistic trajectory."},{"id":"recoil","title":"4. Inspect modifiers without replacing the whole model","text":"The native weapon base exposes getter names for translation/torque absorption, secondary-grip strength, recoil restabilisation and control. Several are only dump-observed entry points in this reference. Do not write arbitrary values to them or assume a getter name reveals the complete recoil equation. Record the selected grip/shoulder state, equipped parts and raw aggregate values. Use the baseline/remove/restore method from Weapon stats so a normal contextual difference is not mistaken for accumulated corruption."},{"id":"proxy","title":"5. Give remote handling a bounded presentation","text":"Use explicit states such as aiming, firing and reloading on the visual proxy. Interpolate an aim pitch within a chosen supported range, retire old states on weapon/world changes, and keep this separate from the local gun’s real mechanics. Walking or handling animations require suitable assets and a valid pose-driving path. Moving a primitive proxy smoothly does not establish a complete skeletal animation system."},{"id":"verification","title":"Verify the transitions","text":"Hip-to-aim and aim-to-hip follow the normal input. Camera, gun and proxy do not drift into different spaces. Grip/shoulder changes are recorded in stat comparisons. Switching guns cancels old handling state. Rapid fire/reload events remain attributed to the right gun. Travel reconstructs both local usable and remote visible weapons."}]},{"id":"targets","title":"Finding & tracking targets","text":"Finding & tracking targets Find the range’s target actors, then inspect the component that actually moves. Discover targets The inspected range uses TargetBase_C and derived classes. Get the active world from the viewport, then query that world: local H = require(\"UEHelpers\") ExecuteInGameThread(function() local viewport = H.GetGameViewportClient() if not viewport or not viewport:IsValid() then return end local world = viewport:GetWorld() if not world or not world:IsValid() then return end local class = LoadAsset( \"/Game/BlueprintLogic/Targets/TargetBase.TargetBase_C\" ) if not class or not class:IsValid() then return end local gameplay = StaticFindObject(\"/Script/Engine.Default__GameplayStatics\") local targets = {} gameplay:GetAllActorsOfClass(world, class, targets) print(\"[MyMod] Targets found: \" .. #targets .. \"\\n\") end Useful fields Field Observed use HasBeenHit Whether the target has been marked hit. ValidForOneHitOnly Reject further scoring hits after the first. HitValue Current target value. MinHitValue Lower bound used when reducing a value. HitValueMultOnHit Multiplier used by the local hit-state implementation. isMoving Hint used to prioritise moving target samples. Actor versus component motion A physics mesh can move while its owning actor’s transform stays unchanged. Capturing only K2_GetActorLocation can therefore show a target standing upright remotely while its mesh has fallen over locally. Inspect the primitive, static-mesh or skeletal-mesh component that carries the visible body. Capture its world transform separately. Different target classes can need different component selection. Sampling and caching Discover actors infrequently and sample known targets on a bounded budget. Give moving and recently hit targets priority. A local registry once contained hundreds of targets; rediscovering or loading assets for every target on every frame is unnecessary. Invalidate the registry after travel. Runtime addresses identify local objects only; they are not stable network IDs. Resetting targets Clearing HasBeenHit alone does not restore a broken target, a displaced physics body, its collision, or a reduced score value. A complete reset needs the original state of each relevant field and component. Restarting the range can be a more reliable first reset implementation. Walkthrough: build a target registry 1. Enter a known range Use the normal range flow and confirm the actual loaded level. A menu world can have no targets, and a table row named after a range does not prove that range was loaded. Resolve the world through the current viewport before querying actors. 2. Discover once and inspect a few examples Get actors of the known base class, then log a bounded sample of full names and actual classes. A derived class can have a different body component, physics setup or hit implementation. Keep a registry with an explicit cap and explain what happens if the cap is exceeded. 3. Select the visible body Enumerate primitive components and identify the mesh that represents the moving target. Record its component name, class and initial world transform. Avoid selecting an unrelated trigger box only because it is the first primitive component returned. For a falling pan or moving figure, compare actor location and mesh location while the motion occurs. If only the mesh moves, actor-only replication will visibly fail even though the actor transform is being sent correctly. 4. Save a plain-data baseline Store copied transforms and the specific flags your mode needs: hit state, value, hidden/collision state and physics state where relevant. Keep the live object reference separately and invalidate it with the world. A baseline is a record of what you observed. It is not a guarantee that resetting those fields is sufficient for every target class; some targets can have additional state or event dependencies. 5. Update with a bounded budget Carry a cursor through ordinary entries and separately sample moving/recently hit entries with a cap. Record the worst time between samples for an ordinary target. A registry of hundreds of targets makes that delay measurable; “we update some targets each tick” is not enough to judge motion quality. If you interpolate a remote component, keep its render pose separate from the received authoritative sample. Treat a teleport or reset as a discontinuity rather than interpolating through the entire range. 6. Verify reset behaviour Hit a normal target, hit a one-shot target, knock over a physics target and trigger a breakable target separately. Compare their required reset state. If a complete reset is not understood, implement a range restart instead of offering a reset button that restores only a flag. Value and presentation can diverge A target’s current HitValue can decrease after an accepted hit. A custom challenge may deliberately use a value captured at round start for fairness. Document which policy you chose. Reading a new value after applying the game’s hit reduction can otherwise score the wrong amount. For one-hit targets, check both ValidForOneHitOnly and HasBeenHit. For hidden or destroyed targets, reject further custom hit processing rather than awarding points from a stale registry entry. Lifetime tests Discover and count in the known world. Capture a body transform before and during motion. Trigger a valid hit and verify the expected state change. Restart the range and build a fresh registry. Confirm no old-world actor is sampled or mutated. What was checked Local discovery, target baselines, distinct synthetic targets and actor/component motion were tested. The documented registry strategy is a mod implementation, not a built-in replication service. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build.","sections":[{"id":"discover-targets","title":"Discover targets","text":"The inspected range uses TargetBase_C and derived classes. Get the active world from the viewport, then query that world: local H = require(\"UEHelpers\") ExecuteInGameThread(function() local viewport = H.GetGameViewportClient() if not viewport or not viewport:IsValid() then return end local world = viewport:GetWorld() if not world or not world:IsValid() then return end local class = LoadAsset( \"/Game/BlueprintLogic/Targets/TargetBase.TargetBase_C\" ) if not class or not class:IsValid() then return end local gameplay = StaticFindObject(\"/Script/Engine.Default__GameplayStatics\") local targets = {} gameplay:GetAllActorsOfClass(world, class, targets) print(\"[MyMod] Targets found: \" .. #targets .. \"\\n\") end"},{"id":"target-fields","title":"Useful fields","text":"Field Observed use HasBeenHit Whether the target has been marked hit. ValidForOneHitOnly Reject further scoring hits after the first. HitValue Current target value. MinHitValue Lower bound used when reducing a value. HitValueMultOnHit Multiplier used by the local hit-state implementation. isMoving Hint used to prioritise moving target samples."},{"id":"actor-versus-component","title":"Actor versus component motion","text":"A physics mesh can move while its owning actor’s transform stays unchanged. Capturing only K2_GetActorLocation can therefore show a target standing upright remotely while its mesh has fallen over locally. Inspect the primitive, static-mesh or skeletal-mesh component that carries the visible body. Capture its world transform separately. Different target classes can need different component selection."},{"id":"sampling-and-caching","title":"Sampling and caching","text":"Discover actors infrequently and sample known targets on a bounded budget. Give moving and recently hit targets priority. A local registry once contained hundreds of targets; rediscovering or loading assets for every target on every frame is unnecessary. Invalidate the registry after travel. Runtime addresses identify local objects only; they are not stable network IDs."},{"id":"resetting-targets","title":"Resetting targets","text":"Clearing HasBeenHit alone does not restore a broken target, a displaced physics body, its collision, or a reduced score value. A complete reset needs the original state of each relevant field and component. Restarting the range can be a more reliable first reset implementation."},{"id":"target-discovery-walkthrough","title":"Walkthrough: build a target registry","text":""},{"id":"target-step-choose-world","title":"1. Enter a known range","text":"Use the normal range flow and confirm the actual loaded level. A menu world can have no targets, and a table row named after a range does not prove that range was loaded. Resolve the world through the current viewport before querying actors."},{"id":"target-step-discover-once","title":"2. Discover once and inspect a few examples","text":"Get actors of the known base class, then log a bounded sample of full names and actual classes. A derived class can have a different body component, physics setup or hit implementation. Keep a registry with an explicit cap and explain what happens if the cap is exceeded."},{"id":"target-step-select-body","title":"3. Select the visible body","text":"Enumerate primitive components and identify the mesh that represents the moving target. Record its component name, class and initial world transform. Avoid selecting an unrelated trigger box only because it is the first primitive component returned. For a falling pan or moving figure, compare actor location and mesh location while the motion occurs. If only the mesh moves, actor-only replication will visibly fail even though the actor transform is being sent correctly."},{"id":"target-step-baseline","title":"4. Save a plain-data baseline","text":"Store copied transforms and the specific flags your mode needs: hit state, value, hidden/collision state and physics state where relevant. Keep the live object reference separately and invalidate it with the world. A baseline is a record of what you observed. It is not a guarantee that resetting those fields is sufficient for every target class; some targets can have additional state or event dependencies."},{"id":"target-step-update","title":"5. Update with a bounded budget","text":"Carry a cursor through ordinary entries and separately sample moving/recently hit entries with a cap. Record the worst time between samples for an ordinary target. A registry of hundreds of targets makes that delay measurable; “we update some targets each tick” is not enough to judge motion quality. If you interpolate a remote component, keep its render pose separate from the received authoritative sample. Treat a teleport or reset as a discontinuity rather than interpolating through the entire range."},{"id":"target-step-reset","title":"6. Verify reset behaviour","text":"Hit a normal target, hit a one-shot target, knock over a physics target and trigger a breakable target separately. Compare their required reset state. If a complete reset is not understood, implement a range restart instead of offering a reset button that restores only a flag."},{"id":"target-state-versus-value","title":"Value and presentation can diverge","text":"A target’s current HitValue can decrease after an accepted hit. A custom challenge may deliberately use a value captured at round start for fairness. Document which policy you chose. Reading a new value after applying the game’s hit reduction can otherwise score the wrong amount. For one-hit targets, check both ValidForOneHitOnly and HasBeenHit. For hidden or destroyed targets, reject further custom hit processing rather than awarding points from a stale registry entry."},{"id":"target-lifetime-tests","title":"Lifetime tests","text":"Discover and count in the known world. Capture a body transform before and during motion. Trigger a valid hit and verify the expected state change. Restart the range and build a fresh registry. Confirm no old-world actor is sampled or mutated."},{"id":"tested-scope","title":"What was checked","text":"Local discovery, target baselines, distinct synthetic targets and actor/component motion were tested. The documented registry strategy is a mod implementation, not a built-in replication service. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."}]},{"id":"scoring","title":"Hits & scoring","text":"Hits & scoring Count one valid gameplay event once, and keep your own score separate from progression. Target callbacks The observed OnHit signature passes ValidHit , player, impact information, hit energy, hit velocity, bonus flag, cartridge-unlock flag and breaking-hit flag. Character targets have their own matching hook path in the inspected build. Inspect validity before scoring. Registering both base and derived handlers without attribution or deduplication can count a single physical shot more than once. Attribute a hit Give each local firing event a monotonically increasing shot ID. Associate created bullet actors with that shot. Use the target’s hit path to connect the bullet and target. Check valid-hit state before adding a score. Retire old shot and bullet references after a short bounded window. Do not attribute every hit to “the most recent shot” when the weapon can have several projectiles in flight. Two players firing at once makes that mistake especially visible. Deduplicate events For a custom per-target hit score, one useful key is round + player + shot + target . Decide explicitly whether pellets or repeated contacts should score independently for your mode. Deduplication policy is game design, not just a networking detail. -- Custom mode state, not the game's persistent score. local seen = {} local score = 0 local function award(roundId, playerId, shotId, targetId, points) local key = table.concat({roundId, playerId, shotId, targetId}, \":\") if seen[key] then return false end if type(points) ~= \"number\" or points ~= points then return false end if points \u003c 0 or points > 100000 then return false end seen[key] = true score = score + math.floor(points) return true end Cosmetics are not rewards A replicated impact can play a reaction without replaying the full local scoring/reward event. The local target fixture used CosmeticOnHit for visual response, with custom score handled separately. That does not make the function a complete substitute for the game’s normal hit logic. Do not replay the ordinary hit event merely to display a remote impact: it may grant progression, unlock ammunition or change target value again. Round timing Use explicit countdown, playing, settling and finished phases. For network play, define whether a hit near the deadline is accepted by host receipt time, synchronised event time or a bounded grace policy. Cancel or pause when the connection changes instead of letting an invisible round keep running. Walkthrough: design a small scoring mode 1. Write the rules before the hook Decide whether a score is per projectile, per shot, per target or per round. Decide how repeated hits and pellets behave, whether a spent target scores again, and whether a target’s value is fixed at round start or read live. These rules determine your identifiers and deduplication key. 2. Log eligible events without awarding Observe firing and target callbacks. Record player identity, shot ID, target identity, valid-hit state and event time. Confirm the same physical action is not entering both a base and derived handler as two awards. Start with one player and one target, then two distinct targets, then rapid shots. Networking should come after local attribution is understandable. 3. Keep custom score in pure state Use a Lua state object for round ID, phase, totals and the seen-event set. Do not store a custom challenge’s points in an unrelated game progression field just because it is already visible somewhere. Validate finite bounded numbers before adding them. Reject malformed IDs and stale round identifiers. Define a maximum session total if packets or logs use a limited numeric representation. 4. Implement explicit phases Phase Allowed behaviour Countdown Show the start time; do not award the competitive round yet. Playing Accept eligible shots and valid hit events. Settling Accept only events permitted by the documented end-of-round timing policy. Finished Show result and permit a rematch action. Cancelled Explain the interruption and require an explicit restart. A settling phase is not permission to accept arbitrary late points. Preserve the event’s original eligible time, round identity and a bounded acceptance window. The local implementation has such a policy; internet fairness still needs real delayed-peer testing. 5. Choose score authority For a host-authoritative challenge, the guest submits an attributed event and the host decides whether to apply it. Both peers display the host’s accepted round state. The guest should not independently overwrite the shared total when another snapshot arrives. Connection to Steam does not supply this rule automatically. It is part of the gameplay protocol you implement. 6. Handle interruptions and rematches Cancel or pause when a participant disconnects, becomes unready or leaves the world. Include the reason in the displayed state. On rematch, increment round identity and clear the relevant dedupe state so an old packet cannot award points to the new round. Acceptance cases Test an ordinary hit, an invalid hit, a duplicate event, an already-spent target, two players hitting the same target, a stale round, a hit near the deadline and a reconnect. Confirm cosmetic effects do not grant points twice and a rematch begins with new identifiers. Keep screenshots of the displayed result and a small event trace. A correct final total alone can hide two wrong awards that happened to cancel each other. What was checked Local fixtures exercised valid native hits, one-hit rejection, cosmetic reactions and separate session scores. Internet-delay fairness and simultaneous real-peer shooting are separate tests, not proven by a local fixture. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build.","sections":[{"id":"target-callbacks","title":"Target callbacks","text":"The observed OnHit signature passes ValidHit , player, impact information, hit energy, hit velocity, bonus flag, cartridge-unlock flag and breaking-hit flag. Character targets have their own matching hook path in the inspected build. Inspect validity before scoring. Registering both base and derived handlers without attribution or deduplication can count a single physical shot more than once."},{"id":"attribute-a-hit","title":"Attribute a hit","text":"Give each local firing event a monotonically increasing shot ID. Associate created bullet actors with that shot. Use the target’s hit path to connect the bullet and target. Check valid-hit state before adding a score. Retire old shot and bullet references after a short bounded window. Do not attribute every hit to “the most recent shot” when the weapon can have several projectiles in flight. Two players firing at once makes that mistake especially visible."},{"id":"deduplicate-events","title":"Deduplicate events","text":"For a custom per-target hit score, one useful key is round + player + shot + target . Decide explicitly whether pellets or repeated contacts should score independently for your mode. Deduplication policy is game design, not just a networking detail. -- Custom mode state, not the game's persistent score. local seen = {} local score = 0 local function award(roundId, playerId, shotId, targetId, points) local key = table.concat({roundId, playerId, shotId, targetId}, \":\") if seen[key] then return false end if type(points) ~= \"number\" or points ~= points then return false end if points \u003c 0 or points > 100000 then return false end seen[key] = true score = score + math.floor(points) return true end"},{"id":"cosmetics-and-rewards","title":"Cosmetics are not rewards","text":"A replicated impact can play a reaction without replaying the full local scoring/reward event. The local target fixture used CosmeticOnHit for visual response, with custom score handled separately. That does not make the function a complete substitute for the game’s normal hit logic. Do not replay the ordinary hit event merely to display a remote impact: it may grant progression, unlock ammunition or change target value again."},{"id":"round-timing","title":"Round timing","text":"Use explicit countdown, playing, settling and finished phases. For network play, define whether a hit near the deadline is accepted by host receipt time, synchronised event time or a bounded grace policy. Cancel or pause when the connection changes instead of letting an invisible round keep running."},{"id":"score-mode-walkthrough","title":"Walkthrough: design a small scoring mode","text":""},{"id":"score-step-rules","title":"1. Write the rules before the hook","text":"Decide whether a score is per projectile, per shot, per target or per round. Decide how repeated hits and pellets behave, whether a spent target scores again, and whether a target’s value is fixed at round start or read live. These rules determine your identifiers and deduplication key."},{"id":"score-step-read-only-events","title":"2. Log eligible events without awarding","text":"Observe firing and target callbacks. Record player identity, shot ID, target identity, valid-hit state and event time. Confirm the same physical action is not entering both a base and derived handler as two awards. Start with one player and one target, then two distinct targets, then rapid shots. Networking should come after local attribution is understandable."},{"id":"score-step-pure-state","title":"3. Keep custom score in pure state","text":"Use a Lua state object for round ID, phase, totals and the seen-event set. Do not store a custom challenge’s points in an unrelated game progression field just because it is already visible somewhere. Validate finite bounded numbers before adding them. Reject malformed IDs and stale round identifiers. Define a maximum session total if packets or logs use a limited numeric representation."},{"id":"score-step-phases","title":"4. Implement explicit phases","text":"Phase Allowed behaviour Countdown Show the start time; do not award the competitive round yet. Playing Accept eligible shots and valid hit events. Settling Accept only events permitted by the documented end-of-round timing policy. Finished Show result and permit a rematch action. Cancelled Explain the interruption and require an explicit restart. A settling phase is not permission to accept arbitrary late points. Preserve the event’s original eligible time, round identity and a bounded acceptance window. The local implementation has such a policy; internet fairness still needs real delayed-peer testing."},{"id":"score-step-authority","title":"5. Choose score authority","text":"For a host-authoritative challenge, the guest submits an attributed event and the host decides whether to apply it. Both peers display the host’s accepted round state. The guest should not independently overwrite the shared total when another snapshot arrives. Connection to Steam does not supply this rule automatically. It is part of the gameplay protocol you implement."},{"id":"score-step-interruption","title":"6. Handle interruptions and rematches","text":"Cancel or pause when a participant disconnects, becomes unready or leaves the world. Include the reason in the displayed state. On rematch, increment round identity and clear the relevant dedupe state so an old packet cannot award points to the new round."},{"id":"score-test-cases","title":"Acceptance cases","text":"Test an ordinary hit, an invalid hit, a duplicate event, an already-spent target, two players hitting the same target, a stale round, a hit near the deadline and a reconnect. Confirm cosmetic effects do not grant points twice and a rematch begins with new identifiers. Keep screenshots of the displayed result and a small event trace. A correct final total alone can hide two wrong awards that happened to cancel each other."},{"id":"tested-scope","title":"What was checked","text":"Local fixtures exercised valid native hits, one-hit rejection, cosmetic reactions and separate session scores. Internet-delay fairness and simultaneous real-peer shooting are separate tests, not proven by a local fixture. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."}]},{"id":"ranges","title":"Range menus & travel","text":"Range menus & travel A range card is UI data. A playable destination also needs a cooked map and a working travel path. Range menu data The range table is /Game/Data/Ranges/RangeData.RangeData . The PistolRange row mapped to Range_SwimmingPool in local checks. Its display label is Azure Swimming Pool. The inspected table also had rows such as Valley and ShoppingMall . Their presence did not prove those maps were usable in the demo. What makes a card appear? Two observed UI entry points are RangeList.PreConstruct and RangeListItem.SetSelected . The menu also checks the game instance’s UnlockedRanges , so adding a row alone may leave the card absent. A runtime-only custom card was made visible locally by adding its row name to the in-memory unlocked list. That was an experimental menu change, not a demonstrated permanent unlock or newly cooked map. Confirm the destination Level Package / result Bunker_Main /Game/Maps/Bunker/Bunker_Main Range_SwimmingPool /Game/Maps/Ranges/Range_SwimmingPool Range_Valley A custom-card travel attempt returned to the main menu; not a working demo destination. Use a small explicit allowlist of destinations you tested. Don’t assemble arbitrary map paths from a user-entered label or assume every table row exists in the archive. Local and shared travel Opening a map for one local player is different from moving a connected listen-server session. The local multiplayer code used the AdvancedSessions ServerTravel path with bUseSeamlessTravel enabled and a ?listen destination. That solution depends on the session and driver already being valid. Appending ?listen to an arbitrary OpenLevel call is not a complete multiplayer implementation. After arrival Confirm the actual current level name and world identity. Reacquire controllers, pawns, weapons, targets and UI. Confirm the local player has a usable weapon and ammunition. For multiplayer, confirm both peers arrived before starting a round. Try restart, return to bunker and repeat travel. Building a detached scene by teleporting within the pool world still uses that world’s lighting and lifecycle. See Custom content . Walkthrough: investigate a custom range entry 1. Verify the normal selection flow Start without the custom entry and follow the actual Enter Range and confirmation flow. Record which screen constructs the list and what selecting a working range does. A confirmation screen is not itself the range list; hooking the wrong stage can miss the actual widgets. 2. Inspect one working row Read the known PistolRange row and its map field. Record the display label, row name, destination ID and verified package separately. Do not use the display label as a package path. 3. Separate visibility from selection If an added row is absent, inspect the list’s unlock check and row iteration. If it appears but cannot be selected, inspect the card’s selection state and callback. If it is selected but travel fails, inspect the destination and travel call. During the recorded runtime-card work, bounded numeric iteration avoided an out-of-bounds iterator freeze. That was a container-binding failure, not a reason to keep enlarging or recolouring the card. 4. Confirm the destination by entering it Check the actual current level after travel. A card can have the desired title and still route to the swimming pool. That is a successful card prototype, not a new playable map. An unavailable destination returning to the main menu should be recorded as a failed travel result. Don’t keep advertising the destination solely because its table row exists. 5. If building a runtime scene, prove placement first Spawn one known mesh in a clearly visible position relative to the player and log the requested and actual transform. Confirm mobility permits the intended operation. Expand the layout gradually, with a safe spawn and an escape/return route. Placing buildings far beyond an indoor pool’s walls can produce a successful spawn log and nothing visible. The requested coordinates, player facing, source-target distance and occlusion all matter. 6. Test presentation independently Verify sky, ambient light, materials, exposure and shadowing in the host world. If the player’s gun is bright but distant runtime structures are dark silhouettes, that is useful evidence against a simple “everything was culled” explanation. Inspect the render context before trying another arbitrary view-distance value. A shared range-arrival handshake For multiplayer, distinguish request, countdown, travel, arrival, object rebuild and ready-to-play phases. The host should not start a round merely because its own loading screen finished. Confirm the guest’s world generation and relevant target/loadout state too. On failure, expose a recovery action such as retry, restart or return to bunker. A hidden retry loop that repeatedly travels both peers can turn one bad destination into a confusing session failure. Verification sequence Test normal entry, custom-card selection, arrival identity, spawn safety, visible targets, usable guns, exit, restart and repeated travel. For a true custom cooked map, document its package/loading pipeline separately from runtime geometry inside an existing map. What was checked Pool travel, restart and return were exercised in local native/listen fixtures. Two-PC travel reliability and absent demo maps are not implied by those checks. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build.","sections":[{"id":"range-menu-data","title":"Range menu data","text":"The range table is /Game/Data/Ranges/RangeData.RangeData . The PistolRange row mapped to Range_SwimmingPool in local checks. Its display label is Azure Swimming Pool. The inspected table also had rows such as Valley and ShoppingMall . Their presence did not prove those maps were usable in the demo."},{"id":"make-a-card-appear","title":"What makes a card appear?","text":"Two observed UI entry points are RangeList.PreConstruct and RangeListItem.SetSelected . The menu also checks the game instance’s UnlockedRanges , so adding a row alone may leave the card absent. A runtime-only custom card was made visible locally by adding its row name to the in-memory unlocked list. That was an experimental menu change, not a demonstrated permanent unlock or newly cooked map."},{"id":"confirm-a-destination","title":"Confirm the destination","text":"Level Package / result Bunker_Main /Game/Maps/Bunker/Bunker_Main Range_SwimmingPool /Game/Maps/Ranges/Range_SwimmingPool Range_Valley A custom-card travel attempt returned to the main menu; not a working demo destination. Use a small explicit allowlist of destinations you tested. Don’t assemble arbitrary map paths from a user-entered label or assume every table row exists in the archive."},{"id":"local-and-shared-travel","title":"Local and shared travel","text":"Opening a map for one local player is different from moving a connected listen-server session. The local multiplayer code used the AdvancedSessions ServerTravel path with bUseSeamlessTravel enabled and a ?listen destination. That solution depends on the session and driver already being valid. Appending ?listen to an arbitrary OpenLevel call is not a complete multiplayer implementation."},{"id":"arrival-checks","title":"After arrival","text":"Confirm the actual current level name and world identity. Reacquire controllers, pawns, weapons, targets and UI. Confirm the local player has a usable weapon and ammunition. For multiplayer, confirm both peers arrived before starting a round. Try restart, return to bunker and repeat travel. Building a detached scene by teleporting within the pool world still uses that world’s lighting and lifecycle. See Custom content ."},{"id":"range-feature-walkthrough","title":"Walkthrough: investigate a custom range entry","text":""},{"id":"range-step-baseline","title":"1. Verify the normal selection flow","text":"Start without the custom entry and follow the actual Enter Range and confirmation flow. Record which screen constructs the list and what selecting a working range does. A confirmation screen is not itself the range list; hooking the wrong stage can miss the actual widgets."},{"id":"range-step-row","title":"2. Inspect one working row","text":"Read the known PistolRange row and its map field. Record the display label, row name, destination ID and verified package separately. Do not use the display label as a package path."},{"id":"range-step-visibility","title":"3. Separate visibility from selection","text":"If an added row is absent, inspect the list’s unlock check and row iteration. If it appears but cannot be selected, inspect the card’s selection state and callback. If it is selected but travel fails, inspect the destination and travel call. During the recorded runtime-card work, bounded numeric iteration avoided an out-of-bounds iterator freeze. That was a container-binding failure, not a reason to keep enlarging or recolouring the card."},{"id":"range-step-destination","title":"4. Confirm the destination by entering it","text":"Check the actual current level after travel. A card can have the desired title and still route to the swimming pool. That is a successful card prototype, not a new playable map. An unavailable destination returning to the main menu should be recorded as a failed travel result. Don’t keep advertising the destination solely because its table row exists."},{"id":"range-step-scene","title":"5. If building a runtime scene, prove placement first","text":"Spawn one known mesh in a clearly visible position relative to the player and log the requested and actual transform. Confirm mobility permits the intended operation. Expand the layout gradually, with a safe spawn and an escape/return route. Placing buildings far beyond an indoor pool’s walls can produce a successful spawn log and nothing visible. The requested coordinates, player facing, source-target distance and occlusion all matter."},{"id":"range-step-lighting","title":"6. Test presentation independently","text":"Verify sky, ambient light, materials, exposure and shadowing in the host world. If the player’s gun is bright but distant runtime structures are dark silhouettes, that is useful evidence against a simple “everything was culled” explanation. Inspect the render context before trying another arbitrary view-distance value."},{"id":"range-shared-arrival","title":"A shared range-arrival handshake","text":"For multiplayer, distinguish request, countdown, travel, arrival, object rebuild and ready-to-play phases. The host should not start a round merely because its own loading screen finished. Confirm the guest’s world generation and relevant target/loadout state too. On failure, expose a recovery action such as retry, restart or return to bunker. A hidden retry loop that repeatedly travels both peers can turn one bad destination into a confusing session failure."},{"id":"range-verification","title":"Verification sequence","text":"Test normal entry, custom-card selection, arrival identity, spawn safety, visible targets, usable guns, exit, restart and repeated travel. For a true custom cooked map, document its package/loading pipeline separately from runtime geometry inside an existing map."},{"id":"tested-scope","title":"What was checked","text":"Pool travel, restart and return were exercised in local native/listen fixtures. Two-PC travel reliability and absent demo maps are not implied by those checks. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."}]},{"id":"workshop-ui","title":"Workshop UI","text":"Workshop UI Reuse the game’s widgets, but create your own controls and callbacks. Find the workshop The observed HUD class is /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/HUD_Workshop.HUD_Workshop_C . Its attachment editor is WeaponEditMOde , with that exact spelling. The HUD and attachment editor are related but have different lifetimes. A button placed on the HUD canvas needs its visibility tied to entering and leaving attachment edit mode. Create a native button The common TheRangeButton_C class provides the game’s existing button appearance. This creates a separate widget; it does not click or duplicate Save and exit: local H = require(\"UEHelpers\") ExecuteInGameThread(function() local pc = H.GetPlayerController() if not pc or not pc:IsValid() then return end local class = LoadAsset(\"/Game/UI/Common/TheRangeButton.TheRangeButton_C\") if not class or not class:IsValid() then return end local library = StaticFindObject(\"/Script/UMG.Default__WidgetBlueprintLibrary\") local button = library:Create(pc, class, pc) if not button or not button:IsValid() then return end button.Text = FText(\"My action\") button.NavText:SetText(FText(\"My action\")) -- Add button to your valid parent panel and retain a reference. end Place the control For a CanvasPanel parent, add with AddChildToCanvas and set the resulting CanvasPanelSlot’s anchors, alignment, position and size. Position numbers are relative to the anchors, not always screen pixels from the top left. A local workbench check found Save and exit at X=50, Y=-50 , size 200×40 , with bottom-left anchors and bottom alignment. A button directly above it used Y=-100 , leaving a ten-unit gap. Those numbers describe that HUD layout, not every resolution or future build. Don’t copy action delegates Copy appearance properties deliberately, not the original widget’s action bindings. A copied Save and exit delegate can execute the original action when your new button is pressed. One local implementation handled the left-mouse keybind and checked the native button’s hover state. That verified a mouse path only; it does not establish controller, keyboard, touch or accessibility support. Prefer a verified button event route when your loader and widget expose one. Rebuilds and input Prevent duplicate construction by checking that your widget still has a valid parent. Hide it outside edit mode, cancel pending actions when the displayed weapon changes, and restore any input mode or cursor state you changed. Test hover, click, closing the menu and opening it again. A widget object existing in memory does not prove that it is visible or clickable. Walkthrough: add a control without stealing another action 1. Observe the normal editor lifecycle Open and close attachment edit mode normally. Log HUD construction, edit-mode entry/exit and editor refreshes. Determine which parent panel survives an attachment change. If the point canvas is cleared during refresh, a button placed inside it may be removed with the points. 2. Create a fresh widget Use the native button class for appearance, but instantiate it separately. Set its text and supported appearance fields deliberately. Do not clone the original HUD button’s delegates or use a raw object copy that carries a Save and exit callback into your new action. Keep a strong, reachable reference through the owning widget tree/panel while the control is displayed. A local Lua variable that falls out of scope is not your complete UI ownership strategy. 3. Understand the canvas coordinates With bottom-left anchors, negative Y positions move upward relative to the bottom edge. Alignment determines which point of the widget is placed at that coordinate. Copy anchors, alignment and size together before calculating a vertical offset. Test the actual black menu bounds at more than one window size. Changing only X/Y can look correct at one resolution while breaking when the canvas scales or the text changes. 4. Make installation idempotent Before creating another button, check whether the existing widget is valid and still parented. Reuse it when possible. If the original panel was rebuilt, attach a replacement once and update the stored reference. Idempotent means repeating the setup produces one control in the correct state, not another copy of it. 5. Bind one action and a busy state Disable the control while a queued operation is active. Recheck the editor/weapon context inside the operation. On completion, cancellation or Lua failure, update the text and enabled state consistently. If your only verified input method is a hovered-button mouse check, document that limitation. A visible native button does not automatically give your custom Lua action keyboard/controller support. 6. Tie visibility to edit mode Hide the control on edit-mode exit and cancel pending work that depends on that mode. Re-entry should restore the appropriate enabled state, not always assume a previous job succeeded. Check behaviour when the player switches weapons without leaving the HUD. UI acceptance matrix Action Expected result Open editor One control appears inside the intended panel. Rebuild attachment points The control remains or is recreated once. Click while busy No duplicate job starts. Switch weapon Old job stops before affecting the new assembly. Close editor Control hides and context-dependent work stops. Resize / reopen Placement, label and focus remain usable. Keep action verification separate from appearance checks. A button with matching font and hover styling can still call the wrong action. What was checked Native button construction, matching canvas layout, duplicate prevention and edit-mode visibility passed local fixtures. Full interaction on every fitted player weapon and input device was not established. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build.","sections":[{"id":"find-the-workshop","title":"Find the workshop","text":"The observed HUD class is /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/HUD_Workshop.HUD_Workshop_C . Its attachment editor is WeaponEditMOde , with that exact spelling. The HUD and attachment editor are related but have different lifetimes. A button placed on the HUD canvas needs its visibility tied to entering and leaving attachment edit mode."},{"id":"create-a-native-button","title":"Create a native button","text":"The common TheRangeButton_C class provides the game’s existing button appearance. This creates a separate widget; it does not click or duplicate Save and exit: local H = require(\"UEHelpers\") ExecuteInGameThread(function() local pc = H.GetPlayerController() if not pc or not pc:IsValid() then return end local class = LoadAsset(\"/Game/UI/Common/TheRangeButton.TheRangeButton_C\") if not class or not class:IsValid() then return end local library = StaticFindObject(\"/Script/UMG.Default__WidgetBlueprintLibrary\") local button = library:Create(pc, class, pc) if not button or not button:IsValid() then return end button.Text = FText(\"My action\") button.NavText:SetText(FText(\"My action\")) -- Add button to your valid parent panel and retain a reference. end"},{"id":"place-the-control","title":"Place the control","text":"For a CanvasPanel parent, add with AddChildToCanvas and set the resulting CanvasPanelSlot’s anchors, alignment, position and size. Position numbers are relative to the anchors, not always screen pixels from the top left. A local workbench check found Save and exit at X=50, Y=-50 , size 200×40 , with bottom-left anchors and bottom alignment. A button directly above it used Y=-100 , leaving a ten-unit gap. Those numbers describe that HUD layout, not every resolution or future build."},{"id":"do-not-copy-delegates","title":"Don’t copy action delegates","text":"Copy appearance properties deliberately, not the original widget’s action bindings. A copied Save and exit delegate can execute the original action when your new button is pressed. One local implementation handled the left-mouse keybind and checked the native button’s hover state. That verified a mouse path only; it does not establish controller, keyboard, touch or accessibility support. Prefer a verified button event route when your loader and widget expose one."},{"id":"rebuilds-and-input","title":"Rebuilds and input","text":"Prevent duplicate construction by checking that your widget still has a valid parent. Hide it outside edit mode, cancel pending actions when the displayed weapon changes, and restore any input mode or cursor state you changed. Test hover, click, closing the menu and opening it again. A widget object existing in memory does not prove that it is visible or clickable."},{"id":"ui-button-walkthrough","title":"Walkthrough: add a control without stealing another action","text":""},{"id":"ui-step-observe-lifetime","title":"1. Observe the normal editor lifecycle","text":"Open and close attachment edit mode normally. Log HUD construction, edit-mode entry/exit and editor refreshes. Determine which parent panel survives an attachment change. If the point canvas is cleared during refresh, a button placed inside it may be removed with the points."},{"id":"ui-step-create-fresh","title":"2. Create a fresh widget","text":"Use the native button class for appearance, but instantiate it separately. Set its text and supported appearance fields deliberately. Do not clone the original HUD button’s delegates or use a raw object copy that carries a Save and exit callback into your new action. Keep a strong, reachable reference through the owning widget tree/panel while the control is displayed. A local Lua variable that falls out of scope is not your complete UI ownership strategy."},{"id":"ui-step-layout","title":"3. Understand the canvas coordinates","text":"With bottom-left anchors, negative Y positions move upward relative to the bottom edge. Alignment determines which point of the widget is placed at that coordinate. Copy anchors, alignment and size together before calculating a vertical offset. Test the actual black menu bounds at more than one window size. Changing only X/Y can look correct at one resolution while breaking when the canvas scales or the text changes."},{"id":"ui-step-idempotent","title":"4. Make installation idempotent","text":"Before creating another button, check whether the existing widget is valid and still parented. Reuse it when possible. If the original panel was rebuilt, attach a replacement once and update the stored reference. Idempotent means repeating the setup produces one control in the correct state, not another copy of it."},{"id":"ui-step-action","title":"5. Bind one action and a busy state","text":"Disable the control while a queued operation is active. Recheck the editor/weapon context inside the operation. On completion, cancellation or Lua failure, update the text and enabled state consistently. If your only verified input method is a hovered-button mouse check, document that limitation. A visible native button does not automatically give your custom Lua action keyboard/controller support."},{"id":"ui-step-visibility","title":"6. Tie visibility to edit mode","text":"Hide the control on edit-mode exit and cancel pending work that depends on that mode. Re-entry should restore the appropriate enabled state, not always assume a previous job succeeded. Check behaviour when the player switches weapons without leaving the HUD."},{"id":"ui-acceptance-matrix","title":"UI acceptance matrix","text":"Action Expected result Open editor One control appears inside the intended panel. Rebuild attachment points The control remains or is recreated once. Click while busy No duplicate job starts. Switch weapon Old job stops before affecting the new assembly. Close editor Control hides and context-dependent work stops. Resize / reopen Placement, label and focus remain usable. Keep action verification separate from appearance checks. A button with matching font and hover styling can still call the wrong action."},{"id":"tested-scope","title":"What was checked","text":"Native button construction, matching canvas layout, duplicate prevention and edit-mode visibility passed local fixtures. Full interaction on every fitted player weapon and input device was not established. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."}]},{"id":"progression","title":"Ownership & unlocks","text":"Ownership & unlocks “Visible”, “unlocked”, “purchased” and “fitted” are different states. The relevant containers Game-instance field Observed representation PurchasedParts Set of soft class references for purchased parts. UnlockedWeapons Array of soft weapon-class references. UnlockedRanges Array of range row names. SavedWeaponTemplates Map containing saved weapon assemblies. A template can contain part paths that aren’t matched by a purchased-set-only check. That does not prove those paths all represent independently owned, currently available attachments. Why simple filters fail Three local attachment-menu experiments illustrate the problem: A button-enabled check let every entry through, so enabled state did not express ownership. PurchasedParts alone matched far fewer menu entries than the player expected. A union of purchased and saved-template paths still missed expected choices in the live menu. Those filters were not a completed ownership API. They were removed from the stable expanded catalog. Match reference types Passing a loaded UClass directly to a soft-reference set caused a native crash in an earlier filter. An isolated class-to-soft-reference conversion probe was safe for one unrelated class, but that did not validate every menu membership check. Inspect the stored type, the API’s expected type and the exact path form. Package paths, object paths and generated Blueprint class paths are related but not identical strings. Design an owned-only menu Define whether “owned” includes default, bought and currently fitted parts. Start with a small set of attachments whose ownership the player can confirm. Compare those parts with the game’s normal menu. Use stable identifiers and explain each exclusion in a diagnostic log. Test pagination after filtering, including an empty result and a single page. Do not present “we found many save paths” as proof the filter is correct. The list the player can actually select is the test that matters. Unlocking content Adding an unlocked row name in memory can make a menu card appear. It doesn’t establish permanent progression, the presence of the associated map, or correct save persistence. A production unlock mod needs those checks separately. Walkthrough: investigate one missing owned part 1. Define “owned” for the feature Write down whether the list includes default parts, explicitly purchased parts, parts fitted to saved templates and content merely available in the shop. Those definitions can produce different correct lists. Get the expected rule clear before implementing membership. 2. Build a tiny ground-truth set Pick a few named parts the player can demonstrate as bought or fitted, plus a few definitely unowned parts. Capture their normal menu entries and the relevant saved/live representations. A 137-entry filtered catalog is harder to debug than six known examples. 3. Record stored types and path forms PurchasedParts uses soft class references in the inspected game instance; an attachment widget can expose a loaded class. Compare these representations deliberately. Record the package/object/generated-class path instead of normalising all strings by blindly deleting _C. A native container membership call can be binding-sensitive. The prior wrong-type call crashed the process; a Lua pcall is not a native pointer-safety mechanism. 4. Explain every inclusion decision part=\u003cstable path> normal_menu_state=\u003cobserved state> matched_purchased=\u003cyes/no/not checked> matched_saved_template=\u003cyes/no/not checked> is_default=\u003cknown/unknown> final_decision=\u003cinclude/exclude/unknown> reason=\u003cspecific rule> Represent unknown explicitly while investigating. Quietly treating an unreadable reference as unowned hides exactly the case you need to inspect. 5. Validate the live list Open multiple real attachment points, compare the expected named parts, switch tabs and fit one allowed part. Then check an unowned control. A plausible save-index count does not establish correct live filtering. Counts are not correctness In the local experiments, purchased-only membership and a larger union with template paths both produced unexpectedly small lists. The player’s expected part names remained the deciding test. Avoid promising six pages just because an index contains enough paths; several paths can refer to content not represented by the current menu. Persistence check If you change an unlock container in memory, leave and re-enter the menu, then restart using a disposable save. Determine whether the change was transient or persisted by a game action. Do not advertise permanent unlocking from a successful in-memory card visibility check. What was checked The container types and failed filtering outcomes are recorded local observations. A complete reliable owned-only attachment filter remains unresolved. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build.","sections":[{"id":"the-relevant-containers","title":"The relevant containers","text":"Game-instance field Observed representation PurchasedParts Set of soft class references for purchased parts. UnlockedWeapons Array of soft weapon-class references. UnlockedRanges Array of range row names. SavedWeaponTemplates Map containing saved weapon assemblies. A template can contain part paths that aren’t matched by a purchased-set-only check. That does not prove those paths all represent independently owned, currently available attachments."},{"id":"why-simple-filters-fail","title":"Why simple filters fail","text":"Three local attachment-menu experiments illustrate the problem: A button-enabled check let every entry through, so enabled state did not express ownership. PurchasedParts alone matched far fewer menu entries than the player expected. A union of purchased and saved-template paths still missed expected choices in the live menu. Those filters were not a completed ownership API. They were removed from the stable expanded catalog."},{"id":"match-reference-types","title":"Match reference types","text":"Passing a loaded UClass directly to a soft-reference set caused a native crash in an earlier filter. An isolated class-to-soft-reference conversion probe was safe for one unrelated class, but that did not validate every menu membership check. Inspect the stored type, the API’s expected type and the exact path form. Package paths, object paths and generated Blueprint class paths are related but not identical strings."},{"id":"design-an-owned-only-menu","title":"Design an owned-only menu","text":"Define whether “owned” includes default, bought and currently fitted parts. Start with a small set of attachments whose ownership the player can confirm. Compare those parts with the game’s normal menu. Use stable identifiers and explain each exclusion in a diagnostic log. Test pagination after filtering, including an empty result and a single page. Do not present “we found many save paths” as proof the filter is correct. The list the player can actually select is the test that matters."},{"id":"unlocking-content","title":"Unlocking content","text":"Adding an unlocked row name in memory can make a menu card appear. It doesn’t establish permanent progression, the presence of the associated map, or correct save persistence. A production unlock mod needs those checks separately."},{"id":"ownership-investigation-walkthrough","title":"Walkthrough: investigate one missing owned part","text":""},{"id":"ownership-step-define-owned","title":"1. Define “owned” for the feature","text":"Write down whether the list includes default parts, explicitly purchased parts, parts fitted to saved templates and content merely available in the shop. Those definitions can produce different correct lists. Get the expected rule clear before implementing membership."},{"id":"ownership-step-ground-truth","title":"2. Build a tiny ground-truth set","text":"Pick a few named parts the player can demonstrate as bought or fitted, plus a few definitely unowned parts. Capture their normal menu entries and the relevant saved/live representations. A 137-entry filtered catalog is harder to debug than six known examples."},{"id":"ownership-step-record-types","title":"3. Record stored types and path forms","text":"PurchasedParts uses soft class references in the inspected game instance; an attachment widget can expose a loaded class. Compare these representations deliberately. Record the package/object/generated-class path instead of normalising all strings by blindly deleting _C. A native container membership call can be binding-sensitive. The prior wrong-type call crashed the process; a Lua pcall is not a native pointer-safety mechanism."},{"id":"ownership-step-explain-decision","title":"4. Explain every inclusion decision","text":"part=\u003cstable path> normal_menu_state=\u003cobserved state> matched_purchased=\u003cyes/no/not checked> matched_saved_template=\u003cyes/no/not checked> is_default=\u003cknown/unknown> final_decision=\u003cinclude/exclude/unknown> reason=\u003cspecific rule> Represent unknown explicitly while investigating. Quietly treating an unreadable reference as unowned hides exactly the case you need to inspect."},{"id":"ownership-step-validate-ui","title":"5. Validate the live list","text":"Open multiple real attachment points, compare the expected named parts, switch tabs and fit one allowed part. Then check an unowned control. A plausible save-index count does not establish correct live filtering."},{"id":"ownership-counts-and-pages","title":"Counts are not correctness","text":"In the local experiments, purchased-only membership and a larger union with template paths both produced unexpectedly small lists. The player’s expected part names remained the deciding test. Avoid promising six pages just because an index contains enough paths; several paths can refer to content not represented by the current menu."},{"id":"ownership-persistence-check","title":"Persistence check","text":"If you change an unlock container in memory, leave and re-enter the menu, then restart using a disposable save. Determine whether the change was transient or persisted by a game action. Do not advertise permanent unlocking from a successful in-memory card visibility check."},{"id":"tested-scope","title":"What was checked","text":"The container types and failed filtering outcomes are recorded local observations. A complete reliable owned-only attachment filter remains unresolved. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."}]},{"id":"saves","title":"Reading & backing up saves","text":"Reading & backing up saves Start read-only. A live game instance and a save file are not the same snapshot. Save location The local demo uses %LOCALAPPDATA%/Ballistic/Saved/SaveGames/BallisticDemo.sav . Close the game and copy the save folder somewhere separate before an experiment that can change progression or assemblies. Keep backups out of your mod release and out of a public repository. A useful backup is a dated copy you can identify and restore, not a file overwritten on every test. Load without applying GameplayStatics can load the persisted slot into a save object. Loading it for inspection is different from replacing the game instance’s active state: local H = require(\"UEHelpers\") ExecuteInGameThread(function() local viewport = H.GetGameViewportClient() if not viewport or not viewport:IsValid() then return end local world = viewport:GetWorld() if not world or not world:IsValid() then return end local gameplay = StaticFindObject(\"/Script/Engine.Default__GameplayStatics\") local instance = gameplay:GetGameInstance(world) if not instance or not instance:IsValid() then return end local save = gameplay:LoadGameFromSlot(\"BallisticDemo\", instance.UserIndex) if save and save:IsValid() then print(\"[MyMod] Save class: \" .. save:GetClass():GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end end Known save class /Game/BlueprintLogic/Core/Game/Save/BallisticSaveGame.BallisticSaveGame_C Do not assume that copying properties from one object to another is a complete restore. The local save-service code also refreshes game-instance data and invalidates selected loadouts and cached range lists after applying a snapshot. Snapshot versus live state A file copy contains the last persisted state. Unsaved workbench edits or in-memory progression can differ. “Host save to guest” is a one-way snapshot transfer unless you explicitly implement a merge or ongoing replication. A sharing feature should distinguish accepting a download from applying it. Bind the final confirmation to the exact peer and transfer, reject incompatible data, back up the guest’s original file, and handle failed loads with a recovery path. Verify a save feature Use disposable profiles for write tests. Record a file hash before a read-only probe and compare it afterwards. For a restore feature, test a controlled backup/apply/restore cycle and then restart the game. A success message from a copy operation does not prove the game can load the result. Walkthrough: inspect without applying 1. Make an identifiable backup Close the game, copy the save folder to a separate dated location and keep the original filenames. Record the source file’s size, modification time and checksum if your test needs to prove no file changed. A backup stored beside a release staging folder can accidentally be shipped. Keep private save material outside the build allowlist and review the archive contents before sharing. 2. Decide which state you want to inspect LoadGameFromSlot reads the persisted slot. The game instance contains current in-memory state. If the player edited a gun but has not saved, the two can legitimately differ. Describe the chosen snapshot in the tool’s UI and logs. 3. Read the class before the fields Use the read-only loading example, confirm the resulting class and inspect a small known field. Do not immediately assign the loaded object to CurrentSaveGame or call a retrieval/apply function; that crosses from inspection into changing the active game state. A field whose name appears in a binary save is a discovery hint. It is not by itself a complete schema or proof that a byte-level edit will remain loadable. 4. Compare before and after For a read-only probe, compare the original file checksum after the test and note any ordinary in-game actions that could have saved independently. For a write test, use a disposable profile and record exactly which operation changed the file. Designing a snapshot transfer Stage Required distinction Offer Host identifies a bounded last-saved snapshot. Accept download Guest consents to receive bytes, not to overwrite progression. Receive / verify Length, format, transfer identity and integrity are checked. Apply confirmation Guest explicitly confirms applying this exact transfer from this peer. Backup / replace Original local data is preserved before replacement. Reload / refresh Game state and cached selections are updated or recovery is triggered. Do not call a one-time file snapshot “shared live progression”. It does not merge simultaneous changes or keep both saves continuously identical. Applying and recovering Apply only in a stable context, with travel and another transfer blocked. Bind the confirmation to the transfer ID so a delayed click cannot apply a newly arrived offer accidentally. If the imported state fails to load, restore the preserved backup and report the failure. After a successful reload, invalidate cached selected weapons, unlocked range lists and UI data that were derived from the previous save. A correct file on disk can still leave the current menu showing stale selections. What to test before releasing a save feature Test valid input, malformed input, oversized input, interruption, reconnect, cancelled acceptance, repeated apply confirmation and failed load/recovery. Then restart and confirm the disposable profile loads. Local transfer simulation can validate a state machine without proving a real guest’s backup/apply flow. What was checked Read-only slot loading and native export checks ran locally. Local save-sharing code exists, but real two-PC guest application was not verified in the recorded test pass. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build.","sections":[{"id":"save-location","title":"Save location","text":"The local demo uses %LOCALAPPDATA%/Ballistic/Saved/SaveGames/BallisticDemo.sav . Close the game and copy the save folder somewhere separate before an experiment that can change progression or assemblies. Keep backups out of your mod release and out of a public repository. A useful backup is a dated copy you can identify and restore, not a file overwritten on every test."},{"id":"load-without-applying","title":"Load without applying","text":"GameplayStatics can load the persisted slot into a save object. Loading it for inspection is different from replacing the game instance’s active state: local H = require(\"UEHelpers\") ExecuteInGameThread(function() local viewport = H.GetGameViewportClient() if not viewport or not viewport:IsValid() then return end local world = viewport:GetWorld() if not world or not world:IsValid() then return end local gameplay = StaticFindObject(\"/Script/Engine.Default__GameplayStatics\") local instance = gameplay:GetGameInstance(world) if not instance or not instance:IsValid() then return end local save = gameplay:LoadGameFromSlot(\"BallisticDemo\", instance.UserIndex) if save and save:IsValid() then print(\"[MyMod] Save class: \" .. save:GetClass():GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end end"},{"id":"known-save-class","title":"Known save class","text":"/Game/BlueprintLogic/Core/Game/Save/BallisticSaveGame.BallisticSaveGame_C Do not assume that copying properties from one object to another is a complete restore. The local save-service code also refreshes game-instance data and invalidates selected loadouts and cached range lists after applying a snapshot."},{"id":"snapshot-versus-live-state","title":"Snapshot versus live state","text":"A file copy contains the last persisted state. Unsaved workbench edits or in-memory progression can differ. “Host save to guest” is a one-way snapshot transfer unless you explicitly implement a merge or ongoing replication. A sharing feature should distinguish accepting a download from applying it. Bind the final confirmation to the exact peer and transfer, reject incompatible data, back up the guest’s original file, and handle failed loads with a recovery path."},{"id":"verify-a-save-feature","title":"Verify a save feature","text":"Use disposable profiles for write tests. Record a file hash before a read-only probe and compare it afterwards. For a restore feature, test a controlled backup/apply/restore cycle and then restart the game. A success message from a copy operation does not prove the game can load the result."},{"id":"save-readonly-walkthrough","title":"Walkthrough: inspect without applying","text":""},{"id":"save-step-close-and-copy","title":"1. Make an identifiable backup","text":"Close the game, copy the save folder to a separate dated location and keep the original filenames. Record the source file’s size, modification time and checksum if your test needs to prove no file changed. A backup stored beside a release staging folder can accidentally be shipped. Keep private save material outside the build allowlist and review the archive contents before sharing."},{"id":"save-step-live-versus-file","title":"2. Decide which state you want to inspect","text":"LoadGameFromSlot reads the persisted slot. The game instance contains current in-memory state. If the player edited a gun but has not saved, the two can legitimately differ. Describe the chosen snapshot in the tool’s UI and logs."},{"id":"save-step-read-class","title":"3. Read the class before the fields","text":"Use the read-only loading example, confirm the resulting class and inspect a small known field. Do not immediately assign the loaded object to CurrentSaveGame or call a retrieval/apply function; that crosses from inspection into changing the active game state. A field whose name appears in a binary save is a discovery hint. It is not by itself a complete schema or proof that a byte-level edit will remain loadable."},{"id":"save-step-compare","title":"4. Compare before and after","text":"For a read-only probe, compare the original file checksum after the test and note any ordinary in-game actions that could have saved independently. For a write test, use a disposable profile and record exactly which operation changed the file."},{"id":"save-transfer-stages","title":"Designing a snapshot transfer","text":"Stage Required distinction Offer Host identifies a bounded last-saved snapshot. Accept download Guest consents to receive bytes, not to overwrite progression. Receive / verify Length, format, transfer identity and integrity are checked. Apply confirmation Guest explicitly confirms applying this exact transfer from this peer. Backup / replace Original local data is preserved before replacement. Reload / refresh Game state and cached selections are updated or recovery is triggered. Do not call a one-time file snapshot “shared live progression”. It does not merge simultaneous changes or keep both saves continuously identical."},{"id":"save-apply-recovery","title":"Applying and recovering","text":"Apply only in a stable context, with travel and another transfer blocked. Bind the confirmation to the transfer ID so a delayed click cannot apply a newly arrived offer accidentally. If the imported state fails to load, restore the preserved backup and report the failure. After a successful reload, invalidate cached selected weapons, unlocked range lists and UI data that were derived from the previous save. A correct file on disk can still leave the current menu showing stale selections."},{"id":"save-test-limits","title":"What to test before releasing a save feature","text":"Test valid input, malformed input, oversized input, interruption, reconnect, cancelled acceptance, repeated apply confirmation and failed load/recovery. Then restart and confirm the disposable profile loads. Local transfer simulation can validate a state machine without proving a real guest’s backup/apply flow."},{"id":"tested-scope","title":"What was checked","text":"Read-only slot loading and native export checks ran locally. Local save-sharing code exists, but real two-PC guest application was not verified in the recorded test pass. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."}]},{"id":"input-focus","title":"Input, focus & cursors","text":"Input, focus & cursors Make keybinds and menus coexist with game input, cursor state and real button interaction. 1. Confirm which window has focus UE4SS keybind callbacks in the referenced API require the game or debug console to have focus. A missing response while typing in an external editor does not prove registration failed. Begin with a log-only callback and a unique startup prefix. Choose a key that does not conflict with another mod or a critical game action. In the teaching examples, F9 or F10 are defaults to change deliberately, not reserved keys guaranteed to be free. 2. Register once and debounce the action you own local queued = false RegisterKeyBind(Key.F9, function() if queued then return end queued = true ExecuteInGameThread(function() local ok, err = pcall(function() -- Your bounded action, with fresh object checks. end) queued = false if not ok then print(\"[MyMod] \" .. tostring(err) .. \"\\n\") end end) end) This prevents overlapping queued operations, not every possible input-repeat pattern. If you need one action per press or a minimum interval, define and test that rule without hiding legitimate second presses indefinitely. 3. Capture input state before opening your menu The local panel code remembered the prior mouse-cursor state and used the widget library’s game/UI input modes. On close it restored an appropriate previous mode instead of assuming the cursor should always be hidden. Do not claim a generic setter name works on every loader/build. Verify the reflected widget/input API you are calling and the owning controller. Menu construction and input ownership need the current world’s context. 4. Keep text entry from firing game actions When chat or an edit box is active, decide which shortcuts still apply. Sending a message must not also clear attachments or trigger a range action. A global key callback may need your feature’s focus/open-state guard. Test Enter, Escape, click-outside behaviour and reopening. If an operation closes the menu, restore input state on success and on cancellation/error paths. 5. Distinguish hover polling from native activation A mouse-hover plus left-button keybind can implement a particular clickable action, as one local workbench control did. It does not automatically provide keyboard/controller activation, focus traversal or accessibility. Copying native styling is not equivalent to binding the native action route. Use UMG layout to verify hit areas and parent visibility. A button can exist and still be behind another panel or outside the visible bounds. Input acceptance checklist Key works once when the intended window is focused. Typing in a field does not trigger an unrelated action. The visible hit area matches where the button responds. Escape/close restores the documented input and cursor state. Reopening does not duplicate callbacks. Unsupported input methods are stated rather than assumed.","sections":[{"id":"focus","title":"1. Confirm which window has focus","text":"UE4SS keybind callbacks in the referenced API require the game or debug console to have focus. A missing response while typing in an external editor does not prove registration failed. Begin with a log-only callback and a unique startup prefix. Choose a key that does not conflict with another mod or a critical game action. In the teaching examples, F9 or F10 are defaults to change deliberately, not reserved keys guaranteed to be free."},{"id":"one-registration","title":"2. Register once and debounce the action you own","text":"local queued = false RegisterKeyBind(Key.F9, function() if queued then return end queued = true ExecuteInGameThread(function() local ok, err = pcall(function() -- Your bounded action, with fresh object checks. end) queued = false if not ok then print(\"[MyMod] \" .. tostring(err) .. \"\\n\") end end) end) This prevents overlapping queued operations, not every possible input-repeat pattern. If you need one action per press or a minimum interval, define and test that rule without hiding legitimate second presses indefinitely."},{"id":"menu-state","title":"3. Capture input state before opening your menu","text":"The local panel code remembered the prior mouse-cursor state and used the widget library’s game/UI input modes. On close it restored an appropriate previous mode instead of assuming the cursor should always be hidden. Do not claim a generic setter name works on every loader/build. Verify the reflected widget/input API you are calling and the owning controller. Menu construction and input ownership need the current world’s context."},{"id":"text-entry","title":"4. Keep text entry from firing game actions","text":"When chat or an edit box is active, decide which shortcuts still apply. Sending a message must not also clear attachments or trigger a range action. A global key callback may need your feature’s focus/open-state guard. Test Enter, Escape, click-outside behaviour and reopening. If an operation closes the menu, restore input state on success and on cancellation/error paths."},{"id":"button-path","title":"5. Distinguish hover polling from native activation","text":"A mouse-hover plus left-button keybind can implement a particular clickable action, as one local workbench control did. It does not automatically provide keyboard/controller activation, focus traversal or accessibility. Copying native styling is not equivalent to binding the native action route. Use UMG layout to verify hit areas and parent visibility. A button can exist and still be behind another panel or outside the visible bounds."},{"id":"tests","title":"Input acceptance checklist","text":"Key works once when the intended window is focused. Typing in a field does not trigger an unrelated action. The visible hit area matches where the button responds. Escape/close restores the documented input and cursor state. Reopening does not duplicate callbacks. Unsupported input methods are stated rather than assumed."}]},{"id":"umg-layout","title":"UMG panels & layout","text":"UMG panels & layout Choose the panel and slot that match your layout instead of treating every position as a screen pixel. 1. Identify the widget tree and parent A UMG control belongs to a widget tree and is attached to a parent panel. The parent decides its slot type. A canvas, vertical list, horizontal row and wrap box do not interpret layout properties the same way. Panel Layout role CanvasPanel Anchored/aligned placement with a CanvasPanelSlot. VerticalBox / HorizontalBox Ordered children along one axis. WrapBox Children wrap into rows according to available space. SizeBox Explicit width/height constraints around a child. Border Background/padding around content. 2. Configure the slot produced by that panel AddChildToCanvas returns the canvas slot used for anchors, alignment, position and size. A list panel instead supplies its own padding/size rules. A SetPadding method seen on one slot is not proof every slot class exposes it. Inspect the original game widget’s parent and slot when matching an existing control. Copy the supported appearance/layout values deliberately; do not copy its action delegates. 3. Reason about anchors and alignment Anchors define the reference area; alignment defines which point of the child is placed at the resulting coordinate. With bottom-left anchoring and bottom alignment, negative Y can place a button above the bottom edge. Changing only position without matching those values often works at one size and breaks at another. Use the tested Save and exit layout in Workshop UI as a build-specific example, not a universal coordinate set for every HUD. 4. Choose visibility intentionally Visible, collapsed, hidden and hit-test-related states have different consequences. Use the actual enum/value mapping verified for your binding. The local controls used visible/collapsed transitions when entering/leaving edit mode; those actions also affected whether a layout reserved space. A non-interactive decorative overlay should not intercept the player’s mouse just because it is visible. Conversely, a hidden/collapsed parent can make a valid child widget impossible to interact with. 5. Check text, wrapping and colour types Use FText where the setter expects display text. Check long labels and translated/Unicode names, not only a short English placeholder. A fixed-width button can clip or stretch the whole row unexpectedly. UMG brush/linear colours and TextRender byte colours are not interchangeable shapes/ranges. The local code used float linear colour values for panels and byte-like values for nameplate text. Match the setter’s reflected type. 6. Test rebuilds and scrolling Open the menu, change the selected attachment, resize and reopen. Confirm the control remains inside the visible panel and is not recreated twice. For long settings, use a deliberate scroll layout rather than extending content beyond the black menu bounds. For a new panel, prove appearance, focus and action separately. A correctly sized button that still invokes Save and exit has failed even if it looks native.","sections":[{"id":"tree","title":"1. Identify the widget tree and parent","text":"A UMG control belongs to a widget tree and is attached to a parent panel. The parent decides its slot type. A canvas, vertical list, horizontal row and wrap box do not interpret layout properties the same way. Panel Layout role CanvasPanel Anchored/aligned placement with a CanvasPanelSlot. VerticalBox / HorizontalBox Ordered children along one axis. WrapBox Children wrap into rows according to available space. SizeBox Explicit width/height constraints around a child. Border Background/padding around content."},{"id":"slots","title":"2. Configure the slot produced by that panel","text":"AddChildToCanvas returns the canvas slot used for anchors, alignment, position and size. A list panel instead supplies its own padding/size rules. A SetPadding method seen on one slot is not proof every slot class exposes it. Inspect the original game widget’s parent and slot when matching an existing control. Copy the supported appearance/layout values deliberately; do not copy its action delegates."},{"id":"anchors","title":"3. Reason about anchors and alignment","text":"Anchors define the reference area; alignment defines which point of the child is placed at the resulting coordinate. With bottom-left anchoring and bottom alignment, negative Y can place a button above the bottom edge. Changing only position without matching those values often works at one size and breaks at another. Use the tested Save and exit layout in Workshop UI as a build-specific example, not a universal coordinate set for every HUD."},{"id":"visibility","title":"4. Choose visibility intentionally","text":"Visible, collapsed, hidden and hit-test-related states have different consequences. Use the actual enum/value mapping verified for your binding. The local controls used visible/collapsed transitions when entering/leaving edit mode; those actions also affected whether a layout reserved space. A non-interactive decorative overlay should not intercept the player’s mouse just because it is visible. Conversely, a hidden/collapsed parent can make a valid child widget impossible to interact with."},{"id":"text-colour","title":"5. Check text, wrapping and colour types","text":"Use FText where the setter expects display text. Check long labels and translated/Unicode names, not only a short English placeholder. A fixed-width button can clip or stretch the whole row unexpectedly. UMG brush/linear colours and TextRender byte colours are not interchangeable shapes/ranges. The local code used float linear colour values for panels and byte-like values for nameplate text. Match the setter’s reflected type."},{"id":"rebuild","title":"6. Test rebuilds and scrolling","text":"Open the menu, change the selected attachment, resize and reopen. Confirm the control remains inside the visible panel and is not recreated twice. For long settings, use a deliberate scroll layout rather than extending content beyond the black menu bounds. For a new panel, prove appearance, focus and action separately. A correctly sized button that still invokes Save and exit has failed even if it looks native."}]},{"id":"hud-nameplates","title":"HUDs, markers & nameplates","text":"HUDs, markers & nameplates Design local overlays and world labels with clear identity, lifetime and presentation rules. 1. Choose screen HUD or world text A screen-space HUD is appropriate for session status and local controls. A world-space nameplate follows an actor and needs a position, orientation and visibility policy. Avoid turning every label into the same kind of widget merely because one creation example is convenient. Keep the displayed name separate from the stable player/session identity. A renamed or duplicate display name must not change who owns a score or remote gun. 2. Create a label with deliberate ownership The local nameplate implementation spawned TextRenderActor, disabled collision and replication for its cosmetic proxy, configured the text component, and updated its transform relative to the player/camera. This was a mod-owned visual actor, not a new authoritative player pawn. Track the label for cleanup and recreate it for the current world if needed. Do not keep an old-world text actor in a global player record after travel. 3. Choose position and facing Use a known offset above the relevant actor, and orient toward the current camera where appropriate. A proxy body, real pawn and text label may have different root origins; inspect their actual transforms before choosing the offset. Distance labels need a documented world-unit conversion. The local marker display divided its measured world distance by 100 for metres in that setup. Confirm scale with a known reference rather than applying that factor blindly to imported geometry. 4. Validate names as display text Remove control characters, keep a bounded UTF-8-safe length and avoid interpreting markup-like input. A plain display name is not code or a file path. See Chat, names & text validation for the shared text problem. Do not replace an unavailable player name with a nil concatenation that crashes every render update. Use a clearly defined fallback while preserving the internal identity. 5. Avoid redundant HUD writes Cache successfully applied text, visibility and enabled state within the current widget tree. Update only when the desired value changes. If a setter fails, keep the entry retryable rather than marking a value as successfully displayed. On menu rebuild, invalidate the cache because the old widget objects no longer describe the new tree. A global text cache detached from widget identity can leave new labels blank. Verify the presentation Labels follow the intended actor through movement and aim. Two players with similar names remain distinct internally. Long/Unicode names fit or truncate safely. Close-range hiding, distance display and away state follow a defined policy. Opening the local menu does not leave a duplicate overlay visible unintentionally. Travel, disconnect and feature close remove the correct labels. Projection into screen space is another transform stage; verify the current camera and the actual projection binding before converting world positions into canvas coordinates.","sections":[{"id":"choose","title":"1. Choose screen HUD or world text","text":"A screen-space HUD is appropriate for session status and local controls. A world-space nameplate follows an actor and needs a position, orientation and visibility policy. Avoid turning every label into the same kind of widget merely because one creation example is convenient. Keep the displayed name separate from the stable player/session identity. A renamed or duplicate display name must not change who owns a score or remote gun."},{"id":"world-text","title":"2. Create a label with deliberate ownership","text":"The local nameplate implementation spawned TextRenderActor, disabled collision and replication for its cosmetic proxy, configured the text component, and updated its transform relative to the player/camera. This was a mod-owned visual actor, not a new authoritative player pawn. Track the label for cleanup and recreate it for the current world if needed. Do not keep an old-world text actor in a global player record after travel."},{"id":"position","title":"3. Choose position and facing","text":"Use a known offset above the relevant actor, and orient toward the current camera where appropriate. A proxy body, real pawn and text label may have different root origins; inspect their actual transforms before choosing the offset. Distance labels need a documented world-unit conversion. The local marker display divided its measured world distance by 100 for metres in that setup. Confirm scale with a known reference rather than applying that factor blindly to imported geometry."},{"id":"names","title":"4. Validate names as display text","text":"Remove control characters, keep a bounded UTF-8-safe length and avoid interpreting markup-like input. A plain display name is not code or a file path. See Chat, names & text validation for the shared text problem. Do not replace an unavailable player name with a nil concatenation that crashes every render update. Use a clearly defined fallback while preserving the internal identity."},{"id":"hud-updates","title":"5. Avoid redundant HUD writes","text":"Cache successfully applied text, visibility and enabled state within the current widget tree. Update only when the desired value changes. If a setter fails, keep the entry retryable rather than marking a value as successfully displayed. On menu rebuild, invalidate the cache because the old widget objects no longer describe the new tree. A global text cache detached from widget identity can leave new labels blank."},{"id":"acceptance","title":"Verify the presentation","text":"Labels follow the intended actor through movement and aim. Two players with similar names remain distinct internally. Long/Unicode names fit or truncate safely. Close-range hiding, distance display and away state follow a defined policy. Opening the local menu does not leave a duplicate overlay visible unintentionally. Travel, disconnect and feature close remove the correct labels. Projection into screen space is another transform stage; verify the current camera and the actual projection binding before converting world positions into canvas coordinates."}]},{"id":"asset-loading","title":"Asset paths & loading","text":"Asset paths & loading Distinguish a content reference, an installed package, a loaded object and a usable asset. Four separate checks Check Evidence Referenced A row, thumbnail or object points at a name. Present The appropriate package appears in the installed archive. Loaded The runtime request returns the expected valid object type. Usable The asset displays or behaves correctly in the intended world. Do not collapse these into one “asset exists” flag. The custom-range work produced a card before a new playable destination; the model experiments loaded materials without establishing a correct held weapon. Those outcomes are useful precisely because their limits are separate. 1. Record the exact path and type Keep the package/object path separate from its filename in an archive browser. A Blueprint class reference commonly ends with a generated class name, while a static mesh, material or sound has its own object name. Preserve case and suffixes from the actual reference. /Engine/BasicShapes/Cube.Cube /Game/VFX/MuzzleFlash/MuzzleFlash.MuzzleFlash /Game/SFX/Cues/Shots/Shot_9mm.Shot_9mm 2. Load on the game thread ExecuteInGameThread(function() local mesh = LoadAsset(\"/Engine/BasicShapes/Cube.Cube\") if not mesh or not mesh:IsValid() then print(\"[MyMod] Cube asset unavailable.\\n\") return end print(\"[MyMod] Loaded \" .. mesh:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end LoadAsset belongs on the game thread in the API used here. StaticFindObject instead searches what is already loaded. Use a low-frequency discovery path and cache successful results; loading the same path every render tick is unnecessary work. 3. Validate the returned type Before passing a result to SetStaticMesh, a material setter or a sound function, confirm it is the type that function expects. A valid UObject with a familiar name is not sufficient. The local audio code explicitly checked SoundBase compatibility before playback. If the asset type or dependency is missing, report that specific result. Do not substitute an unrelated object and let the next native call become the first diagnostic. 4. Cache without hiding failures Cache an asset after a successful load. If the first attempt happens before its context is ready, distinguish “retry later” from “known unsupported path”. Keep bounded retries and an explicit error state. A permanent false cache created during early startup can make a later valid asset look unavailable forever. World-specific spawned actors are not the same as loaded content assets. Even when the asset remains loaded after travel, reacquire the current world and create new world-owned instances as needed. 5. Prove usability in one place first Use one disposable mesh/effect in a safe location with known scale and material. Verify requested versus actual transform, visibility and cleanup. Only then build a large scene or proxy assembly. This makes a material/loading problem distinguishable from an offset or occlusion problem. For new external content, continue to Meshes, materials & textures and Custom content . An FBX filename is not directly a cooked Unreal mesh reference.","sections":[{"id":"four-checks","title":"Four separate checks","text":"Check Evidence Referenced A row, thumbnail or object points at a name. Present The appropriate package appears in the installed archive. Loaded The runtime request returns the expected valid object type. Usable The asset displays or behaves correctly in the intended world. Do not collapse these into one “asset exists” flag. The custom-range work produced a card before a new playable destination; the model experiments loaded materials without establishing a correct held weapon. Those outcomes are useful precisely because their limits are separate."},{"id":"paths","title":"1. Record the exact path and type","text":"Keep the package/object path separate from its filename in an archive browser. A Blueprint class reference commonly ends with a generated class name, while a static mesh, material or sound has its own object name. Preserve case and suffixes from the actual reference. /Engine/BasicShapes/Cube.Cube /Game/VFX/MuzzleFlash/MuzzleFlash.MuzzleFlash /Game/SFX/Cues/Shots/Shot_9mm.Shot_9mm"},{"id":"load","title":"2. Load on the game thread","text":"ExecuteInGameThread(function() local mesh = LoadAsset(\"/Engine/BasicShapes/Cube.Cube\") if not mesh or not mesh:IsValid() then print(\"[MyMod] Cube asset unavailable.\\n\") return end print(\"[MyMod] Loaded \" .. mesh:GetFullName() .. \"\\n\") end LoadAsset belongs on the game thread in the API used here. StaticFindObject instead searches what is already loaded. Use a low-frequency discovery path and cache successful results; loading the same path every render tick is unnecessary work."},{"id":"validate","title":"3. Validate the returned type","text":"Before passing a result to SetStaticMesh, a material setter or a sound function, confirm it is the type that function expects. A valid UObject with a familiar name is not sufficient. The local audio code explicitly checked SoundBase compatibility before playback. If the asset type or dependency is missing, report that specific result. Do not substitute an unrelated object and let the next native call become the first diagnostic."},{"id":"cache","title":"4. Cache without hiding failures","text":"Cache an asset after a successful load. If the first attempt happens before its context is ready, distinguish “retry later” from “known unsupported path”. Keep bounded retries and an explicit error state. A permanent false cache created during early startup can make a later valid asset look unavailable forever. World-specific spawned actors are not the same as loaded content assets. Even when the asset remains loaded after travel, reacquire the current world and create new world-owned instances as needed."},{"id":"test","title":"5. Prove usability in one place first","text":"Use one disposable mesh/effect in a safe location with known scale and material. Verify requested versus actual transform, visibility and cleanup. Only then build a large scene or proxy assembly. This makes a material/loading problem distinguishable from an offset or occlusion problem. For new external content, continue to Meshes, materials & textures and Custom content . An FBX filename is not directly a cooked Unreal mesh reference."}]},{"id":"spawning","title":"Spawning & actor ownership","text":"Spawning & actor ownership Create disposable actors deliberately and clean up only what your feature owns. 1. Define what the actor represents Decide whether the new object is a cosmetic proxy, an interactive target, a physics prop or scenery. Those choices affect collision, replication, ownership and cleanup. A mesh actor with collision disabled can be useful for a tracer but is not a playable target. Start with one object. Record its intended world, class, transform and lifetime before spawning many copies. A successful SpawnActor result does not establish that the mesh, material and behaviour are all configured. 2. Acquire the current world and class -- Context: current world and position were validated on the game thread. local class = StaticFindObject(\"/Script/Engine.StaticMeshActor\") if not class or not class:IsValid() then return end local actor = world:SpawnActor(class, position, {Pitch = 0, Yaw = 0, Roll = 0}) if not actor or not actor:IsValid() then return end owned[#owned + 1] = actor -- Configure only after the actor has been tracked for cleanup. Keep the owning list reachable for the feature’s lifetime. Track the object immediately so a later Lua error while assigning a mesh does not leave an untracked actor behind. The surrounding action should clean up partial construction before reporting failure. 3. Configure one property group at a time For scenery or a visual proxy, inspect mobility, component mesh assignment, scale, material, collision and replication deliberately. Some assignments require a movable component or a particular actor class. Confirm the actual result instead of assuming every setter succeeded. Use a verified component reference such as the static-mesh actor’s StaticMeshComponent in the tested build. Do not invent an unreflected getter because the C++ engine has a similarly named method. 4. Keep engine ownership distinct from your bookkeeping The engine actor’s owner/outer/attachment relationships and your Lua owned list serve different purposes. Your list says which objects the feature is responsible for cleaning up. It does not itself attach a component, assign network authority or make a pointer safe after travel. When building an assembly, keep the parent/child relationship explicit and test what destroying a parent does to descendants. Don’t attempt to destroy every descendant twice through stale references. 5. Clean up on close, replacement and travel Remove replaced proxies before losing their handles. On feature close, destroy the valid actors you created in that world. On world change, retire the old registry; don’t reach into a new world and perform a broad class-based deletion to simulate cleanup. If you modify an existing game actor instead of spawning one, save the specific original state you intend to restore. Destroying that actor is not an appropriate general teardown for a borrowed object. Acceptance checks One actor appears at the intended transform. A configuration failure removes the partially created actor. Repeating setup does not duplicate the object. Replacing a mesh/proxy does not leak the previous actor. Closing and travelling retire the correct objects. No unrelated game actor is deleted. The local effects/proxy code used owned lists and per-world cleanup. This documents that pattern, not a guarantee every arbitrary spawned class supports the same setup sequence.","sections":[{"id":"plan","title":"1. Define what the actor represents","text":"Decide whether the new object is a cosmetic proxy, an interactive target, a physics prop or scenery. Those choices affect collision, replication, ownership and cleanup. A mesh actor with collision disabled can be useful for a tracer but is not a playable target. Start with one object. Record its intended world, class, transform and lifetime before spawning many copies. A successful SpawnActor result does not establish that the mesh, material and behaviour are all configured."},{"id":"spawn","title":"2. Acquire the current world and class","text":"-- Context: current world and position were validated on the game thread. local class = StaticFindObject(\"/Script/Engine.StaticMeshActor\") if not class or not class:IsValid() then return end local actor = world:SpawnActor(class, position, {Pitch = 0, Yaw = 0, Roll = 0}) if not actor or not actor:IsValid() then return end owned[#owned + 1] = actor -- Configure only after the actor has been tracked for cleanup. Keep the owning list reachable for the feature’s lifetime. Track the object immediately so a later Lua error while assigning a mesh does not leave an untracked actor behind. The surrounding action should clean up partial construction before reporting failure."},{"id":"configure","title":"3. Configure one property group at a time","text":"For scenery or a visual proxy, inspect mobility, component mesh assignment, scale, material, collision and replication deliberately. Some assignments require a movable component or a particular actor class. Confirm the actual result instead of assuming every setter succeeded. Use a verified component reference such as the static-mesh actor’s StaticMeshComponent in the tested build. Do not invent an unreflected getter because the C++ engine has a similarly named method."},{"id":"ownership","title":"4. Keep engine ownership distinct from your bookkeeping","text":"The engine actor’s owner/outer/attachment relationships and your Lua owned list serve different purposes. Your list says which objects the feature is responsible for cleaning up. It does not itself attach a component, assign network authority or make a pointer safe after travel. When building an assembly, keep the parent/child relationship explicit and test what destroying a parent does to descendants. Don’t attempt to destroy every descendant twice through stale references."},{"id":"cleanup","title":"5. Clean up on close, replacement and travel","text":"Remove replaced proxies before losing their handles. On feature close, destroy the valid actors you created in that world. On world change, retire the old registry; don’t reach into a new world and perform a broad class-based deletion to simulate cleanup. If you modify an existing game actor instead of spawning one, save the specific original state you intend to restore. Destroying that actor is not an appropriate general teardown for a borrowed object."},{"id":"acceptance","title":"Acceptance checks","text":"One actor appears at the intended transform. A configuration failure removes the partially created actor. Repeating setup does not duplicate the object. Replacing a mesh/proxy does not leak the previous actor. Closing and travelling retire the correct objects. No unrelated game actor is deleted. The local effects/proxy code used owned lists and per-world cleanup. This documents that pattern, not a guarantee every arbitrary spawned class supports the same setup sequence."}]},{"id":"meshes-materials","title":"Meshes, materials & textures","text":"Meshes, materials & textures Diagnose the mesh, transform, material and texture as separate parts of a visual result. 1. Split the visual problem A replacement model can be present but too small, reversed, offset, white or incorrectly lit. Treat geometry, coordinate conversion, parent transform, material assignment and texture parameters as separate checks. Fixing one does not establish the rest. Symptom First checks Wrong way around Imported basis, local rotation, attachment root. Too small or offset Units, origin, parent scale and relative transform. White or flat colour Material assignment and required texture parameters. Looks right only when static Pose/root relationship and update path. 2. Prove the mesh independently Use a known cooked mesh first and inspect its component/transform in a visible place. For procedural geometry, a triangle proves mesh-section calls can produce geometry; it does not prove a whole imported weapon has correct topology, normals, UVs, scale and attachment transforms. The recorded runtime importer experiments are API/prototype findings. A finished FBX-to-usable-gun pipeline was not validated by them. See Custom content before treating a source model as a ready Unreal asset. 3. Inspect the material slot Confirm which mesh component and material index are being changed. A weapon can have several components and child actors; setting material zero on the root does not recolour every attachment. Record the original assignment before a reversible preview. A dynamic material instance lets a feature adjust parameters without editing a shared source material object. The parameter must actually exist under the expected name. A guessed parameter name can do nothing while the instance itself is valid. 4. Check the texture parameter and UVs A local probe imported a PNG at runtime and created a dynamic material instance from a cooked material with a Tex parameter. That demonstrated the calls and parameter availability in that case, not correct mapping on every model. If the image loads but the model is white or distorted, inspect the assigned material, texture parameter, UV coordinates and the material’s expected colour/normal roles. A loaded texture object is only one stage in that chain. 5. Compare lit and diagnostic presentation An emissive material can help establish that geometry is visible, but it removes much of the lighting response you may want in the final scene. Keep that as a diagnostic result. If the final goal is a believable outdoor range, investigate the hosting world’s lighting separately. Do not compensate for a wrong material with unlimited exposure. Keep a controlled reference object and compare its appearance in the normal range and your runtime placement. Record a visual verification Mesh identity, component and material slot. Requested and actual transform, including parent/root space. Material instance and parameter names actually found. Texture load result and the expected mapping. Appearance while moving, aiming and changing context. Cleanup/restoration after closing the feature. This checklist is useful even without a new model: a remote gun proxy can fail any of the same stages while reusing only cooked game meshes.","sections":[{"id":"separate","title":"1. Split the visual problem","text":"A replacement model can be present but too small, reversed, offset, white or incorrectly lit. Treat geometry, coordinate conversion, parent transform, material assignment and texture parameters as separate checks. Fixing one does not establish the rest. Symptom First checks Wrong way around Imported basis, local rotation, attachment root. Too small or offset Units, origin, parent scale and relative transform. White or flat colour Material assignment and required texture parameters. Looks right only when static Pose/root relationship and update path."},{"id":"mesh","title":"2. Prove the mesh independently","text":"Use a known cooked mesh first and inspect its component/transform in a visible place. For procedural geometry, a triangle proves mesh-section calls can produce geometry; it does not prove a whole imported weapon has correct topology, normals, UVs, scale and attachment transforms. The recorded runtime importer experiments are API/prototype findings. A finished FBX-to-usable-gun pipeline was not validated by them. See Custom content before treating a source model as a ready Unreal asset."},{"id":"materials","title":"3. Inspect the material slot","text":"Confirm which mesh component and material index are being changed. A weapon can have several components and child actors; setting material zero on the root does not recolour every attachment. Record the original assignment before a reversible preview. A dynamic material instance lets a feature adjust parameters without editing a shared source material object. The parameter must actually exist under the expected name. A guessed parameter name can do nothing while the instance itself is valid."},{"id":"textures","title":"4. Check the texture parameter and UVs","text":"A local probe imported a PNG at runtime and created a dynamic material instance from a cooked material with a Tex parameter. That demonstrated the calls and parameter availability in that case, not correct mapping on every model. If the image loads but the model is white or distorted, inspect the assigned material, texture parameter, UV coordinates and the material’s expected colour/normal roles. A loaded texture object is only one stage in that chain."},{"id":"lighting","title":"5. Compare lit and diagnostic presentation","text":"An emissive material can help establish that geometry is visible, but it removes much of the lighting response you may want in the final scene. Keep that as a diagnostic result. If the final goal is a believable outdoor range, investigate the hosting world’s lighting separately. Do not compensate for a wrong material with unlimited exposure. Keep a controlled reference object and compare its appearance in the normal range and your runtime placement."},{"id":"verification","title":"Record a visual verification","text":"Mesh identity, component and material slot. Requested and actual transform, including parent/root space. Material instance and parameter names actually found. Texture load result and the expected mapping. Appearance while moving, aiming and changing context. Cleanup/restoration after closing the feature. This checklist is useful even without a new model: a remote gun proxy can fail any of the same stages while reusing only cooked game meshes."}]},{"id":"lighting","title":"Lighting & exposure diagnosis","text":"Lighting & exposure diagnosis Use controlled observations to separate invisible geometry from a dark rendering environment. 1. Establish a visible reference Enter the original working range and record a known object’s appearance, camera location and material. Then create one simple reference object in the runtime scene at a confirmed visible transform. This gives you something to compare besides an entire dark apartment block. Keep world, material and object placement fixed while changing one lighting-related variable. A different scene location behind an indoor wall is not a fair exposure comparison. 2. Rule out placement and occlusion Log player position, requested/actual object position and facing. Check whether a wall or enclosing pool geometry blocks the line of sight. Temporarily placing one diagnostic object closer can establish visibility without building a whole map. A successful spawn log does not guarantee the object is in the view. Conversely, a visible dark silhouette proves some geometry rendered; it does not prove that view distance is the main problem. 3. Compare material response Try a known cooked material on one diagnostic object and inspect its response. An emissive diagnostic can distinguish a lighting/material issue from total absence, but it is not evidence that the intended lit material now works. The local custom-range experiments produced dark silhouettes despite geometry appearing. Several light/exposure changes did not yield a finished acceptable scene. That is an unresolved rendering/layout result, not a completed outdoor-lighting recipe. 4. Verify the component you changed A light actor existing does not prove the intended component was resolved and configured. Check the actual light/skylight component, mobility, world context and relevant property types. A call made against the wrong or unavailable component can leave the scene unchanged. Likewise, confirm the postprocess volume or camera setting affecting the current view. Reading or setting a property elsewhere in the world does not establish that it contributes to this camera’s final image. 5. Keep an experiment log World and camera: Reference object and material: Object requested / actual transform: Changed light or exposure component: Property and original / new value: Observed reference-object result: What stayed unchanged: Restoration result: Capture screenshots from comparable positions. When an experiment has no visible effect, record that fact and narrow the cause rather than repeatedly raising unrelated settings. When to stop calling it a lighting fix A scene is not finished because an API call returned or one emissive panel became visible. Verify the desired layout, readable targets, player view, material response and return flow. For a true new map, the cooked-content pipeline is a separate requirement. See Plan a runtime range .","sections":[{"id":"baseline","title":"1. Establish a visible reference","text":"Enter the original working range and record a known object’s appearance, camera location and material. Then create one simple reference object in the runtime scene at a confirmed visible transform. This gives you something to compare besides an entire dark apartment block. Keep world, material and object placement fixed while changing one lighting-related variable. A different scene location behind an indoor wall is not a fair exposure comparison."},{"id":"visibility","title":"2. Rule out placement and occlusion","text":"Log player position, requested/actual object position and facing. Check whether a wall or enclosing pool geometry blocks the line of sight. Temporarily placing one diagnostic object closer can establish visibility without building a whole map. A successful spawn log does not guarantee the object is in the view. Conversely, a visible dark silhouette proves some geometry rendered; it does not prove that view distance is the main problem."},{"id":"materials","title":"3. Compare material response","text":"Try a known cooked material on one diagnostic object and inspect its response. An emissive diagnostic can distinguish a lighting/material issue from total absence, but it is not evidence that the intended lit material now works. The local custom-range experiments produced dark silhouettes despite geometry appearing. Several light/exposure changes did not yield a finished acceptable scene. That is an unresolved rendering/layout result, not a completed outdoor-lighting recipe."},{"id":"components","title":"4. Verify the component you changed","text":"A light actor existing does not prove the intended component was resolved and configured. Check the actual light/skylight component, mobility, world context and relevant property types. A call made against the wrong or unavailable component can leave the scene unchanged. Likewise, confirm the postprocess volume or camera setting affecting the current view. Reading or setting a property elsewhere in the world does not establish that it contributes to this camera’s final image."},{"id":"experiment","title":"5. Keep an experiment log","text":"World and camera: Reference object and material: Object requested / actual transform: Changed light or exposure component: Property and original / new value: Observed reference-object result: What stayed unchanged: Restoration result: Capture screenshots from comparable positions. When an experiment has no visible effect, record that fact and narrow the cause rather than repeatedly raising unrelated settings."},{"id":"limit","title":"When to stop calling it a lighting fix","text":"A scene is not finished because an API call returned or one emissive panel became visible. Verify the desired layout, readable targets, player view, material response and return flow. For a true new map, the cooked-content pipeline is a separate requirement. See Plan a runtime range ."}]},{"id":"physics","title":"Collision & physics","text":"Collision & physics Keep actor motion, component physics, collision and target scoring as separate states. 1. Identify the moving body A target’s mesh component can simulate physics while the actor root remains near its original transform. Inspect both before building a motion overlay, reset button or replication scheme. Selecting the first primitive component can return a trigger or collider rather than the visible body. Use target discovery to record the actual body component and its class. Keep per-class selection explicit when different targets use different structures. 2. Record collision separately from visibility Hiding an actor does not by itself establish the collision state your mode needs. A broken target may need both hidden and collision changes, while a cosmetic tracer should generally have no gameplay collision at all. Before a reversible preview modifies a game actor, record its original hidden/collision/tick/physics values. Restore the observed values rather than assuming every actor should finish visible, ticking and simulating physics. 3. Apply impulses only in a controlled action The local target-effects code used AddImpulseAtLocation on a physics body after validating target state and hit geometry. Its impulse scaling was a mod policy, not a recovered physical formula for every base-game impact. local function boundedDirection(from, to) local x, y, z = to.X-from.X, to.Y-from.Y, to.Z-from.Z local length = math.sqrt(x*x+y*y+z*z) if length \u003c 0.001 then return nil end return {X=x/length, Y=y/length, Z=z/length} end A zero-length vector must not be normalised by dividing by zero. Keep the requested magnitude bounded and explain its units/policy rather than sending arbitrary peer-supplied impulse values to the engine. 4. Treat player recovery as another operation Moving a stuck player back to spawn is not the same action as resetting a target mesh. The local recovery path stopped movement, used a recorded spawn transform, checked teleport success and requested a net update under host authority. A failed teleport because the destination is obstructed should produce a recovery message or range-restart option. Repeating it invisibly every tick does not make the spawn safe. 5. Test reset on several body states Try an upright target, a fallen physics target, an already-hit one-shot target and a broken/hidden target. A successful transform assignment on one does not recover all of the others. Some classes can carry additional state beyond the flags you have observed. When a complete reset is not understood, expose a clearly defined range restart rather than a partial reset labelled as universal. Keep score reset and physical restoration as separate mode decisions. Networked motion Apply authoritative target state and render motion without re-triggering rewards. For physics bodies, decide whether remote clients simulate or follow snapshots; two competing simulations can diverge. Restore borrowed body state when leaving the feature and discard old-world references after travel.","sections":[{"id":"separate","title":"1. Identify the moving body","text":"A target’s mesh component can simulate physics while the actor root remains near its original transform. Inspect both before building a motion overlay, reset button or replication scheme. Selecting the first primitive component can return a trigger or collider rather than the visible body. Use target discovery to record the actual body component and its class. Keep per-class selection explicit when different targets use different structures."},{"id":"collision","title":"2. Record collision separately from visibility","text":"Hiding an actor does not by itself establish the collision state your mode needs. A broken target may need both hidden and collision changes, while a cosmetic tracer should generally have no gameplay collision at all. Before a reversible preview modifies a game actor, record its original hidden/collision/tick/physics values. Restore the observed values rather than assuming every actor should finish visible, ticking and simulating physics."},{"id":"impulse","title":"3. Apply impulses only in a controlled action","text":"The local target-effects code used AddImpulseAtLocation on a physics body after validating target state and hit geometry. Its impulse scaling was a mod policy, not a recovered physical formula for every base-game impact. local function boundedDirection(from, to) local x, y, z = to.X-from.X, to.Y-from.Y, to.Z-from.Z local length = math.sqrt(x*x+y*y+z*z) if length \u003c 0.001 then return nil end return {X=x/length, Y=y/length, Z=z/length} end A zero-length vector must not be normalised by dividing by zero. Keep the requested magnitude bounded and explain its units/policy rather than sending arbitrary peer-supplied impulse values to the engine."},{"id":"teleport","title":"4. Treat player recovery as another operation","text":"Moving a stuck player back to spawn is not the same action as resetting a target mesh. The local recovery path stopped movement, used a recorded spawn transform, checked teleport success and requested a net update under host authority. A failed teleport because the destination is obstructed should produce a recovery message or range-restart option. Repeating it invisibly every tick does not make the spawn safe."},{"id":"reset","title":"5. Test reset on several body states","text":"Try an upright target, a fallen physics target, an already-hit one-shot target and a broken/hidden target. A successful transform assignment on one does not recover all of the others. Some classes can carry additional state beyond the flags you have observed. When a complete reset is not understood, expose a clearly defined range restart rather than a partial reset labelled as universal. Keep score reset and physical restoration as separate mode decisions."},{"id":"network","title":"Networked motion","text":"Apply authoritative target state and render motion without re-triggering rewards. For physics bodies, decide whether remote clients simulate or follow snapshots; two competing simulations can diverge. Restore borrowed body state when leaving the feature and discard old-world references after travel."}]},{"id":"audio","title":"Weapon audio & spatial sound","text":"Weapon audio & spatial sound Reuse verified cooked cues and separate remote cosmetic sound from local weapon simulation. 1. Inventory actual sound assets The local code used cooked shot cues under /Game/SFX/Cues/Shots and handling cues under /Game/SFX/Cues/Manipulation/Generic. Native checks resolved the selected sound assets and exercised spatial playback calls. That does not establish the exact acoustic result for every gun and distance. Cue example Folder Shot_9mm / Shot_556mm / Shot_AirPellet Shots Shot_Supressed_S / Shot_Supressed_M / Shot_Supressed_L Shots; preserve the asset’s spelling. Release_mag_rifle_Cue / Insert_mag_rifle_Cue Manipulation/Generic Release_mag_pistol_Cue / Insert_mag_pistol_Cue Manipulation/Generic 2. Load and validate before playing Load on the game thread and confirm SoundBase compatibility in the tested binding. Keep a bounded allowlist of supported cue identifiers. A network peer should choose an ID from that list, not an arbitrary path to load. local sound = LoadAsset(\"/Game/SFX/Cues/Shots/Shot_9mm.Shot_9mm\") local base = StaticFindObject(\"/Script/Engine.SoundBase\") if not sound or not sound:IsValid() or not base or not base:IsValid() or not sound:IsA(base) then return end 3. Use the right source position For a remote shot, use its captured muzzle/weapon position rather than the listener’s camera position. For a UI confirmation, world spatialisation may not be the intended presentation. Keep sound policy tied to the event type. The local implementation passed a known shot attenuation asset to PlaySoundAtLocation. Inspect the reflected signature for your build before copying a long parameter list. A valid cue with inappropriate attenuation can sound wrong even though the function call succeeds. 4. Document classification as a heuristic The local selector used cartridge/weapon names, suppressor-related information and black-powder state to choose among a small cue list. That is a practical cosmetic mapping, not proof that it reproduces every weapon’s true base-game sound selection. If the cue mapping is unknown, use a deliberate fallback and keep the diagnostic visible. Do not fabricate a precise per-calibre audio claim from one matching substring. 5. Avoid duplicate playback The firing player already hears the normal local weapon sound. A remote cosmetic event should not blindly play it again on the sender. Retransmitted reliable events also need deduplication so one shot does not produce several echoes from repeated packets. Reload sounds should follow actual magazine/handling events rather than an assumed fixed animation timer where possible. Empty, cancelled or different reload paths can have different event sequences. Listening test matrix Local shot without remote replay remains unchanged. Remote shot plays once at the remote source position. Distance and attenuation behave acceptably. Suppressed/black-powder/fallback choices are documented. Magazine release/insertion are not confused. Mute/volume settings and travel cleanup work. Use actual listening checks in addition to asset-resolution logs. For network events, see Messages & reliable events .","sections":[{"id":"inventory","title":"1. Inventory actual sound assets","text":"The local code used cooked shot cues under /Game/SFX/Cues/Shots and handling cues under /Game/SFX/Cues/Manipulation/Generic. Native checks resolved the selected sound assets and exercised spatial playback calls. That does not establish the exact acoustic result for every gun and distance. Cue example Folder Shot_9mm / Shot_556mm / Shot_AirPellet Shots Shot_Supressed_S / Shot_Supressed_M / Shot_Supressed_L Shots; preserve the asset’s spelling. Release_mag_rifle_Cue / Insert_mag_rifle_Cue Manipulation/Generic Release_mag_pistol_Cue / Insert_mag_pistol_Cue Manipulation/Generic"},{"id":"load","title":"2. Load and validate before playing","text":"Load on the game thread and confirm SoundBase compatibility in the tested binding. Keep a bounded allowlist of supported cue identifiers. A network peer should choose an ID from that list, not an arbitrary path to load. local sound = LoadAsset(\"/Game/SFX/Cues/Shots/Shot_9mm.Shot_9mm\") local base = StaticFindObject(\"/Script/Engine.SoundBase\") if not sound or not sound:IsValid() or not base or not base:IsValid() or not sound:IsA(base) then return end"},{"id":"position","title":"3. Use the right source position","text":"For a remote shot, use its captured muzzle/weapon position rather than the listener’s camera position. For a UI confirmation, world spatialisation may not be the intended presentation. Keep sound policy tied to the event type. The local implementation passed a known shot attenuation asset to PlaySoundAtLocation. Inspect the reflected signature for your build before copying a long parameter list. A valid cue with inappropriate attenuation can sound wrong even though the function call succeeds."},{"id":"classification","title":"4. Document classification as a heuristic","text":"The local selector used cartridge/weapon names, suppressor-related information and black-powder state to choose among a small cue list. That is a practical cosmetic mapping, not proof that it reproduces every weapon’s true base-game sound selection. If the cue mapping is unknown, use a deliberate fallback and keep the diagnostic visible. Do not fabricate a precise per-calibre audio claim from one matching substring."},{"id":"duplicates","title":"5. Avoid duplicate playback","text":"The firing player already hears the normal local weapon sound. A remote cosmetic event should not blindly play it again on the sender. Retransmitted reliable events also need deduplication so one shot does not produce several echoes from repeated packets. Reload sounds should follow actual magazine/handling events rather than an assumed fixed animation timer where possible. Empty, cancelled or different reload paths can have different event sequences."},{"id":"test","title":"Listening test matrix","text":"Local shot without remote replay remains unchanged. Remote shot plays once at the remote source position. Distance and attenuation behave acceptably. Suppressed/black-powder/fallback choices are documented. Magazine release/insertion are not confused. Mute/volume settings and travel cleanup work. Use actual listening checks in addition to asset-resolution logs. For network events, see Messages & reliable events ."}]},{"id":"effects","title":"Muzzle flashes, tracers & impacts","text":"Muzzle flashes, tracers & impacts Build bounded cosmetic effects without confusing them with the actual ballistic simulation. 1. Map an effect to a specific event A muzzle flash belongs to a shot creation event. An impact belongs to an actual impact location. A straight cosmetic tracer may start with a camera/muzzle approximation and be corrected when the impact arrives. None of those establishes the full simulated bullet trajectory. Effect Source data Muzzle flash Shot ID, source position and direction. Tracer Source and selected endpoint, plus a short lifetime. Impact Actual impact location and a verified material/effect policy. Handling cue The relevant aiming/reload/weapon state event. 2. Use known cooked systems first The local implementation requested /Game/VFX/MuzzleFlash/MuzzleFlash.MuzzleFlash and /Game/VFX/Impacts/Impact_Concrete.Impact_Concrete through the Niagara function library. The concrete effect was a chosen cosmetic fallback, not universal material detection. Verify that the asset is available in the current build and check a single visible effect before adding network playback. A successful load alone does not prove the effect appears in the intended view or has an acceptable lifetime. 3. Track each owned component or actor Store what you spawned, whether cleanup requires actor destruction or component destruction, and its expiry time in the current world’s clock. Use a cap on live cosmetic objects. A missed impact packet must not leave a permanent beam in the range. local effect = {object = component, expiresAt = now + 0.5, worldId = currentWorldId} -- Store this in your feature-owned registry. -- Recheck identity/validity and destroy through the correct API at expiry. 4. Calibrate a simple beam carefully A local visual tracer used a thin cooked cube mesh aligned between two points, with an emissive material and collision disabled. Its length-to-scale conversion assumed that specific mesh’s dimensions. Don’t reuse the same divisor for an unrelated model without checking bounds. Validate finite endpoints, a non-zero length and a maximum reasonable distance. Keep visual-only collision and replication settings explicit so the tracer does not block shots or become another authoritative gameplay actor. 5. Correct and deduplicate Keep a shot-to-effect mapping. When the actual impact arrives, replace or adjust the corresponding temporary beam rather than spawning several unrelated permanent lines. Retire the mapping after a bounded period so it cannot grow forever. Deduplicate a resent shot/impact event before playing another effect. Cosmetic retries and gameplay score retries need consistent IDs, but effect display does not itself award points. Verify motion and teardown Test a close shot, a distant shot, a missed impact, rapid fire up to the object cap, repeated packets and travel during an effect. Check that every actor/component your feature owns is eventually retired. Then verify on both real peers; a local effect fixture alone does not demonstrate delivery or timing over Steam.","sections":[{"id":"event-map","title":"1. Map an effect to a specific event","text":"A muzzle flash belongs to a shot creation event. An impact belongs to an actual impact location. A straight cosmetic tracer may start with a camera/muzzle approximation and be corrected when the impact arrives. None of those establishes the full simulated bullet trajectory. Effect Source data Muzzle flash Shot ID, source position and direction. Tracer Source and selected endpoint, plus a short lifetime. Impact Actual impact location and a verified material/effect policy. Handling cue The relevant aiming/reload/weapon state event."},{"id":"assets","title":"2. Use known cooked systems first","text":"The local implementation requested /Game/VFX/MuzzleFlash/MuzzleFlash.MuzzleFlash and /Game/VFX/Impacts/Impact_Concrete.Impact_Concrete through the Niagara function library. The concrete effect was a chosen cosmetic fallback, not universal material detection. Verify that the asset is available in the current build and check a single visible effect before adding network playback. A successful load alone does not prove the effect appears in the intended view or has an acceptable lifetime."},{"id":"lifetime","title":"3. Track each owned component or actor","text":"Store what you spawned, whether cleanup requires actor destruction or component destruction, and its expiry time in the current world’s clock. Use a cap on live cosmetic objects. A missed impact packet must not leave a permanent beam in the range. local effect = {object = component, expiresAt = now + 0.5, worldId = currentWorldId} -- Store this in your feature-owned registry. -- Recheck identity/validity and destroy through the correct API at expiry."},{"id":"beam","title":"4. Calibrate a simple beam carefully","text":"A local visual tracer used a thin cooked cube mesh aligned between two points, with an emissive material and collision disabled. Its length-to-scale conversion assumed that specific mesh’s dimensions. Don’t reuse the same divisor for an unrelated model without checking bounds. Validate finite endpoints, a non-zero length and a maximum reasonable distance. Keep visual-only collision and replication settings explicit so the tracer does not block shots or become another authoritative gameplay actor."},{"id":"correction","title":"5. Correct and deduplicate","text":"Keep a shot-to-effect mapping. When the actual impact arrives, replace or adjust the corresponding temporary beam rather than spawning several unrelated permanent lines. Retire the mapping after a bounded period so it cannot grow forever. Deduplicate a resent shot/impact event before playing another effect. Cosmetic retries and gameplay score retries need consistent IDs, but effect display does not itself award points."},{"id":"verification","title":"Verify motion and teardown","text":"Test a close shot, a distant shot, a missed impact, rapid fire up to the object cap, repeated packets and travel during an effect. Check that every actor/component your feature owns is eventually retired. Then verify on both real peers; a local effect fixture alone does not demonstrate delivery or timing over Steam."}]},{"id":"multiplayer","title":"Multiplayer architecture","text":"Multiplayer architecture Connecting two players is the beginning. Shared game state still needs explicit rules. Connection isn’t replication A Steam session or invitation identifies and connects peers. It does not automatically make every single-player actor, UI action, weapon part or score replicate. The local multiplayer implementation combines a listen-server travel path with a native Steam bridge and custom state messages. That architecture is a mod implementation, not an official multiplayer API supplied by this wiki. Separate state from events Send as state Send as events Player pose, aim and equipped appearance A shot, a hit or a chat message. Target pose and current hit state A round-start or recovery request. Current round phase and scores A one-time save offer or acceptance. Snapshots can supersede older snapshots. One-time events need IDs, acknowledgement and duplicate handling. Replaying the same acknowledged hit must not award its points again. Authority and validation Choose who decides travel, target state and scoring. A host-authoritative mode should validate the sender, session, round, target identity and bounded event values before applying a guest request. Never accept arbitrary asset paths, file paths or Lua source from a network message. Use explicit allowlists and fixed save slots where appropriate. Movement and visible guns Render remote movement from buffered snapshots rather than snapping directly to each packet. Reset the buffer for teleport or world changes. Interpolation hides normal packet gaps; it does not fix wrong authority or missing state. Remote weapon appearance can be reconstructed from mesh references and relative transforms. Reuse unchanged meshes and clean up replaced ones. A cosmetic proxy is not another fully simulated gun actor. Interruptions and testing Give every session and world generation a distinct identity. Discard stale packets after reconnect or travel. Cancel active rounds when a player disconnects, and recover through an explicit ready/start flow. Test on two PCs/accounts: simultaneous shots, the same target hit by both peers, gun switching, range restart, repeated travel and a disconnect near round end. A local simulated-peer test cannot establish real network behaviour under delay. Step-by-step development order 1. Prove connection and identity Use two PCs and separate accounts. Confirm who is host, who is guest and which session each belongs to. Log session identity and protocol version in diagnostics without exposing private IDs in public reports. A successful invitation is a connection milestone. It does not prove that range travel, guns, targets or progression are shared. 2. Build a stable roster Resolve current player states, controller/pawn links and display names. Rebuild those references after travel. Keep local/remote role distinct from list order so sorting the UI does not swap ownership. 3. Share appearance and pose Send bounded state snapshots with sequence and session information. Buffer remote poses for interpolation, reject old generations and reset on teleport. Separate cosmetic pose cadence from reliable gameplay events. A proxy model and nameplate can provide useful presence while the game’s real remote pawn has a different replication path. Document which object actually moves and which object is cosmetic. 4. Move both players through a known range Have the host select an allowlisted destination, require the needed readiness, perform the correct shared travel operation and wait for both arrivals. Reacquire weapons and targets only in the new world. Verify both local usable guns and remote visible guns; either can be missing independently. 5. Add reliable gameplay events Give shots, hits and round actions explicit IDs. Keep an acknowledgement/deduplication policy so a retry is safe. Bound the queue and define what happens when it is full rather than endlessly allocating memory or silently losing scoring events. 6. Make one accepted result authoritative For a host-authoritative challenge, validate guest events against the current world/round and known target registry, then publish the accepted state. Cosmetic impact playback should not invoke the game’s reward path again. Validate asset identifiers against a narrow known set. Never interpret a received string as arbitrary Lua, a filesystem path to overwrite or a map to travel to. 7. Handle interruption before adding more modes Disconnect, reconnect, host travel and save transfer can interrupt a round. Retire old session/world generations, explain the cancellation, and clear stale pending actions. A reconnect should not replay last session’s hit queue into a new round. 8. Test the real peer path Repeat simultaneous firing, same-target hits, gun switching, target motion, restart and several travel cycles. Add delay and loss experiments when possible, but keep them distinct from the actual internet session result. Two windows in one process or a simulated peer are not two independent clients. Fields worth designing explicitly Field Purpose Protocol version Reject incompatible packet formats. Session / peer generation Discard an old connection’s data. World / travel generation Avoid applying a previous range’s objects. Round ID Keep events attached to the correct challenge. Sequence / acknowledgement Order replaceable state and retire reliable events. Event ID / target ID Deduplicate actions and resolve game entities without remote pointers. Keep save sharing separate Live gameplay replication and host-save snapshot transfer have different consent, integrity and recovery requirements. The save flow should pause conflicting travel/actions and require a separate apply confirmation. Do not make joining a lobby silently overwrite a guest’s progression. Write a release claim that matches the test “Both players connected and saw models” is a useful result. It is not evidence that both could fire simultaneously, score consistently under delay or restore saves after disconnect. Publish the supported scope and the checks performed, and keep unverified later scenarios distinct from earlier user-confirmed connection. What was checked Local transport fixtures, actor rendering and native gameplay checks exist; the user previously confirmed basic two-PC connection. Later simultaneous shooting, save application and delay-fairness scenarios were not verified in that recorded pass. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build.","sections":[{"id":"connection-is-not-replication","title":"Connection isn’t replication","text":"A Steam session or invitation identifies and connects peers. It does not automatically make every single-player actor, UI action, weapon part or score replicate. The local multiplayer implementation combines a listen-server travel path with a native Steam bridge and custom state messages. That architecture is a mod implementation, not an official multiplayer API supplied by this wiki."},{"id":"state-and-events","title":"Separate state from events","text":"Send as state Send as events Player pose, aim and equipped appearance A shot, a hit or a chat message. Target pose and current hit state A round-start or recovery request. Current round phase and scores A one-time save offer or acceptance. Snapshots can supersede older snapshots. One-time events need IDs, acknowledgement and duplicate handling. Replaying the same acknowledged hit must not award its points again."},{"id":"authority-and-validation","title":"Authority and validation","text":"Choose who decides travel, target state and scoring. A host-authoritative mode should validate the sender, session, round, target identity and bounded event values before applying a guest request. Never accept arbitrary asset paths, file paths or Lua source from a network message. Use explicit allowlists and fixed save slots where appropriate."},{"id":"movement-and-visible-guns","title":"Movement and visible guns","text":"Render remote movement from buffered snapshots rather than snapping directly to each packet. Reset the buffer for teleport or world changes. Interpolation hides normal packet gaps; it does not fix wrong authority or missing state. Remote weapon appearance can be reconstructed from mesh references and relative transforms. Reuse unchanged meshes and clean up replaced ones. A cosmetic proxy is not another fully simulated gun actor."},{"id":"interruptions-and-testing","title":"Interruptions and testing","text":"Give every session and world generation a distinct identity. Discard stale packets after reconnect or travel. Cancel active rounds when a player disconnects, and recover through an explicit ready/start flow. Test on two PCs/accounts: simultaneous shots, the same target hit by both peers, gun switching, range restart, repeated travel and a disconnect near round end. A local simulated-peer test cannot establish real network behaviour under delay."},{"id":"multiplayer-development-order","title":"Step-by-step development order","text":""},{"id":"mp-step-connection","title":"1. Prove connection and identity","text":"Use two PCs and separate accounts. Confirm who is host, who is guest and which session each belongs to. Log session identity and protocol version in diagnostics without exposing private IDs in public reports. A successful invitation is a connection milestone. It does not prove that range travel, guns, targets or progression are shared."},{"id":"mp-step-roster","title":"2. Build a stable roster","text":"Resolve current player states, controller/pawn links and display names. Rebuild those references after travel. Keep local/remote role distinct from list order so sorting the UI does not swap ownership."},{"id":"mp-step-pose","title":"3. Share appearance and pose","text":"Send bounded state snapshots with sequence and session information. Buffer remote poses for interpolation, reject old generations and reset on teleport. Separate cosmetic pose cadence from reliable gameplay events. A proxy model and nameplate can provide useful presence while the game’s real remote pawn has a different replication path. Document which object actually moves and which object is cosmetic."},{"id":"mp-step-travel","title":"4. Move both players through a known range","text":"Have the host select an allowlisted destination, require the needed readiness, perform the correct shared travel operation and wait for both arrivals. Reacquire weapons and targets only in the new world. Verify both local usable guns and remote visible guns; either can be missing independently."},{"id":"mp-step-events","title":"5. Add reliable gameplay events","text":"Give shots, hits and round actions explicit IDs. Keep an acknowledgement/deduplication policy so a retry is safe. Bound the queue and define what happens when it is full rather than endlessly allocating memory or silently losing scoring events."},{"id":"mp-step-authority","title":"6. Make one accepted result authoritative","text":"For a host-authoritative challenge, validate guest events against the current world/round and known target registry, then publish the accepted state. Cosmetic impact playback should not invoke the game’s reward path again. Validate asset identifiers against a narrow known set. Never interpret a received string as arbitrary Lua, a filesystem path to overwrite or a map to travel to."},{"id":"mp-step-interrupt","title":"7. Handle interruption before adding more modes","text":"Disconnect, reconnect, host travel and save transfer can interrupt a round. Retire old session/world generations, explain the cancellation, and clear stale pending actions. A reconnect should not replay last session’s hit queue into a new round."},{"id":"mp-step-real-tests","title":"8. Test the real peer path","text":"Repeat simultaneous firing, same-target hits, gun switching, target motion, restart and several travel cycles. Add delay and loss experiments when possible, but keep them distinct from the actual internet session result. Two windows in one process or a simulated peer are not two independent clients."},{"id":"mp-packet-fields","title":"Fields worth designing explicitly","text":"Field Purpose Protocol version Reject incompatible packet formats. Session / peer generation Discard an old connection’s data. World / travel generation Avoid applying a previous range’s objects. Round ID Keep events attached to the correct challenge. Sequence / acknowledgement Order replaceable state and retire reliable events. Event ID / target ID Deduplicate actions and resolve game entities without remote pointers."},{"id":"mp-save-separation","title":"Keep save sharing separate","text":"Live gameplay replication and host-save snapshot transfer have different consent, integrity and recovery requirements. The save flow should pause conflicting travel/actions and require a separate apply confirmation. Do not make joining a lobby silently overwrite a guest’s progression."},{"id":"mp-release-claim","title":"Write a release claim that matches the test","text":"“Both players connected and saw models” is a useful result. It is not evidence that both could fire simultaneously, score consistently under delay or restore saves after disconnect. Publish the supported scope and the checks performed, and keep unverified later scenarios distinct from earlier user-confirmed connection."},{"id":"tested-scope","title":"What was checked","text":"Local transport fixtures, actor rendering and native gameplay checks exist; the user previously confirmed basic two-PC connection. Later simultaneous shooting, save application and delay-fairness scenarios were not verified in that recorded pass. The examples describe the local Windows demo setup. See Checking game updates before using them on another build."}]},{"id":"steam","title":"Steam sessions & invites","text":"Steam sessions & invites Understand sessions, invitations, transport and gameplay authority as separate layers. 1. Separate four layers Layer What it establishes Steam identity / invite Who is being invited and how a join request is delivered. Session / connection Which peers belong to the active lobby/driver. Transport How messages move between peers. Gameplay protocol What a received message means and who may apply it. Valve’s Steam Networking documentation describes messaging/sockets APIs and relay or direct connectivity where appropriate. That transport does not automatically implement your game’s target state, scoring, interpolation or save rules. Do not describe every session as gameplay running on a Valve-hosted game server. 2. Check the actual game/loader integration The local project uses the game’s SteamSocketsNetDriver path together with a mod-owned native bridge. Its Lua code validates the available driver definition, remembers the previous configuration and has a restoration path. That is a particular implementation, not a universal one-line method for turning every Unreal game into multiplayer. Before changing a driver definition, record the original value and confirm the expected reflected structure exists. A game update can change what is exposed. Don’t overwrite arbitrary networking settings merely because a class name appeared in another game’s guide. 3. Bind an invite to a valid join state The local invite helper loads a matching native DLL from its mod-owned directory, checks a bounded status result and advertises joining only in the appropriate hosting state. The Lua and native pieces need compatible exports/versions. An invite accepted while the host is no longer accepting players should fail or explain the new state cleanly. Old advertised join data must be cleared when hosting ends; otherwise a friend can enter a stale flow long after the UI says the session closed. 4. Preserve identifiers accurately Keep Steam account identifiers as validated strings when passing them through formats that may not preserve all 64-bit integer values. Display names are not connection IDs. A syntax-shaped number alone also does not establish that the sender is an authorised member of the current session. Bind connection, protocol version, peer identity and session generation together. Retire the previous generation on reconnect so a late packet cannot become a valid event for the new session. 5. Observe the connection you actually have A relay-capable API does not eliminate delay, jitter, loss or application bugs. The route and connection quality need runtime observation. Keep transport status and gameplay readiness as different diagnostics so “connected” does not imply both target registries are synchronised. Avoid promising a particular route or latency from a successful invite screenshot. Confirm both PCs/accounts, the driver/session state and the real feature sequence. The local records establish basic user-confirmed connection and narrower later local test results, not every later internet scenario. A minimum invite/connection matrix Host advertises a session only while joinable. Guest accepts while the host is ready. Guest accepts a stale invite after hosting ended. Disconnect/reconnect creates a new session generation. Range travel preserves or re-establishes the intended connection. Wrong protocol/native bridge versions produce a clear incompatibility result. Continue with Messages & reliable events for gameplay meaning and Multiplayer architecture for the development order.","sections":[{"id":"layers","title":"1. Separate four layers","text":"Layer What it establishes Steam identity / invite Who is being invited and how a join request is delivered. Session / connection Which peers belong to the active lobby/driver. Transport How messages move between peers. Gameplay protocol What a received message means and who may apply it. Valve’s Steam Networking documentation describes messaging/sockets APIs and relay or direct connectivity where appropriate. That transport does not automatically implement your game’s target state, scoring, interpolation or save rules. Do not describe every session as gameplay running on a Valve-hosted game server."},{"id":"local-context","title":"2. Check the actual game/loader integration","text":"The local project uses the game’s SteamSocketsNetDriver path together with a mod-owned native bridge. Its Lua code validates the available driver definition, remembers the previous configuration and has a restoration path. That is a particular implementation, not a universal one-line method for turning every Unreal game into multiplayer. Before changing a driver definition, record the original value and confirm the expected reflected structure exists. A game update can change what is exposed. Don’t overwrite arbitrary networking settings merely because a class name appeared in another game’s guide."},{"id":"invite","title":"3. Bind an invite to a valid join state","text":"The local invite helper loads a matching native DLL from its mod-owned directory, checks a bounded status result and advertises joining only in the appropriate hosting state. The Lua and native pieces need compatible exports/versions. An invite accepted while the host is no longer accepting players should fail or explain the new state cleanly. Old advertised join data must be cleared when hosting ends; otherwise a friend can enter a stale flow long after the UI says the session closed."},{"id":"identifiers","title":"4. Preserve identifiers accurately","text":"Keep Steam account identifiers as validated strings when passing them through formats that may not preserve all 64-bit integer values. Display names are not connection IDs. A syntax-shaped number alone also does not establish that the sender is an authorised member of the current session. Bind connection, protocol version, peer identity and session generation together. Retire the previous generation on reconnect so a late packet cannot become a valid event for the new session."},{"id":"transport-result","title":"5. Observe the connection you actually have","text":"A relay-capable API does not eliminate delay, jitter, loss or application bugs. The route and connection quality need runtime observation. Keep transport status and gameplay readiness as different diagnostics so “connected” does not imply both target registries are synchronised. Avoid promising a particular route or latency from a successful invite screenshot. Confirm both PCs/accounts, the driver/session state and the real feature sequence. The local records establish basic user-confirmed connection and narrower later local test results, not every later internet scenario."},{"id":"test","title":"A minimum invite/connection matrix","text":"Host advertises a session only while joinable. Guest accepts while the host is ready. Guest accepts a stale invite after hosting ended. Disconnect/reconnect creates a new session generation. Range travel preserves or re-establishes the intended connection. Wrong protocol/native bridge versions produce a clear incompatibility result. Continue with Messages & reliable events for gameplay meaning and Multiplayer architecture for the development order."}]},{"id":"network-protocol","title":"Messages & reliable events","text":"Messages & reliable events Design bounded messages, revisions, acknowledgements and safe event retries. 1. Separate replaceable state from one-time events A new pose can supersede an older pose. A shot, chat message or accepted hit must not be silently replaced by the next packet. Model those as different streams with different retry and deduplication rules. The local implementation sends revisioned appearance/state plus acknowledged event streams. Sending everything every tick without identity can appear to work locally while duplicating rewards or losing gun changes under packet loss. 2. Define byte framing before decoding fields Length-prefixed fields permit text to contain delimiter characters without guessing where a field ends. Count bytes consistently at both ends. Reject an invalid prefix, over-limit length, truncated body, forbidden bytes and unexpected trailing data before touching the engine. local function decodeOneField(payload, limit) if type(payload) ~= \"string\" or #payload > limit + 6 then return nil end local colon = payload:find(\":\", 1, true) if not colon or colon > 6 then return nil end local digits = payload:sub(1, colon - 1) if not digits:match(\"^%d+$\") then return nil end local count = tonumber(digits) if count > limit then return nil end local body = payload:sub(colon + 1) if #body ~= count or body:find(\"\\0\", 1, true) then return nil end return body end A real envelope also needs a version/magic value, the required field count and per-field validation. Do not accept extra fields quietly unless the protocol explicitly defines forward compatibility. 3. Distinguish sequence and content revision A snapshot sequence orders snapshots. A gear revision identifies a changed weapon appearance. An acknowledgement says which content/event was accepted. Keeping those concepts separate lets unchanged large gear data stay out of routine pose packets while still retrying a lost gear update. If sequence numbers wrap, define a bounded modular “newer” comparison. A simple numerical greater-than test stops working at the wrap boundary. Test duplicate, older and half-range ambiguous inputs explicitly. 4. Make event application idempotent Give each event an ID within a session/round generation. Apply the next accepted event once, then acknowledge it. A repeated event should confirm the already accepted result without awarding points or playing effects again. If an ordered event fails validation or application, decide whether the stream rejects/cancels or waits for recovery. Do not advance acknowledgement past work you did not apply, and do not let an invalid head event block the queue forever without a visible error policy. 5. Bound queues and values Set maximum payload size, field sizes, pending events, history and retained generations. Validate numeric ranges, finite coordinates, allowed message kinds and known entity IDs. A decoded string is not yet an authorised gameplay action. When backpressure occurs, show the user that the connection is catching up or reject the new action. Silently dropping a scored hit is not equivalent to dropping an obsolete cosmetic pose. 6. Validate the sender and context Bind acknowledgements to the peer/session they belong to. A forged or stale acknowledgement must not retire unrelated pending events. Verify world/round generation and host/guest permissions before applying travel, target or score changes. Use allowlisted cue/asset/destination IDs. Never turn a peer-supplied string into arbitrary Lua execution, an unrestricted file path or an unchecked engine object lookup. Protocol test cases Encode/decode valid boundary-sized fields. Reject truncated, oversized, malformed and trailing data. Retry an event after acknowledgement loss without applying it twice. Drop stale session/world packets after reconnect/travel. Preserve a large appearance revision across lost snapshots. Recover or clearly stop when the reliable queue fills.","sections":[{"id":"state-events","title":"1. Separate replaceable state from one-time events","text":"A new pose can supersede an older pose. A shot, chat message or accepted hit must not be silently replaced by the next packet. Model those as different streams with different retry and deduplication rules. The local implementation sends revisioned appearance/state plus acknowledged event streams. Sending everything every tick without identity can appear to work locally while duplicating rewards or losing gun changes under packet loss."},{"id":"framing","title":"2. Define byte framing before decoding fields","text":"Length-prefixed fields permit text to contain delimiter characters without guessing where a field ends. Count bytes consistently at both ends. Reject an invalid prefix, over-limit length, truncated body, forbidden bytes and unexpected trailing data before touching the engine. local function decodeOneField(payload, limit) if type(payload) ~= \"string\" or #payload > limit + 6 then return nil end local colon = payload:find(\":\", 1, true) if not colon or colon > 6 then return nil end local digits = payload:sub(1, colon - 1) if not digits:match(\"^%d+$\") then return nil end local count = tonumber(digits) if count > limit then return nil end local body = payload:sub(colon + 1) if #body ~= count or body:find(\"\\0\", 1, true) then return nil end return body end A real envelope also needs a version/magic value, the required field count and per-field validation. Do not accept extra fields quietly unless the protocol explicitly defines forward compatibility."},{"id":"revisions","title":"3. Distinguish sequence and content revision","text":"A snapshot sequence orders snapshots. A gear revision identifies a changed weapon appearance. An acknowledgement says which content/event was accepted. Keeping those concepts separate lets unchanged large gear data stay out of routine pose packets while still retrying a lost gear update. If sequence numbers wrap, define a bounded modular “newer” comparison. A simple numerical greater-than test stops working at the wrap boundary. Test duplicate, older and half-range ambiguous inputs explicitly."},{"id":"journal","title":"4. Make event application idempotent","text":"Give each event an ID within a session/round generation. Apply the next accepted event once, then acknowledge it. A repeated event should confirm the already accepted result without awarding points or playing effects again. If an ordered event fails validation or application, decide whether the stream rejects/cancels or waits for recovery. Do not advance acknowledgement past work you did not apply, and do not let an invalid head event block the queue forever without a visible error policy."},{"id":"limits","title":"5. Bound queues and values","text":"Set maximum payload size, field sizes, pending events, history and retained generations. Validate numeric ranges, finite coordinates, allowed message kinds and known entity IDs. A decoded string is not yet an authorised gameplay action. When backpressure occurs, show the user that the connection is catching up or reject the new action. Silently dropping a scored hit is not equivalent to dropping an obsolete cosmetic pose."},{"id":"authority","title":"6. Validate the sender and context","text":"Bind acknowledgements to the peer/session they belong to. A forged or stale acknowledgement must not retire unrelated pending events. Verify world/round generation and host/guest permissions before applying travel, target or score changes. Use allowlisted cue/asset/destination IDs. Never turn a peer-supplied string into arbitrary Lua execution, an unrestricted file path or an unchecked engine object lookup."},{"id":"cases","title":"Protocol test cases","text":"Encode/decode valid boundary-sized fields. Reject truncated, oversized, malformed and trailing data. Retry an event after acknowledgement loss without applying it twice. Drop stale session/world packets after reconnect/travel. Preserve a large appearance revision across lost snapshots. Recover or clearly stop when the reliable queue fills."}]},{"id":"interpolation","title":"Movement interpolation","text":"Movement interpolation Render remote movement smoothly without confusing smoothing with authority or prediction. 1. Start from correct snapshots Record received position, orientation, receipt time and any sender timestamp under the correct session/world identity. Validate finite values and reject stale/out-of-order generations. Interpolation cannot repair a pose belonging to the wrong player or a previous range. First prove the remote actor follows the correct target with direct samples. Then add smoothing and compare the same movement sequence; otherwise two bugs can mask each other. 2. Render slightly behind the latest data Keep a small bounded history and sample an earlier render time so two surrounding snapshots are usually available. This trades some display delay for stability. The chosen delay should be a measured policy, not a magic number promised for every connection. local function between(a, b, t) local duration = b.time - a.time if duration \u003c= 0 then return nil end local alpha = math.max(0, math.min(1, (t - a.time) / duration)) return { X = a.position.X + (b.position.X-a.position.X) * alpha, Y = a.position.Y + (b.position.Y-a.position.Y) * alpha, Z = a.position.Z + (b.position.Z-a.position.Z) * alpha } end 3. Handle orientation separately Yaw wraps at the angular boundary. Use the short-arc calculation from Vectors, rotations & spaces rather than averaging 179 and -179 into zero. General skeletal or full 3D orientation needs its own representation and tested interpolation. For proxy walking/aiming, keep the displayed pose separate from authoritative player movement. A cosmetic character model should not move the real local pawn as part of smoothing. 4. Bound extrapolation If the render time extends beyond your latest sample, a short velocity extrapolation can hide a gap. Cap its duration and velocity, then hold or indicate stale data rather than letting a proxy fly forever. The local buffer used bounded history, a teleport threshold and capped extrapolation. Its numerical defaults were chosen for that implementation. They are not evidence of an optimal universal setting for every weapon, map or internet route. 5. Reset on discontinuities A teleport, world change, backwards clock or restarted sender stream should clear the interpolation history. Interpolating across a teleport can sweep a character through the entire scene, and reusing a previous world’s clock can stall updates. Do not calculate velocity from equal timestamps or a non-positive interval. Repeated stationary samples may be skipped deliberately, but ensure a real state change still reaches the visual model. 6. Measure jitter and visible delay Log sample intervals, buffer age and stale periods separately from average ping. A low average ping does not guarantee evenly spaced samples. Increasing the buffer can smooth motion while making it visibly lag farther behind; show that trade-off in tests. A useful test path includes standing, walking, sharp turns, aim changes, a pause in updates, teleport and reconnect. Compare both screens in a real session after the pure buffer tests pass.","sections":[{"id":"baseline","title":"1. Start from correct snapshots","text":"Record received position, orientation, receipt time and any sender timestamp under the correct session/world identity. Validate finite values and reject stale/out-of-order generations. Interpolation cannot repair a pose belonging to the wrong player or a previous range. First prove the remote actor follows the correct target with direct samples. Then add smoothing and compare the same movement sequence; otherwise two bugs can mask each other."},{"id":"buffer","title":"2. Render slightly behind the latest data","text":"Keep a small bounded history and sample an earlier render time so two surrounding snapshots are usually available. This trades some display delay for stability. The chosen delay should be a measured policy, not a magic number promised for every connection. local function between(a, b, t) local duration = b.time - a.time if duration \u003c= 0 then return nil end local alpha = math.max(0, math.min(1, (t - a.time) / duration)) return { X = a.position.X + (b.position.X-a.position.X) * alpha, Y = a.position.Y + (b.position.Y-a.position.Y) * alpha, Z = a.position.Z + (b.position.Z-a.position.Z) * alpha } end"},{"id":"yaw","title":"3. Handle orientation separately","text":"Yaw wraps at the angular boundary. Use the short-arc calculation from Vectors, rotations & spaces rather than averaging 179 and -179 into zero. General skeletal or full 3D orientation needs its own representation and tested interpolation. For proxy walking/aiming, keep the displayed pose separate from authoritative player movement. A cosmetic character model should not move the real local pawn as part of smoothing."},{"id":"loss","title":"4. Bound extrapolation","text":"If the render time extends beyond your latest sample, a short velocity extrapolation can hide a gap. Cap its duration and velocity, then hold or indicate stale data rather than letting a proxy fly forever. The local buffer used bounded history, a teleport threshold and capped extrapolation. Its numerical defaults were chosen for that implementation. They are not evidence of an optimal universal setting for every weapon, map or internet route."},{"id":"resets","title":"5. Reset on discontinuities","text":"A teleport, world change, backwards clock or restarted sender stream should clear the interpolation history. Interpolating across a teleport can sweep a character through the entire scene, and reusing a previous world’s clock can stall updates. Do not calculate velocity from equal timestamps or a non-positive interval. Repeated stationary samples may be skipped deliberately, but ensure a real state change still reaches the visual model."},{"id":"measure","title":"6. Measure jitter and visible delay","text":"Log sample intervals, buffer age and stale periods separately from average ping. A low average ping does not guarantee evenly spaced samples. Increasing the buffer can smooth motion while making it visibly lag farther behind; show that trade-off in tests. A useful test path includes standing, walking, sharp turns, aim changes, a pause in updates, teleport and reconnect. Compare both screens in a real session after the pure buffer tests pass."}]},{"id":"clock-sync","title":"Clocks, latency & round timing","text":"Clocks, latency & round timing Keep event time, receipt time and render time on explicit clock bases. 1. Name every time value Value Meaning Local event time When the sender observed the action on its clock. Receipt time When this peer received it. Host-aligned time An estimated conversion into the authoritative round clock. Render time The earlier time sampled by an interpolation buffer. Wall-clock file time Useful for logs/backups, not automatically comparable to world seconds. Do not subtract os.time from GetRealTimeSeconds or compare two worlds’ elapsed seconds without a conversion. A world clock can restart during travel while a process/network session continues. 2. Exchange timestamp echoes The local clock code sends a timestamp, echoes a remote one and records how long the echo was held. It uses bounded recent requests to estimate round trip and offset. This is a mod-level time estimate, not a Steam guarantee. local rtt = receivedNow - originallySentAt - heldByPeer if rtt \u003c 0 then return nil end local estimatedOffset = remoteStamp + rtt / 2 - receivedNow The half-round-trip assumption does not reveal actual one-way delay on an asymmetric connection. Reject impossible or stale echoes and keep the uncertainty in mind when using the estimate for gameplay decisions. 3. Track estimate quality Require enough recent accepted samples before treating a conversion as usable. Record age, round-trip estimate and rejected samples. A single fortunate packet is not a stable clock synchronisation result. Do not let arbitrary remote timestamps create unbounded offsets. Validate sample ranges and define how the estimate resets or ages out after a pause, reconnect or world transition. 4. Define the scoring deadline policy Choose whether eligibility is based on host receipt time, a validated host-aligned event time or another explicit rule. Host receipt time is simple but disadvantages delayed guest hits near the deadline. A grace period needs bounded timestamps and a settling phase, not an open invitation to submit late scores. Preserve round ID and shot identity with the event. A correctly converted timestamp still belongs to the wrong round if its generation is stale. 5. Keep presentation smoothing separate The interpolation buffer’s render delay should not silently become extra competitive time. The displayed remote player can be intentionally behind the authoritative event stream. Explain countdown/results using the shared accepted state, not the last rendered pose timestamp. A countdown shown locally before both peers are synchronised is a presentation error even if the host’s internal timer is correct. Tie readiness to the state your challenge actually requires. Timing acceptance cases A valid timestamp echo updates the estimate. A stale/malformed/negative sample is rejected. Travel and reconnect reset the correct histories. A guest hit near the deadline follows the documented rule. A settling window closes and cannot reopen from an old packet. The result is checked on real peers, not inferred from a local simulation.","sections":[{"id":"clocks","title":"1. Name every time value","text":"Value Meaning Local event time When the sender observed the action on its clock. Receipt time When this peer received it. Host-aligned time An estimated conversion into the authoritative round clock. Render time The earlier time sampled by an interpolation buffer. Wall-clock file time Useful for logs/backups, not automatically comparable to world seconds. Do not subtract os.time from GetRealTimeSeconds or compare two worlds’ elapsed seconds without a conversion. A world clock can restart during travel while a process/network session continues."},{"id":"exchange","title":"2. Exchange timestamp echoes","text":"The local clock code sends a timestamp, echoes a remote one and records how long the echo was held. It uses bounded recent requests to estimate round trip and offset. This is a mod-level time estimate, not a Steam guarantee. local rtt = receivedNow - originallySentAt - heldByPeer if rtt \u003c 0 then return nil end local estimatedOffset = remoteStamp + rtt / 2 - receivedNow The half-round-trip assumption does not reveal actual one-way delay on an asymmetric connection. Reject impossible or stale echoes and keep the uncertainty in mind when using the estimate for gameplay decisions."},{"id":"quality","title":"3. Track estimate quality","text":"Require enough recent accepted samples before treating a conversion as usable. Record age, round-trip estimate and rejected samples. A single fortunate packet is not a stable clock synchronisation result. Do not let arbitrary remote timestamps create unbounded offsets. Validate sample ranges and define how the estimate resets or ages out after a pause, reconnect or world transition."},{"id":"deadline","title":"4. Define the scoring deadline policy","text":"Choose whether eligibility is based on host receipt time, a validated host-aligned event time or another explicit rule. Host receipt time is simple but disadvantages delayed guest hits near the deadline. A grace period needs bounded timestamps and a settling phase, not an open invitation to submit late scores. Preserve round ID and shot identity with the event. A correctly converted timestamp still belongs to the wrong round if its generation is stale."},{"id":"rendering","title":"5. Keep presentation smoothing separate","text":"The interpolation buffer’s render delay should not silently become extra competitive time. The displayed remote player can be intentionally behind the authoritative event stream. Explain countdown/results using the shared accepted state, not the last rendered pose timestamp. A countdown shown locally before both peers are synchronised is a presentation error even if the host’s internal timer is correct. Tie readiness to the state your challenge actually requires."},{"id":"tests","title":"Timing acceptance cases","text":"A valid timestamp echo updates the estimate. A stale/malformed/negative sample is rejected. Travel and reconnect reset the correct histories. A guest hit near the deadline follows the documented rule. A settling window closes and cannot reopen from an old packet. The result is checked on real peers, not inferred from a local simulation."}]},{"id":"chat","title":"Chat, names & text validation","text":"Chat, names & text validation Bound user text, preserve valid UTF-8 and separate display names from trusted identities. 1. Define text policy Decide whether chat is single-line, how many bytes a message may contain, how names are displayed and which characters your actual widget treats as markup. A plain TextBlock and a rich-text renderer can require different handling. Treat the display name as presentation. The connection/session identity decides ownership and permissions; a guest renaming themselves “Host” should not change authority. 2. Validate type, encoding and length local function cleanLine(value, limit) if type(value) ~= \"string\" then return nil, \"not text\" end if not utf8.len(value) then return nil, \"invalid UTF-8\" end local text = value:gsub(\"[%c\u003c>]\", \" \"):gsub(\"%s+\", \" \") text = text:match(\"^%s*(.-)%s*$\") text = text:sub(1, limit) while #text > 0 and not utf8.len(text) do text = text:sub(1, -2) end if text == \"\" then return nil, \"empty\" end return text end Lua string length counts bytes. Truncating in the middle of a multi-byte character can create invalid UTF-8, so trim to a valid boundary after applying a byte cap. This example strips angle/control characters for a simple display policy; it is not a universal HTML or rich-text sanitizer. 3. Give messages identities A chat message is a one-time event. Include an event ID and stream/session identity, keep pending messages until acknowledged, and display a retry only once. A new snapshot sequence is not by itself the identity of a particular message. Bind acknowledgement to the correct peer generation. Otherwise an old acknowledgement can retire a new stream’s messages, or reconnect can replay old history as new unread text. 4. Bound queue, history and send rate Choose maximum pending messages, local history and a send interval. The local implementation has such caps and a visible “waiting for your friend” path. Those are usability and backpressure rules, not proof every delivery succeeded. Muted display should not break protocol acknowledgement if the message was accepted. Otherwise a peer can keep retransmitting a message just because the recipient hid its presentation. 5. Integrate with input and notices Focus the text field deliberately, prevent send keys from triggering unrelated gameplay actions, and restore cursor/input state when chat closes. Keep system notices distinct from player messages so a player cannot impersonate a trusted status just through its text. For an unread counter, decide whether messages arriving while chat is visible count as unread. Reset on the intended user action rather than on every UI rebuild. Text test cases Empty, whitespace-only, long ASCII and long multi-byte messages. Control characters and markup-like strings. Duplicate delivery and lost acknowledgement. Mute/unmute while messages arrive. Reconnect with a new stream generation. Typing, sending, Escape and reopening without stolen game input. Use synthetic text in public examples, not private chat logs. See Input, focus & cursors for the menu side.","sections":[{"id":"policy","title":"1. Define text policy","text":"Decide whether chat is single-line, how many bytes a message may contain, how names are displayed and which characters your actual widget treats as markup. A plain TextBlock and a rich-text renderer can require different handling. Treat the display name as presentation. The connection/session identity decides ownership and permissions; a guest renaming themselves “Host” should not change authority."},{"id":"validation","title":"2. Validate type, encoding and length","text":"local function cleanLine(value, limit) if type(value) ~= \"string\" then return nil, \"not text\" end if not utf8.len(value) then return nil, \"invalid UTF-8\" end local text = value:gsub(\"[%c\u003c>]\", \" \"):gsub(\"%s+\", \" \") text = text:match(\"^%s*(.-)%s*$\") text = text:sub(1, limit) while #text > 0 and not utf8.len(text) do text = text:sub(1, -2) end if text == \"\" then return nil, \"empty\" end return text end Lua string length counts bytes. Truncating in the middle of a multi-byte character can create invalid UTF-8, so trim to a valid boundary after applying a byte cap. This example strips angle/control characters for a simple display policy; it is not a universal HTML or rich-text sanitizer."},{"id":"delivery","title":"3. Give messages identities","text":"A chat message is a one-time event. Include an event ID and stream/session identity, keep pending messages until acknowledged, and display a retry only once. A new snapshot sequence is not by itself the identity of a particular message. Bind acknowledgement to the correct peer generation. Otherwise an old acknowledgement can retire a new stream’s messages, or reconnect can replay old history as new unread text."},{"id":"bounds","title":"4. Bound queue, history and send rate","text":"Choose maximum pending messages, local history and a send interval. The local implementation has such caps and a visible “waiting for your friend” path. Those are usability and backpressure rules, not proof every delivery succeeded. Muted display should not break protocol acknowledgement if the message was accepted. Otherwise a peer can keep retransmitting a message just because the recipient hid its presentation."},{"id":"focus","title":"5. Integrate with input and notices","text":"Focus the text field deliberately, prevent send keys from triggering unrelated gameplay actions, and restore cursor/input state when chat closes. Keep system notices distinct from player messages so a player cannot impersonate a trusted status just through its text. For an unread counter, decide whether messages arriving while chat is visible count as unread. Reset on the intended user action rather than on every UI rebuild."},{"id":"test","title":"Text test cases","text":"Empty, whitespace-only, long ASCII and long multi-byte messages. Control characters and markup-like strings. Duplicate delivery and lost acknowledgement. Mute/unmute while messages arrive. Reconnect with a new stream generation. Typing, sending, Escape and reopening without stolen game input. Use synthetic text in public examples, not private chat logs. See Input, focus & cursors for the menu side."}]},{"id":"paths","title":"Game paths & classes","text":"Game paths & classes Useful locations and names observed in the Windows demo. Build-specific reference These paths were checked locally on Steam demo build 26083142 . They are a starting point for discovery, not a promise that another release has the same objects. File locations Location Purpose Ballistic/Binaries/Win64/ Shipping executable and loader entry point. Win64/ue4ss/Mods/ Mod folders in the tested loader layout. Win64/ue4ss/UE4SS.log Loader and Lua output; paths here start at Binaries. %LOCALAPPDATA%/Ballistic/Saved/SaveGames/ Player saves. Keep these out of mod releases. Ballistic/Content/Paks/ Cooked game content archives. Workshop functions The attachment point widget uses this class path: /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/BunkerHUDItems/Attach_POI.Attach_POI_C Function Observed role GetCompatibleMountsRecursive Mount-compatibility lookup. OnGetUserMenuContent Attachment popup creation. SetupAttachment Fitting or removing an attachment. The workshop HUD is /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/HUD_Workshop.HUD_Workshop_C . Its attachment editor is named WeaponEditMOde ; preserve that spelling when looking it up. Weapon classes /Game/BlueprintLogic/Weapons/A_WeaponBase.A_WeaponBase_C /Script/Ballistic.C_WeaponBase /Script/Ballistic.C_WeaponPart The Blueprint weapon base exposes UpdateCharacteristicsFromParts . In the inspected build it has nine boolean inputs. Check the full signature in your own dump before calling it. Maps Name Observed use Bunker_Main Bunker and workshop. Range_SwimmingPool Azure Swimming Pool range. The confirmed pool package is /Game/Maps/Ranges/Range_SwimmingPool . A range row or menu label alone does not prove that its associated map is included in the demo. For the behaviour behind these names, see Weapon assemblies , Attachments & rails , Shots & ballistics and Range menus & travel . Check your build Use the loader’s object viewer or dump to confirm paths. Keep a small text record of the build, object name, signature and action you tested. See Debugging for a repeatable check. Walkthrough: turn a path into a usable reference 1. Copy the full reflected name A dump entry can include a type prefix, an object path and a member suffix. Preserve the exact case and spelling first. Only remove a type prefix where the lookup API expects the object/function name without it. Do not copy a runtime address from an old dump into a script. Addresses belong to a particular process and can change after garbage collection, travel or a restart. 2. Separate class, object and package names A map package such as /Game/Maps/Ranges/Range_SwimmingPool is not the same identifier as a generated widget class such as Attach_POI_C. A display label such as Azure Swimming Pool is not either of those paths. Keep fields named mapId, packagePath and displayName distinct in your own data structures. 3. Use context to select an instance Load or locate a class, then find the live object used by the current menu or range. Record its world and full name. If the player switches weapons, the same class can produce a different instance; the feature needs the new instance. 4. Check the member’s signature Before calling a function, read its input/out/return fields in the dump. Before writing a property, record its type and owner. A generated struct field with a long suffix should be copied as a complete identifier, not reconstructed from its display label. Useful research sheets Keep a small per-build sheet for each subsystem: exact class path, observed instance context, relevant functions, parameter types and one successful action. Link that sheet to the guide that explains behaviour; an unannotated catalog alone does not tell a modder what is safe to call. Use Reading data tables for range rows, Weapon assemblies for equipped actors, Workshop UI for editor ownership and Shots & ballistics for hit attribution.","sections":[{"id":"file-locations","title":"File locations","text":"Location Purpose Ballistic/Binaries/Win64/ Shipping executable and loader entry point. Win64/ue4ss/Mods/ Mod folders in the tested loader layout. Win64/ue4ss/UE4SS.log Loader and Lua output; paths here start at Binaries. %LOCALAPPDATA%/Ballistic/Saved/SaveGames/ Player saves. Keep these out of mod releases. Ballistic/Content/Paks/ Cooked game content archives."},{"id":"workshop-functions","title":"Workshop functions","text":"The attachment point widget uses this class path: /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/BunkerHUDItems/Attach_POI.Attach_POI_C Function Observed role GetCompatibleMountsRecursive Mount-compatibility lookup. OnGetUserMenuContent Attachment popup creation. SetupAttachment Fitting or removing an attachment. The workshop HUD is /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/HUD_Workshop.HUD_Workshop_C . Its attachment editor is named WeaponEditMOde ; preserve that spelling when looking it up."},{"id":"weapon-classes","title":"Weapon classes","text":"/Game/BlueprintLogic/Weapons/A_WeaponBase.A_WeaponBase_C /Script/Ballistic.C_WeaponBase /Script/Ballistic.C_WeaponPart The Blueprint weapon base exposes UpdateCharacteristicsFromParts . In the inspected build it has nine boolean inputs. Check the full signature in your own dump before calling it."},{"id":"maps","title":"Maps","text":"Name Observed use Bunker_Main Bunker and workshop. Range_SwimmingPool Azure Swimming Pool range. The confirmed pool package is /Game/Maps/Ranges/Range_SwimmingPool . A range row or menu label alone does not prove that its associated map is included in the demo. For the behaviour behind these names, see Weapon assemblies , Attachments & rails , Shots & ballistics and Range menus & travel ."},{"id":"check-your-build","title":"Check your build","text":"Use the loader’s object viewer or dump to confirm paths. Keep a small text record of the build, object name, signature and action you tested. See Debugging for a repeatable check."},{"id":"path-discovery-workflow","title":"Walkthrough: turn a path into a usable reference","text":""},{"id":"path-step-copy-full-name","title":"1. Copy the full reflected name","text":"A dump entry can include a type prefix, an object path and a member suffix. Preserve the exact case and spelling first. Only remove a type prefix where the lookup API expects the object/function name without it. Do not copy a runtime address from an old dump into a script. Addresses belong to a particular process and can change after garbage collection, travel or a restart."},{"id":"path-step-separate-names","title":"2. Separate class, object and package names","text":"A map package such as /Game/Maps/Ranges/Range_SwimmingPool is not the same identifier as a generated widget class such as Attach_POI_C. A display label such as Azure Swimming Pool is not either of those paths. Keep fields named mapId, packagePath and displayName distinct in your own data structures."},{"id":"path-step-use-context","title":"3. Use context to select an instance","text":"Load or locate a class, then find the live object used by the current menu or range. Record its world and full name. If the player switches weapons, the same class can produce a different instance; the feature needs the new instance."},{"id":"path-step-check-the-member","title":"4. Check the member’s signature","text":"Before calling a function, read its input/out/return fields in the dump. Before writing a property, record its type and owner. A generated struct field with a long suffix should be copied as a complete identifier, not reconstructed from its display label."},{"id":"path-reference-sheets","title":"Useful research sheets","text":"Keep a small per-build sheet for each subsystem: exact class path, observed instance context, relevant functions, parameter types and one successful action. Link that sheet to the guide that explains behaviour; an unannotated catalog alone does not tell a modder what is safe to call. Use Reading data tables for range rows, Weapon assemblies for equipped actors, Workshop UI for editor ownership and Shots & ballistics for hit attribution."}]},{"id":"custom-content","title":"Custom content","text":"Custom content Know which changes can reuse game assets and which need an asset pipeline. Lua-only mods Lua is useful for changing behaviour, creating UI and assembling objects from assets already cooked into the game. You can prototype layouts by spawning existing meshes and components in the current world. That does not create a new cooked map. The scene still belongs to the existing world, with its lighting, streaming and lifecycle. New models and textures An external .fbx file is a source model, not a game-ready Unreal asset. A Lua script cannot simply use an FBX path as a normal skeletal mesh reference. Proper mesh, material and animation integration generally needs compatible cooked assets or a dedicated runtime importer. This wiki does not provide an importer. Opening the game in Unreal The installed game contains cooked content, rather than the developer’s editable source project. Installing Unreal Editor does not restore the original Blueprints, levels or source assets. The local executable reported the UE5.7 release branch. That is useful context when investigating tools, but it does not establish that any specific editor patch will produce compatible content. Testing a runtime scene Check mesh and material availability in the actual game build. Test lighting and exposure in the world where you spawn objects. Verify collision and a safe player spawn. Keep references to the actors your mod owns. Remove them when the scene closes or the world changes. For a runtime prototype, start with Range menus & travel and Timers & cleanup . For distributing an asset-free Lua mod, see Packaging a Lua mod . Further reading Dmgvol’s UE modding guides cover several Unreal modding approaches. Start with Tools & resources for the loader and archive-inspection references used here. Walkthrough: choose the smallest content pipeline 1. Does the change need a new asset? If the goal is a stat display, a menu action or rearranging existing targets, start with runtime scripting and existing cooked assets. You avoid a cooking pipeline and can verify the behaviour before investing in new art. If the goal is a genuinely new weapon model with correct materials, collision and animation, list those requirements explicitly. A replacement mesh preview satisfies only one part of that list. 2. Inventory available assets Use a browser such as FModel to look for relevant classes, meshes, materials and sounds. Then verify a small set can actually load in the game. A thumbnail or menu row is evidence of a reference, not proof the full asset and its dependencies are usable. 3. Build a runtime prototype Spawn one object, confirm its transform, mobility and visibility, then add collision and interaction. Keep a handle to it for cleanup. Expand to a small layout only after the one-object case is visibly correct. For procedural geometry, the local records established that the procedural mesh component API could create a triangle and a runtime texture import could load a PNG. Those API checks did not establish a correctly lit, textured and positioned RPG model on a held weapon. Keep the capability claim that narrow. 4. Check lighting in the actual world A detached runtime scene still inherits the hosting world’s rendering environment. If structures render but are very dark, inspect sky/ambient lighting, material response and postprocess settings instead of assuming a view-distance problem. A bright emissive workaround is not equivalent to a complete outdoor lighting setup. 5. Use an asset pipeline when the requirement needs it For cooked content, you need a compatible asset/cooking approach and a way to load the packaged result. The installed game is not the developer’s editable project. An engine branch string is context for compatibility research, not a complete reconstruction recipe. Document the exact toolchain, asset types and load mechanism you actually tested. Don’t tell another modder that a particular editor patch will work based only on a matching major/minor version. Acceptance checklist Test appearance, scale, orientation, materials, collision, interaction, cleanup and travel separately. For a weapon, add handling, ammunition, firing and persistence. For a range, add spawn safety, targets, scoring and return flow. Decide which of those are actually in your prototype’s scope before calling it a finished mod.","sections":[{"id":"lua-only-mods","title":"Lua-only mods","text":"Lua is useful for changing behaviour, creating UI and assembling objects from assets already cooked into the game. You can prototype layouts by spawning existing meshes and components in the current world. That does not create a new cooked map. The scene still belongs to the existing world, with its lighting, streaming and lifecycle."},{"id":"new-models-and-textures","title":"New models and textures","text":"An external .fbx file is a source model, not a game-ready Unreal asset. A Lua script cannot simply use an FBX path as a normal skeletal mesh reference. Proper mesh, material and animation integration generally needs compatible cooked assets or a dedicated runtime importer. This wiki does not provide an importer."},{"id":"opening-the-game-in-unreal","title":"Opening the game in Unreal","text":"The installed game contains cooked content, rather than the developer’s editable source project. Installing Unreal Editor does not restore the original Blueprints, levels or source assets. The local executable reported the UE5.7 release branch. That is useful context when investigating tools, but it does not establish that any specific editor patch will produce compatible content."},{"id":"runtime-scene-checklist","title":"Testing a runtime scene","text":"Check mesh and material availability in the actual game build. Test lighting and exposure in the world where you spawn objects. Verify collision and a safe player spawn. Keep references to the actors your mod owns. Remove them when the scene closes or the world changes. For a runtime prototype, start with Range menus & travel and Timers & cleanup . For distributing an asset-free Lua mod, see Packaging a Lua mod ."},{"id":"further-reading","title":"Further reading","text":"Dmgvol’s UE modding guides cover several Unreal modding approaches. Start with Tools & resources for the loader and archive-inspection references used here."},{"id":"custom-content-decision","title":"Walkthrough: choose the smallest content pipeline","text":""},{"id":"custom-step-behaviour","title":"1. Does the change need a new asset?","text":"If the goal is a stat display, a menu action or rearranging existing targets, start with runtime scripting and existing cooked assets. You avoid a cooking pipeline and can verify the behaviour before investing in new art. If the goal is a genuinely new weapon model with correct materials, collision and animation, list those requirements explicitly. A replacement mesh preview satisfies only one part of that list."},{"id":"custom-step-inventory","title":"2. Inventory available assets","text":"Use a browser such as FModel to look for relevant classes, meshes, materials and sounds. Then verify a small set can actually load in the game. A thumbnail or menu row is evidence of a reference, not proof the full asset and its dependencies are usable."},{"id":"custom-step-runtime-prototype","title":"3. Build a runtime prototype","text":"Spawn one object, confirm its transform, mobility and visibility, then add collision and interaction. Keep a handle to it for cleanup. Expand to a small layout only after the one-object case is visibly correct. For procedural geometry, the local records established that the procedural mesh component API could create a triangle and a runtime texture import could load a PNG. Those API checks did not establish a correctly lit, textured and positioned RPG model on a held weapon. Keep the capability claim that narrow."},{"id":"custom-step-lighting","title":"4. Check lighting in the actual world","text":"A detached runtime scene still inherits the hosting world’s rendering environment. If structures render but are very dark, inspect sky/ambient lighting, material response and postprocess settings instead of assuming a view-distance problem. A bright emissive workaround is not equivalent to a complete outdoor lighting setup."},{"id":"custom-step-cooked-assets","title":"5. Use an asset pipeline when the requirement needs it","text":"For cooked content, you need a compatible asset/cooking approach and a way to load the packaged result. The installed game is not the developer’s editable project. An engine branch string is context for compatibility research, not a complete reconstruction recipe. Document the exact toolchain, asset types and load mechanism you actually tested. Don’t tell another modder that a particular editor patch will work based only on a matching major/minor version."},{"id":"custom-acceptance-checklist","title":"Acceptance checklist","text":"Test appearance, scale, orientation, materials, collision, interaction, cleanup and travel separately. For a weapon, add handling, ammunition, firing and persistence. For a range, add spawn safety, targets, scoring and return flow. Decide which of those are actually in your prototype’s scope before calling it a finished mod."}]},{"id":"packaging","title":"Packaging a Lua mod","text":"Packaging a Lua mod Make the archive easy to install and leave your development setup out of it. Pick an archive root For an archive intended to be extracted into ue4ss/Mods , include the mod folder itself: MyMod/ ├── enabled.txt └── Scripts/ ├── main.lua ├── config.lua └── feature.lua README.md Another valid approach is to include the full Ballistic/Binaries/Win64/ue4ss/Mods/MyMod path and instruct the user to extract into the game folder. Choose one root and describe it exactly. Mixing the instructions produces doubled or missing folders. Include only release files Use an explicit file list in the build script instead of zipping the whole project. Include the Lua files the mod loads, its enable marker, required mod-owned binaries and the player instructions. Exclude saves, backup saves, captured screenshots, object dumps, runtime reports, logs, tests, browser profiles and unrelated mods. Don’t package the game or the entire loader with a small Lua mod. Version the whole mod Put a version in the startup log and give the archive the same version. A native bridge and its Lua scripts can depend on each other, so ship a complete matching release rather than telling users to update just one file. If a protocol changes, check the peer’s protocol version before accepting gameplay messages. An application version label alone does not prove wire compatibility. Write installation instructions Name the loader requirement and tested game build. Show the exact extraction destination and final folder layout. List controls and configuration file locations. Explain the full-restart requirement used by your build. Explain how to disable the mod and what persisted changes remain. Removing a compatibility script does not necessarily undo an assembly already saved by the game. Check the archive Inspect the file list and compare packaged files with the ones you tested. Try extracting into an empty staging folder and reading the instructions as a new user. For an updated release, confirm that renamed or removed modules cannot remain active from the old install. A checksum can identify the exact artifact you tested; it is not a substitute for testing the artifact’s behaviour. Walkthrough: build a reviewable release 1. Stage a clean copy Create an empty staging directory and copy only the explicitly allowed production files into it. Never use the live Mods directory as the staging root. A development install can contain obsolete modules that make the feature work locally while the archive is missing a dependency. 2. Extract as a new user Open the resulting archive and follow your own instructions into another empty directory. Check the final paths rather than only the archive’s name. A mod-root archive should produce WorkshopInspector/Scripts/main.lua under Mods; a game-root archive should produce its full game-relative tree. 3. Compare the tested files List the staged files and compare hashes with the exact source/installed files used for the test. Include any native DLL needed by that release, but exclude the game and unrelated loader binaries. Check that documentation names the same version as the startup message and archive. 4. Test an upgrade Overlay the new release on a copy of an old install and inspect renamed/removed files. If a stale module can remain active, provide a clear replacement instruction or design the loader entry point so only the intended modules are required. 5. Test disabling With the game closed, disable the mod using its documented method and confirm its callbacks stop after restart. Separately document persisted assemblies or progression that disabling does not undo. A disabled script and a restored save are different outcomes. Write useful release notes Describe the user-visible trigger and result: “Clear attachments now stops when the selected weapon changes” is more useful than “refactored state handling”. Separate new behaviour from fixes and explain important migration steps. For this wiki, edit Next update rather than creating a second patch-notes file. The repository’s contribution guide explains how a dated entry is published.","sections":[{"id":"pick-an-archive-root","title":"Pick an archive root","text":"For an archive intended to be extracted into ue4ss/Mods , include the mod folder itself: MyMod/ ├── enabled.txt └── Scripts/ ├── main.lua ├── config.lua └── feature.lua README.md Another valid approach is to include the full Ballistic/Binaries/Win64/ue4ss/Mods/MyMod path and instruct the user to extract into the game folder. Choose one root and describe it exactly. Mixing the instructions produces doubled or missing folders."},{"id":"include-only-release-files","title":"Include only release files","text":"Use an explicit file list in the build script instead of zipping the whole project. Include the Lua files the mod loads, its enable marker, required mod-owned binaries and the player instructions. Exclude saves, backup saves, captured screenshots, object dumps, runtime reports, logs, tests, browser profiles and unrelated mods. Don’t package the game or the entire loader with a small Lua mod."},{"id":"version-the-whole-mod","title":"Version the whole mod","text":"Put a version in the startup log and give the archive the same version. A native bridge and its Lua scripts can depend on each other, so ship a complete matching release rather than telling users to update just one file. If a protocol changes, check the peer’s protocol version before accepting gameplay messages. An application version label alone does not prove wire compatibility."},{"id":"write-install-instructions","title":"Write installation instructions","text":"Name the loader requirement and tested game build. Show the exact extraction destination and final folder layout. List controls and configuration file locations. Explain the full-restart requirement used by your build. Explain how to disable the mod and what persisted changes remain. Removing a compatibility script does not necessarily undo an assembly already saved by the game."},{"id":"check-the-archive","title":"Check the archive","text":"Inspect the file list and compare packaged files with the ones you tested. Try extracting into an empty staging folder and reading the instructions as a new user. For an updated release, confirm that renamed or removed modules cannot remain active from the old install. A checksum can identify the exact artifact you tested; it is not a substitute for testing the artifact’s behaviour."},{"id":"release-walkthrough","title":"Walkthrough: build a reviewable release","text":""},{"id":"release-step-stage","title":"1. Stage a clean copy","text":"Create an empty staging directory and copy only the explicitly allowed production files into it. Never use the live Mods directory as the staging root. A development install can contain obsolete modules that make the feature work locally while the archive is missing a dependency."},{"id":"release-step-extract","title":"2. Extract as a new user","text":"Open the resulting archive and follow your own instructions into another empty directory. Check the final paths rather than only the archive’s name. A mod-root archive should produce WorkshopInspector/Scripts/main.lua under Mods; a game-root archive should produce its full game-relative tree."},{"id":"release-step-compare","title":"3. Compare the tested files","text":"List the staged files and compare hashes with the exact source/installed files used for the test. Include any native DLL needed by that release, but exclude the game and unrelated loader binaries. Check that documentation names the same version as the startup message and archive."},{"id":"release-step-upgrade","title":"4. Test an upgrade","text":"Overlay the new release on a copy of an old install and inspect renamed/removed files. If a stale module can remain active, provide a clear replacement instruction or design the loader entry point so only the intended modules are required."},{"id":"release-step-disable","title":"5. Test disabling","text":"With the game closed, disable the mod using its documented method and confirm its callbacks stop after restart. Separately document persisted assemblies or progression that disabling does not undo. A disabled script and a restored save are different outcomes."},{"id":"release-notes","title":"Write useful release notes","text":"Describe the user-visible trigger and result: “Clear attachments now stops when the selected weapon changes” is more useful than “refactored state handling”. Separate new behaviour from fixes and explain important migration steps. For this wiki, edit Next update rather than creating a second patch-notes file. The repository’s contribution guide explains how a dated entry is published."}]},{"id":"compatibility","title":"Checking game updates","text":"Checking game updates Treat an update as a fresh compatibility check, not an automatic reason to change code. Record the build Write down the Steam build ID, executable version, UE4SS version/commit and your mod version. Keep a copy of the previous release so you can distinguish a new game change from a changed script. The local demo records used build 26083142 and a UE5.7 release-branch executable. These are recorded reference points, not a claim that the reader’s installed game is that build. Check the integration surface Area Check Hooks Function exists, class is loaded, signature and callback parameters still match. Properties Field type and generated name still match; don’t reuse an old offset. Assets Expected class, mesh, material and sound actually load. UI Construction, input, layout, close/reopen and duplicate prevention. Gameplay Equip, fire, hit, switch weapon, restart and travel. Saves Read-only checks first; controlled write/restore tests on a disposable profile. Test in stages Launch the loader without your feature enabled. Enable the mod and confirm clean startup. Trigger one relevant callback and inspect its arguments. Use the actual menu or gameplay feature. Repeat operations that rebuild objects or travel worlds. Run separate multi-PC tests for a multiplayer feature. Stop changing code once a check passes unless there is a new failure or unresolved concern. A large refactor during a compatibility audit makes the result harder to interpret. Record what passed Keep test descriptions specific: “registered eight hooks” is different from “both peers fired and hit the same target”. Record the test method and its limits along with the result. The recorded October demo update passed local menu, travel, model and synthetic gameplay checks. The real two-PC scenarios still had a narrower verified scope. Those two facts can coexist. Report a regression Include reproduction steps, exact content names, version details, a fresh relevant log excerpt and what changed from the last working build. Remove personal paths and IDs before posting logs publicly. If you cannot reproduce a reported issue, preserve the report and the tested limits. Don’t mark it fixed because an unrelated synthetic case passed. Walkthrough: audit one update without guessing 1. Freeze the comparison Keep the previous tested archive and record its checksum. Save a copy of the known-good mod configuration. Make a note of what the player actually confirmed in the old version; do not retroactively treat every planned feature as tested. 2. Test bootstrap independently Confirm the updated game starts, then the loader starts, then your mod starts. Use distinct log milestones. If the initial loader check fails, changing target-scoring code is not a focused response. 3. Compare the integration schema For every required hook or property, compare the full owner/path and reflected types. Record missing, moved or changed entries. For assets, validate type as well as path; an object loaded under a name is not automatically the class or mesh you expected. 4. Re-run relevant native checks Use small fixtures for binding-sensitive calls such as container conversion, child-actor removal and widget construction. If they pass, keep their limits in the report. A synthetic component test is not proof a full player assembly persists correctly. 5. Repeat the player path Open the actual menu, trigger the feature, switch context, travel and restart as appropriate. A UI rework can preserve a class name but change parent panels, event order or focus behaviour, so schema equality does not end the audit. 6. Decide whether a code change is needed If the relevant checks pass and no specific regression remains, report compatibility instead of inventing a fix. If a check fails, make the smallest supported change and repeat the affected checks. Keep unrelated feature work out of the compatibility patch. Results template Build and loader: Baseline release: Schema differences: Startup result: Native fixture results: Actual menu / gameplay results: Real-peer results, if applicable: Save / backup checks: Remaining limits: Artifact checksum:","sections":[{"id":"record-the-build","title":"Record the build","text":"Write down the Steam build ID, executable version, UE4SS version/commit and your mod version. Keep a copy of the previous release so you can distinguish a new game change from a changed script. The local demo records used build 26083142 and a UE5.7 release-branch executable. These are recorded reference points, not a claim that the reader’s installed game is that build."},{"id":"check-the-surface","title":"Check the integration surface","text":"Area Check Hooks Function exists, class is loaded, signature and callback parameters still match. Properties Field type and generated name still match; don’t reuse an old offset. Assets Expected class, mesh, material and sound actually load. UI Construction, input, layout, close/reopen and duplicate prevention. Gameplay Equip, fire, hit, switch weapon, restart and travel. Saves Read-only checks first; controlled write/restore tests on a disposable profile."},{"id":"test-in-stages","title":"Test in stages","text":"Launch the loader without your feature enabled. Enable the mod and confirm clean startup. Trigger one relevant callback and inspect its arguments. Use the actual menu or gameplay feature. Repeat operations that rebuild objects or travel worlds. Run separate multi-PC tests for a multiplayer feature. Stop changing code once a check passes unless there is a new failure or unresolved concern. A large refactor during a compatibility audit makes the result harder to interpret."},{"id":"record-what-passed","title":"Record what passed","text":"Keep test descriptions specific: “registered eight hooks” is different from “both peers fired and hit the same target”. Record the test method and its limits along with the result. The recorded October demo update passed local menu, travel, model and synthetic gameplay checks. The real two-PC scenarios still had a narrower verified scope. Those two facts can coexist."},{"id":"report-a-regression","title":"Report a regression","text":"Include reproduction steps, exact content names, version details, a fresh relevant log excerpt and what changed from the last working build. Remove personal paths and IDs before posting logs publicly. If you cannot reproduce a reported issue, preserve the report and the tested limits. Don’t mark it fixed because an unrelated synthetic case passed."},{"id":"compatibility-lab","title":"Walkthrough: audit one update without guessing","text":""},{"id":"compat-step-freeze","title":"1. Freeze the comparison","text":"Keep the previous tested archive and record its checksum. Save a copy of the known-good mod configuration. Make a note of what the player actually confirmed in the old version; do not retroactively treat every planned feature as tested."},{"id":"compat-step-bootstrap","title":"2. Test bootstrap independently","text":"Confirm the updated game starts, then the loader starts, then your mod starts. Use distinct log milestones. If the initial loader check fails, changing target-scoring code is not a focused response."},{"id":"compat-step-schema","title":"3. Compare the integration schema","text":"For every required hook or property, compare the full owner/path and reflected types. Record missing, moved or changed entries. For assets, validate type as well as path; an object loaded under a name is not automatically the class or mesh you expected."},{"id":"compat-step-native","title":"4. Re-run relevant native checks","text":"Use small fixtures for binding-sensitive calls such as container conversion, child-actor removal and widget construction. If they pass, keep their limits in the report. A synthetic component test is not proof a full player assembly persists correctly."},{"id":"compat-step-gameplay","title":"5. Repeat the player path","text":"Open the actual menu, trigger the feature, switch context, travel and restart as appropriate. A UI rework can preserve a class name but change parent panels, event order or focus behaviour, so schema equality does not end the audit."},{"id":"compat-step-decide","title":"6. Decide whether a code change is needed","text":"If the relevant checks pass and no specific regression remains, report compatibility instead of inventing a fix. If a check fails, make the smallest supported change and repeat the affected checks. Keep unrelated feature work out of the compatibility patch."},{"id":"compatibility-results-template","title":"Results template","text":"Build and loader: Baseline release: Schema differences: Startup result: Native fixture results: Actual menu / gameplay results: Real-peer results, if applicable: Save / backup checks: Remaining limits: Artifact checksum:"}]},{"id":"coverage","title":"Coverage & testing notes","text":"Coverage & testing notes What the wiki explains, what was exercised, and what still needs research. How to read coverage The README percentage is a rough estimate of game-specific documentation, not a measured fraction of all source code, assets or possible mods. Adding setup pages does not establish that more gameplay systems are fully understood. The useful measure is whether a guide gives you a reliable entry point, an example and a clear limit. The table below makes those limits visible without treating every topic as complete. System coverage Area Covered here Still missing Lua setup & lifecycle Loader layout, hooks, discovery, threading, cleanup and debugging. Every loader version and complete API reference. Weapons & attachments Assembly references, rail nesting, native removal and selected stat fields. Every weapon, assembly rule and save/reload combination. Shots & ammunition Shot, projectile-impact and target callback entry points. Complete cartridge construction and ballistic equations. Targets & scoring Discovery, component motion, valid hits, deduplication and custom round design. All target types, rewards and full progression rules. Workshop UI Native button construction, canvas layout and edit-mode lifecycle. Every menu, controller navigation and all accessibility paths. Ranges Table rows, unlock checks, pool travel and restart workflow. A custom cooked-map pipeline and all full-game destinations. Saves & ownership Known slot/class, read-only loading and container distinctions. Full save schema, reliable owned-only filter and all migration cases. Multiplayer State/event separation, authority, visual proxies and interruption handling. A general-purpose SDK and completed real-peer testing of every feature. Expanded subsystem guide map The wiki now has 60 guides plus the changelog. This map shows the practical surface covered by the chapters, not a claim that every internal formula or full-game content path is known. Area Go deeper Engine fundamentals Reflection , names/references , containers , spaces , world/player context , dump reading . Weapons Cartridges/projectiles , magazines , aim/camera/recoil , with the existing assembly/attachment/stat walkthroughs. Input and UI Focus/cursors , panels/layout , HUDs/nameplates . Runtime content Loading , ownership/spawning , materials/textures , lighting , physics , audio , effects . Networking Sessions/invites , protocol/events , interpolation , timing , validated text . Worked investigations Target snapshot source , runtime range plan , curated hook evidence , failure diagnosis . Evidence levels Found in a dump: the name or field existed in the inspected build. Native fixture: an operation ran against actual engine objects in an isolated test. Local gameplay check: the feature was exercised in the game’s menu or range. Real peer check: separate PCs/accounts performed the scenario together. These levels are different, not interchangeable. A native fixture does not automatically prove a player’s saved assembly or an internet session works. Known open questions The exact owned-only attachment rule remains unresolved. A reported rail-removal stat penalty was not reproduced by the recorded synthetic fixture. A visible custom range card did not establish a new map, and runtime-scene lighting experiments did not produce a finished custom range. The ballistics , ownership and multiplayer guides explain the relevant boundaries in more detail. Add a finding Record the game build, loader version, exact object/function and steps you tested. A small reproducible result is more useful than a long list of untested classes. Follow the repository’s CONTRIBUTING.md when opening a documentation PR. How to add a documented capability Describe the exact player or modder action the guide is meant to enable. List the required game objects and loader APIs. Mark which names are only observed in a dump and which operations were actually exercised. Provide prerequisites, an example’s required context and an expected observable result. Include at least one failure case and a way to return to a known state. Record the game and loader builds that support the result. A new page can make an existing capability easier to use without expanding the known game API. Conversely, one carefully verified function can add useful coverage even before it warrants a long article. Do not use page count as a substitute for evidence. How the examples are labelled Complete files include their required imports and startup wiring. Context fragments explicitly name variables that must already be valid. Pure Lua patterns operate on ordinary tables and values. Illustrative output shows a format and is not presented as a captured run. The downloadable inspector is educational source adapted from recorded local patterns. Documentation builds, HTML checks and mock tests do not establish that a real engine object or future game build has the same behaviour. The testing guide explains the separate checks. Research priorities The largest remaining gaps include complete cartridge construction and ballistics internals, a reliable ownership rule for every attachment, full save schema/migrations, every weapon/target type and a supported custom cooked-content pipeline. New beginner material helps people start, but those gaps need direct investigation rather than longer generic explanations.","sections":[{"id":"how-to-read-coverage","title":"How to read coverage","text":"The README percentage is a rough estimate of game-specific documentation, not a measured fraction of all source code, assets or possible mods. Adding setup pages does not establish that more gameplay systems are fully understood. The useful measure is whether a guide gives you a reliable entry point, an example and a clear limit. The table below makes those limits visible without treating every topic as complete."},{"id":"system-coverage","title":"System coverage","text":"Area Covered here Still missing Lua setup & lifecycle Loader layout, hooks, discovery, threading, cleanup and debugging. Every loader version and complete API reference. Weapons & attachments Assembly references, rail nesting, native removal and selected stat fields. Every weapon, assembly rule and save/reload combination. Shots & ammunition Shot, projectile-impact and target callback entry points. Complete cartridge construction and ballistic equations. Targets & scoring Discovery, component motion, valid hits, deduplication and custom round design. All target types, rewards and full progression rules. Workshop UI Native button construction, canvas layout and edit-mode lifecycle. Every menu, controller navigation and all accessibility paths. Ranges Table rows, unlock checks, pool travel and restart workflow. A custom cooked-map pipeline and all full-game destinations. Saves & ownership Known slot/class, read-only loading and container distinctions. Full save schema, reliable owned-only filter and all migration cases. Multiplayer State/event separation, authority, visual proxies and interruption handling. A general-purpose SDK and completed real-peer testing of every feature."},{"id":"expanded-guide-map","title":"Expanded subsystem guide map","text":"The wiki now has 60 guides plus the changelog. This map shows the practical surface covered by the chapters, not a claim that every internal formula or full-game content path is known. Area Go deeper Engine fundamentals Reflection , names/references , containers , spaces , world/player context , dump reading . Weapons Cartridges/projectiles , magazines , aim/camera/recoil , with the existing assembly/attachment/stat walkthroughs. Input and UI Focus/cursors , panels/layout , HUDs/nameplates . Runtime content Loading , ownership/spawning , materials/textures , lighting , physics , audio , effects . Networking Sessions/invites , protocol/events , interpolation , timing , validated text . Worked investigations Target snapshot source , runtime range plan , curated hook evidence , failure diagnosis ."},{"id":"evidence-levels","title":"Evidence levels","text":"Found in a dump: the name or field existed in the inspected build. Native fixture: an operation ran against actual engine objects in an isolated test. Local gameplay check: the feature was exercised in the game’s menu or range. Real peer check: separate PCs/accounts performed the scenario together. These levels are different, not interchangeable. A native fixture does not automatically prove a player’s saved assembly or an internet session works."},{"id":"known-open-questions","title":"Known open questions","text":"The exact owned-only attachment rule remains unresolved. A reported rail-removal stat penalty was not reproduced by the recorded synthetic fixture. A visible custom range card did not establish a new map, and runtime-scene lighting experiments did not produce a finished custom range. The ballistics , ownership and multiplayer guides explain the relevant boundaries in more detail."},{"id":"add-a-finding","title":"Add a finding","text":"Record the game build, loader version, exact object/function and steps you tested. A small reproducible result is more useful than a long list of untested classes. Follow the repository’s CONTRIBUTING.md when opening a documentation PR."},{"id":"coverage-audit-method","title":"How to add a documented capability","text":"Describe the exact player or modder action the guide is meant to enable. List the required game objects and loader APIs. Mark which names are only observed in a dump and which operations were actually exercised. Provide prerequisites, an example’s required context and an expected observable result. Include at least one failure case and a way to return to a known state. Record the game and loader builds that support the result. A new page can make an existing capability easier to use without expanding the known game API. Conversely, one carefully verified function can add useful coverage even before it warrants a long article. Do not use page count as a substitute for evidence."},{"id":"coverage-example-labels","title":"How the examples are labelled","text":"Complete files include their required imports and startup wiring. Context fragments explicitly name variables that must already be valid. Pure Lua patterns operate on ordinary tables and values. Illustrative output shows a format and is not presented as a captured run. The downloadable inspector is educational source adapted from recorded local patterns. Documentation builds, HTML checks and mock tests do not establish that a real engine object or future game build has the same behaviour. The testing guide explains the separate checks."},{"id":"coverage-priorities","title":"Research priorities","text":"The largest remaining gaps include complete cartridge construction and ballistics internals, a reliable ownership rule for every attachment, full save schema/migrations, every weapon/target type and a supported custom cooked-content pipeline. New beginner material helps people start, but those gaps need direct investigation rather than longer generic explanations."}]},{"id":"tools","title":"Tools & resources","text":"Tools & resources The main references to keep open while you work. UE4SS The loader runs Lua mods and exposes Unreal objects and functions. Match API guidance to your installed version; the development documentation may describe APIs newer than your local loader. Releases and downloads Installation guide Development documentation Lua API overview Game inspection Start with the UE4SS object viewer and object dump to inspect what the current game session has loaded. Use an archive browser when you need to look for cooked content that isn’t loaded yet. FModel — Unreal archive browser. FModel getting started — configuration and usage. Tool support depends on the game’s engine and archive format. Finding an asset in an archive does not guarantee it can be loaded in every game mode. Unreal modding UE_Modding by Dmgvol collects techniques and guides for Unreal games. Use it to research asset-based mods alongside the Lua approach described here. The game Ballistic.exe on Steam This wiki documents local observations and practical examples. It does not ship a game framework, SDK or game assets. Choose a tool by the question Question Start with Expected evidence Did my script load? A fresh UE4SS log. Your unique startup prefix and version. What functions exist now? Object viewer / object dump. Exact loaded names and reflected signatures. Is an asset in the installation? Archive browser. Package presence and dependencies to investigate. Can that asset run here? A small game-thread load/spawn probe. A valid object and a visible/functional result. Did my logic handle duplicates? A pure Lua test or controlled fixture. An asserted state transition. Does this work over Steam? Two real PCs/accounts. Both screens agree under the tested conditions. A practical discovery session Pick one behaviour and reproduce it normally in the game. Locate the relevant class/function in the loaded object view. Copy the exact path and parameter types into a small research note. Install a read-only logging hook after the class is loaded. Trigger the normal behaviour and compare callback arguments. Only then prototype a change using a disposable context. Archive inspection and object inspection answer different questions. Keep both results when they disagree instead of assuming one tool is wrong: the asset may exist without being loaded, or a reference may point at content unavailable in this build. Version drift in documentation The development UE4SS docs can describe features newer than the local loader. Prefer a small known API surface when writing a portable example, and check the installed version before adopting a new delayed-action or mod-management API. Keep links to the primary references with your notes. A copied snippet without its API version and required context is harder to maintain than a short explained example.","sections":[{"id":"ue4ss","title":"UE4SS","text":"The loader runs Lua mods and exposes Unreal objects and functions. Match API guidance to your installed version; the development documentation may describe APIs newer than your local loader. Releases and downloads Installation guide Development documentation Lua API overview"},{"id":"game-inspection","title":"Game inspection","text":"Start with the UE4SS object viewer and object dump to inspect what the current game session has loaded. Use an archive browser when you need to look for cooked content that isn’t loaded yet. FModel — Unreal archive browser. FModel getting started — configuration and usage. Tool support depends on the game’s engine and archive format. Finding an asset in an archive does not guarantee it can be loaded in every game mode."},{"id":"unreal-modding","title":"Unreal modding","text":"UE_Modding by Dmgvol collects techniques and guides for Unreal games. Use it to research asset-based mods alongside the Lua approach described here."},{"id":"the-game","title":"The game","text":"Ballistic.exe on Steam This wiki documents local observations and practical examples. It does not ship a game framework, SDK or game assets."},{"id":"tool-selection-workflow","title":"Choose a tool by the question","text":"Question Start with Expected evidence Did my script load? A fresh UE4SS log. Your unique startup prefix and version. What functions exist now? Object viewer / object dump. Exact loaded names and reflected signatures. Is an asset in the installation? Archive browser. Package presence and dependencies to investigate. Can that asset run here? A small game-thread load/spawn probe. A valid object and a visible/functional result. Did my logic handle duplicates? A pure Lua test or controlled fixture. An asserted state transition. Does this work over Steam? Two real PCs/accounts. Both screens agree under the tested conditions."},{"id":"tool-use-step-by-step","title":"A practical discovery session","text":"Pick one behaviour and reproduce it normally in the game. Locate the relevant class/function in the loaded object view. Copy the exact path and parameter types into a small research note. Install a read-only logging hook after the class is loaded. Trigger the normal behaviour and compare callback arguments. Only then prototype a change using a disposable context. Archive inspection and object inspection answer different questions. Keep both results when they disagree instead of assuming one tool is wrong: the asset may exist without being loaded, or a reference may point at content unavailable in this build."},{"id":"tool-version-drift","title":"Version drift in documentation","text":"The development UE4SS docs can describe features newer than the local loader. Prefer a small known API surface when writing a portable example, and check the installed version before adopting a new delayed-action or mod-management API. Keep links to the primary references with your notes. A copied snippet without its API version and required context is harder to maintain than a short explained example."}]},{"id":"hook-reference","title":"Game hook reference","text":"Game hook reference Look up exact reflected paths with a statement of what was observed and what was tested. Read the evidence before the call This is a curated reference, not a recovered SDK. Each path below was checked against the local object dump when the catalog was created. “Recorded local integration path” means the related callback/action appears in the recorded local checks; the notes preserve the narrower test scope. It does not promise every parameter or assembly works on your build. A dump-observed getter is a discovery point until a runtime check establishes the intended call. Verify your game/loader version and the loaded owning class before installing a hook. The current RegisterHook semantics are explained in Working with hooks . Curated function catalog GetCompatibleMountsRecursive Workbench · Recorded local integration path /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/BunkerHUDItems/Attach_POI.Attach_POI_C:GetCompatibleMountsRecursive Mount-compatibility lookup used by the expanded attachment-list integration. Signature note: Mounts and an output name array; inspect wrapper/output semantics. Scope: Recorded lookup expansion and menu checks do not validate every possible assembly. OnGetUserMenuContent Workbench · Recorded local integration path /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/BunkerHUDItems/Attach_POI.Attach_POI_C:OnGetUserMenuContent Attachment popup construction path. Signature note: Widget return value in the schema; recorded Blueprint post callback did not supply it as first expected. Scope: The local tab code used the stored AttachmentsList property instead. SetupAttachment Workbench · Recorded local integration path /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/BunkerHUDItems/Attach_POI.Attach_POI_C:SetupAttachment Fit an attachment class or take the native removal path. Signature note: Attachment is a class reference; nil removed the test child. Scope: Synthetic removal/batch fixtures passed; all real saved assemblies were not covered. UpdateWeaponEditDisplay Workbench · Recorded local integration path /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/BunkerHUDItems/WeaponEditMOde.WeaponEditMOde_C:UpdateWeaponEditDisplay Editor refresh point used by control-lifecycle code. Signature note: Context observed; inspect full current signature before using additional inputs. Scope: A refresh can rebuild point/widget references. EnterWeaponEditMode Workbench · Recorded local integration path /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/HUD_Workshop.HUD_Workshop_C:EnterWeaponEditMode HUD edit-mode entry lifecycle. Signature note: Context observed; this catalog does not reconstruct all inputs. Scope: Used to align custom control visibility with the actual mode. LeaveWeaponEditMode Workbench · Recorded local integration path /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/HUD_Workshop.HUD_Workshop_C:LeaveWeaponEditMode HUD edit-mode exit lifecycle. Signature note: Context observed; inspect the current owning class/signature. Scope: Pending context-dependent actions must cancel on exit. UpdateCharacteristicsFromParts Weapons · Recorded local integration path /Game/BlueprintLogic/Weapons/A_WeaponBase.A_WeaponBase_C:UpdateCharacteristicsFromParts Recalculate selected groups of derived characteristics. Signature note: Nine booleans: Weight, Ergo, CenterMassLoc, Recoil, GripCenter, BarrelLength, EquippedScopeAndLaser, Cooling, TotalLength. Scope: A mutating gameplay recalculation, not simply a redraw call. ProcessBulletFired Weapons · Recorded local integration path /Game/BlueprintLogic/Weapons/A_WeaponBase.A_WeaponBase_C:ProcessBulletFired Firing event used for shot identity and projectile capture. Signature note: Cartridge, two recoil forces, recoil location/direction, Barrel, PowderAmount, ExitPressure, PowderType, Bullet array. Scope: Hook resolution/local native integration does not prove every real-peer firing scenario. RegisterHit Ballistics · Recorded local integration path /Script/Ballistic.C_BulletBase:RegisterHit Bullet-level impact entry used by local impact capture. Signature note: ImpactInfo struct; copy needed ordinary values within the callback. Scope: An impact is not automatically an accepted scoring target hit. CurrentEnergyInJoules Ballistics · Dump-observed; separate runtime check needed /Script/Ballistic.C_BulletBase:CurrentEnergyInJoules An energy-named native function with a surface-type input, discovered in the schema. Signature note: SurfaceType enum input and a float return in the inspected dump; verify the enum domain before calling. Scope: Not a proven zero-argument raw-energy getter or a recovered ballistic formula; no fresh runtime call in this documentation pass. TargetBase Hit Targets · Recorded local integration path /Game/BlueprintLogic/Targets/TargetBase.TargetBase_C:Hit Target-side path used to connect a bullet to the target. Signature note: Observed capture uses impact info, player, velocity, stance and bullet context; confirm exact current names/types. Scope: Keep attribution separate from the valid-hit award decision. TargetBase OnHit Targets · Recorded local integration path /Game/BlueprintLogic/Targets/TargetBase.TargetBase_C:OnHit Valid-hit result and target/reward-related flags. Signature note: ValidHit, Player, ImpactInfo, HitEnergy, HitVelocity, TriggeredBonus, TriggeredCartUnlock, BreakingHit. Scope: Replaying reward paths for remote cosmetics can duplicate progression. CharacterTargetBase Hit Targets · Recorded local integration path /Game/BlueprintLogic/Targets/CharacterTargets/CharacterTargetBase.CharacterTargetBase_C:Hit Character-target attribution path. Signature note: Inspect the current derived-class signature; do not assume one base callback covers every class. Scope: Deduplicate if a single action reaches more than one observed handler. CharacterTargetBase OnHit Targets · Recorded local integration path /Game/BlueprintLogic/Targets/CharacterTargets/CharacterTargetBase.CharacterTargetBase_C:OnHit Character-target result path. Signature note: The inspected result has validity/player/impact/energy/velocity/bonus/unlock/breaking fields. Scope: Local native callback checks are narrower than universal target support. ReleaseMagazine Handling · Recorded local integration path /Game/BlueprintLogic/Weapons/A_WeaponBase.A_WeaponBase_C:ReleaseMagazine Weapon-level handling capture entry. Signature note: Weapon context is used; confirm remaining signature in the current build. Scope: Do not count every related class release function as a distinct reload. OnSnappedToSlot Handling · Recorded local integration path /Game/BlueprintLogic/Weapons/Parts/Base_BoxMagazine.Base_BoxMagazine_C:OnSnappedToSlot Magazine insertion/snapping capture entry. Signature note: Part context is used with a validated ParentWeapon reference. Scope: A cosmetic reload event is not proof every inventory/feed operation completed. SetAiming Handling · Dump-observed; separate runtime check needed /Game/BlueprintLogic/Weapons/A_WeaponBase.A_WeaponBase_C:SetAiming Weapon-base aiming entry point. Signature note: NewAiming boolean observed in the schema. Scope: Do not assume calling it alone reproduces the pawn/camera/stance flow. GetCurrentErgo Weapons · Dump-observed; separate runtime check needed /Script/Ballistic.C_WeaponPart:GetCurrentErgo Part-level ergonomics getter name in the native schema. Signature note: Float return observed. Scope: Part getter, current weapon total and installed total are different references. PreConstruct Range UI · Recorded local integration path /Game/UI/Menus/RangeSelectionMenu/RangeList.RangeList_C:PreConstruct Range-list construction integration point. Signature note: Inspect the actual loaded class and current preconstruct signature. Scope: A visible card does not prove its destination map is usable. SetSelected Range UI · Recorded local integration path /Game/UI/Menus/MenuItems/RangeListItem.RangeListItem_C:SetSelected Range-card selection integration point. Signature note: Selection/context observed; verify current inputs before writing selection. Scope: Recorded custom-card work eventually opened the existing pool world. How this reference is maintained The table of entries comes from observed-hooks.json . The build validates identifiers/evidence labels and regenerates the catalog from that file. It does not inspect your running game or certify compatibility with a newly installed update. For a new finding, include the actual path, types/context, test method and what remains untested. Do not promote a symbol from dump-only to exercised because a similarly named function worked elsewhere. Follow Reading object dumps and the testing workflow .","sections":[{"id":"using-reference","title":"Read the evidence before the call","text":"This is a curated reference, not a recovered SDK. Each path below was checked against the local object dump when the catalog was created. “Recorded local integration path” means the related callback/action appears in the recorded local checks; the notes preserve the narrower test scope. It does not promise every parameter or assembly works on your build. A dump-observed getter is a discovery point until a runtime check establishes the intended call. Verify your game/loader version and the loaded owning class before installing a hook. The current RegisterHook semantics are explained in Working with hooks ."},{"id":"catalog","title":"Curated function catalog","text":""},{"id":"ref-mount-lookup","title":"GetCompatibleMountsRecursive","text":"Workbench · Recorded local integration path /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/BunkerHUDItems/Attach_POI.Attach_POI_C:GetCompatibleMountsRecursive Mount-compatibility lookup used by the expanded attachment-list integration. Signature note: Mounts and an output name array; inspect wrapper/output semantics. Scope: Recorded lookup expansion and menu checks do not validate every possible assembly."},{"id":"ref-popup","title":"OnGetUserMenuContent","text":"Workbench · Recorded local integration path /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/BunkerHUDItems/Attach_POI.Attach_POI_C:OnGetUserMenuContent Attachment popup construction path. Signature note: Widget return value in the schema; recorded Blueprint post callback did not supply it as first expected. Scope: The local tab code used the stored AttachmentsList property instead."},{"id":"ref-fit-remove","title":"SetupAttachment","text":"Workbench · Recorded local integration path /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/BunkerHUDItems/Attach_POI.Attach_POI_C:SetupAttachment Fit an attachment class or take the native removal path. Signature note: Attachment is a class reference; nil removed the test child. Scope: Synthetic removal/batch fixtures passed; all real saved assemblies were not covered."},{"id":"ref-editor-refresh","title":"UpdateWeaponEditDisplay","text":"Workbench · Recorded local integration path /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/BunkerHUDItems/WeaponEditMOde.WeaponEditMOde_C:UpdateWeaponEditDisplay Editor refresh point used by control-lifecycle code. Signature note: Context observed; inspect full current signature before using additional inputs. Scope: A refresh can rebuild point/widget references."},{"id":"ref-edit-enter","title":"EnterWeaponEditMode","text":"Workbench · Recorded local integration path /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/HUD_Workshop.HUD_Workshop_C:EnterWeaponEditMode HUD edit-mode entry lifecycle. Signature note: Context observed; this catalog does not reconstruct all inputs. Scope: Used to align custom control visibility with the actual mode."},{"id":"ref-edit-leave","title":"LeaveWeaponEditMode","text":"Workbench · Recorded local integration path /Game/UI/HUD/Workshop/HUD_Workshop.HUD_Workshop_C:LeaveWeaponEditMode HUD edit-mode exit lifecycle. Signature note: Context observed; inspect the current owning class/signature. Scope: Pending context-dependent actions must cancel on exit."},{"id":"ref-characteristics","title":"UpdateCharacteristicsFromParts","text":"Weapons · Recorded local integration path /Game/BlueprintLogic/Weapons/A_WeaponBase.A_WeaponBase_C:UpdateCharacteristicsFromParts Recalculate selected groups of derived characteristics. Signature note: Nine booleans: Weight, Ergo, CenterMassLoc, Recoil, GripCenter, BarrelLength, EquippedScopeAndLaser, Cooling, TotalLength. Scope: A mutating gameplay recalculation, not simply a redraw call."},{"id":"ref-fired","title":"ProcessBulletFired","text":"Weapons · Recorded local integration path /Game/BlueprintLogic/Weapons/A_WeaponBase.A_WeaponBase_C:ProcessBulletFired Firing event used for shot identity and projectile capture. Signature note: Cartridge, two recoil forces, recoil location/direction, Barrel, PowderAmount, ExitPressure, PowderType, Bullet array. Scope: Hook resolution/local native integration does not prove every real-peer firing scenario."},{"id":"ref-impact","title":"RegisterHit","text":"Ballistics · Recorded local integration path /Script/Ballistic.C_BulletBase:RegisterHit Bullet-level impact entry used by local impact capture. Signature note: ImpactInfo struct; copy needed ordinary values within the callback. Scope: An impact is not automatically an accepted scoring target hit."},{"id":"ref-energy","title":"CurrentEnergyInJoules","text":"Ballistics · Dump-observed; separate runtime check needed /Script/Ballistic.C_BulletBase:CurrentEnergyInJoules An energy-named native function with a surface-type input, discovered in the schema. Signature note: SurfaceType enum input and a float return in the inspected dump; verify the enum domain before calling. Scope: Not a proven zero-argument raw-energy getter or a recovered ballistic formula; no fresh runtime call in this documentation pass."},{"id":"ref-target-hit","title":"TargetBase Hit","text":"Targets · Recorded local integration path /Game/BlueprintLogic/Targets/TargetBase.TargetBase_C:Hit Target-side path used to connect a bullet to the target. Signature note: Observed capture uses impact info, player, velocity, stance and bullet context; confirm exact current names/types. Scope: Keep attribution separate from the valid-hit award decision."},{"id":"ref-target-result","title":"TargetBase OnHit","text":"Targets · Recorded local integration path /Game/BlueprintLogic/Targets/TargetBase.TargetBase_C:OnHit Valid-hit result and target/reward-related flags. Signature note: ValidHit, Player, ImpactInfo, HitEnergy, HitVelocity, TriggeredBonus, TriggeredCartUnlock, BreakingHit. Scope: Replaying reward paths for remote cosmetics can duplicate progression."},{"id":"ref-character-hit","title":"CharacterTargetBase Hit","text":"Targets · Recorded local integration path /Game/BlueprintLogic/Targets/CharacterTargets/CharacterTargetBase.CharacterTargetBase_C:Hit Character-target attribution path. Signature note: Inspect the current derived-class signature; do not assume one base callback covers every class. Scope: Deduplicate if a single action reaches more than one observed handler."},{"id":"ref-character-result","title":"CharacterTargetBase OnHit","text":"Targets · Recorded local integration path /Game/BlueprintLogic/Targets/CharacterTargets/CharacterTargetBase.CharacterTargetBase_C:OnHit Character-target result path. Signature note: The inspected result has validity/player/impact/energy/velocity/bonus/unlock/breaking fields. Scope: Local native callback checks are narrower than universal target support."},{"id":"ref-mag-release","title":"ReleaseMagazine","text":"Handling · Recorded local integration path /Game/BlueprintLogic/Weapons/A_WeaponBase.A_WeaponBase_C:ReleaseMagazine Weapon-level handling capture entry. Signature note: Weapon context is used; confirm remaining signature in the current build. Scope: Do not count every related class release function as a distinct reload."},{"id":"ref-mag-insert","title":"OnSnappedToSlot","text":"Handling · Recorded local integration path /Game/BlueprintLogic/Weapons/Parts/Base_BoxMagazine.Base_BoxMagazine_C:OnSnappedToSlot Magazine insertion/snapping capture entry. Signature note: Part context is used with a validated ParentWeapon reference. Scope: A cosmetic reload event is not proof every inventory/feed operation completed."},{"id":"ref-aim","title":"SetAiming","text":"Handling · Dump-observed; separate runtime check needed /Game/BlueprintLogic/Weapons/A_WeaponBase.A_WeaponBase_C:SetAiming Weapon-base aiming entry point. Signature note: NewAiming boolean observed in the schema. Scope: Do not assume calling it alone reproduces the pawn/camera/stance flow."},{"id":"ref-current-ergo","title":"GetCurrentErgo","text":"Weapons · Dump-observed; separate runtime check needed /Script/Ballistic.C_WeaponPart:GetCurrentErgo Part-level ergonomics getter name in the native schema. Signature note: Float return observed. Scope: Part getter, current weapon total and installed total are different references."},{"id":"ref-range-list","title":"PreConstruct","text":"Range UI · Recorded local integration path /Game/UI/Menus/RangeSelectionMenu/RangeList.RangeList_C:PreConstruct Range-list construction integration point. Signature note: Inspect the actual loaded class and current preconstruct signature. Scope: A visible card does not prove its destination map is usable."},{"id":"ref-range-selected","title":"SetSelected","text":"Range UI · Recorded local integration path /Game/UI/Menus/MenuItems/RangeListItem.RangeListItem_C:SetSelected Range-card selection integration point. Signature note: Selection/context observed; verify current inputs before writing selection. Scope: Recorded custom-card work eventually opened the existing pool world."},{"id":"data","title":"How this reference is maintained","text":"The table of entries comes from observed-hooks.json . The build validates identifiers/evidence labels and regenerates the catalog from that file. It does not inspect your running game or certify compatibility with a newly installed update. For a new finding, include the actual path, types/context, test method and what remains untested. Do not promote a symbol from dump-only to exercised because a similarly named function worked elsewhere. Follow Reading object dumps and the testing workflow ."}]},{"id":"glossary","title":"Modding glossary","text":"Modding glossary A practical vocabulary for reading the guides, logs and reflected game surface. Use definitions as a starting point These short definitions explain how the wiki uses common terms. Follow the linked guide for examples, required context and limitations. The same word can have different meanings at different layers: a map container is not a playable map, and a display name is not a network identity. Actor A world-owned gameplay object. A visible mesh may be on a component or child actor rather than on the root itself. Read the related guide → Component An object providing part of an actor’s structure or behaviour, such as its visible mesh or physics body. Read the related guide → Class The type used to create or identify instances. It is not interchangeable with a live object of that type. Read the related guide → Class default object The class’s defaults, distinct from the player’s current live instance. Changing defaults is not a targeted edit of one gun. Read the related guide → Reflection The named type/property/function surface exposed by Unreal and consumed by the binding. Not every C++ symbol is exposed. Read the related guide → UFunction A reflected function candidate. Hook availability also depends on loading, signature and the installed binding. Read the related guide → Delegate An event/callback binding concept; not automatically a function supported by RegisterHook. Read the related guide → Parameter wrapper A binding object around callback/container data. Unwrap and copy needed values according to the actual type and lifetime. Read the related guide → FName An Unreal identifier type. Use the representation expected by the target API instead of assuming it is a Lua string. Read the related guide → FText Unreal display/localisation text. Useful for native widget text setters, distinct from a persistent gameplay ID. Read the related guide → Soft reference A deferred content reference, not proof that the referenced class/object is currently loaded. Read the related guide → Array An ordered container. Reflected array binding/index rules must be checked separately from ordinary Lua lists. Read the related guide → Set A typed membership collection. A loaded class and a soft class reference are not automatically equivalent keys. Read the related guide → Map container A key/value collection. Distinct from “map” meaning a playable world/package. Read the related guide → World The current runtime context containing actors. World-relative references and clocks need invalidation on travel. Read the related guide → Player controller Input/pawn/camera-related local context. Not a permanent identity or a substitute for host authority. Read the related guide → Pawn The possessed player actor. A cosmetic proxy model is not automatically this actor. Read the related guide → Game instance Cross-map runtime context holding observed selections/progression fields in the inspected game. Read the related guide → Game mode Authoritative mode context where available. Clients should not assume the host’s mode object is local. Read the related guide → Game state Shared gameplay/roster context; the local roster reads its player-state array. Read the related guide → World space Coordinates in the current world’s frame, distinct from a weapon-local or screen-space value. Read the related guide → Local space Coordinates relative to a chosen root/parent. Correct proxy reconstruction needs a consistent reference transform. Read the related guide → Quaternion A rotation representation with X/Y/Z/W in the observed transform shape, not Pitch/Yaw/Roll copied under new names. Read the related guide → Mobility An engine actor/component setting relevant to allowed runtime movement/assignment; verify the intended component. Read the related guide → Collision Physical/query interaction state, separate from whether an actor is visible. Read the related guide → Material instance A material-derived object used for per-instance parameter choices; parameter existence and slot still need checking. Read the related guide → Emissive diagnostic A way to help prove geometry visibility; not a complete replacement for the intended lit scene. Read the related guide → Attenuation Spatial sound presentation policy, including how a selected cue is heard with distance/context. Read the related guide → Attachment POI A workbench point widget referring to a mount component, current gun and popup list in the observed UI. Read the related guide → Weapon template Selected/persisted assembly data used to reconstruct a live gun; not itself the spawned usable actor. Read the related guide → Baseline A recorded state before the tested operation. It must use the same context/fields as the later comparison. Read the related guide → Native fixture A controlled test using actual engine objects, with narrower scope than all player saves or real network sessions. Read the related guide → Authority The role allowed to decide accepted gameplay state. A display name or received packet does not establish it. Read the related guide → Snapshot A state sample that may supersede an older sample. A save-file snapshot is also not continuous progression merging. Read the related guide → Reliable event A one-time action represented with identity/retry/acknowledgement rules so repeats can be handled safely. Read the related guide → Acknowledgement A bound statement about accepted stream content, not a licence to retire another session’s events. Read the related guide → Revision An identity for changed content such as weapon appearance, separate from a general snapshot sequence. Read the related guide → Session/world generation A boundary that prevents old connection/range messages from being applied to the new context. Read the related guide → Interpolation Rendering between known samples, often at a buffered time. It adds smoothness, not authority. Read the related guide → Extrapolation A bounded estimate beyond the latest sample. It must not move a stale proxy indefinitely. Read the related guide → Jitter Variation in update arrival intervals; different from just average latency. Read the related guide → Settling phase A bounded round phase implementing a documented end-time acceptance policy before the final result. Read the related guide → Cooked content Game-ready packaged Unreal assets, distinct from the developer’s editable source project or an external FBX. Read the related guide → Idempotent action Repeating the request does not duplicate the intended effect, such as one installed button or one accepted hit. Read the related guide → Backpressure A deliberate response when pending work exceeds capacity, rather than unbounded queues or silent loss. Read the related guide → When a name is ambiguous State the actual field, class, API or coordinate/time basis instead of relying on a broad label. “Weapon stats” can mean a per-part modifier, live current aggregate, installed aggregate or displayed UI text. “Connected” can mean invited, transport-linked or gameplay-ready. Precise terms make a bug report reproducible.","sections":[{"id":"use","title":"Use definitions as a starting point","text":"These short definitions explain how the wiki uses common terms. Follow the linked guide for examples, required context and limitations. The same word can have different meanings at different layers: a map container is not a playable map, and a display name is not a network identity."},{"id":"term-actor","title":"Actor","text":"A world-owned gameplay object. A visible mesh may be on a component or child actor rather than on the root itself. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-component","title":"Component","text":"An object providing part of an actor’s structure or behaviour, such as its visible mesh or physics body. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-class","title":"Class","text":"The type used to create or identify instances. It is not interchangeable with a live object of that type. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-default-object","title":"Class default object","text":"The class’s defaults, distinct from the player’s current live instance. Changing defaults is not a targeted edit of one gun. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-reflection","title":"Reflection","text":"The named type/property/function surface exposed by Unreal and consumed by the binding. Not every C++ symbol is exposed. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-ufunction","title":"UFunction","text":"A reflected function candidate. Hook availability also depends on loading, signature and the installed binding. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-delegate","title":"Delegate","text":"An event/callback binding concept; not automatically a function supported by RegisterHook. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-wrapper","title":"Parameter wrapper","text":"A binding object around callback/container data. Unwrap and copy needed values according to the actual type and lifetime. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-fname","title":"FName","text":"An Unreal identifier type. Use the representation expected by the target API instead of assuming it is a Lua string. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-ftext","title":"FText","text":"Unreal display/localisation text. Useful for native widget text setters, distinct from a persistent gameplay ID. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-soft-reference","title":"Soft reference","text":"A deferred content reference, not proof that the referenced class/object is currently loaded. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-array","title":"Array","text":"An ordered container. Reflected array binding/index rules must be checked separately from ordinary Lua lists. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-set","title":"Set","text":"A typed membership collection. A loaded class and a soft class reference are not automatically equivalent keys. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-map","title":"Map container","text":"A key/value collection. Distinct from “map” meaning a playable world/package. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-world","title":"World","text":"The current runtime context containing actors. World-relative references and clocks need invalidation on travel. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-controller","title":"Player controller","text":"Input/pawn/camera-related local context. Not a permanent identity or a substitute for host authority. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-pawn","title":"Pawn","text":"The possessed player actor. A cosmetic proxy model is not automatically this actor. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-game-instance","title":"Game instance","text":"Cross-map runtime context holding observed selections/progression fields in the inspected game. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-game-mode","title":"Game mode","text":"Authoritative mode context where available. Clients should not assume the host’s mode object is local. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-game-state","title":"Game state","text":"Shared gameplay/roster context; the local roster reads its player-state array. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-world-space","title":"World space","text":"Coordinates in the current world’s frame, distinct from a weapon-local or screen-space value. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-local-space","title":"Local space","text":"Coordinates relative to a chosen root/parent. Correct proxy reconstruction needs a consistent reference transform. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-quaternion","title":"Quaternion","text":"A rotation representation with X/Y/Z/W in the observed transform shape, not Pitch/Yaw/Roll copied under new names. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-mobility","title":"Mobility","text":"An engine actor/component setting relevant to allowed runtime movement/assignment; verify the intended component. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-collision","title":"Collision","text":"Physical/query interaction state, separate from whether an actor is visible. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-material-instance","title":"Material instance","text":"A material-derived object used for per-instance parameter choices; parameter existence and slot still need checking. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-emissive","title":"Emissive diagnostic","text":"A way to help prove geometry visibility; not a complete replacement for the intended lit scene. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-attenuation","title":"Attenuation","text":"Spatial sound presentation policy, including how a selected cue is heard with distance/context. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-poi","title":"Attachment POI","text":"A workbench point widget referring to a mount component, current gun and popup list in the observed UI. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-template","title":"Weapon template","text":"Selected/persisted assembly data used to reconstruct a live gun; not itself the spawned usable actor. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-baseline","title":"Baseline","text":"A recorded state before the tested operation. It must use the same context/fields as the later comparison. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-fixture","title":"Native fixture","text":"A controlled test using actual engine objects, with narrower scope than all player saves or real network sessions. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-authority","title":"Authority","text":"The role allowed to decide accepted gameplay state. A display name or received packet does not establish it. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-snapshot","title":"Snapshot","text":"A state sample that may supersede an older sample. A save-file snapshot is also not continuous progression merging. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-event","title":"Reliable event","text":"A one-time action represented with identity/retry/acknowledgement rules so repeats can be handled safely. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-acknowledgement","title":"Acknowledgement","text":"A bound statement about accepted stream content, not a licence to retire another session’s events. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-revision","title":"Revision","text":"An identity for changed content such as weapon appearance, separate from a general snapshot sequence. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-generation","title":"Session/world generation","text":"A boundary that prevents old connection/range messages from being applied to the new context. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-interpolation","title":"Interpolation","text":"Rendering between known samples, often at a buffered time. It adds smoothness, not authority. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-extrapolation","title":"Extrapolation","text":"A bounded estimate beyond the latest sample. It must not move a stale proxy indefinitely. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-jitter","title":"Jitter","text":"Variation in update arrival intervals; different from just average latency. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-settling","title":"Settling phase","text":"A bounded round phase implementing a documented end-time acceptance policy before the final result. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-cooked","title":"Cooked content","text":"Game-ready packaged Unreal assets, distinct from the developer’s editable source project or an external FBX. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-idempotent","title":"Idempotent action","text":"Repeating the request does not duplicate the intended effect, such as one installed button or one accepted hit. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"term-backpressure","title":"Backpressure","text":"A deliberate response when pending work exceeds capacity, rather than unbounded queues or silent loss. Read the related guide →"},{"id":"ambiguity","title":"When a name is ambiguous","text":"State the actual field, class, API or coordinate/time basis instead of relying on a broad label. “Weapon stats” can mean a per-part modifier, live current aggregate, installed aggregate or displayed UI text. “Connected” can mean invited, transport-linked or gameplay-ready. Precise terms make a bug report reproducible."}]},{"id":"faq","title":"Troubleshooting FAQ","text":"Troubleshooting FAQ Work through common failures by identifying the first layer that is actually broken. Why is there no startup message? Check a fresh loader log, then the actual installed folder, enabled marker, file extensions and script path. A source project and a ZIP existing on disk do not mean the loader is running either. Add a unique version prefix and follow Your first mod . The mod loads, but the key does nothing Focus the game, verify the configured key and remove duplicate/conflicting registrations in the test setup. Start with a log-only callback. If that works, the input registration is not the first broken layer; inspect object context and the scheduled action next. See Input and focus . Can I use Roblox Luau code? The examples target UE4SS Lua and its APIs. Roblox services, Instance APIs and Luau-only syntax are not supplied by the game. Translate the actual language/API assumptions rather than changing the extension on a Roblox script. Begin with Lua basics . Why does a method say TrivialObject is not callable? Confirm the member is a reflected function exposed by this build. A familiar C++ name is not proof it is available through this binding. Inspect the dump for the actual K2/reflected method and verify the owning instance before calling it. See Reflection . Why is a reference nil after it worked before? A menu rebuild, weapon switch or travel can replace the native owner. Reacquire world-specific references and cancel pending jobs with the old context. A Lua variable retaining a wrapper does not guarantee the native object remained valid. See Timers and cleanup . Why did an array loop freeze? Check whether it is a reflected Unreal container rather than a plain Lua table, and whether iteration can exceed the reported count. Also check callback recursion. The recorded wrapper issue was avoided with a bounded numeric loop; do not apply ipairs blindly to every engine container. See Arrays, sets and maps . Removing a grip makes the stat negative—is that a bug? Compare the same field/context before fitting, while fitted and after removal. A negative baseline returning to itself is different from an accumulated penalty. Repeating the exact rail/part case and checking the editor/live/save lifetimes is essential. The recorded synthetic test did not reproduce the player’s alleged residue. See Weapon stats . Why are known owned parts missing? Purchased, default, fitted, unlocked and visible are not the same states. Verify the stored reference type and a small set of named positive/negative examples against the real menu. Earlier save-index filters were not a completed reliable ownership API. See Ownership and unlocks . The custom range card works, but it opens the pool The card’s title/layout and the destination are separate. Check the actual map ID/package selected and the loaded world after travel. A new UI row does not create a new cooked map; a runtime scene can still be hosted by the pool world. See Range menus and travel . The scene spawned but is almost black Keep a known object/material fixed and inspect placement, occlusion and the actual light/postprocess components affecting the view. Visible dark silhouettes are not proof of a view-distance failure. An emissive diagnostic is not a finished outdoor lighting solution. Use Lighting diagnosis . Can a Lua script use my FBX as a normal weapon mesh? An FBX source file is not a cooked Unreal mesh/class. The local procedural/texture probes did not establish a finished usable weapon import pipeline. Decide whether you need a runtime preview or proper asset integration and document that scope. See Custom content . Both players arrive, but a gun is missing Check the local usable gun, selected template and remote cosmetic proxy separately. Travel can require reacquiring the pawn and rebuilding loadout/visual state. A friend’s visible model is not proof the local inventory weapon exists, and vice versa. See Weapon assemblies . Does Steam automatically synchronise every target and score? No. Identity, session and transport are separate from your gameplay protocol. Choose authority, event identity, target state and interruption rules, then test them on both peers. A successful invite or connection is only the first milestone. See Steam sessions . Does relaying mean there cannot be lag? Transport does not eliminate latency, jitter, loss or application mistakes. Observe the actual session and keep movement interpolation, reliable events and score timing as separate concerns. Don’t promise a particular route or performance from an invite screenshot. See Movement interpolation . Why are hits or chat displayed twice? Look for duplicate callback registration, base/derived handling of the same event, resend without deduplication, or a local cosmetic event replaying on its sender. Track event ID, session/round context and accepted acknowledgement. See Reliable events . Does sharing the host save merge progression? A one-way persisted snapshot does not merge simultaneous progress or replicate future unsaved changes. Receiving and applying must be separate actions with a verified backup/reload path. See Reading and backing up saves . A local test passed—can I claim full compatibility? State exactly which test passed. Language/mocks, native fixtures, real menu use, save/reload and independent real peers establish different things. A narrow result is still useful; expanding the claim without another test is not. See Test a mod step by step . Does this wiki now cover the whole game? It covers a broad practical modding surface, with explicit gaps in areas such as complete ballistic internals, universal ownership rules, full save schema and cooked-content support. Page count is not a measured fraction of the game. The coverage page records those limits and evidence levels.","sections":[{"id":"no-mod","title":"Why is there no startup message?","text":"Check a fresh loader log, then the actual installed folder, enabled marker, file extensions and script path. A source project and a ZIP existing on disk do not mean the loader is running either. Add a unique version prefix and follow Your first mod ."},{"id":"no-key","title":"The mod loads, but the key does nothing","text":"Focus the game, verify the configured key and remove duplicate/conflicting registrations in the test setup. Start with a log-only callback. If that works, the input registration is not the first broken layer; inspect object context and the scheduled action next. See Input and focus ."},{"id":"luau","title":"Can I use Roblox Luau code?","text":"The examples target UE4SS Lua and its APIs. Roblox services, Instance APIs and Luau-only syntax are not supplied by the game. Translate the actual language/API assumptions rather than changing the extension on a Roblox script. Begin with Lua basics ."},{"id":"trivial","title":"Why does a method say TrivialObject is not callable?","text":"Confirm the member is a reflected function exposed by this build. A familiar C++ name is not proof it is available through this binding. Inspect the dump for the actual K2/reflected method and verify the owning instance before calling it. See Reflection ."},{"id":"nil","title":"Why is a reference nil after it worked before?","text":"A menu rebuild, weapon switch or travel can replace the native owner. Reacquire world-specific references and cancel pending jobs with the old context. A Lua variable retaining a wrapper does not guarantee the native object remained valid. See Timers and cleanup ."},{"id":"freeze","title":"Why did an array loop freeze?","text":"Check whether it is a reflected Unreal container rather than a plain Lua table, and whether iteration can exceed the reported count. Also check callback recursion. The recorded wrapper issue was avoided with a bounded numeric loop; do not apply ipairs blindly to every engine container. See Arrays, sets and maps ."},{"id":"negative","title":"Removing a grip makes the stat negative—is that a bug?","text":"Compare the same field/context before fitting, while fitted and after removal. A negative baseline returning to itself is different from an accumulated penalty. Repeating the exact rail/part case and checking the editor/live/save lifetimes is essential. The recorded synthetic test did not reproduce the player’s alleged residue. See Weapon stats ."},{"id":"owned","title":"Why are known owned parts missing?","text":"Purchased, default, fitted, unlocked and visible are not the same states. Verify the stored reference type and a small set of named positive/negative examples against the real menu. Earlier save-index filters were not a completed reliable ownership API. See Ownership and unlocks ."},{"id":"card","title":"The custom range card works, but it opens the pool","text":"The card’s title/layout and the destination are separate. Check the actual map ID/package selected and the loaded world after travel. A new UI row does not create a new cooked map; a runtime scene can still be hosted by the pool world. See Range menus and travel ."},{"id":"dark","title":"The scene spawned but is almost black","text":"Keep a known object/material fixed and inspect placement, occlusion and the actual light/postprocess components affecting the view. Visible dark silhouettes are not proof of a view-distance failure. An emissive diagnostic is not a finished outdoor lighting solution. Use Lighting diagnosis ."},{"id":"fbx","title":"Can a Lua script use my FBX as a normal weapon mesh?","text":"An FBX source file is not a cooked Unreal mesh/class. The local procedural/texture probes did not establish a finished usable weapon import pipeline. Decide whether you need a runtime preview or proper asset integration and document that scope. See Custom content ."},{"id":"gun","title":"Both players arrive, but a gun is missing","text":"Check the local usable gun, selected template and remote cosmetic proxy separately. Travel can require reacquiring the pawn and rebuilding loadout/visual state. A friend’s visible model is not proof the local inventory weapon exists, and vice versa. See Weapon assemblies ."},{"id":"steam","title":"Does Steam automatically synchronise every target and score?","text":"No. Identity, session and transport are separate from your gameplay protocol. Choose authority, event identity, target state and interruption rules, then test them on both peers. A successful invite or connection is only the first milestone. See Steam sessions ."},{"id":"lag","title":"Does relaying mean there cannot be lag?","text":"Transport does not eliminate latency, jitter, loss or application mistakes. Observe the actual session and keep movement interpolation, reliable events and score timing as separate concerns. Don’t promise a particular route or performance from an invite screenshot. See Movement interpolation ."},{"id":"duplicate","title":"Why are hits or chat displayed twice?","text":"Look for duplicate callback registration, base/derived handling of the same event, resend without deduplication, or a local cosmetic event replaying on its sender. Track event ID, session/round context and accepted acknowledgement. See Reliable events ."},{"id":"save","title":"Does sharing the host save merge progression?","text":"A one-way persisted snapshot does not merge simultaneous progress or replicate future unsaved changes. Receiving and applying must be separate actions with a verified backup/reload path. See Reading and backing up saves ."},{"id":"tests","title":"A local test passed—can I claim full compatibility?","text":"State exactly which test passed. Language/mocks, native fixtures, real menu use, save/reload and independent real peers establish different things. A narrow result is still useful; expanding the claim without another test is not. See Test a mod step by step ."},{"id":"coverage","title":"Does this wiki now cover the whole game?","text":"It covers a broad practical modding surface, with explicit gaps in areas such as complete ballistic internals, universal ownership rules, full save schema and cooked-content support. Page count is not a measured fraction of the game. The coverage page records those limits and evidence levels."}]}];
